const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish of trumpets: then hautboys. Enter the KING, GLOSTER, SALISBURY, WARWICK, and CARDINAL BEAUFORT, on the one side; the QUEEN, SUFFOLK, YORK, SOMERSET, and BUCKINGHAM, on the other.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["As by your high imperial Majesty","I had in charge at my depart for France,","As procurator to your excellence,","To marry Princess Margaret for your grace,","So, in the famous ancient city Tours,","In presence of the Kings of France and Sicil,","The Dukes of Orleans, Calaber, Bretagne, and Alencon,","Seven earls, twelve barons, and twenty reverend bishops,","I have perform'd my task and was espous'd,","And humbly now upon my bended knee,","In sight of England and her lordly peers,","Deliver up my title in the queen","To your most gracious hands, that are the substance","Of that great shadow I did represent:","The happiest gift that ever marquess gave,","The fairest queen that ever king receiv'd."],"id":2,"m":"Your Majesty, when I left for France you ordered me, as your representative, to marry Princess Margaret on your behalf. So in the famous old city of Tours, before the Kings of France and Sicily, the Dukes of Orleans, Calabria, Brittany and Alencon, seven earls, twelve barons and twenty bishops, I carried out my task and married her for you. Now, kneeling humbly before England and her lords, I hand over my claim to the queen into your gracious hands. You are the real man of whom I was only the stand-in. It is the happiest gift a marquess ever gave, and the most beautiful queen a king ever received."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Suffolk, arise.—Welcome, Queen Margaret.","I can express no kinder sign of love","Than this kind kiss.—O Lord, that lends me life,","Lend me a heart replete with thankfulness!","For thou hast given me in this beauteous face","A world of earthly blessings to my soul,","If sympathy of love unite our thoughts."],"id":3,"m":"Stand up, Suffolk. Welcome, Queen Margaret. I can show my love no more kindly than with this kiss. O Lord who gives me life, give me a heart full of thanks! In this lovely face you have given my soul a whole world of earthly blessings, if our thoughts are joined in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Great King of England and my gracious lord,","The mutual conference that my mind hath had,","By day, by night, waking and in my dreams,","In courtly company or at my beads,","With you, mine alder-liefest sovereign,","Makes me the bolder to salute my king","With ruder terms, such as my wit affords","And over-joy of heart doth minister."],"id":4,"m":"Great King of England, my gracious lord, I have talked with you in my mind day and night, awake and dreaming, at court and at my prayers, my dearest sovereign. That makes me bold enough to greet you in plain words, as well as my wit and my overjoyed heart allow."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Her sight did ravish, but her grace in speech,","Her words yclad with wisdom's majesty,","Makes me from wondering fall to weeping joys;","Such is the fulness of my heart's content.—","Lords, with one cheerful voice welcome my love."],"id":5,"m":"Her looks delighted me, but her graceful speech, dressed in wise and royal words, turns my wonder into tears of joy, my heart is so full. Lords, all welcome my love with one cheerful voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["[Kneeling] Long live Queen Margaret, England's","happiness!"],"id":6,"m":"(Kneeling) Long live Queen Margaret, England's joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["We thank you all."],"id":7,"m":"We thank you all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":8},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["My Lord Protector, so it please your grace,","Here are the articles of contracted peace","Between our sovereign and the French king Charles,","For eighteen months concluded by consent."],"id":9,"m":"My Lord Protector, if it please you, here are the articles of the peace agreed between our king and the French king Charles, a truce settled for eighteen months."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Reads] 'Imprimis, It is agreed between the French king","Charles and William de la Pole, Marquess of Suffolk, ambassador","for Henry King of England, that the said Henry shall espouse the","Lady Margaret, daughter unto Reignier King of Naples, Sicilia,","and Jerusalem, and crown her Queen of England ere the thirtieth","of May next ensuing. Item, that the duchy of Anjou and the","county of Maine shall be released and delivered to the king her","father'—"],"id":10,"m":"(Reading) \"First, it is agreed between Charles, King of France, and William de la Pole, Marquess of Suffolk, ambassador for Henry King of England, that Henry shall marry Lady Margaret, daughter of Reignier King of Naples, Sicily and Jerusalem, and crown her Queen of England before the thirtieth of May. Also, that the duchy of Anjou and the county of Maine shall be released and handed over to her father—\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Lets the paper fall.","id":11},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Uncle, how now!"],"id":12,"m":"Uncle, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Pardon me, gracious lord;","Some sudden qualm hath struck me at the heart","And dimm'd mine eyes, that I can read no further."],"id":13,"m":"Forgive me, my lord. A sudden sickness has struck my heart and blurred my eyes, so that I can't read any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Uncle of Winchester, I pray, read on."],"id":14,"m":"Uncle Winchester, please read on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Reads] 'Item, It is further agreed between them,","that the duchies of Anjou and Maine shall be released and","delivered over to the king her father, and she sent over of the","King of","England's own proper cost and charges, without having any dowry.'"],"id":15,"m":"(Reading) \"Also, it is further agreed that the duchies of Anjou and Maine shall be released and handed over to her father, and that she shall be brought over at the King of England's own cost, without any dowry.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["They please us well.—Lord marquess, kneel down.","We here create thee the first duke of Suffolk,","And girt thee with the sword.—Cousin of York,","We here discharge your grace from being regent","I' the parts of France, till term of eighteen months","Be full expir'd.—Thanks, uncle Winchester,","Gloster, York, Buckingham, Somerset,","Salisbury, and Warwick;","We thank you all for this great favour done","In entertainment to my princely queen.","Come, let us in, and with all speed provide","To see her coronation be perform'd."],"id":16,"m":"They please us well. Lord Marquess, kneel. We make you the first Duke of Suffolk and fasten on your sword. Cousin York, we relieve you of your post as regent in France until eighteen months are over. Thank you, Uncle Winchester, Gloucester, York, Buckingham, Somerset, Salisbury and Warwick, for this great kindness in welcoming my queen. Come, let's go in and quickly arrange her coronation."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King, Queen, and Suffolk.","id":17},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Brave peers of England, pillars of the state,","To you Duke Humphrey must unload his grief,","Your grief, the common grief of all the land.","What! did my brother Henry spend his youth,","His valour, coin, and people, in the wars?","Did he so often lodge in open field,","In winter's cold and summer's parching heat,","To conquer France, his true inheritance?","And did my brother Bedford toil his wits","To keep by policy what Henry got?","Have you yourselves, Somerset, Buckingham,","Brave York, Salisbury, and victorious Warwick,","Receiv'd deep scars in France and Normandy?","Or hath mine uncle Beaufort and myself,","With all the learned counsel of the realm,","Studied so long, sat in the council-house","Early and late, debating to and fro","How France and Frenchmen might be kept in awe,","And had his highness in his infancy","Crowned in Paris in despite of foes?","And shall these labours and these honours die?","Shall Henry's conquest, Bedford's vigilance,","Your deeds of war, and all our counsel die?","O peers of England, shameful is this league!","Fatal this marriage, cancelling your fame,","Blotting your names from books of memory,","Razing the characters of your renown,","Defacing monuments of conquer'd France,","Undoing all, as all had never been!"],"id":18,"m":"Noble lords of England, pillars of the state, I must unload my grief on you, Duke Humphrey's grief, which is the grief of the whole country. What! Did my brother Henry the Fifth spend his youth, courage, money and men in the wars? Did he so often camp in open fields in winter cold and summer heat to conquer France, his rightful inheritance? Did my brother Bedford wear out his brains to keep by clever policy what Henry won? Have you yourselves, Somerset, Buckingham, brave York, Salisbury and victorious Warwick, taken deep wounds in France and Normandy? Have my uncle Beaufort and I, with the realm's wisest counsellors, sat in council early and late, arguing how to keep France and the French in fear, and had the king crowned in Paris as a child in spite of his enemies? And shall all this work and honour die? Henry's conquest, Bedford's watchfulness, your fighting and all our counsel? O lords of England, this treaty is shameful! This marriage is fatal: it cancels your fame, blots your names out of history, erases the record of your glory, defaces the monuments of conquered France, and undoes everything as if it had never been!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Nephew, what means this passionate discourse,","This peroration with such circumstance?","For France, 't is ours; and we will keep it still."],"id":19,"m":"Nephew, what is this emotional speech, this long-winded oration? As for France, it's ours, and we will keep it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ay, uncle, we will keep it if we can,","But now it is impossible we should.","Suffolk, the new-made duke that rules the roast,","Hath given the duchy of Anjou and Maine","Unto the poor King Reignier, whose large style","Agrees not with the leanness of his purse."],"id":20,"m":"Yes, uncle, we'll keep it if we can, but now it's impossible. Suffolk, the new duke who runs everything, has given Anjou and Maine to poor King Reignier, whose grand titles don't match his thin purse."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Now, by the death of Him that died for all,","These counties were the keys of Normandy!—","But wherefore weeps Warwick, my valiant son?"],"id":21,"m":"By Christ who died for us all, those counties were the keys to Normandy! But why is Warwick, my brave son, weeping?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["For grief that they are past recovery;","For, were there hope to conquer them again,","My sword should shed hot blood, mine eyes no tears.","Anjou and Maine! myself did win them both,","Those provinces these arms of mine did conquer;","And are the cities that I got with wounds","Deliver'd up again with peaceful words?","Mort Dieu!"],"id":22,"m":"In grief that they can never be won back. If there were hope of conquering them again, my sword would shed hot blood, not my eyes tears. Anjou and Maine! I won them both myself; these arms conquered those provinces. Are cities I won with wounds handed back with peaceful words? God's death!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["For Suffolk's duke, may he be suffocate,","That dims the honour of this warlike isle!","France should have torn and rent my very heart,","Before I would have yielded to this league.","I never read but England's kings have had","Large sums of gold and dowries with their wives;","And our King Henry gives away his own,","To match with her that brings no vantages."],"id":23,"m":"As for Suffolk's dukedom, may he be suffocated, since he dims the honour of this warlike island! France should have torn out my heart before I agreed to this treaty. I've only ever read that England's kings got big sums of gold and dowries with their wives, and our King Henry gives away his own land to marry a woman who brings no advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["A proper jest, and never heard before,","That Suffolk should demand a whole fifteenth","For costs and charges in transporting her!","She should have staid in France, and starv'd in France,","Before—"],"id":24,"m":"A fine joke, never heard before: Suffolk demands a fifteenth of the nation's wealth as a tax to pay for bringing her over! She should have stayed in France and starved in France before—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["My Lord of Gloster, now ye grow too hot;","It was the pleasure of my lord the King."],"id":25,"m":"My Lord of Gloucester, you're getting too heated. This was the king's wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["My Lord of Winchester, I know your mind;","'T is not my speeches that you do mislike,","But 't is my presence that doth trouble ye.","Rancour will out.","Proud prelate, in thy face","I see thy fury; if I longer stay,","We shall begin our ancient bickerings.—","Lordings, farewell; and say, when I am gone,","I prophesied France will be lost ere long."],"id":26,"m":"My Lord of Winchester, I know what you think. It's not my speeches you dislike but my presence that troubles you. Hatred will show itself. Proud churchman, I see your fury in your face; if I stay longer we'll start our old quarrels again. Lords, farewell, and say when I'm gone that I foretold France will soon be lost."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":27},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["So, there goes our protector in a rage.","'T is known to you he is mine enemy,","Nay, more, an enemy unto you all,","And no great friend, I fear me, to the king.","Consider, lords, he is the next of blood,","And heir apparent to the English crown.","Had Henry got an empire by his marriage,","And all the wealthy kingdoms of the west,","There's reason he should be displeas'd at it.","Look to it, lords.","Let not his smoothing words","Bewitch your hearts; be wise and circumspect.","What though the common people favour him,","Calling him 'Humphrey, the good Duke of Gloster,'","Clapping their hands, and crying with loud voice,","'Jesu maintain your royal excellence!'","With 'God preserve the good Duke Humphrey!'","I fear me, lords, for all this flattering gloss,","He will be found a dangerous protector."],"id":28,"m":"So there goes our Protector in a rage. You know he is my enemy, and an enemy to all of you too, and, I fear, no great friend to the king. Remember, lords, he is next in blood and heir to the English crown. If Henry had won an empire and all the rich western kingdoms by his marriage, Gloucester would still have reason to be displeased. Watch out, lords. Don't let his smooth words bewitch you; be wise and careful. Even if the common people love him, calling him \"Humphrey, the good Duke of Gloucester\", clapping and shouting \"Jesus keep your royal excellence!\" and \"God save the good Duke Humphrey!\", I fear, lords, that for all this flattering show he will prove a dangerous Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Why should he, then, protect our sovereign,","He being of age to govern of himself?—","Cousin of Somerset, join you with me,","And all together, with the Duke of Suffolk,","We'll quickly hoise Duke Humphrey from his seat."],"id":29,"m":"Then why should he protect our king, when the king is old enough to rule for himself? Cousin Somerset, join with me, and together with the Duke of Suffolk we'll soon heave Duke Humphrey out of his place."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["This weighty business will not brook delay;","I'll to the Duke of Suffolk presently."],"id":30,"m":"This important business won't wait. I'll go to the Duke of Suffolk at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":31},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Cousin of Buckingham, though Humphrey's pride","And greatness of his place be grief to us,","Yet let us watch the haughty cardinal;","His insolence is more intolerable","Than all the princes in the land beside;","If Gloster be displac'd, he 'll be protector."],"id":32,"m":"Cousin Buckingham, though Humphrey's pride and great position annoy us, we must also watch the arrogant cardinal. His insolence is harder to bear than that of all the other princes in the land. If Gloucester is removed, he'll be Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Or thou or I, Somerset, will be protector,","Despite Duke Humphrey or the cardinal."],"id":33,"m":"Either you or I will be Protector, Somerset, in spite of Duke Humphrey or the cardinal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Buckingham and Somerset.","id":34},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Pride went before, ambition follows him.","While these do labour for their own preferment,","Behoves it us to labour for the realm.","I never saw but Humphrey Duke of Gloster","Did bear him like a noble gentleman.","Oft have I seen the haughty cardinal,","More like a soldier than a man o' the church,","As stout and proud as he were lord of all,","Swear like a ruffian and demean himself","Unlike the ruler of a commonweal.—","Warwick my son, the comfort of my age,","Thy deeds, thy plainness, and thy housekeeping,","Hath won the greatest favour of the commons,","Excepting none but good Duke Humphrey;—","And, brother York, thy acts in Ireland,","In bringing them to civil discipline,","Thy late exploits done in the heart of France,","When thou wert regent for our sovereign,","Have made thee fear'd and honour'd of the people.—","Join we together, for the public good,","In what we can, to bridle and suppress","The pride of Suffolk and the cardinal,","With Somerset's and Buckingham's ambition,","And, as we may, cherish Duke Humphrey's deeds","While they do tend the profit of the land."],"id":35,"m":"Pride went first; ambition follows it. While these men work for their own advancement, we must work for the kingdom. I have never seen Humphrey Duke of Gloucester behave other than as a noble gentleman. I have often seen the arrogant cardinal, more like a soldier than a churchman, as bold and proud as if he owned everything, swear like a thug and behave unlike a ruler of the state. Warwick, my son, comfort of my old age, your deeds, your honesty and your generous hospitality have won the commons' greatest love, second only to good Duke Humphrey. And brother York, your work in Ireland bringing it to order, and your recent exploits in the heart of France as regent, have made the people fear and honour you. Let's join together for the public good to check and hold down the pride of Suffolk and the cardinal, and the ambition of Somerset and Buckingham, and, as far as we can, support Duke Humphrey's actions while they serve the country."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["So God help Warwick, as he loves the land","And common profit of his country!"],"id":36,"m":"God help Warwick as surely as he loves the country and the common good!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["[Aside] And so says York, for he hath greatest cause."],"id":37,"m":"(Aside) And so says York, for he has the greatest reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Then let's make haste away, and look unto the main."],"id":38,"m":"Then let's hurry and look to the main business."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Unto the main! O father, Maine is lost;","That Maine which by main force Warwick did win,","And would have kept so long as breath did last!","Main chance, father, you meant; but I meant Maine,","Which I will win from France, or else be slain."],"id":39,"m":"The main! Father, Maine is lost, the Maine that Warwick won by main force and would have held as long as he had breath! You meant the main chance, father, but I meant Maine, and I will win it back from France or die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Warwick and Salisbury.","id":40},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Anjou and Maine are given to the French;","Paris is lost; the state of Normandy","Stands on a tickle point now they are gone.","Suffolk concluded on the articles,","The peers agreed; and Henry was well pleas'd","To changes two dukedoms for a duke's fair daughter.","I cannot blame them all: what is't to them?","'T is thine they give away, and not their own.","Pirates may make cheap pennyworths of their pillage,","And purchase friends, and give to courtesans,","Still revelling like lords till all be gone;","Whileas the silly owner of the goods","Weeps over them and wrings his hapless hands","And shakes his head and trembling stands aloof,","While all is shar'd and all is borne away,","Ready to starve and dare not touch his own.","So York must sit and fret and bite his tongue,","While his own lands are bargain'd for and sold.","Methinks the realms of England, France, and Ireland","Bear that proportion to my flesh and blood","As did the fatal brand Althaea burn'd","Unto the prince's heart of Calydon.","Anjou and Maine both given unto the French!","Cold news for me, for I had hope of France,","Even as I have of fertile England's soil.","A day will come when York shall claim his own;","And therefore I will take the Nevils' parts,","And make a show of love to proud Duke Humphrey,","And when I spy advantage, claim the crown,","For that 's the golden mark I seek to hit.","Nor shall proud Lancaster usurp my right,","Nor hold the sceptre in his childish fist,","Nor wear the diadem upon his head,","Whose church-like humours fits not for a crown.","Then, York, be still awhile till time do serve;","Watch thou and wake when others be asleep,","To pry into the secrets of the state;","Till Henry, surfeiting in joys of love,","With his new bride and England's dear-bought queen,","And Humphrey with the peers be fallen at jars.","Then will I raise aloft the milk-white rose,","With whose sweet smell the air shall be perfum'd,","And in my standard bear the arms of York,","To grapple with the house of Lancaster;","And, force perforce, I 'll make him yield the crown","Whose bookish rule hath pull'd fair England down."],"id":41,"m":"Anjou and Maine are given to the French. Paris is lost, and the state of Normandy is on a knife edge now they're gone. Suffolk settled the terms, the lords agreed, and Henry was happy to swap two dukedoms for a duke's pretty daughter. I can't blame them all; what does it matter to them? It's yours they're giving away, York, not their own. Pirates can sell their loot cheaply, buy friends and give to whores, partying like lords till it's all gone, while the poor owner of the goods weeps over them, wrings his hands, shakes his head and stands back trembling while everything is shared out and carried off, starving and not daring to touch his own. So York must sit, fret and bite his tongue while his own lands are bargained away and sold. England, France and Ireland seem as bound up with my flesh and blood as the fatal log that Althaea burned was with the life of the prince of Calydon. Anjou and Maine both given to the French! Cold news for me, for I had hopes of France, just as I have of fertile England. A day will come when York will claim what's his. So I'll take the Nevilles' side, pretend to love proud Duke Humphrey, and when I see my chance, claim the crown, for that's the golden target I aim at. Proud Lancaster won't steal my right, or hold the sceptre in his childish fist, or wear the crown on his head, when his churchy temper is unfit for a crown. So, York, keep quiet for a while until the time is right. Stay awake while others sleep, and pry into the secrets of the state, until Henry is glutted with love for his new bride, England's expensive queen, and Humphrey and the lords are at odds. Then I'll raise the white rose high, whose sweet smell will perfume the air, carry the arms of York on my banner to wrestle with the house of Lancaster, and by force make him give up the crown whose bookish rule has dragged fair England down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":42},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The Duke of Gloster's House.","id":43},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DUKE HUMPHREY and his wife ELEANOR.","id":44},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Why droops my lord, like over-ripen'd corn,","Hanging the head at Ceres' plenteous load?","Why doth the great Duke Humphrey knit his brows,","As frowning at the favours of the world?","Why are thine eyes fix'd to the sullen earth,","Gazing on that which seems to dim thy sight?","What see'st thou there? King Henry's diadem,","Enchas'd with all the honours of the world?","If so, gaze on, and grovel on thy face,","Until thy head be circled with the same.","Put forth thy hand, reach at the glorious gold.","What, is't too short? I'll lengthen it with mine,","And, having both together heav'd it up,","We'll both together lift our heads to heaven,","And never more abase our sight so low","As to vouchsafe one glance unto the ground."],"id":45,"m":"Why does my lord droop like over-ripe corn, its head bowed under the weight of the harvest? Why does great Duke Humphrey frown as if the world's favours displeased him? Why are your eyes fixed on the gloomy ground, staring at something that seems to darken your sight? What do you see there? King Henry's crown, set with all the world's honours? If so, keep gazing, and crawl on your face until that crown circles your head. Reach out your hand to the glorious gold. What, is it too short? I'll lengthen it with mine, and when we've lifted it up together, we'll both raise our heads to heaven and never again lower our eyes so far as to glance at the ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["O Nell, sweet Nell, if thou dost love thy lord,","Banish the canker of ambitious thoughts;","And may that thought when I imagine ill","Against my king and nephew, virtuous Henry,","Be my last breathing in this mortal world!","My troublous dreams this night doth make me sad."],"id":46,"m":"O Nell, sweet Nell, if you love your husband, get rid of these cankerous ambitious thoughts. And if I ever think ill against my king and nephew, virtuous Henry, may that thought be my last breath in this world! My troubled dreams last night have made me sad."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["What dream'd my lord? Tell me, and I'll requite it","With sweet rehearsal of my morning's dream."],"id":47,"m":"What did you dream, my lord? Tell me, and I'll repay you with a sweet account of my dream this morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Methought this staff, mine office-badge in court,","Was broke in twain;—by whom I have forgot,","But, as I think, it was by the cardinal,—","And on the pieces of the broken wand","Were plac'd the heads of Edmund Duke of Somerset","And William de la Pole, first duke of Suffolk.","This was my dream; what it doth bode, God knows."],"id":48,"m":"I dreamed that this staff, my badge of office at court, was broken in two, by whom I've forgotten, but I think it was the cardinal. And on the broken pieces were placed the heads of Edmund Duke of Somerset and William de la Pole, first Duke of Suffolk. That was my dream; God knows what it means."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Tut, this was nothing but an argument","That he that breaks a stick of Gloster's grove","Shall lose his head for his presumption.","But list to me, my Humphrey, my sweet duke:","Methought I sat in seat of majesty","In the cathedral church of Westminster","And in that chair where kings and queens are crown'd,","Where Henry and Dame Margaret kneel'd to me","And on my head did set the diadem."],"id":49,"m":"Nonsense, that only meant that anyone who breaks a stick in Gloucester's grove will lose his head for his cheek. But listen to me, my Humphrey, my sweet duke. I dreamed I sat on the throne in Westminster Abbey, in the chair where kings and queens are crowned, and Henry and Margaret knelt to me and set the crown on my head."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Nay, Eleanor, then must I chide outright.","Presumptuous dame, ill-nurtur'd Eleanor,","Art thou not second woman in the realm,","And the protector's wife, belov'd of him?","Hast thou not worldly pleasure at command,","Above the reach or compass of thy thought?","And wilt thou still be hammering treachery,","To tumble down thy husband and thyself","From top of honour to disgrace's feet?","Away from me, and let me hear no more!"],"id":50,"m":"No, Eleanor, now I must scold you plainly. Arrogant woman, badly brought up Eleanor! Aren't you the second lady in the kingdom, the Protector's wife, and loved by him? Don't you have every worldly pleasure at your command, beyond what you can even imagine? And will you still keep forging treason, to throw your husband and yourself down from the height of honour to the feet of disgrace? Leave me, and let me hear no more!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["What, what, my lord! are you so choleric","With Eleanor for telling but her dream?","Next time I'll keep my dreams unto myself,","And not be check'd."],"id":51,"m":"What, my lord! Are you so angry with Eleanor just for telling her dream? Next time I'll keep my dreams to myself and not be told off."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Nay, be not angry; I am pleas'd again."],"id":52,"m":"No, don't be angry; I'm happy again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Messenger.","id":53},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord protector, 't is his highness' pleasure","You do prepare to ride unto Saint Alban's,","Whereas the king and queen do mean to hawk."],"id":54,"m":"My Lord Protector, his Highness wants you to prepare to ride to St Albans, where the king and queen intend to go hawking."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["I go.—Come, Nell, thou wilt ride with us?"],"id":55,"m":"I'm going. Come, Nell, will you ride with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Yes, my good lord, I'll follow presently."],"id":56,"m":"Yes, my good lord, I'll follow shortly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gloster and Messenger.","id":57},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","cont":1,"lines":["Follow I must; I cannot go before","While Gloster bears this base and humble mind.","Were I a man, a duke, and next of blood,","I would remove these tedious stumbling-blocks","And smooth my way upon their headless necks;","And, being a woman, I will not be slack","To play my part in Fortune's pageant.—","Where are you there? Sir John! nay, fear not, man,","We are alone; here's none but thee and I."],"id":58,"m":"I must follow; I can't go in front while Gloucester has such a low, humble mind. If I were a man, a duke and next in line to the throne, I'd remove these tiresome obstacles and smooth my way over their headless necks. And though I'm a woman, I won't be slow to play my part in Fortune's show. Who's there? Sir John! Don't be afraid, man, we're alone; there's nobody but you and me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HUME.","id":59},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["Jesus preserve your royal majesty!"],"id":60,"m":"Jesus keep your royal majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["What say'st thou? majesty! I am but grace."],"id":61,"m":"What did you say? Majesty! I'm only \"your grace\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["But, by the grace of God, and Hume's advice,","Your grace's title shall be multiplied."],"id":62,"m":"But by the grace of God and Hume's advice, your grace's title will be raised higher."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["What say'st thou, man? hast thou as yet conferr'd","With Margery Jourdain, the cunning witch,","With Roger Bolingbroke, the conjurer?","And will they undertake to do me good?"],"id":63,"m":"What do you say, man? Have you met with Margery Jourdain, the clever witch, and Roger Bolingbroke, the conjurer? Will they do what I want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["This they have promised,—to show your highness","A spirit rais'd from depth of underground,","That shall make answer to such questions","As by your Grace shall be propounded him."],"id":64,"m":"They have promised this: to show your highness a spirit raised from deep underground, which will answer whatever questions your grace puts to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["It is enough; I'll think upon the questions.","When from Saint Alban's we do make return,","We'll see these things effected to the full.","Here, Hume, take this reward; make merry, man,","With thy confederates in this weighty cause."],"id":65,"m":"That's enough. I'll think about the questions. When we come back from St Albans, we'll see this carried out fully. Here, Hume, take this reward. Enjoy yourself, man, with your partners in this important business."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":66},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["Hume must make merry with the duchess' gold,","Marry, and shall. But, how now, Sir John Hume!","Seal up your lips, and give no words but mum;","The business asketh silent secrecy.","Dame Eleanor gives gold to bring the witch;","Gold cannot come amiss, were she a devil.","Yet have I gold flies from another coast.","I dare not say, from the rich cardinal","And from the great and new-made Duke of Suffolk,","Yet I do find it so; for, to be plain,","They, knowing Dame Eleanor's aspiring humour,","Have hired me to undermine the duchess","And buzz these conjurations in her brain.","They say ' A crafty knave does need no broker;'","Yet am I Suffolk and the cardinal's broker.","Hume, if you take not heed, you shall go near","To call them both a pair of crafty knaves.","Well, so its stands; and thus, I fear, at last","Hume's knavery will be the duchess' wrack,","And her attainture will be Humphrey's fall.","Sort how it will, I shall have gold for all."],"id":67,"m":"Hume is to enjoy himself with the duchess's gold? Indeed he will. But careful, Sir John Hume! Seal your lips and say nothing but \"mum\"; this business needs silence. Dame Eleanor gives gold to bring the witch; gold is always welcome, even from a devil. But I also have gold coming from another direction. I daren't say from the rich cardinal and the great new Duke of Suffolk, but that's how it is. To be plain, knowing Dame Eleanor's ambitious temper, they've hired me to bring down the duchess and fill her head with these conjurings. They say \"a clever rogue doesn't need an agent\", yet I'm Suffolk's and the cardinal's agent. Hume, if you're not careful you'll end up calling them both a pair of clever rogues. Well, that's how it stands, and I fear in the end Hume's trickery will wreck the duchess, and her disgrace will be Humphrey's fall. However it turns out, I'll get gold from everyone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":68},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"London. The palace.","id":69},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PETER and other PETITIONERS.","id":70},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PETITIONER","lines":["My masters, let's stand close; my lord protector","will come this way by and by, and then we may deliver our","supplications in the quill."],"id":71,"m":"Friends, let's stand close together. My Lord Protector will come this way soon, and then we can hand in our petitions in one go."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND PETITIONER","lines":["Marry, the Lord protect him, for he's a good","man! Jesu bless him!"],"id":72,"m":"Indeed, the Lord protect him, for he's a good man! Jesus bless him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SUFFOLK and QUEEN.","id":73},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["Here 'a comes, methinks, and the queen with him.","I'll be the first, sure."],"id":74,"m":"Here he comes, I think, and the queen with him. I'll be first, for sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND PETITIONER","lines":["Come back, fool; this is the Duke of Suffolk and","not my lord protector."],"id":75,"m":"Come back, fool; this is the Duke of Suffolk, not my Lord Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["How now, fellow! wouldst any thing with me?"],"id":76,"m":"Well, fellow, do you want something from me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PETITIONER","lines":["I pray, my lord, pardon me; I took ye for my lord","protector."],"id":77,"m":"I beg your pardon, my lord; I mistook you for my Lord Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["[Reading] 'To my Lord Protector!' Are your supplications","to his lordship? Let me see them; what is thine?"],"id":78,"m":"(Reading) \"To my Lord Protector!\" Are your petitions for him? Let me see them. What's yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PETITIONER","lines":["Mine is, an 't please your grace, against John","Goodman, my lord cardinal's man, for keeping my house and lands,","and wife and all, from me."],"id":79,"m":"Mine, if it please your grace, is against John Goodman, my lord cardinal's servant, for keeping my house, my land, my wife and everything from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thy wife too! that's some wrong, indeed.—What's","yours?—What's here! [Reads] 'Against the Duke of Suffolk for","enclosing","the commons of Melford.'—How now, sir knave!"],"id":80,"m":"Your wife too! That is a real wrong. What's yours? What's this! (Reading) \"Against the Duke of Suffolk, for fencing off the common land of Melford.\" What, you rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND PETITIONER","lines":["Alas, sir, I am but a poor petitioner of our","whole township."],"id":81,"m":"Sorry, sir, I'm only a poor petitioner speaking for our whole town."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["[Giving his petition] Against my master, Thomas Horner,","for saying that the Duke of York was rightful heir to the crown."],"id":82,"m":"(Giving his petition) Against my master, Thomas Horner, for saying that the Duke of York was the rightful heir to the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What say'st thou? did the Duke of York say he was","rightful heir to the crown?"],"id":83,"m":"What are you saying? Did the Duke of York say he was rightful heir to the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["That my master was? no, forsooth; my master said that he","was, and that the king was an usurper."],"id":84,"m":"That my master was? No, truly; my master said that York was, and that the king was a usurper."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Who is there? [Enter Servant] Take this fellow in, and","send for his master with a pursuivant presently.—We'll hear more","of your matter before the king."],"id":85,"m":"Who's there? (Enter Servant) Take this fellow inside, and send an officer to fetch his master at once. We'll hear more about this in front of the king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant with Peter.","id":86},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And as for you, that love to be protected","Under the wings of our protector's grace,","Begin your suits anew and sue to him."],"id":87,"m":"And as for you, who like to be protected under the wings of our Protector, start your petitions again and take them to him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Tears the supplications.","id":88},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Away, base cullions!—Suffolk, let them go."],"id":89,"m":"Get out, you scum! Suffolk, let them go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Come, let's be gone."],"id":90,"m":"Come, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":91},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["My Lord of Suffolk, say, is this the guise,","Is this the fashion in the court of England?","Is this the government of Britain's isle,","And this the royalty of Albion's king?","What, shall King Henry be a pupil still","Under the surly Gloster's governance?","Am I a queen in title and in style,","And must be made a subject to a duke?","I tell thee, Pole, when in the city Tours","Thou ran'st a tilt in honour of my love","And stol'st away the ladies' hearts of France,","I thought King Henry had resembled thee","In courage, courtship, and proportion;","But all his mind is bent to holiness,","To number Ave-Maries on his beads,","His champions are the prophets and apostles,","His weapons holy saws of sacred writ,","His study is his tilt-yard, and his loves","Are brazen images of canoniz'd saints.","I would the college of the cardinals","Would choose him pope and carry him to Rome,","And set the triple crown upon his head;","That were a state fit for his holiness."],"id":92,"m":"My Lord of Suffolk, tell me, is this the custom, the fashion at the English court? Is this how Britain is governed, and this the royalty of England's king? What, is King Henry to stay a schoolboy under surly Gloucester's control? Am I a queen in title and name, only to be made a duke's subject? I tell you, Pole, when you jousted in Tours in honour of my love and stole the hearts of the French ladies, I thought King Henry would be like you in courage, courtliness and looks. But his mind is all set on holiness, counting Hail Marys on his rosary. His champions are the prophets and apostles, his weapons holy sayings from scripture, his study is his jousting ground, and his loves are bronze statues of saints. I wish the college of cardinals would elect him pope, carry him off to Rome and put the triple crown on his head. That would be a position to suit his holiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Madam, be patient; as I was cause","Your highness came to England, so will I","In England work your grace's full content."],"id":93,"m":"Madam, be patient. As I was the reason you came to England, so I will make sure you are fully satisfied in England."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Beside the haughty protector, have we Beaufort","The imperious churchman, Somerset, Buckingham,","And grumbling York; and not the least of these","But can do more in England than the king."],"id":94,"m":"Besides the arrogant Protector, we have Beaufort, the domineering churchman, Somerset, Buckingham and grumbling York, and even the least of them has more power in England than the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And he of these that can do most of all","Cannot do more in England than the Nevils;","Salisbury and Warwick are no simple peers."],"id":95,"m":"And the most powerful of them can do no more in England than the Nevilles. Salisbury and Warwick are no ordinary lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Not all these lords do vex me half so much","As that proud dame, the lord protector's wife.","She sweeps it through the court with troops of ladies,","More like an empress than Duke Humphrey's wife.","Strangers in court do take her for the queen;","She bears a duke's revenues on her back,","And in her heart she scorns our poverty.","Shall I not live to be aveng'd on her?","Contemptuous base-born callat as she is,","She vaunted 'mongst her minions t' other day,","The very train of her worst wearing gown","Was better worth than all my father's land","Till Suffolk gave two dukedoms for his daughter."],"id":96,"m":"None of these lords annoys me half as much as that proud woman, the Protector's wife. She sweeps through the court with crowds of ladies, more like an empress than Duke Humphrey's wife. Foreigners at court take her for the queen. She wears a duke's income on her back, and in her heart she sneers at our poverty. Won't I live to get revenge on her? The contemptuous, low-born slut boasted among her favourites the other day that the train of her worst gown was worth more than all my father's land, until Suffolk gave two dukedoms for his daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Madam, myself have lim'd a bush for her,","And plac'd a quire of such enticing birds","That she will light to listen to the lays,","And never mount to trouble you again.","So, let her rest; and, madam, list to me,","For I am bold to counsel you in this.","Although we fancy not the cardinal,","Yet must we join with him and with the lords","Till we have brought Duke Humphrey in disgrace.","As for the Duke of York, this late complaint","Will make but little for his benefit.","So, one by one, we'll weed them all at last,","And you yourself shall steer the happy helm."],"id":97,"m":"Madam, I've set a trap for her, smeared a bush with birdlime and placed a choir of such tempting birds there that she'll land to listen to their songs and never fly up to trouble you again. So leave her alone, and listen to me, madam, for I'm bold enough to advise you. Though we don't like the cardinal, we must join with him and the lords until we've brought Duke Humphrey into disgrace. As for the Duke of York, this recent accusation won't do him much good. So one by one we'll weed them all out at last, and you yourself will steer the ship of state."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Enter the KING, DUKE HUMPHREY, CARDINAL BEAUFORT, BUCKINGHAM, YORK, SOMERSET, SALISBURY, WARWICK, and the DUCHESS OF GLOSTER.","id":98},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["For my part, noble lords, I care not which;","Or Somerset or York, all's one to me."],"id":99,"m":"For my part, noble lords, I don't mind which, Somerset or York; it's all the same to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["If York have ill demean'd himself in France,","Then let him be denay'd the regentship."],"id":100,"m":"If York has behaved badly in France, then let him be refused the regentship."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["If Somerset be unworthy of the place,","Let York be regent; I will yield to him."],"id":101,"m":"If Somerset is unworthy of the post, let York be regent; I'll give way to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Whether your grace be worthy, yea or no,","Dispute not that; York is the worthier."],"id":102,"m":"Whether your grace is worthy or not, don't argue about it: York is the worthier."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Ambitious Warwick, let thy betters speak."],"id":103,"m":"Ambitious Warwick, let your betters speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["The cardinal's not my better in the field."],"id":104,"m":"The cardinal is not my better on the battlefield."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["All in this presence are thy betters, Warwick."],"id":105,"m":"Everyone here is your better, Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Warwick may live to be the best of all."],"id":106,"m":"Warwick may live to be the best of all."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Peace, son!—and show some reason, Buckingham,","Why Somerset should be preferr'd in this."],"id":107,"m":"Quiet, son! And Buckingham, give some reason why Somerset should be preferred."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Because the king, forsooth, will have it so."],"id":108,"m":"Because the king, of course, wants it so."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Madam, the King is old enough himself","To give his censure; these are no women's matters."],"id":109,"m":"Madam, the king is old enough to give his own judgement. These are not women's affairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["If he be old enough, what needs your grace","To be protector of his excellence?"],"id":110,"m":"If he's old enough, why does your grace need to be his Protector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Madam, I am protector of the realm,","And, at his pleasure, will resign my place."],"id":111,"m":"Madam, I am Protector of the kingdom, and I will resign my post when he wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Resign it then, and leave thine insolence.","Since thou wert king—as who is king but thou?—","The commonwealth hath daily run to wrack;","The Dauphin hath prevail'd beyond the seas;","And all the peers and nobles of the realm","Have been as bondmen to thy sovereignty."],"id":112,"m":"Then resign it, and stop your insolence. Since you became king, for who is king but you?, the state has been going to ruin every day. The Dauphin has won abroad, and all the lords of the realm have been like slaves under your rule."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["The commons hast thou rack'd; the clergy's bags","Are lank and lean with thy extortions."],"id":113,"m":"You have squeezed the common people, and the clergy's purses are thin and empty from your demands."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Thy sumptuous buildings and thy wife's attire","Have cost a mass of public treasury."],"id":114,"m":"Your expensive buildings and your wife's clothes have cost a fortune of public money."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Thy cruelty in execution","Upon offenders hath exceeded law,","And left thee to the mercy of the law."],"id":115,"m":"Your cruelty in punishing criminals has gone beyond the law, and leaves you at the law's mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Thy sale of offices and towns in France,","If they were known, as the suspect is great,","Would make thee quickly hop without thy head.—"],"id":116,"m":"Your sale of offices and towns in France, if it were known, and it is strongly suspected, would soon have you hopping about without your head."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Gloster. The Queen drops her fan.","id":117},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Give me my fan. What minion! can ye not?"],"id":118,"m":"Give me my fan. What, you hussy! Can't you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"She gives the Duchess a box on the ear.","id":119},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["I cry your mercy, madam; was it you?"],"id":120,"m":"I beg your pardon, madam; was it you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Was 't I! yea, I it was, proud Frenchwoman.","Could I come near your beauty with my nails,","I'd set my ten commandments in your face."],"id":121,"m":"Was it me? Yes, it was me, you proud Frenchwoman. If I could get near your pretty face with my nails, I'd write my ten commandments on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Sweet aunt, be quiet; 't was against her will."],"id":122,"m":"Sweet aunt, be quiet. She didn't mean it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Against her will! good king, look to 't in time;","She'll hamper thee and dandle thee like a baby.","Though in this place most master wear no breeches,","She shall not strike Dame Eleanor unreveng'd."],"id":123,"m":"Didn't mean it! Good king, watch out in time. She'll tie you up and dandle you like a baby. Though the person who wears the trousers here is no man, she won't strike Dame Eleanor without revenge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":124},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Lord cardinal, I will follow Eleanor,","And listen after Humphrey, how he proceeds.","She's tickled now; her fume needs no spurs,","She'll gallop far enough to her destruction."],"id":125,"m":"Lord Cardinal, I'll follow Eleanor and keep an ear on what Humphrey does. She's stirred up now; her temper needs no spurs; she'll gallop far enough to her own ruin."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":126},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter GLOSTER.","id":127},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Now, lords, my choler being overblown","With walking once about the quadrangle,","I come to talk of commonwealth affairs.","As for your spiteful false objections,","Prove them, and I lie open to the law;","But God in mercy so deal with my soul","As I in duty love my king and country!","But, to the matter that we have in hand:","I say, my sovereign, York is meetest man","To be your regent in the realm of France."],"id":128,"m":"Now, lords, my anger has blown over after one walk around the courtyard, and I've come to talk about state business. As for your spiteful false charges, prove them and I'll answer to the law. But may God treat my soul as mercifully as I dutifully love my king and country! But to the matter in hand: I say, my sovereign, York is the best man to be your regent in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Before we make election, give me leave","To show some reason, of no little force,","That York is most unmeet of any man."],"id":129,"m":"Before we choose, let me give a strong reason why York is the most unfit man of all."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I'll tell thee, Suffolk, why I am unmeet:","First, for I cannot flatter thee in pride;","Next, if I be appointed for the place,","My Lord of Somerset will keep me here,","Without discharge, money, or furniture,","Till France be won into the Dauphin's hands.","Last time, I danc'd attendance on his will","Till Paris was besieg'd, famish'd, and lost."],"id":130,"m":"I'll tell you why I'm unfit, Suffolk. First, because I can't flatter your pride. Next, if I'm appointed, my Lord of Somerset will keep me waiting here without commission, money or equipment until France falls into the Dauphin's hands. Last time I hung about at his pleasure until Paris was besieged, starved and lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["That can I witness; and a fouler fact","Did never traitor in the land commit."],"id":131,"m":"I can swear to that, and no traitor in this land ever did a fouler deed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Peace, headstrong Warwick!"],"id":132,"m":"Quiet, hot-headed Warwick!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Image of pride, why should I hold my peace?"],"id":133,"m":"You picture of pride, why should I keep quiet?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HORNER and his man PETER, guarded.","id":134},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Because here is a man accus'd of treason.","Pray God the Duke of York excuse himself!"],"id":135,"m":"Because here is a man accused of treason. Pray God the Duke of York can clear himself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Doth any one accuse York for a traitor?"],"id":136,"m":"Does anyone accuse York of being a traitor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What mean'st thou, Suffolk? tell me, what are these?"],"id":137,"m":"What do you mean, Suffolk? Tell me, who are these?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Please it your majesty, this is the man","That doth accuse his master of high treason.","His words were these: that Richard Duke of York","Was rightful heir unto the English crown,","And that your majesty was an usurper."],"id":138,"m":"If it please your Majesty, this is the man who accuses his master of high treason. His words were that Richard Duke of York was the rightful heir to the English crown and that your Majesty was a usurper."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Say, man, were these thy words?"],"id":139,"m":"Tell me, man, did you say this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["An 't shall please your majesty, I never said nor","thought any such matter; God is my witness, I am","falsely accused by the villain."],"id":140,"m":"If it please your Majesty, I never said or thought any such thing. God is my witness, this villain accuses me falsely."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["By these ten bones, my lords, he did speak them to","me in the garret one night, as we were scouring my Lord of","York's armour."],"id":141,"m":"By these ten fingers, my lords, he said it to me one night in the attic while we were cleaning my Lord of York's armour."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Base dunghill villain and mechanical,","I'll have thy head for this thy traitor's speech.—","I do beseech your royal majesty,","Let him have all the rigour of the law."],"id":142,"m":"Low, filthy, working-class villain, I'll have your head for this treasonous talk. I beg your royal Majesty, give him the full force of the law."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["Alas, my lord, hang me if ever I spake the words. My","accuser is my prentice; and when I did correct him for his fault","the other day, he did vow upon his knees he would be even with","me. I have good witness of this; therefore I beseech your","majesty, do not cast away an honest man for a villain's","accusation."],"id":143,"m":"Alas, my lord, hang me if I ever said those words. My accuser is my apprentice, and when I punished him for a fault the other day, he swore on his knees he'd get even with me. I have good witnesses to this, so I beg your Majesty not to throw away an honest man on a villain's accusation."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Uncle, what shall we say to this in law?"],"id":144,"m":"Uncle, what does the law say about this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["This doom, my lord, if I may judge:","Let Somerset be Regent o'er the French,","Because in York this breeds suspicion;","And let these have a day appointed them","For single combat in convenient place,","For he hath witness of his servant's malice.","This is the law, and this Duke Humphrey's doom."],"id":145,"m":"This is my judgement, my lord. Let Somerset be regent over France, since this case puts York under suspicion. And let these two be given a day for single combat in a suitable place, since the master has witnesses to his servant's malice. This is the law, and Duke Humphrey's judgement."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["I humbly thank your royal Majesty."],"id":146,"m":"I humbly thank your royal Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["And I accept the combat willingly."],"id":147,"m":"And I accept the combat willingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["Alas, my lord, I cannot fight; for God's sake, pity my case.","The spite of man prevaileth against me. O Lord, have mercy","upon me! I shall never be able to fight a blow! O Lord, my heart!"],"id":148,"m":"Alas, my lord, I can't fight! For God's sake, pity me. Man's spite is beating me. O Lord, have mercy on me! I'll never be able to strike a blow! O Lord, my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Sirrah, or you must fight or else be hang'd."],"id":149,"m":"Fellow, you must either fight or be hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Away with them to prison; and the day of combat shall","be the last of the next month.—Come, Somerset, we'll see thee","sent away."],"id":150,"m":"Take them away to prison, and the day of combat will be the last day of next month. Come, Somerset, we'll see you off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":151},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"Gloster's Garden.","id":152},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MARGERY JOURDAIN, HUME, SOUTHWELL, and BOLINGBROKE.","id":153},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["Come, my masters; the duchess, I tell you, expects","performance of your promises."],"id":154,"m":"Come, gentlemen, the duchess, I tell you, expects you to keep your promises."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Master Hume, we are therefore provided;","will her ladyship behold and hear our exorcisms?"],"id":155,"m":"Master Hume, we're ready for that. Will her ladyship watch and listen to our conjuring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HUME","lines":["Ay, what else? fear you not her courage."],"id":156,"m":"Yes, what else? Don't worry about her courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I have heard her reported to be a woman of an invincible spirit:","but it shall be convenient, Master Hume, that you be by her","aloft while we be busy below; and so, I pray you go, in God's","name, and leave us.—[Exit Hume] Mother Jourdain, be you","prostrate and grovel on the earth.—John Southwell, read you; and","let us to our work."],"id":157,"m":"I've heard she is a woman of unbeatable spirit. But it would be best, Master Hume, if you were up above with her while we're busy down here. So please go, in God's name, and leave us. (Exit Hume) Mother Jourdain, lie flat and grovel on the ground. John Southwell, you read, and let's get to work."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DUCHESS aloft, HUME following.","id":158},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Well said, my masters; and welcome all. To this gear","the sooner the better."],"id":159,"m":"Well said, gentlemen, and welcome all. Let's get on with this business, the sooner the better."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Patience, good lady, wizards know their times:","Deep night, dark night, the silent of the night,","The time of night when Troy was set on fire,","The time when screech-owls cry and ban-dogs howl","And spirits walk and ghosts break up their graves,","That time best fits the work we have in hand.","Madam, sit you and fear not; whom we raise,","We will make fast within a hallow'd verge."],"id":160,"m":"Patience, good lady; wizards know their hours. Deep night, dark night, the silence of the night, the hour when Troy was set on fire, when screech owls cry and watchdogs howl, when spirits walk and ghosts burst from their graves: that time best suits our work. Madam, sit down and don't be afraid. Whatever we raise, we'll hold fast inside a holy circle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they do the ceremonies belonging, and make the circle; Bolingbroke or Southwell reads, Conjuro te, etc. It thunders and lightens terribly; then the Spirit riseth.","id":161},{"t":"sp","who":"SPIRIT","lines":["Adsum."],"id":162,"m":"I am here."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOURDAIN","lines":["Asmath,","By the eternal God, whose name and power","Thou tremblest at, answer that I shall ask;","For till thou speak thou shalt not pass from hence."],"id":163,"m":"Asmath, by the eternal God whose name and power make you tremble, answer what I ask, for until you speak you shall not leave here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPIRIT","lines":["Ask what thou wilt. That I had said and done!"],"id":164,"m":"Ask what you like. I wish I'd answered and was done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["[Reads] 'First of the king: what shall","of him become?'"],"id":165,"m":"(Reading) \"First, about the king: what will become of him?\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SPIRIT","lines":["The duke yet lives that Henry shall depose,","But him outlive and die a violent death."],"id":166,"m":"The duke is still alive who will depose Henry, but Henry will outlive him, and the duke will die a violent death."},{"t":"sd","text":"As the Spirit speaks, Southwell writes the answer.","id":167},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["'What fates await the Duke of Suffolk?'"],"id":168,"m":"\"What fate awaits the Duke of Suffolk?\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SPIRIT","lines":["By water shall he die and take his end."],"id":169,"m":"He will die by water and meet his end."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["[Reads] 'What shall befall the Duke of Somerset?'"],"id":170,"m":"(Reading) \"What will happen to the Duke of Somerset?\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SPIRIT","lines":["Let him shun castles;","Safer shall he be upon the sandy plains","Than where castles mounted stand.","Have done, for more I hardly can endure."],"id":171,"m":"Let him avoid castles. He will be safer on the sandy plains than where castles stand on hills. Finish, for I can hardly bear any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Descend to darkness and the burning lake!","False fiend, avoid!"],"id":172,"m":"Go down to darkness and the burning lake! Lying devil, be gone!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder and lightning. Exit Spirit.","id":173},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the DUKE OF YORK and the DUKE OF BUCKINGHAM with their Guard and break in.","id":174},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Lay hands upon these traitors and their trash.—","Beldam, I think we watch'd you at an inch.","What, madam, are you there? the king and commonweal","Are deeply indebted for this piece of pains;","My lord protector will, I doubt it not,","See you well guerdon'd for these good deserts."],"id":175,"m":"Arrest these traitors and their junk. Old hag, I think we watched you closely. What, madam, are you there? The king and the country owe you a great deal for this effort. My Lord Protector, I'm sure, will see you well rewarded for these good services."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Not half so bad as thine to England's king,","Injurious duke, that threatest where's no cause."],"id":176,"m":"Not half as well as you deserve from England's king, insulting duke, threatening where there's no cause."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["True, madam, none at all; what call you this?—","Away with them! let them be clapp'd up close,","And kept asunder.—You, madam, shall with us.—","Stafford, take her to thee.—"],"id":177,"m":"True, madam, no cause at all. What do you call this? Take them away! Lock them up tight and keep them apart. You, madam, will come with us. Stafford, take charge of her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt above, Duchess and Hume, guarded.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","cont":1,"lines":["We'll see your trinkets here all forthcoming.—","All, away!"],"id":179,"m":"We'll make sure all your trinkets here are produced as evidence. Everyone, away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt guard with Jourdain, Southwell, etc.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Lord Buckingham, methinks you watch'd her well;","A pretty plot, well chosen to build upon!","Now, pray, my lord, let's see the devil's writ.","What have we here?","[Reads] 'The duke yet lives that Henry shall depose.","But him outlive and die a violent death.'","Why, this is just","'Aio te, Aeacida, Romanos vincere posse.'","Well, to the rest:","'Tell me what fate awaits the Duke of Suffolk?","By water shall he die and take his end.","What shall betide the Duke of Somerset?","Let him shun castles;","Safer shall he be upon the sandy plains","Than where castles mounted stand.'—","Come, come, my lords;","These oracles are hardly attain'd,","And hardly understood.","The king is now in progress towards Saint Alban's,","With him the husband of this lovely lady.","Thither go these news, as fast as horse can carry them;","A sorry breakfast for my lord protector."],"id":181,"m":"Lord Buckingham, I think you watched her well. A nice plot, a good one to build on! Now, my lord, let's see the devil's writing. What have we here? (Reading) \"The duke is still alive who will depose Henry, but Henry will outlive him, and the duke will die a violent death.\" Why, this is exactly like the old oracle \"I say that you, son of Aeacus, the Romans can conquer\", which could mean either. Well, the rest: \"Tell me what fate awaits the Duke of Suffolk? He will die by water and meet his end. What will happen to the Duke of Somerset? Let him avoid castles; he will be safer on the sandy plains than where castles stand on hills.\" Come, come, my lords, these oracles are hard to get and hard to understand. The king is now on his way to St Albans, and with him this lovely lady's husband. This news must go there as fast as a horse can carry it. A sad breakfast for my Lord Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Your Grace shall give me leave, my","Lord of York,","To be the post, in hope of his reward."],"id":182,"m":"Your grace will allow me, my Lord of York, to be the messenger, in hope of his reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["At your pleasure, my good lord.—","Who's within there, ho!"],"id":183,"m":"As you please, my good lord. Who's in there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servingman.","id":184},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Invite my Lords of Salisbury and Warwick","To sup with me to-morrow night. Away!"],"id":185,"m":"Invite my Lords of Salisbury and Warwick to have supper with me tomorrow night. Go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":186},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Saint Alban's.","id":187},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the KING, QUEEN, GLOSTER, CARDINAL, and SUFFOLK, with FALCONERS halloing.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Believe me, lords, for flying at the brook,","I saw not better sport these seven years' day;","Yet, by your leave, the wind was very high,","And, ten to one, old Joan had not gone out."],"id":189,"m":"Believe me, lords, for hawking at water birds I haven't seen better sport in seven years. Though, if I may say so, the wind was very high, and ten to one old Joan wouldn't have flown at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But what a point, my lord, your falcon made,","And what a pitch she flew above the rest!","To see how God in all His creatures works!","Yea, man and birds are fain of climbing high."],"id":190,"m":"But what a position your falcon took, my lord, and how high she flew above the rest! See how God works in all His creatures! Yes, men and birds both love to climb high."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["No marvel, an it like your majesty,","My lord protector's hawks do tower so well;","They know their master loves to be aloft,","And bears his thoughts above his falcon's pitch."],"id":191,"m":"No wonder, if it please your Majesty, my Lord Protector's hawks fly so high. They know their master loves to be up high and carries his thoughts above his falcon's flight."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["My lord, 't is but a base ignoble mind","That mounts no higher than a bird can soar."],"id":192,"m":"My lord, it's a low, mean mind that rises no higher than a bird can fly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["I thought as much; he would be above the clouds."],"id":193,"m":"I thought so; he'd like to be above the clouds."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ay, my lord cardinal? how think you by that?","Were it not good your grace could fly to heaven?"],"id":194,"m":"Yes, my lord cardinal? What do you mean by that? Wouldn't it be good if your grace could fly to heaven?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The treasury of everlasting joy."],"id":195,"m":"The treasure house of everlasting joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Thy heaven is on earth; thine eyes and thoughts","Beat on a crown, the treasure of thy heart,","Pernicious protector, dangerous peer,","That smooth'st it so with king and commonweal."],"id":196,"m":"Your heaven is on earth. Your eyes and thoughts are fixed on a crown, your heart's treasure, you harmful Protector, you dangerous lord, who flatter your way so smoothly with king and country."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What, cardinal, is your priesthood grown peremptory?","Tantaene animis coelestibus irae?","Churchmen so hot? good uncle, hide such malice;","With such holiness can you do it?"],"id":197,"m":"What, cardinal, has your priesthood become so bossy? \"Is there such anger in heavenly minds?\" Churchmen so hot-tempered? Good uncle, hide such malice. Can you do it, being so holy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["No malice, sir; no more than well becomes","So good a quarrel and so bad a peer."],"id":198,"m":"No malice, sir; no more than suits so good a quarrel against so bad a lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["As who, my lord?"],"id":199,"m":"Like whom, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Why, as you, my lord,","An 't like your lordly lord-protectorship."],"id":200,"m":"Why, like you, my lord, if it please your lordly Lord Protectorship."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why, Suffolk, England knows thine insolence."],"id":201,"m":"Why, Suffolk, all England knows your insolence."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And thy ambition, Gloster."],"id":202,"m":"And your ambition, Gloucester."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I prithee, peace, good queen,","And whet not on these furious peers;","For blessed are the peacemakers on earth."],"id":203,"m":"Please, peace, good queen, and don't stir up these furious lords, for blessed are the peacemakers on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Let me be blessed for the peace I make","Against this proud protector, with my sword!"],"id":204,"m":"Then let me be blessed for the peace I make with my sword against this proud Protector!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Cardinal] Faith, holy uncle, would 't","were come to that!"],"id":205,"m":"(Aside to Cardinal) Truly, holy uncle, I wish it would come to that!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] Marry, when thou dar'st."],"id":206,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) Indeed, whenever you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Cardinal] Make up no factious numbers","for the matter;","In thine own person answer thy abuse."],"id":207,"m":"(Aside to Cardinal) Don't gather a gang of supporters for this; answer for your insults in person."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] Ay, where thou dar'st not peep;","an if thou dar'st,","This evening, on the east side of the grove."],"id":208,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) Yes, where you won't dare show your face; and if you do dare, this evening, on the east side of the wood."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How now, my lords!"],"id":209,"m":"What's this, my lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Believe me, cousin Gloster,","Had not your man put up the fowl so suddenly,","We had had more sport.—[Aside to Gloster] Come with thy","two-hand sword."],"id":210,"m":"Believe me, cousin Gloucester, if your servant hadn't flushed the bird so suddenly, we'd have had more sport. (Aside to Gloucester) Bring your two-handed sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["True, uncle."],"id":211,"m":"True, uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] Are ye advis'd? the east side","of the grove?"],"id":212,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) Are you agreed? The east side of the wood?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to CARDINAL] Cardinal, I am with you."],"id":213,"m":"(Aside to Cardinal) Cardinal, I'll be there."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, how now, uncle Gloster!"],"id":214,"m":"What's the matter, uncle Gloucester?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Talking of hawking; nothing else, my lord.—","[Aside to Cardinal] Now, by God's mother, priest,","I'll shave your crown for this,","Or all my fence shall fail."],"id":215,"m":"Talking about hawking, nothing else, my lord. (Aside to Cardinal) By God's mother, priest, I'll shave your tonsured head for this, or all my swordsmanship will fail."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] Medice, teipsum—","Protector, see to 't well, protect yourself."],"id":216,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) \"Physician, heal yourself.\" Protector, take care; protect yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The winds grow high; so do your stomachs, lords.","How irksome is this music to my heart!","When such strings jar, what hope of harmony?","I pray, my lords, let me compound this strife."],"id":217,"m":"The winds are getting high, and so are your tempers, lords. How painful this music is to my heart! When such strings clash, what hope is there of harmony? Please, my lords, let me settle this quarrel."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Townsman of Saint Alban's, crying 'A miracle!'.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What means this noise?","Fellow, what miracle dost thou proclaim?"],"id":219,"m":"What's this noise? Fellow, what miracle are you announcing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TOWNSMAN","lines":["A miracle! A miracle!"],"id":220,"m":"A miracle! A miracle!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Come to the king, and tell him what miracle."],"id":221,"m":"Come to the king and tell him what miracle."},{"t":"sp","who":"TOWNSMAN","lines":["Forsooth, a blind man at Saint Alban's shrine,","Within this half hour, hath receiv'd his sight;","A man that ne'er saw in his life before."],"id":222,"m":"Truly, a blind man at St Alban's shrine has received his sight within the last half hour, a man who never saw in his life before."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, God be prais'd, that to believing souls","Gives light in darkness, comfort in despair!"],"id":223,"m":"God be praised, who gives light in darkness and comfort in despair to believers!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Mayor of Saint Alban's and his brethren, bearing SIMPCOX, between two in a chair, SIMPCOX's Wife following.","id":224},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Here comes the townsmen on procession,","To present your highness with the man."],"id":225,"m":"Here come the townspeople in procession to present the man to your highness."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Great is his comfort in this earthly vale,","Although by his sight his sin be multiplied."],"id":226,"m":"Great is his comfort in this earthly valley, even though seeing may multiply his chances to sin."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Stand by, my masters.","Bring him near the king;","His highness' pleasure is to talk with him."],"id":227,"m":"Stand aside, friends. Bring him near the king; his highness wants to talk with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Good fellow, tell us here the circumstance,","That we for thee may glorify the Lord.","What, hast thou been long blind and now restor'd?"],"id":228,"m":"Good fellow, tell us what happened, so that we can praise the Lord for you. Have you been blind long, and now been cured?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Born blind, an 't please your grace."],"id":229,"m":"Born blind, if it please your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["Ay indeed was he."],"id":230,"m":"Yes, indeed he was."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["What woman is this?"],"id":231,"m":"Who is this woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["His wife, an 't like your worship."],"id":232,"m":"His wife, if it please your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Hadst thou been his mother, thou couldst","have better told."],"id":233,"m":"If you'd been his mother, you could have told us better."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where wert thou born?"],"id":234,"m":"Where were you born?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["At Berwick in the north, an 't like your grace."],"id":235,"m":"At Berwick in the north, if it please your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Poor soul, God's goodness hath been great to thee;","Let never day nor night unhallow'd pass,","But still remember what the Lord hath done."],"id":236,"m":"Poor soul, God has been very good to you. Never let a day or night pass without giving thanks, but always remember what the Lord has done."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Tell me, good fellow, cam'st thou here by chance,","Or of devotion, to this holy shrine?"],"id":237,"m":"Tell me, good fellow, did you come to this holy shrine by chance, or out of devotion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["God knows, of pure devotion; being call'd","A hundred times and oftener, in my sleep,","By good Saint Alban, who said 'Simpcox, come,","Come, offer at my shrine, and I will help thee.'"],"id":238,"m":"God knows, out of pure devotion, having been called a hundred times and more in my sleep by good St Alban, who said, \"Simpcox, come, come and make an offering at my shrine, and I'll help you.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["Most true, forsooth; and many time and oft","Myself have heard a voice to call him so."],"id":239,"m":"Very true, indeed; and many times I've heard a voice call him like that myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["What, art thou lame?"],"id":240,"m":"What, are you lame?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Ay, God Almighty help me!"],"id":241,"m":"Yes, God Almighty help me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["How cam'st thou so?"],"id":242,"m":"How did that happen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["A fall off of a tree."],"id":243,"m":"I fell out of a tree."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["A plum-tree, master."],"id":244,"m":"A plum tree, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["How long hast thou been blind?"],"id":245,"m":"How long have you been blind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["O, born so, master!"],"id":246,"m":"Oh, I was born that way, master!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What, and wouldst climb a tree?"],"id":247,"m":"What, and you climbed a tree?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["But that in all my life, when I was a youth."],"id":248,"m":"Only once in my life, when I was young."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["Too true; and bought his climbing very dear."],"id":249,"m":"Too true, and he paid dearly for that climb."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Mass, thou lov'dst plums well that wouldst venture so."],"id":250,"m":"By the mass, you must have loved plums to take such a risk."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Alas, good master, my wife desir'd some damsons,","And made me climb, with danger of my life."],"id":251,"m":"Alas, good master, my wife wanted some damsons and made me climb, at risk of my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["A subtle knave! but yet it shall not serve.—","Let me see thine eyes.—Wink now;—now open them.","In my opinion yet thou seest not well."],"id":252,"m":"A cunning rogue! But it won't work. Let me see your eyes. Shut them now; now open them. In my opinion you still don't see well."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Yes, master, clear as day, I thank God and Saint Alban."],"id":253,"m":"Yes, master, clear as day, thanks to God and St Alban."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Say'st thou me so? What colour is this cloak of?"],"id":254,"m":"Is that so? What colour is this cloak?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Red, master, red as blood."],"id":255,"m":"Red, master, red as blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why, that's well said. What colour is my gown of?"],"id":256,"m":"Well said. What colour is my gown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Black, forsooth, coal-black as jet."],"id":257,"m":"Black, truly, coal-black like jet."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, then, thou know'st what colour jet is of?"],"id":258,"m":"Why, then, you know what colour jet is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And yet, I think, jet did he never see."],"id":259,"m":"And yet I think he never saw jet."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["But cloaks and gowns before this day, a many."],"id":260,"m":"But he's seen plenty of cloaks and gowns before today."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["Never before this day in all his life."],"id":261,"m":"Never before today in all his life."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Tell me, sirrah, what's my name?"],"id":262,"m":"Tell me, fellow, what's my name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Alas, master, I know not."],"id":263,"m":"Alas, master, I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What's his name?"],"id":264,"m":"What's his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["I know not."],"id":265,"m":"I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Nor his?"],"id":266,"m":"Or his?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["No, indeed, master."],"id":267,"m":"No, indeed, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What's thine own name?"],"id":268,"m":"What's your own name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Saunder Simpcox, an if it please you, master."],"id":269,"m":"Saunder Simpcox, if it please you, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Then, Saunder, sit there, the lyingest knave in","Christendom. If thou hadst been born blind, thou mightst as well","have known all our names as thus to name the several colours we","do wear. Sight may distinguish of colours; but suddenly to","nominate them all, it is impossible.—My lords, Saint Alban here","hath done a miracle; and would ye not think his cunning to be","great that could restore this cripple to his legs again?"],"id":270,"m":"Then, Saunder, sit there, the biggest liar in Christendom. If you'd been born blind, you could just as easily have known all our names as named the different colours we're wearing. Sight can tell colours apart, but to name them all suddenly is impossible. My lords, St Alban has done a miracle here; but wouldn't you think a man very clever who could restore this cripple to his legs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["O master, that you could!"],"id":271,"m":"Oh master, if only you could!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["My masters of Saint Alban's, have you not beadles in","your town, and things called whips?"],"id":272,"m":"Gentlemen of St Albans, don't you have parish officers in your town, and things called whips?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["Yes, my lord, if it please your grace."],"id":273,"m":"Yes, my lord, if it please your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Then send for one presently."],"id":274,"m":"Then send for one at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["Sirrah, go fetch the beadle hither straight."],"id":275,"m":"You there, go and fetch the beadle here straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":276},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Now fetch me a stool hither by and by.—Now, sirrah,","if you mean to save yourself from whipping, leap me over this","stool and run away."],"id":277,"m":"Now bring me a stool here quickly. Now, fellow, if you want to save yourself from a whipping, jump over this stool and run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Alas, master, I am not able to stand alone;","You go about to torture me in vain."],"id":278,"m":"Alas, master, I can't even stand on my own. You're trying to torture me for nothing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Beadle with whips.","id":279},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Well, sir, we must have you find your legs.—","Sirrah beadle, whip him till he leap over that same stool."],"id":280,"m":"Well, sir, we must help you find your legs. Beadle, whip him till he jumps over that stool."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEADLE","lines":["I will, my lord.—Come on, sirrah; off with your doublet","quickly."],"id":281,"m":"I will, my lord. Come on, fellow, take off your jacket quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPCOX","lines":["Alas, master, what shall I do? I am not able to stand."],"id":282,"m":"Alas, master, what shall I do? I can't stand."},{"t":"sd","text":"After the Beadle hath hit him once, he leaps over the stool and runs away; and they follow and cry, 'A miracle!'.","id":283},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O God, seest Thou this, and bearest so long?"],"id":284,"m":"O God, do you see this, and put up with it so long?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["It made me laugh to see the villain run."],"id":285,"m":"It made me laugh to see the villain run."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Follow the knave, and take this drab away."],"id":286,"m":"Follow the rogue, and take this slut away."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIFE","lines":["Alas, sir, we did it for pure need!"],"id":287,"m":"Alas, sir, we did it out of sheer poverty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Let them be whipped through every market-town","till they come to Berwick, from whence they came."],"id":288,"m":"Let them be whipped through every market town until they get back to Berwick, where they came from."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Wife, Beadle, Mayor, etc.","id":289},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Duke Humphrey has done a miracle to-day."],"id":290,"m":"Duke Humphrey has done a miracle today."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["True; made the lame to leap and fly away."],"id":291,"m":"True: made the lame leap and run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["But you have done more miracles than I;","You made in a day, my lord, whole towns to fly."],"id":292,"m":"But you've done more miracles than I have. In a single day, my lord, you made whole towns run away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What tidings with our cousin Buckingham?"],"id":294,"m":"What news, cousin Buckingham?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Such as my heart doth tremble to unfold.","A sort of naughty persons, lewdly bent,","Under the countenance and confederacy","Of Lady Eleanor, the protector's wife,","The ringleader and head of all this rout,","Have practis'd dangerously against your state,","Dealing with witches and with conjurers,","Whom we have apprehended in the fact,","Raising up wicked spirits from underground,","Demanding of King Henry's life and death,","And other of your highness' privy-council,","As more at large your Grace shall understand."],"id":295,"m":"News my heart trembles to reveal. A gang of wicked people, bent on evil, with the support and partnership of Lady Eleanor, the Protector's wife, the ringleader and head of the whole crew, have plotted dangerously against your state, dealing with witches and conjurers, whom we caught in the act raising evil spirits from underground and asking about King Henry's life and death, and about others of your highness's privy council, as your grace will hear in more detail."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] And so, my lord protector,","by this means","Your lady is forthcoming yet at London.","This news, I think, hath turn'd your weapon's edge;","'T is like, my lord, you will not keep your hour."],"id":296,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) And so, my Lord Protector, your lady is now in custody in London. This news, I think, has blunted your sword; it seems, my lord, you won't keep our appointment."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ambitious churchman, leave to afflict my heart.","Sorrow and grief have vanquish'd all my powers;","And, vanquish'd as I am, I yield to thee,","Or to the meanest groom."],"id":297,"m":"Ambitious churchman, stop tormenting my heart. Sorrow and grief have beaten all my strength, and beaten as I am, I give in to you, or to the lowest servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O God, what mischiefs work the wicked ones,","Heaping confusion on their own heads thereby!"],"id":298,"m":"O God, what harm the wicked do, heaping confusion on their own heads by it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Gloster, see here the tainture of thy nest;","And look thyself be faultless, thou wert best."],"id":299,"m":"Gloucester, look at the stain on your nest, and you'd better make sure you yourself are blameless."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Madam, for myself, to heaven I do appeal,","How I have lov'd my king and commonweal;","And, for my wife, I know not how it stands.","Sorry I am to hear what I have heard;","Noble she is; but if she have forgot","Honour and virtue, and convers'd with such","As like to pitch defile nobility,","I banish her my bed and company,","And give her as a prey to law and shame,","That hath dishonoured Gloster's honest name."],"id":300,"m":"Madam, as for myself, I appeal to heaven to witness how I have loved my king and country. As for my wife, I don't know how things stand. I'm sorry to hear what I've heard. She is noble, but if she has forgotten honour and virtue and mixed with people who, like pitch, stain nobility, I banish her from my bed and company and hand her over to the law and to shame, since she has dishonoured Gloucester's honest name."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Well, for this night we will repose us here;","To-morrow toward London back again,","To look into this business thoroughly,","And call these foul offenders to their answers,","And poise the cause in justice' equal scales,","Whose beam stands sure, whose rightful cause prevails."],"id":301,"m":"Well, we'll rest here tonight. Tomorrow we'll go back to London to look into this business thoroughly, call these foul offenders to answer, and weigh the case in the even scales of justice, whose beam stays steady and where the right side wins."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":302},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"London. The Duke of York's Garden.","id":303},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK, SALISBURY, and WARWICK.","id":304},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Now, my good Lords of Salisbury and Warwick,","Our simple supper ended, give me leave","In this close walk to satisfy myself,","In craving your opinion of my title,","Which is infallible, to England's crown."],"id":305,"m":"Now, my good Lords of Salisbury and Warwick, our simple supper is over. Let me take this private walk to satisfy myself by asking your opinion of my claim to England's crown, which cannot be faulted."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["My lord, I long to hear it at full."],"id":306,"m":"My lord, I long to hear it in full."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Sweet York, begin; and if thy claim be good,","The Nevils are thy subjects to command."],"id":307,"m":"Sweet York, begin; and if your claim is good, the Nevilles are your subjects to command."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Then thus:","Edward the Third, my lords, had seven sons:","The first, Edward the Black Prince, Prince of Wales;","The second, William of Hatfield; and the third,","Lionel Duke of Clarence; next to whom","Was John of Gaunt, the Duke of Lancaster;","The fifth was Edmund Langley, Duke of York;","The sixth was Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloster;","William of Windsor was the seventh and last.","Edward the Black Prince died before his father","And left behind him Richard, his only son,","Who after Edward the Third's death reign'd as king;","Till Henry Bolingbroke, Duke of Lancaster,","The eldest son and heir of John of Gaunt,","Crown'd by the name of Henry the Fourth,","Seiz'd on the realm, depos'd the rightful king,","Sent his poor queen to France, from whence she came,","And him to Pomfret, where, as all you know,","Harmless Richard was murther'd traitorously."],"id":308,"m":"Then here it is. Edward the Third, my lords, had seven sons. The first was Edward the Black Prince, Prince of Wales; the second, William of Hatfield; the third, Lionel Duke of Clarence; next came John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster; the fifth was Edmund Langley, Duke of York; the sixth was Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester; William of Windsor was the seventh and last. Edward the Black Prince died before his father and left Richard, his only son, who reigned as king after Edward the Third's death, until Henry Bolingbroke, Duke of Lancaster, eldest son and heir of John of Gaunt, crowned as Henry the Fourth, seized the kingdom, deposed the rightful king, sent his poor queen back to France where she came from, and sent him to Pomfret, where, as you all know, harmless Richard was treacherously murdered."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Father, the duke hath told the truth;","Thus got the house of Lancaster the crown."],"id":309,"m":"Father, the duke has told the truth. That's how the house of Lancaster got the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Which now they hold by force and not by right;","For Richard, the first son's heir, being dead,","The issue of the next son should have reign'd."],"id":310,"m":"Which they now hold by force and not by right. For since Richard, the first son's heir, was dead, the descendants of the next son should have reigned."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["But William of Hatfield died without an heir."],"id":311,"m":"But William of Hatfield died without an heir."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The third son, Duke of Clarence, from whose line","I claim the crown, had issue, Philippe, a daughter,","Who married Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March.","Edmund had issue, Roger Earl of March;","Roger had issue, Edmund, Anne, and Eleanor."],"id":312,"m":"The third son, the Duke of Clarence, from whose line I claim the crown, had a daughter, Philippa, who married Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March. Edmund had a son, Roger, Earl of March; Roger had children Edmund, Anne and Eleanor."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["This Edmund, in the reign of Bolingbroke,","As I have read, laid claim unto the crown;","And, but for Owen Glendower, had been king,","Who kept him in captivity till he died.","But to the rest."],"id":313,"m":"This Edmund, in Bolingbroke's reign, as I've read, claimed the crown, and would have been king but for Owen Glendower, who kept him prisoner until he died. But go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["His eldest sister, Anne,","My mother, being heir unto the crown,","Married Richard Earl of Cambridge, who was son","To Edmund Langley, Edward the Third's fifth son.","By her I claim the kingdom; she was heir","To Roger Earl of March, who was the son","Of Edmund Mortimer, who married Philippe,","Sole daughter unto Lionel Duke of Clarence.","So, if the issue of the elder son","Succeed before the younger, I am king."],"id":314,"m":"His eldest sister, Anne, my mother, heir to the crown, married Richard Earl of Cambridge, son of Edmund Langley, Edward the Third's fifth son. Through her I claim the kingdom. She was heir to Roger Earl of March, son of Edmund Mortimer, who married Philippa, only daughter of Lionel Duke of Clarence. So if the descendants of the elder son come before the younger, I am king."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What plain proceeding is more plain than this?","Henry doth claim the crown from John of Gaunt,","The fourth son; York claims it from the third.","Till Lionel's issue fails, his should not reign;","It fails not yet, but flourishes in thee","And in thy sons, fair slips of such a stock.—","Then, father Salisbury, kneel we together;","And in this private plot be we the first","That shall salute our rightful sovereign","With honour of his birthright to the crown."],"id":315,"m":"What could be plainer than this? Henry claims the crown from John of Gaunt, the fourth son; York claims it from the third. Until Lionel's line dies out, Gaunt's should not reign. It hasn't died out but flourishes in you and your sons, fine shoots from such a stem. So, father Salisbury, let's kneel together, and in this private place be the first to salute our rightful sovereign and honour his birthright to the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY AND WARWICK","lines":["Long live our sovereign Richard, England's king!"],"id":316,"m":"Long live our sovereign Richard, England's king!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["We thank you, lords. But I am not your king","Till I be crown'd, and that my sword be stain'd","With heart-blood of the house of Lancaster;","And that's not suddenly to be perform'd,","But with advice and silent secrecy.","Do you as I do in these dangerous days,—","Wink at the Duke of Suffolk's insolence,","At Beaufort's pride, at Somerset's ambition,","At Buckingham, and all the crew of them,","Till they have snar'd the shepherd of the flock,","That virtuous prince, the good Duke Humphrey;","'T is that they seek, and they in seeking that","Shall find their deaths, if York can prophesy."],"id":317,"m":"Thank you, lords. But I'm not your king until I'm crowned and my sword is stained with the heart's blood of the house of Lancaster. That can't be done suddenly, but with careful planning and silent secrecy. In these dangerous days, do as I do: turn a blind eye to the Duke of Suffolk's insolence, Beaufort's pride, Somerset's ambition, Buckingham and their whole crew, until they have trapped the shepherd of the flock, that virtuous prince, good Duke Humphrey. That's what they're after, and in getting it they'll find their own deaths, if York can foretell anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["My lord, break we off; we know your mind at full."],"id":318,"m":"My lord, let's stop here; we know your mind fully."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["My heart assures me that the Earl of Warwick","Shall one day make the Duke of York a king."],"id":319,"m":"My heart tells me that the Earl of Warwick will one day make the Duke of York a king."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["And, Nevil, this I do assure myself:","Richard shall live to make the Earl of Warwick","The greatest man in England but the king."],"id":320,"m":"And Neville, I promise myself this: Richard will live to make the Earl of Warwick the greatest man in England after the king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":321},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A Hall of Justice.","id":322},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets. Enter the KING, the QUEEN, GLOSTER, YORK, SUFFOLK, and SALISBURY; the DUCHESS OF GLOSTER, MARGERY JOURDAIN, SOUTHWELL, HUME, and BOLINGBROKE, under guard.","id":323},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Stand forth, Dame Eleanor Cobham, Gloster's wife.","In sight of God and us, your guilt is great;","Receive the sentence of the law for sins","Such as by God's book are adjudg'd to death.—","You four, from hence to prison back again,","From thence unto the place of execution.","The witch in Smithfield shall be burn'd to ashes,","And you three shall be strangled on the gallows.—","You, madam, for you are more nobly born,","Despoiled of your honour in your life,","Shall, after three days' open penance done,","Live in your country here in banishment,","With Sir John Stanley, in the Isle of Man."],"id":324,"m":"Step forward, Dame Eleanor Cobham, Gloucester's wife. In the sight of God and us, your guilt is great. Receive the law's sentence for sins that God's book judges worthy of death. You four, go back to prison from here, and from there to the place of execution. The witch will be burned to ashes in Smithfield, and you three will be hanged on the gallows. You, madam, because you are more nobly born, will lose your honour for the rest of your life, and after three days of public penance will live in banishment within this country, in the Isle of Man, in Sir John Stanley's charge."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Welcome is banishment; welcome were my death."],"id":325,"m":"Banishment is welcome; death would be welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Eleanor, the law, thou seest, hath judged thee;","I cannot justify whom the law condemns.—"],"id":326,"m":"Eleanor, you see the law has judged you; I can't defend someone the law condemns."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Duchess and the other prisoners, guarded.","id":327},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","cont":1,"lines":["Mine eyes are full of tears, my heart of grief.","Ah, Humphrey, this dishonour in thine age","Will bring thy head with sorrow to the ground!—","I beseech your majesty, give me leave to go;","Sorrow would solace, and mine age would ease."],"id":328,"m":"My eyes are full of tears, my heart of grief. Ah, Humphrey, this disgrace in your old age will bring your head down to the grave with sorrow! I beg your Majesty to let me go; my grief wants comfort and my age wants rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Stay, Humphrey Duke of Gloster.","Ere thou go,","Give up thy staff; Henry will to himself","Protector be, and God shall be my hope,","My stay, my guide, and lantern to my feet.","And go in peace, Humphrey, no less belov'd","Than when thou wert protector to thy king."],"id":329,"m":"Wait, Humphrey Duke of Gloucester. Before you go, hand over your staff. Henry will be his own Protector, and God will be my hope, my support, my guide and the lamp for my feet. And go in peace, Humphrey, loved no less than when you were your king's Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["I see no reason why a king of years","Should be to be protected like a child.—","God and King Henry govern England's realm.","Give up your staff, sir, and the king his realm."],"id":330,"m":"I see no reason why a grown-up king needs to be protected like a child. God and King Henry govern England. Give up your staff, sir, and give the king his kingdom."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["My staff? Here, noble Henry, is my staff.","As willingly do I the same resign","As e'er thy father Henry made it mine;","And even as willingly at thy feet I leave it","As others would ambitiously receive it.","Farewell, good king; when I am dead and gone,","May honourable peace attend thy throne!"],"id":331,"m":"My staff? Here, noble Henry, is my staff. I give it up as willingly as your father Henry ever gave it to me, and I leave it at your feet as gladly as others would greedily take it. Farewell, good king. When I'm dead and gone, may honourable peace surround your throne!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":332},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Why, now is Henry king, and Margaret queen;","And Humphrey Duke of Gloster scarce himself,","That bears so shrewd a maim; two pulls at once—","His lady banish'd, and a limb lopp'd off.","This staff of honour raught, there let it stand","Where it best fits to be, in Henry's hand."],"id":333,"m":"Why, now Henry is king and Margaret queen, and Humphrey Duke of Gloucester hardly himself after such a wound; two blows at once, his wife banished and a limb cut off. Now this staff of honour has been taken, let it stay where it belongs, in Henry's hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thus droops this lofty pine and hangs his sprays;","Thus Eleanor's pride dies in her youngest days."],"id":334,"m":"So this tall pine droops and hangs its branches; so Eleanor's pride dies while it's still young."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Lords, let him go.—Please it your majesty,","This is the day appointed for the combat;","And ready are the appellant and defendant,","The armourer and his man, to enter the lists,","So please your highness to behold the fight."],"id":335,"m":"Lords, let him go. If it please your Majesty, this is the day set for the combat, and the accuser and defendant, the armourer and his man, are ready to enter the lists, if your highness wishes to watch the fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Ay, good my lord; for purposely therefore","Left I the court, to see this quarrel tried."],"id":336,"m":"Yes, my good lord; I left the court on purpose to see this quarrel settled."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O' God's name, see the lists and all things fit.","Here let them end it; and God defend the right!"],"id":337,"m":"In God's name, see that the lists and everything are ready. Let them end it here, and God defend the right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I never saw a fellow worse bested,","Or more afraid to fight, than is the appellant,","The servant of his armourer, my lords."],"id":338,"m":"I never saw anyone in a worse state or more afraid to fight than the accuser, the armourer's servant, my lords."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter at one door, HORNER the Armourer, and his Neighbours, drinking to him so much that he is drunk; and he enters with a drum before him and his staff with a sand-bag fastened to it; and at the other door PETER, his man, with a drum and sandbag, and Prentices drinking to him.","id":339},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST NEIGHBOUR","lines":["Here, neighbour Horner, I drink to you in a cup of","sack; and fear not, neighbour, you shall do well enough."],"id":340,"m":"Here, neighbour Horner, I drink to you in a cup of sherry; don't be afraid, neighbour, you'll do well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND NEIGHBOUR","lines":["And here, neighbour, here's a cup of charneco."],"id":341,"m":"And here, neighbour, here's a cup of Portuguese wine."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD NEIGHBOUR","lines":["And here's a pot of good double beer, neighbour;","drink, and fear not your man."],"id":342,"m":"And here's a pot of good strong beer, neighbour. Drink, and don't be afraid of your man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["Let it come, i' faith, and I'll pledge you all; and a","fig for Peter!"],"id":343,"m":"Bring it on, truly, and I'll drink to you all; and a fig for Peter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PRENTICE","lines":["Here, Peter, I drink to thee; and be not afraid."],"id":344,"m":"Here, Peter, I drink to you; don't be afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND PRENTICE","lines":["Be merry, Peter, and fear not thy master: fight","for credit of the prentices."],"id":345,"m":"Cheer up, Peter, and don't fear your master. Fight for the honour of the apprentices."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["I thank you all; drink, and pray for me, I pray you, for I","think I have taken my last draught in this world.—Here, Robin,","an if I die, I give thee my apron;—and, Will, thou shalt have my","hammer;—and here, Tom, take all the money that I have.—O Lord","bless me! I pray God! for I am never able to deal with my master,","he hath learnt so much fence already."],"id":346,"m":"Thank you all. Drink, and pray for me, please, for I think I've had my last drink in this world. Here, Robin, if I die, I give you my apron; and Will, you can have my hammer; and here, Tom, take all the money I have. O Lord bless me! I pray God! For I'll never be able to beat my master; he's learned so much swordplay already."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Come, leave your drinking and fall to blows.—","Sirrah, what's thy name?"],"id":347,"m":"Come, stop drinking and start fighting. You, what's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["Peter, forsooth."],"id":348,"m":"Peter, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Peter? what more?"],"id":349,"m":"Peter? Peter what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["Thump."],"id":350,"m":"Thump."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Thump! then see thou thump thy master well."],"id":351,"m":"Thump! Then make sure you thump your master well."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["Masters, I am come hither, as it were, upon my man's instigation,","to prove him a knave and myself an honest man; and touching the","Duke of York, I will take my death, I never meant him any ill,","nor the","king, nor the queen;—and therefore, Peter, have at thee with a","downright","blow!"],"id":352,"m":"Gentlemen, I've come here because my man forced me to, to prove him a rogue and myself an honest man. As for the Duke of York, I'll swear on my life I never meant him any harm, nor the king, nor the queen. And so, Peter, take this straight blow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Dispatch; this knave's tongue begins to double.—","Sound, trumpets, alarum to the combatants!"],"id":353,"m":"Hurry up; this rogue's tongue is starting to slur. Sound the trumpets, call the fighters to battle!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. They fight, and Peter strikes him down.","id":354},{"t":"sp","who":"HORNER","lines":["Hold, Peter, hold! I confess, I confess treason."],"id":355,"m":"Stop, Peter, stop! I confess, I confess treason."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":356},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Take away his weapon.—Fellow, thank God, and the good","wine in thy master's way."],"id":357,"m":"Take away his weapon. Fellow, thank God, and the good wine that got in your master's way."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["O God, have I overcome mine enemies in this presence? O","Peter, thou hast prevail'd in right!"],"id":358,"m":"O God, have I beaten my enemy in front of all these people? O Peter, you've won because you were in the right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Go, take hence that traitor from our sight,","For by his death we do perceive his guilt;","And God in justice hath reveal'd to us","The truth and innocence of this poor fellow,","Which he had thought to have murther'd wrongfully.—","Come, fellow, follow us for thy reward."],"id":359,"m":"Go, take that traitor out of our sight, for by his death we see his guilt. God has justly shown us the truth and innocence of this poor fellow, whom he meant to have wrongly killed. Come, fellow, follow us for your reward."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound a flourish. Exeunt.","id":360},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Street.","id":361},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GLOSTER and his Servingmen, in mourning cloaks.","id":362},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Thus sometimes hath the brightest day a cloud,","And after summer evermore succeeds","Barren winter, with his wrathful nipping cold;","So cares and joys abound, as seasons fleet.","Sirs, what's o'clock?"],"id":363,"m":"So even the brightest day sometimes has a cloud, and after summer always comes barren winter with its fierce biting cold. So sorrows and joys come and go like the passing seasons. Men, what time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVINGMEN","lines":["Ten, my lord."],"id":364,"m":"Ten o'clock, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ten is the hour that was appointed me","To watch the coming of my punish'd duchess.","Uneath may she endure the flinty streets,","To tread them with her tender-feeling feet.—","Sweet Nell, ill can thy noble mind abrook","The abject people gazing on thy face","With envious looks, laughing at thy shame,","That erst did follow thy proud chariot-wheels","When thou didst ride in triumph through the streets.—","But, soft! I think she comes; and I'll prepare","My tear-stain'd eyes to see her miseries."],"id":365,"m":"Ten is the hour I was told to wait for my punished duchess to come by. She can hardly bear to walk on the stony streets with her tender feet. Sweet Nell, your noble mind can hardly endure the low people staring at your face with spiteful looks and laughing at your shame, who once followed your proud chariot wheels when you rode through the streets in triumph. But wait! I think she's coming, and I'll ready my tear-stained eyes to see her misery."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the DUCHESS OF GLOSTER in a white sheet, and a taper burning in her hand; with SIR JOHN STANLEY, the Sheriff, and Officers.","id":366},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVINGMEN","lines":["So please your Grace, we'll take her from the","sheriff."],"id":367,"m":"If it please your grace, we'll take her from the sheriff."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["No, stir not for your lives; let her pass by."],"id":368,"m":"No, don't move, on your lives. Let her pass."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Come you, my lord, to see my open shame?","Now thou dost penance too. Look how they gaze!","See how the giddy multitude do point,","And nod their heads, and throw their eyes on thee!","Ah, Gloster, hide thee from their hateful looks,","And, in thy closet pent up, rue my shame,","And ban thine enemies, both mine and thine!"],"id":369,"m":"Have you come, my lord, to see my public shame? Now you're doing penance too. Look how they stare! See how the fickle crowd points and nods and casts their eyes on you! Ah, Gloucester, hide from their hateful looks, shut yourself in your room, mourn my shame, and curse your enemies, both mine and yours!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Be patient, gentle Nell; forget this grief."],"id":370,"m":"Be patient, gentle Nell; forget this grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Ah, Gloster, teach me to forget myself!","For whilst I think I am thy married wife,","And thou a prince, protector of this land,","Methinks I should not thus be led along,","Mail'd up in shame, with papers on my back,","And follow'd with a rabble that rejoice","To see my tears and hear my deep-fet groans.","The ruthless flint doth cut my tender feet,","And when I start, the envious people laugh","And bid me be advised how I tread.","Ah, Humphrey, can I bear this shameful yoke?","Trow'st thou that e'er I'll look upon the world,","Or count them happy that enjoy the sun?","No; dark shall be my light and night my day;","To think upon my pomp shall be my hell.","Sometimes I'll say, I am Duke Humphrey's wife,","And he a prince and ruler of the land;","Yet so he rul'd and such a prince he was","As he stood by whilst I, his forlorn duchess,","Was made a wonder and a pointing-stock","To every idle rascal follower.","But be thou mild and blush not at my shame,","Nor stir at nothing till the axe of death","Hang over thee, as, sure, it shortly will;","For Suffolk, he that can do all in all","With her that hateth thee and hates us all,","And York, and impious Beaufort, that false priest,","Have all lim'd bushes to betray thy wings,","And, fly thou how thou canst, they'll tangle thee;","But fear not thou until thy foot be snar'd,","Nor never seek prevention of thy foes."],"id":371,"m":"Ah, Gloucester, teach me to forget who I am! For while I think I'm your married wife and you are a prince, Protector of this land, I feel I shouldn't be led along like this, wrapped in shame, with papers on my back listing my crimes, followed by a mob who love to see my tears and hear my deep groans. The cruel stones cut my tender feet, and when I flinch, the spiteful people laugh and tell me to watch my step. Ah, Humphrey, can I bear this shameful yoke? Do you think I'll ever look at the world again, or think those who enjoy the sun are happy? No; darkness will be my light, and night my day; to remember my grandeur will be my hell. Sometimes I'll say I am Duke Humphrey's wife, and he a prince and ruler of the land. Yet he ruled so, and was such a prince, that he stood by while I, his miserable duchess, was made a spectacle and a laughing stock for every idle rascal. But you be gentle and don't blush at my shame, and don't stir until the axe of death hangs over you, as it surely will soon. For Suffolk, who can do anything with her who hates you and all of us, and York, and wicked Beaufort, that false priest, have all set traps for your wings, and however you fly, they'll tangle you. But don't be afraid till your foot is caught, and never try to forestall your enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ah, Nell, forbear! thou aimest all awry.","I must offend before I be attainted;","And had I twenty times so many foes,","And each of them had twenty times their power,","All these could not procure me any scath","So long as I am loyal, true, and crimeless.","Wouldst have me rescue thee from this reproach?","Why, yet thy scandal were not wip'd away,","But I in danger for the breach of law.","Thy greatest help is quiet, gentle Nell.","I pray thee, sort thy heart to patience;","These few days' wonder will be quickly worn."],"id":372,"m":"Ah, Nell, stop! You're completely wrong. I must commit a crime before I can be condemned. If I had twenty times as many enemies and each had twenty times their power, they couldn't do me any harm as long as I'm loyal, true and innocent. Would you have me rescue you from this disgrace? Your scandal still wouldn't be wiped away, and I'd be in danger for breaking the law. Your best help is quiet, gentle Nell. Please, settle your heart to patience; this nine days' wonder will soon wear off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Herald.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"HERALD","lines":["I summon your grace to his majesty's parliament,","Holden at Bury the first of this next month."],"id":374,"m":"I summon your grace to his Majesty's parliament, held at Bury on the first of next month."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And my consent ne'er ask'd herein before!","This is close dealing.—Well, I will be there.—"],"id":375,"m":"And my consent was never asked before this! This is secret dealing. Well, I'll be there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Herald.","id":376},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","cont":1,"lines":["My Nell, I take my leave;—and, master sheriff,","Let not her penance exceed the king's commission."],"id":377,"m":"My Nell, I take my leave. And master sheriff, don't let her penance go beyond what the king ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["An 't please your grace, here my commission stays,","And Sir John Stanley is appointed now","To take her with him to the Isle of Man."],"id":378,"m":"If it please your grace, my orders end here, and Sir John Stanley is now appointed to take her with him to the Isle of Man."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Must you, Sir John, protect my lady here?"],"id":379,"m":"Are you, Sir John, to guard my lady here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STANLEY","lines":["So am I given in charge, may 't please your grace."],"id":380,"m":"That is my charge, if it please your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Entreat her not the worse in that I pray","You use her well.","The world may laugh again,","And I may live to do you kindness if","You do it her; and so, Sir John, farewell!"],"id":381,"m":"Don't treat her worse because I beg you to treat her well. The world may smile on me again, and I may live to do you a kindness if you do her one. And so, Sir John, farewell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["What, gone, my lord, and bid me not farewell!"],"id":382,"m":"What, going, my lord, without saying goodbye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Witness my tears, I cannot stay to speak."],"id":383,"m":"My tears are my witness; I can't stay to speak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gloster and Servingmen.","id":384},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Art thou gone too? all comfort go with thee!","For none abides with me; my joy is death,","Death, at whose name I oft have been afeard,","Because I wish'd this world's eternity.—","Stanley, I prithee, go, and take me hence;","I care not whither, for I beg no favour,","Only convey me where thou art commanded."],"id":385,"m":"Are you gone too? All comfort go with you! For none stays with me. My only joy is death, death whose name I've often feared because I wanted to stay in this world forever. Stanley, please go and take me away. I don't care where, for I ask no favour; just take me where you're ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"STANLEY","lines":["Why, madam, that is to the Isle of Man;","There to be us'd according to your state."],"id":386,"m":"Why, madam, that's to the Isle of Man, to be treated there as befits your rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["That's bad enough, for I am but reproach;","And shall I then be us'd reproachfully?"],"id":387,"m":"That's bad enough, for I am nothing but disgrace. Shall I be treated disgracefully, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STANLEY","lines":["Like to a duchess, and Duke Humphrey's lady;","According to that state you shall be us'd."],"id":388,"m":"Like a duchess, and Duke Humphrey's wife; you'll be treated according to that rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Sheriff, farewell, and better than I fare,","Although thou hast been conduct of my shame."],"id":389,"m":"Sheriff, farewell, and may you fare better than I do, though you've been the escort of my shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["It is my office; and, madam, pardon me."],"id":390,"m":"It is my job; and madam, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["Ay, ay, farewell; thy office is discharg'd.—","Come, Stanley, shall we go?"],"id":391,"m":"Yes, yes, farewell; your job is done. Come, Stanley, shall we go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STANLEY","lines":["Madam, your penance done, throw off this sheet,","And go we to attire you for our journey."],"id":392,"m":"Madam, now your penance is done, throw off this sheet, and let's go and dress you for our journey."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS","lines":["My shame will not be shifted with my sheet;","No, it will hang upon my richest robes","And show itself, attire me how I can.","Go, lead the way; I long to see my prison."],"id":393,"m":"My shame won't be changed with my sheet. No, it will hang on my richest robes and show itself however I dress. Go, lead the way; I long to see my prison."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":394},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"The Abbey at Bury St. Edmund's.","id":395},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound a sennet. Enter the KING, the QUEEN, CARDINAL BEAUFORT, SUFFOLK, YORK, BUCKINGHAM, SALISBURY, and WARWICK to the Parliament.","id":396},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I muse my Lord of Gloster is not come;","'Tis not his wont to be the hindmost man,","Whate'er occasion keeps him from us now."],"id":397,"m":"I'm surprised my Lord of Gloucester hasn't come. It's not like him to be the last, whatever is keeping him from us now."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Can you not see? or will ye not observe","The strangeness of his alter'd countenance?","With what a majesty he bears himself,","How insolent of late he is become,","How proud, how peremptory, and unlike himself?","We know the time since he was mild and affable,","And if we did but glance a far-off look,","Immediately he was upon his knee,","That all the court admir'd him for submission;","But meet him now, and be it in the morn","When every one will give the time of day,","He knits his brow, and shows an angry eye,","And passeth by with stiff unbowed knee,","Disdaining duty that to us belongs.","Small curs are not regarded when they grin,","But great men tremble when the lion roars;","And Humphrey is no little man in England.","First note that he is near you in descent,","And should you fall, he is the next will mount.","Me seemeth then it is no policy,","Respecting what a rancorous mind he bears","And his advantage following your decease,","That he should come about your royal person","Or be admitted to your highness' council.","By flattery hath he won the commons' hearts,","And when he please to make commotion","'T is to be fear'd they all will follow him.","Now 't is the spring and weeds are shallow-rooted;","Suffer them now, and they'll o'ergrow the garden","And choke the herbs for want of husbandry.","The reverent care I bear unto my lord","Made me collect these dangers in the duke.","If it be fond, can it a woman's fear;","Which fear if better reasons can supplant,","I will subscribe and say I wrong'd the duke.—","My Lord of Suffolk, Buckingham, and York,","Reprove my allegation if you can,","Or else conclude my words effectual."],"id":398,"m":"Can't you see, or won't you notice, how strangely his face has changed? How majestically he carries himself, how insolent he's become lately, how proud, how bossy, how unlike himself? We remember when he was mild and friendly, and if we even glanced at him from far off, he was immediately on his knee, so that the whole court admired his humility. But meet him now, even in the morning when everyone says good day, and he frowns, gives an angry look, and walks past with a stiff knee that won't bow, scorning the respect he owes us. Little dogs aren't noticed when they snarl, but great men tremble when the lion roars, and Humphrey is no little man in England. First, remember that he is close to you by birth, and if you fell, he would be the next to rise. So it seems to me unwise, considering his bitter mind and what he'd gain from your death, that he should come near your royal person or be allowed into your highness's council. He has won the commons' hearts by flattery, and when he chooses to stir up trouble, it's to be feared they'll all follow him. Now it's spring and the weeds have shallow roots; leave them now, and they'll overgrow the garden and choke the plants for lack of care. The loving concern I have for my lord made me gather these dangers in the duke. If it's foolish, call it a woman's fear; and if better reasons can replace that fear, I'll agree and say I wronged the duke. My Lords of Suffolk, Buckingham and York, disprove my charge if you can, or else agree my words are right."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Well hath your highness seen into this duke;","And, had I first been put to speak my mind,","I think I should have told your grace's tale.","The duchess by his subornation,","Upon my life, began her devilish practices;","Or, if he were not privy to those faults,","Yet, by reputing of his high descent,","As next the king he was successive heir,","And such high vaunts of his nobility,","Did instigate the bedlam brain-sick duchess","By wicked means to frame our sovereign's fall.","Smooth runs the water where the brook is deep,","And in his simple show he harbours treason.","The fox barks not when he would steal the lamb.—","No, no, my sovereign; Gloster is a man","Unsounded yet and full of deep deceit."],"id":399,"m":"Your highness has seen right into this duke, and if I'd been asked to speak first, I think I would have said the same. On my life, the duchess began her devilish practices because he encouraged her. Or if he didn't know of those crimes, still, by boasting of his high birth, that he was next heir after the king, and by bragging so much about his nobility, he egged on the mad, brain-sick duchess to plot our sovereign's fall by wicked means. Still waters run deep, and under his simple appearance he hides treason. The fox doesn't bark when it means to steal the lamb. No, no, my sovereign, Gloucester is a man not yet sounded out, and full of deep deceit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Did he not, contrary to form of law,","Devise strange deaths for small offences done?"],"id":400,"m":"Didn't he, against the proper process of law, invent cruel deaths for small crimes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["And did he not, in his protectorship,","Levy great sums of money through the realm","For soldiers' pay in France, and never sent it?","By means whereof the towns each day revolted."],"id":401,"m":"And didn't he, as Protector, raise large sums across the country to pay the soldiers in France and never send it? That's why towns there revolted every day."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Tut, these are petty faults to faults unknown,","Which time will bring to light in smooth Duke Humphrey."],"id":402,"m":"Nonsense, these are small faults compared with the hidden ones that time will bring to light in smooth Duke Humphrey."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My lords, at once: the care you have of us,","To mow down thorns that would annoy our foot,","Is worthy praise; but, shall I speak my conscience,","Our kinsman Gloster is as innocent","From meaning treason to our royal person","As is the sucking lamb or harmless dove.","The duke is virtuous, mild, and too well given","To dream on evil or to work my downfall."],"id":403,"m":"My lords, to answer you all at once: your care for us, cutting down thorns that might hurt our feet, deserves praise. But to speak my conscience, our kinsman Gloucester is as innocent of meaning treason to us as a suckling lamb or a harmless dove. The duke is virtuous, gentle, and too good-natured to dream of evil or plot my downfall."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Ah, what's more dangerous than this fond affiance!","Seems he a dove? his feathers are but borrow'd,","For he's disposed as the hateful raven;","Is he a lamb? his skin is surely lent him,","For he's inclin'd as is the ravenous wolf.","Who cannot steal a shape that means deceit?","Take heed, my lord; the welfare of us all","Hangs on the cutting short that fraudful man."],"id":404,"m":"Ah, what's more dangerous than this foolish trust! Does he seem a dove? His feathers are only borrowed, for he has the nature of the hateful raven. Is he a lamb? His skin is surely lent to him, for he has the instincts of the greedy wolf. Who can't put on a disguise to deceive? Be careful, my lord; all our safety depends on cutting that cheat down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SOMERSET.","id":405},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["All health unto my gracious sovereign!"],"id":406,"m":"Good health to my gracious sovereign!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Welcome, Lord Somerset. What news from France?"],"id":407,"m":"Welcome, Lord Somerset. What news from France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["That all your interest in those territories","Is utterly bereft you; all is lost."],"id":408,"m":"That all your claims in those lands are completely taken from you. Everything is lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Cold news, Lord Somerset; but God's will be done!"],"id":409,"m":"Cold news, Lord Somerset; but God's will be done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["[Aside] Cold news for me, for I had hope of France","As firmly as I hope for fertile England.","Thus are my blossoms blasted in the bud,","And caterpillars eat my leaves away;","But I will remedy this gear ere long","Or sell my title for a glorious grave."],"id":410,"m":"(Aside) Cold news for me, for I hoped for France as firmly as I hope for fertile England. So my blossoms are blighted in the bud, and caterpillars eat away my leaves. But I'll put this business right before long, or trade my claim for a glorious grave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GLOSTER.","id":411},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["All happiness unto my lord the king!","Pardon, my liege, that I have staid so long."],"id":412,"m":"All happiness to my lord the king! Forgive me, my lord, for being so late."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Nay, Gloster, know that thou art come too soon,","Unless thou wert more loyal than thou art.","I do arrest thee of high treason here."],"id":413,"m":"No, Gloucester, you've come too soon, unless you were more loyal than you are. I arrest you here for high treason."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Well, Suffolk, thou shalt not see me blush,","Nor change my countenance for this arrest;","A heart unspotted is not easily daunted.","The purest spring is not so free from mud","As I am clear from treason to my sovereign.","Who can accuse me? wherein am I guilty?"],"id":414,"m":"Well, Suffolk, you won't see me blush or change my expression at this arrest; an unstained heart is not easily scared. The purest spring is not as free of mud as I am free of treason against my sovereign. Who accuses me? What am I guilty of?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["'T is thought, my lord, that you took bribes of France,","And, being protector, stay'd the soldiers' pay,","By means whereof his highness hath lost France."],"id":415,"m":"It's said, my lord, that you took bribes from France, and as Protector held back the soldiers' pay, and that's how his highness has lost France."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Is it but thought so? what are they that think it?","I never robb'd the soldiers of their pay,","Nor ever had one penny bribe from France.","So help me God, as I have watch'd the night,","Ay, night by night, in studying good for England!","That doit that e'er I wrested from the king,","Or any groat I hoarded to my use,","Be brought against me at my trial-day!","No; many a pound of mine own proper store,","Because I would not tax the needy commons,","Have I dispursed to the garrisons,","And never ask'd for restitution."],"id":416,"m":"Is it only \"said\"? Who says it? I never robbed the soldiers of their pay, nor took one penny in bribes from France. So help me God, as I have stayed awake night after night working for England's good! Let any coin I ever squeezed from the king, or any penny I hoarded for myself, be brought against me on the day of my trial! No; many pounds of my own money I've paid out to the garrisons, because I wouldn't tax the poor commons, and I never asked to be paid back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["It serves you well, my lord, to say so much."],"id":417,"m":"It suits you well, my lord, to say so much."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["I say no more than truth, so help me God!"],"id":418,"m":"I say nothing but the truth, so help me God!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["In your protectorship you did devise","Strange tortures for offenders never heard of,","That England was defam'd by tyranny."],"id":419,"m":"As Protector you invented strange, unheard-of tortures for criminals, so that England got a name for tyranny."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why, 't is well known that, whiles I was protector,","Pity was all the fault that was in me;","For I should melt at an offender's tears,","And lowly words were ransom for their fault.","Unless it were a bloody murtherer,","Or foul felonious thief that fleec'd poor passengers,","I never gave them condign punishment.","Murther indeed, that bloody sin, I tortur'd","Above the felon or what trespass else."],"id":420,"m":"Why, everyone knows that while I was Protector, my only fault was pity. I would melt at a criminal's tears, and humble words paid for their crime. Unless it was a bloody murderer or a foul thief who robbed poor travellers, I never gave the full punishment. Murder, that bloody sin, I did punish more than theft or any other crime."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["My lord, these faults are easy, quickly answer'd;","But mightier crimes are laid unto your charge,","Whereof you cannot easily purge yourself.","I do arrest you in his highness' name,","And here commit you to my lord cardinal","To keep until your further time of trial."],"id":421,"m":"My lord, these faults are slight and quickly answered; but greater crimes are charged against you, which you can't easily clear yourself of. I arrest you in his highness's name, and hand you over to my lord cardinal to keep until your trial."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My Lord of Gloster, 't is my special hope","That you will clear yourself from all suspect;","My conscience tells me you are innocent."],"id":422,"m":"My Lord of Gloucester, I truly hope you will clear yourself of all suspicion. My conscience tells me you are innocent."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ah, gracious lord, these days are dangerous.","Virtue is chok'd with foul ambition,","And charity chas'd hence by rancour's hand;","Foul subornation is predominant,","And equity exil'd your highness' land.","I know their complot is to have my life,","And if my death might make this island happy","And prove the period of their tyranny,","I would expend it with all willingness;","But mine is made the prologue to their play,","For thousands more, that yet suspect no peril,","Will not conclude their plotted tragedy.","Beaufort's red sparkling eyes blab his heart's malice,","And Suffolk's cloudy brow his stormy hate;","Sharp Buckingham unburthens with his tongue","The envious load that lies upon his heart;","And dogged York, that reaches at the moon,","Whose overweening arm I have pluck'd back,","By false accuse doth level at my life.—","And you, my sovereign lady, with the rest,","Causeless have laid disgraces on my head","And with your best endeavour have stirr'd up","My liefest liege to be mine enemy.—","Ay, all of you have laid your heads together—","Myself had notice of your conventicles—","And all to make away my guiltless life.","I shall not want false witness to condemn me,","Nor store of treasons to augment my guilt;","The ancient proverb will be well effected,—","'A staff is quickly found to beat a dog.'"],"id":423,"m":"Ah, gracious lord, these are dangerous days. Virtue is choked by foul ambition and charity driven out by hatred; foul bribery rules and justice is exiled from your land. I know their plot is to take my life. If my death could make this island happy and end their tyranny, I'd give it gladly. But mine is only the prologue to their play, for thousands more who don't yet suspect danger won't finish their planned tragedy. Beaufort's red, sparkling eyes give away his heart's malice, and Suffolk's cloudy frown his stormy hatred. Sharp-tongued Buckingham unloads the envy that weighs on his heart, and stubborn York, who reaches for the moon and whose overreaching arm I have pulled back, aims at my life with false accusations. And you, my sovereign lady, along with the rest, have heaped disgrace on my head for no reason and done your best to stir up my dearest lord to be my enemy. Yes, you've all put your heads together, I heard about your secret meetings, all to get rid of my innocent life. I won't lack false witnesses to condemn me, or invented treasons to add to my guilt. The old proverb will come true: \"Any stick will do to beat a dog.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["My liege, his railing is intolerable;","If those that care to keep your royal person","From treason's secret knife and traitor's rage","Be thus upbraided, chid, and rated at,","And the offender granted scope of speech,","'T will make them cool in zeal unto your grace."],"id":424,"m":"My lord, his abuse is unbearable. If those who care to keep your royal person safe from treason's hidden knife and traitors' rage are insulted, scolded and abused like this, and the offender is allowed to say what he likes, it will cool their zeal for your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Hath he not twit our sovereign lady here","With ignominious words, though clerkly couch'd,","As if she had suborned some to swear","False allegations to o'erthrow his state?"],"id":425,"m":"Hasn't he taunted our sovereign lady here with disgraceful words, though cleverly phrased, as if she had bribed people to swear false charges to bring him down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["But I can give the loser leave to chide."],"id":426,"m":"But I can allow the loser to complain."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Far truer spoke than meant; I lose, indeed.","Beshrew the winners, for they play'd me false!","And well such losers may have leave to speak."],"id":427,"m":"Spoken more truly than you meant: I do lose indeed. Curse the winners, for they cheated me! And losers like that may well be allowed to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["He'll wrest the sense and hold us here all day.—","Lord Cardinal, he is your prisoner."],"id":428,"m":"He'll twist our meaning and keep us here all day. Lord Cardinal, he is your prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Sirs, take away the Duke, and guard him sure."],"id":429,"m":"Men, take the duke away, and guard him well."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ah, thus King Henry throws away his crutch","Before his legs be firm to bear his body.","Thus is the shepherd beaten from thy side,","And wolves are gnarling who shall gnaw thee first.","Ah, that my fear were false! ah, that it were!","For, good King Henry, thy decay I fear."],"id":430,"m":"Ah, so King Henry throws away his crutch before his legs are strong enough to carry his body. So the shepherd is beaten from your side, and wolves are snarling over who will bite you first. Ah, I wish my fear were false! I wish it were! For, good King Henry, I fear your ruin."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, guarded.","id":431},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My lords, what to your wisdoms seemeth best,","Do or undo, as if ourself were here."],"id":432,"m":"My lords, do or undo whatever seems best to your wisdom, as if we were here ourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What, will your highness leave the parliament?"],"id":433,"m":"What, will your highness leave the parliament?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, Margaret; my heart is drown'd with grief,","Whose flood begins to flow within mine eyes,","My body round engirt with misery,","For what's more miserable than discontent?—","Ah, uncle Humphrey! in thy face I see","The map of honour, truth, and loyalty;","And yet, good Humphrey, is the hour to come","That e'er I prov'd thee false or fear'd thy faith.","What lowering star now envies thy estate,","That these great lords and Margaret our queen","Do seek subversion of thy harmless life?","Thou never didst them wrong nor no man wrong;","And as the butcher takes away the calf","And binds the wretch and beats it when it strays,","Bearing it to the bloody slaughter-house,","Even so remorseless have they borne him hence;","And as the dam runs lowing up and down,","Looking the way her harmless young one went,","And can do nought but wail her darling's loss,","Even so myself bewails good Gloster's case","With sad unhelpful tears, and with dimm'd eyes","Look after him, and cannot do him good,","So mighty are his vowed enemies.","His fortunes I will weep and 'twixt each groan","Say 'Who's a traitor? Gloster he is none.'"],"id":434,"m":"Yes, Margaret. My heart is drowned in grief, whose flood begins to pour from my eyes, and my body is wrapped in misery, for what is more miserable than unhappiness? Ah, uncle Humphrey! In your face I see the map of honour, truth and loyalty, and the hour has yet to come when I found you false or doubted your faith. What gloomy star now envies your fortune, that these great lords and our queen Margaret seek to destroy your harmless life? You never wronged them or any man. And as the butcher takes away the calf, ties up the poor thing and beats it when it strays, carrying it to the bloody slaughterhouse, so pitilessly have they carried him away. And as the mother cow runs up and down lowing, looking the way her harmless young one went, and can do nothing but wail for her darling, so I mourn good Gloucester's fate with sad, useless tears, and watch after him with dim eyes, unable to help him, his sworn enemies are so powerful. I'll weep for his fortunes, and between each groan say, \"Who's a traitor? Gloucester is not.\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Queen, Cardinal Beaufort, Suffolk and York; Somerset remains apart.","id":435},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Free lords, cold snow melts with the sun's hot beams.","Henry my lord is cold in great affairs,","Too full of foolish pity, and Gloster's show","Beguiles him as the mournful crocodile","With sorrow snares relenting passengers,","Or as the snake roll'd in a flowering bank,","With shining checker'd slough, doth sting a child","That for the beauty thinks it excellent.","Believe me, lords, were none more wise than I—","And yet herein I judge mine own wit good—","This Gloster should be quickly rid the world,","To rid us from the fear we have of him."],"id":436,"m":"Noble lords, cold snow melts in the sun's hot rays. My lord Henry is cold in great matters, too full of foolish pity, and Gloucester's show of goodness fools him, as the weeping crocodile traps soft-hearted travellers with its sorrow, or as the snake coiled on a flowery bank, with its shining speckled skin, stings a child who thinks it lovely because of its beauty. Believe me, lords, if nobody were wiser than me, and in this I think my judgement good, this Gloucester would soon be got rid of, to rid us of the fear we have of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["That he should die is worthy policy,","But yet we want a colour for his death,","'T is meet he be condemn'd by course of law."],"id":437,"m":"His death would be good policy, but we still need a pretext for it. It's right he should be condemned by the course of law."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["But, in my mind, that were no policy.","The king will labour still to save his life;","The commons haply rise to save his life,","And yet we have but trivial argument,","More than mistrust, that shows him worthy death."],"id":438,"m":"But in my view that would be bad policy. The king will keep working to save his life, the commons might rise to save it, and so far we have only trivial evidence, nothing beyond suspicion, to show he deserves death."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["So that, by this, you would not have him die."],"id":439,"m":"So by this, you'd rather he didn't die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Ah, York, no man alive so fain as I!"],"id":440,"m":"Ah, York, no man alive wants it more than I!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["'T is York that hath more reason for his death.—","But, my lord cardinal, and you, my Lord of Suffolk,","Say as you think, and speak it from your souls,","Were 't not all one an empty eagle were set","To guard the chicken from a hungry kite,","As place Duke Humphrey for the king's protector?"],"id":441,"m":"It's York who has more reason to want him dead. But my lord cardinal, and you, my Lord of Suffolk, say what you think, from your souls: wouldn't putting Duke Humphrey in charge as the king's Protector be like setting a hungry eagle to guard a chicken from a hungry hawk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["So the poor chicken should be sure of death."],"id":442,"m":"Then the poor chicken would be sure to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Madam, 't is true; and were 't not madness, then,","To make the fox surveyor of the fold?","Who being accus'd a crafty murtherer,","His guilt should be but idly posted over,","Because his purpose is not executed.","No; let him die, in that he is a fox,","By nature prov'd an enemy to the flock,","Before his chaps be stain'd with crimson blood,","As Humphrey, prov'd by reasons, to my liege.","And do not stand on quillets how to slay him.","Be it by gins, by snares, by subtlety,","Sleeping or waking, 't is no matter how,","So he be dead; for that is good deceit","Which mates him first that first intends deceit."],"id":443,"m":"Madam, that's true. And wouldn't it be madness, then, to make the fox guardian of the sheepfold? If he were accused of being a cunning murderer, his guilt shouldn't be idly passed over just because he hasn't carried out his plan yet. No, let him die because he's a fox, proven by nature to be an enemy to the flock, before his jaws are stained with blood, as Humphrey is proven by argument to be my lord's enemy. And don't quibble about how to kill him. By traps, by snares, by cunning, sleeping or awake, it doesn't matter how, as long as he's dead. For it's good deceit that checkmates the man who first planned to deceive."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Thrice-noble Suffolk, 't is resolutely spoke."],"id":444,"m":"Thrice-noble Suffolk, that's boldly spoken."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Not resolute, except so much were done,","For things are often spoke and seldom meant;","But that my heart accordeth with my tongue,—","Seeing the deed is meritorious,","And to preserve my sovereign from his foe,—","Say but the word, and I will be his priest."],"id":445,"m":"It's not bold unless it's done, for things are often said and seldom meant. But to show my heart agrees with my tongue, since the deed deserves credit and will protect my sovereign from his enemy, just say the word and I'll be his priest at his deathbed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["But I would have him dead, my Lord of Suffolk,","Ere you can take due orders for a priest.","Say you consent and censure well the deed,","And I'll provide his executioner,","I tender so the safety of my liege."],"id":446,"m":"But I'd have him dead, my Lord of Suffolk, before you could be properly ordained a priest. Say you agree and approve the deed, and I'll provide his executioner, I care so much for my lord's safety."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Here is my hand, the deed is worthy doing."],"id":447,"m":"Here's my hand; the deed is worth doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And so say I."],"id":448,"m":"And so say I."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["And I; and now we three have spoke it,","It skills not greatly who impugns our doom."],"id":449,"m":"And I. And now that we three have said it, it hardly matters who disputes our verdict."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Post.","id":450},{"t":"sp","who":"POST","lines":["Great lords, from Ireland am I come amain,","To signify that rebels there are up","And put the Englishmen unto the sword.","Send succours, lords, and stop the rage betime,","Before the wound do grow uncurable;","For, being green, there is great hope of help."],"id":451,"m":"Great lords, I've come at full speed from Ireland to report that rebels there have risen and are putting the English to the sword. Send help, lords, and stop the violence in time, before the wound becomes incurable; while it's fresh there's great hope of a cure."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["A breach that craves a quick expedient stop!","What council give you in this weighty cause?"],"id":452,"m":"A breach that needs to be stopped quickly! What do you advise in this serious matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["That Somerset be sent as regent thither.","'T is meet that lucky ruler be employ'd;","Witness the fortune he hath had in France."],"id":453,"m":"That Somerset be sent there as regent. It's right to use such a lucky ruler; look at the luck he's had in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["If York, with all his far-fet policy,","Had been the regent there instead of me,","He never would have stay'd in France so long."],"id":454,"m":"If York, with all his far-fetched cunning, had been regent there instead of me, he wouldn't have stayed in France as long as I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["No, not to lose it all as thou hast done;","I rather would have lost my life betimes","Than bring a burden of dishonour home","By staying there so long till all were lost.","Show me one scar character'd on thy skin;","Men's flesh preserv'd so whole do seldom win."],"id":455,"m":"No, not to lose it all as you have done. I'd rather have lost my life early than bring home a load of dishonour by staying until everything was lost. Show me one scar written on your skin; men who keep their flesh so whole rarely win."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Nay then, this spark will prove a raging fire,","If wind and fuel be brought to feed it with.","No more, good York.—Sweet Somerset, be still.—","Thy fortune, York, hadst thou been regent there,","Might happily have prov'd far worse than his."],"id":456,"m":"Now then, this spark will become a raging fire if wind and fuel are added. No more, good York. Sweet Somerset, be quiet. If you'd been regent there, York, your luck might well have been far worse than his."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["What, worse than nought? nay, then a shame take all!"],"id":457,"m":"What, worse than nothing? Then shame take everyone!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["And, in the number, thee that wishest shame!"],"id":458,"m":"Including you, who wish shame on us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["My Lord of York, try what your fortune is.","The uncivil kerns of Ireland are in arms,","And temper clay with blood of Englishmen.","To Ireland will you lead a band of men,","Collected choicely, from each county some,","And try your hap against the Irishmen?"],"id":459,"m":"My Lord of York, try your own luck. The savage Irish foot soldiers are up in arms and mixing mud with English blood. Will you lead a band of men to Ireland, picked carefully from every county, and try your luck against the Irish?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I will, my lord, so please his majesty."],"id":460,"m":"I will, my lord, if it please his Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Why, our authority is his consent,","And what we do establish he confirms.—","Then, noble York, take thou this task in hand."],"id":461,"m":"Why, our authority is his consent, and what we decide, he confirms. So, noble York, take on this task."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I am content.—Provide me soldiers, lords,","Whiles I take order for mine own affairs."],"id":462,"m":"I'm happy to. Lords, get soldiers ready for me while I put my own affairs in order."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["A charge, Lord York, that I will see perform'd.","But now return we to the false Duke Humphrey."],"id":463,"m":"A responsibility, Lord York, I'll make sure is carried out. But now back to the false Duke Humphrey."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["No more of him; for I will deal with him","That henceforth he shall trouble us no more.","And so break off; the day is almost spent.—","Lord Suffolk, you and I must talk of that event."],"id":464,"m":"No more about him, for I'll deal with him so that he won't trouble us any more. So let's end here; the day is almost over. Lord Suffolk, you and I must talk about that business."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["My Lord of Suffolk, within fourteen days","At Bristol I expect my soldiers;","For there I'll ship them all for Ireland."],"id":465,"m":"My Lord of Suffolk, I expect my soldiers at Bristol within fourteen days, for there I'll ship them all to Ireland."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I'll see it truly done, my Lord of York."],"id":466,"m":"I'll see it faithfully done, my Lord of York."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but York.","id":467},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Now, York, or never, steel thy fearful thoughts,","And change misdoubt to resolution.","Be that thou hop'st to be, or what thou art","Resign to death; it is not worth the enjoying.","Let pale-fac'd fear keep with the mean-born man,","And find no harbour in a royal heart.","Faster than spring-time showers comes thought on thought,","And not a thought but thinks on dignity.","My brain more busy than the labouring spider","Weaves tedious snares to trap mine enemies.","Well, nobles, well, 't is politicly done,","To send me packing with an host of men;","I fear me you but warm the starved snake,","Who, cherish'd in your breasts, will sting your hearts.","'T was men I lack'd, and you will give them me;","I take it kindly, yet be well-assur'd","You put sharp weapons in a madman's hands.","Whiles I in Ireland nourish a mighty band,","I will stir up in England some black storm","Shall blow ten thousand souls to heaven or hell;","And this fell tempest shall not cease to rage","Until the golden circuit on my head,","Like to the glorious sun's transparent beams,","Do calm the fury of this mad-bred flaw.","And for a minister of my intent,","I have seduc'd a headstrong Kentishman,","John Cade of Ashford,","To make commotion, as full well he can,","Under the tide of John Mortimer.","In Ireland have I seen this stubborn Cade","Oppose himself against a troop of kerns,","And fought so long till that his thighs with darts","Were almost like a sharp-quill'd porpentine;","And, in the end being rescu'd, I have seen","Him caper upright like a wild Morisco,","Shaking the bloody darts as he his bells.","Full often, like a shag-hair'd crafty kern,","Hath he conversed with the enemy,","And undiscover'd come to me again","And given me notice of their villainies.","This devil here shall be my substitute;","For that John Mortimer, which now is dead,","In face, in gait, in speech, he doth resemble.","By this I shall perceive the commons' mind,","How they affect the house and claim of York.","Say he be taken, rack'd, and tortured,","I know no pain they can inflict upon him","Will make him say I mov'd him to those arms.","Say that he thrive, as 't is great like he will,","Why, then from Ireland come I with my strength","And reap the harvest which that rascal sow'd;","For Humphrey being dead, as he shall be,","And Henry put apart, the next for me."],"id":468,"m":"Now, York, or never, harden your fearful thoughts and turn doubt into determination. Be what you hope to be, or give up what you are to death; it isn't worth keeping. Let pale-faced fear stay with low-born men and find no shelter in a royal heart. Thoughts come faster than spring showers, and every thought is about high rank. My brain, busier than the working spider, weaves elaborate traps for my enemies. Well, nobles, well; it's clever of you to send me off with an army. I fear you're just warming the frozen snake that, cherished in your bosoms, will sting your hearts. It was men I lacked, and you're giving them to me. I take it kindly, but be sure you're putting sharp weapons in a madman's hands. While I build up a mighty army in Ireland, I'll stir up some black storm in England that will blow ten thousand souls to heaven or hell, and this fierce storm won't stop raging until the golden circle on my head, like the sun's glorious bright rays, calms the fury of this mad gust. And as the agent of my plan, I've persuaded a headstrong man from Kent, John Cade of Ashford, to stir up a rising, as he well can, under the name of John Mortimer. In Ireland I've seen this stubborn Cade take on a troop of Irish soldiers and fight so long that his thighs were stuck with darts like a porcupine's quills. When he was finally rescued, I saw him leap about like a wild morris dancer, shaking the bloody darts as a dancer shakes his bells. Very often, disguised as a shaggy-haired, crafty Irish soldier, he has mixed with the enemy and come back to me undiscovered with news of their plots. This devil will be my stand-in, for he looks, walks and talks like John Mortimer, who is now dead. Through him I'll find out what the commons think, how they feel about the house of York and its claim. Say he's caught, racked and tortured; I know of no pain they can inflict that will make him say I put him up to this rising. Say he succeeds, as is very likely; why, then I'll come from Ireland with my army and reap the harvest that rascal sowed. For with Humphrey dead, as he will be, and Henry pushed aside, the crown comes next to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":469},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Bury St. Edmund's. A Room of State.","id":470},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain Murderers, hastily.","id":471},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST MURDERER","lines":["Run to my Lord of Suffolk; let him know","We have dispatch'd the duke, as he commanded."],"id":472,"m":"Run to my Lord of Suffolk and tell him we've killed the duke, as he ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MURDERER","lines":["O that it were to do! What have we done?","Didst ever hear a man so penitent?"],"id":473,"m":"Oh, if only it were still to do! What have we done? Did you ever hear a man so repentant?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SUFFOLK.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST MURDERER","lines":["Here comes my lord."],"id":475,"m":"Here comes my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Now, sirs, have you dispatch'd this thing?"],"id":476,"m":"Now, men, have you finished this business?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST MURDERER","lines":["Ay, my good lord, he's dead."],"id":477,"m":"Yes, my good lord, he's dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Why, that's well said. Go, get you to my house;","I will reward you for this venturous deed.","The king and all the peers are here at hand.","Have you laid fair the bed? Is all things well,","According as I gave directions?"],"id":478,"m":"Good, well said. Go to my house; I'll reward you for this daring deed. The king and all the lords are close by. Have you smoothed the bed? Is everything in order, as I told you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST MURDERER","lines":["'T is, my good lord."],"id":479,"m":"It is, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Away! be gone."],"id":480,"m":"Away! Go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Murderers.","id":481},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets. Enter the KING, the QUEEN, CARDINAL BEAUFORT, SOMERSET, with attendants.","id":482},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Go, call our uncle to our presence straight;","Say we intend to try his grace to-day,","If he be guilty, as 't is published."],"id":483,"m":"Go and call our uncle here at once. Tell him we mean to try his case today, if he is guilty, as people say."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I'll call him presently, my noble lord."],"id":484,"m":"I'll call him at once, my noble lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":485},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Lords, take your places; and, I pray you all,","Proceed no straiter 'gainst our uncle Gloster","Than from true evidence of good esteem","He be approv'd in practice culpable."],"id":486,"m":"Lords, take your seats, and I beg you all not to deal more harshly with our uncle Gloucester than true and reliable evidence proves he is guilty in fact."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["God forbid any malice should prevail","That faultless may condemn a nobleman!","Pray God he may acquit him of suspicion!"],"id":487,"m":"God forbid that any malice should win and condemn an innocent nobleman! I pray God he can clear himself of suspicion!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I thank thee, Meg; these words content me much.—"],"id":488,"m":"Thank you, Meg; those words please me very much."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter SUFFOLK.","id":489},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["How now! why look'st thou pale? why tremblest thou?","Where is our uncle? what's the matter, Suffolk?"],"id":490,"m":"What's this! Why do you look pale? Why are you trembling? Where is our uncle? What's the matter, Suffolk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Dead in his bed, my lord; Gloster is dead."],"id":491,"m":"Dead in his bed, my lord. Gloucester is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Marry, God forfend!"],"id":492,"m":"Indeed, God forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["God's secret judgment!—I did dream to-night","The duke was dumb and could not speak a word."],"id":493,"m":"God's secret judgement! I dreamed last night that the duke was dumb and couldn't speak a word."},{"t":"sd","text":"The King swoons.","id":494},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["How fares my lord?—Help, lords! the king is dead."],"id":495,"m":"How is my lord? Help, lords! The king is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Rear up his body; wring him by the nose."],"id":496,"m":"Lift him up; pinch his nose."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Run, go, help, help!—O Henry, ope thine eyes!"],"id":497,"m":"Run, go, help, help! O Henry, open your eyes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["He doth revive again.—Madam, be patient."],"id":498,"m":"He's coming round. Madam, be calm."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O heavenly God!"],"id":499,"m":"O heavenly God!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["How fares my gracious lord?"],"id":500,"m":"How is my gracious lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Comfort, my sovereign! gracious Henry, comfort!"],"id":501,"m":"Take comfort, my sovereign! Gracious Henry, take comfort!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What, doth my Lord of Suffolk comfort me?","Came he right now to sing a raven's note","Whose dismal tune bereft my vital powers,","And thinks he that the chirping of a wren,","By crying comfort from a hollow breast,","Can chase away the first-conceived sound?","Hide not thy poison with such sugar'd words;","Lay not thy hands on me; forbear, I say!","Their touch affrights me as a serpent's sting.","Thou baleful messenger, out of my sight!","Upon thy eye-balls murtherous tyranny","Sits in grim majesty, to fright the world.","Look not upon me, for thine eyes are wounding.","Yet do not go away; come, basilisk,","And kill the innocent gazer with thy sight,","For in the shade of death I shall find joy,","In life but double death, now Gloster's dead."],"id":502,"m":"What, does my Lord of Suffolk comfort me? Did he just now come to croak like a raven, whose deadly tune took away my strength, and does he think that the chirping of a wren, crying \"comfort\" from a hollow heart, can drive away that first sound? Don't hide your poison with such sugary words. Don't touch me; stop, I say! Your hands frighten me like a snake's sting. You deadly messenger, out of my sight! Murderous tyranny sits in grim majesty on your eyeballs to frighten the world. Don't look at me, for your eyes wound. Yet don't go. Come, basilisk, and kill the innocent onlooker with your glance, for I'll find joy in the shadow of death, and only double death in life, now Gloucester is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Why do you rate my Lord of Suffolk thus?","Although the duke was enemy to him,","Yet he most Christian-like laments his death;","And for myself, foe as he was to me,","Might liquid tears or heart-offending groans","Or blood-consuming sighs recall his life,","I would be blind with weeping, sick with groans,","Look pale as primrose with blood-drinking sighs,","And all to have the noble duke alive.","What know I how the world may deem of me?","For it is known we were but hollow friends.","It may be judg'd I made the duke away;","So shall my name with slander's tongue be wounded","And princes' courts be fill'd with my reproach.","This get I by his death. Ay me, unhappy!","To be a queen, and crown'd with infamy!"],"id":503,"m":"Why do you scold my Lord of Suffolk like this? Though the duke was his enemy, he mourns his death like a true Christian. And as for me, enemy though he was to me, if flowing tears or heartbreaking groans or life-draining sighs could bring him back, I'd blind myself weeping, sicken myself groaning, turn pale as a primrose with sighs, all to have the noble duke alive. How do I know what the world will think of me? For it's known we were only false friends. People may think I got rid of the duke, so my name will be wounded by slander and royal courts all over will be full of my disgrace. That's what I get from his death. Oh, how unhappy I am, to be a queen and crowned with shame!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ah, woe is me for Gloster, wretched man!"],"id":504,"m":"Ah, woe is me for Gloucester, poor man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Be woe for me, more wretched than he is.","What, dost thou turn away and hide thy face?","I am no loathsome leper; look on me.","What! art thou, like the adder, waxen deaf?","Be poisonous too and kill thy forlorn queen.","Is all thy comfort shut in Gloster's tomb?","Why, then, dame Margaret was ne'er thy joy.","Erect his statue and worship it,","And make my image but an alehouse sign.","Was I for this nigh wrack'd upon the sea,","And twice by awkward wind from England's bank","Drove back again unto my native clime?","What boded this but well forewarning wind","Did seem to say 'Seek not a scorpion's nest,","Nor set no footing on this unkind shore?'","What did I then, but curs'd the gentle gusts","And he that loos'd them forth their brazen caves,","And bid them blow towards England's blessed shore,","Or turn our stern upon a dreadful rock?","Yet Aeolus would not be a murtherer,","But left that hateful office unto thee.","The pretty-vaulting sea refus'd to drown me,","Knowing that thou wouldst have me drown'd on shore,","With tears as salt as sea, through thy unkindness.","The splitting rocks cower'd in the sinking sands","And would not dash me with their ragged sides,","Because thy flinty heart, more hard than they,","Might in thy palace perish Margaret.","As far as I could ken thy chalky cliffs,","When from thy shore the tempest beat us back,","I stood upon the hatches in the storm,","And when the dusky sky began to rob","My earnest-gaping sight of thy land's view,","I took a costly jewel from my neck—","A heart it was, bound in with diamonds—","And threw it towards thy land; the sea receiv'd it,","And so I wish'd thy body might my heart.","And even with this I lost fair England's view,","And bid mine eyes be packing with my heart,","And call'd them blind and dusky spectacles,","For losing ken of Albion's wished coast.","How often have I tempted Suffolk's tongue,","The agent of thy foul inconstancy,","To sit and witch me, as Ascanius did","When he to madding Dido would unfold","His father's acts commenc'd in burning Troy!","Am I not witch'd like her? or thou not false like him?","Ay me, I can no more! die, Margaret!","For Henry weeps that thou dost live so long."],"id":505,"m":"Say woe for me, more wretched than he is. What, are you turning away and hiding your face? I'm not a disgusting leper; look at me. What! Have you gone deaf like the adder? Then be poisonous too and kill your abandoned queen. Is all your comfort shut up in Gloucester's tomb? Then Queen Margaret was never your joy. Put up his statue and worship it, and make my picture into a pub sign. Was it for this that I was nearly shipwrecked at sea, and twice driven back home from England's coast by contrary winds? What did that mean except that the warning wind seemed to say, \"Don't seek a scorpion's nest; don't set foot on this unkind shore\"? And what did I do but curse the gentle winds and the god who let them out of their caves, and order them to blow towards England's blessed shore or wreck us on a terrible rock? Yet Aeolus wouldn't be a murderer, but left that hateful job to you. The heaving sea refused to drown me, knowing you'd have me drowned on shore in tears as salt as the sea through your unkindness. The splitting rocks hid themselves in the sinking sands and wouldn't dash me with their jagged sides, so that your heart, harder than flint, could kill Margaret in your palace. As long as I could see your chalk cliffs, when the storm beat us back from your shore, I stood on deck in the storm, and when the dark sky began to rob my eager eyes of the sight of your land, I took a precious jewel from my neck, a heart set in diamonds, and threw it towards your land. The sea took it, and so I wished your body would take my heart. And with that I lost sight of fair England, told my eyes to go away with my heart, and called them blind, dim glasses for losing sight of England's longed-for coast. How often have I tempted Suffolk's tongue, the agent of your foul fickleness, to sit and charm me, as Ascanius did when he told the frantic Dido his father's deeds that began in burning Troy! Am I not bewitched like her? Or you not false like him? Oh, I can't go on! Die, Margaret! For Henry weeps that you live so long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Noise within. Enter WARWICK, SALISBURY, and many Commons.","id":506},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["It is reported, mighty sovereign,","That good Duke Humphrey traitorously is murther'd","By Suffolk and the Cardinal Beaufort's means.","The commons, like an angry hive of bees","That want their leader, scatter up and down","And care not who they sting in his revenge.","Myself have calm'd their spleenful mutiny","Until they hear the order of his death."],"id":507,"m":"Mighty sovereign, it's reported that good Duke Humphrey has been treacherously murdered by Suffolk and Cardinal Beaufort. The commons, like an angry hive of bees that have lost their leader, are swarming up and down and don't care whom they sting to avenge him. I've calmed their furious rioting until they hear how he died."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["That he is dead, good Warwick, 't is too true;","But how he died God knows, not Henry.","Enter his chamber, view his breathless corpse,","And comment then upon his sudden death."],"id":508,"m":"That he is dead, good Warwick, is too true; but how he died, God knows, not Henry. Go into his room, look at his lifeless body, and then explain his sudden death."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["That shall I do, my liege.—Stay, Salisbury,","With the rude multitude till I return."],"id":509,"m":"I will, my lord. Stay, Salisbury, with the rough crowd until I come back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":510},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O Thou that judgest all things, stay my thoughts,","My thoughts, that labour to persuade my soul","Some violent hands were laid on Humphrey's life!","If my suspect be false, forgive me, God,","For judgment only doth belong to thee.","Fain would I go to chafe his paly lips","With twenty thousand kisses, and to drain","Upon his face an ocean of salt tears","To tell my love unto his dumb deaf trunk,","And with my fingers feel his hand unfeeling;","But all in vain are these mean obsequies;","And to survey his dead and earthy image,","What were it but to make my sorrow greater?"],"id":511,"m":"O You who judge all things, restrain my thoughts, which are trying to persuade my soul that violent hands were laid on Humphrey's life! If my suspicion is false, forgive me, God, for judgement belongs only to you. I'd gladly go and warm his pale lips with twenty thousand kisses, pour an ocean of salt tears on his face, tell my love to his dumb, deaf body, and feel his unfeeling hand with my fingers. But these poor funeral rites are all useless, and what would looking at his dead, earthly form do but make my sorrow greater?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter WARWICK and others, bearing GLOSTER's body on a bed.","id":512},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Come hither, gracious sovereign, view this body."],"id":513,"m":"Come here, gracious sovereign, and look at this body."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["That is to see how deep my grave is made;","For with his soul fled all my worldly solace,","For seeing him I see my life in death."],"id":514,"m":"That's like seeing how deep my grave is dug, for all my earthly comfort fled with his soul, and seeing him I see my life in death."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["As surely as my soul intends to live","With that dread King that took our state upon him","To free us from his father's wrathful curse,","I do believe that violent hands were laid","Upon the life of this thrice-famed duke."],"id":515,"m":"As surely as my soul hopes to live with that awesome King who took on human form to free us from his father's angry curse, I believe violent hands were laid on the life of this most famous duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["A dreadful oath, sworn with a solemn tongue!","What instance gives Lord Warwick for his vow?"],"id":516,"m":"A dreadful oath, sworn solemnly! What evidence does Lord Warwick give for his oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["See how the blood is settled in his face.","Oft have I seen a timely-parted ghost,","Of ashy semblance, meagre, pale, and bloodless,","Being all descended to the labouring heart,","Who, in the conflict that it holds with death,","Attracts the same for aidance 'gainst the enemy,","Which with the heart there cools and ne'er returneth","To blush and beautify the cheek again.","But see, his face is black and full of blood,","His eyeballs further out than when he liv'd,","Staring full ghastly like a strangled man;","His hair uprear'd, his nostrils stretch'd with struggling,","His hands abroad display'd, as one that grasp'd","And tugg'd for life and was by strength subdu'd.","Look, on the sheets his hair, you see, is sticking;","His well-proportion'd beard made rough and rugged,","Like to the summer's corn by tempest lodged.","It cannot be but he was murther'd here;","The least of all these signs were probable."],"id":517,"m":"See how the blood has settled in his face. I've often seen a body whose soul left it naturally, ashen-looking, thin, pale and bloodless, because all the blood has gone down to the struggling heart, which in its fight with death draws the blood to help it against the enemy; there it cools with the heart and never returns to colour and beautify the cheek again. But look, his face is black and full of blood, his eyeballs bulge further than when he lived, staring hideously like a strangled man's; his hair stands on end, his nostrils stretched with struggling, his hands spread out like one who grabbed and fought for life and was overpowered. Look, you see his hair is stuck to the sheets, and his neat beard is rough and messy, like summer corn flattened by a storm. He must have been murdered here; the least of these signs would make it likely."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Why, Warwick, who should do the duke to death?","Myself and Beaufort had him in protection;","And we, I hope, sir, are no murtherers."],"id":518,"m":"Why, Warwick, who would have killed the duke? Beaufort and I had him in our custody, and we, I hope, sir, are not murderers."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["But both of you were vow'd Duke Humphrey's foes,","And you, forsooth, had the good duke to keep;","'T is like you would not feast him like a friend,","And 't is well seen he found an enemy."],"id":519,"m":"But you were both Duke Humphrey's sworn enemies, and you, indeed, had charge of the good duke. It's not likely you'd entertain him like a friend, and it's plain to see he met an enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Then you, belike, suspect these noblemen","As guilty of Duke Humphrey's timeless death."],"id":520,"m":"Then you suspect, apparently, that these noblemen are guilty of Duke Humphrey's untimely death."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Who finds the heifer dead and bleeding fresh","And sees fast by a butcher with an axe","But will suspect 't was he that made the slaughter?","Who finds the partridge in the puttock's nest","But may imagine how the bird was dead,","Although the kite soar with unbloodied beak?","Even so suspicious is this tragedy."],"id":521,"m":"Who finds a young cow dead and freshly bleeding, and sees a butcher with an axe close by, but suspects that he did the killing? Who finds a partridge in a hawk's nest and can't imagine how the bird died, even if the hawk flies about with a clean beak? This tragedy is just as suspicious."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Are you the butcher, Suffolk? Where's your knife?","Is Beaufort term'd a kite? Where are his talons?"],"id":522,"m":"Are you the butcher, Suffolk? Where's your knife? Is Beaufort called a hawk? Where are his claws?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I wear no knife to slaughter sleeping men;","But here's a vengeful sword, rusted with ease,","That shall be scoured in his rancorous heart","That slanders me with murther's crimson badge.—","Say, if thou dar'st, proud Lord of Warwickshire,","That I am faulty in Duke Humphrey's death."],"id":523,"m":"I don't carry a knife to slaughter sleeping men; but here's an avenging sword, rusty from lack of use, which will be cleaned in the spiteful heart of the man who slanders me with the bloody badge of murder. Say, if you dare, proud Lord of Warwickshire, that I'm guilty of Duke Humphrey's death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Cardinal, Somerset, and others.","id":524},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What dares not Warwick, if false Suffolk dare him?"],"id":525,"m":"What won't Warwick dare, if false Suffolk dares him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["He dares not calm his contumelious spirit,","Nor cease to be an arrogant controller,","Though Suffolk dare him twenty thousand times."],"id":526,"m":"He doesn't dare calm his insulting temper or stop being an arrogant critic, even if Suffolk dared him twenty thousand times."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Madam, be still,—with reverence may I say;","For every word you speak in his behalf","Is slander to your royal dignity."],"id":527,"m":"Madam, be quiet, if I may say so with respect, for every word you speak for him is a slander on your royal dignity."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Blunt-witted lord, ignoble in demeanour!","If ever lady wrong'd her lord so much,","Thy mother took into her blameful bed","Some stern untutor'd churl, and noble stock","Was graft with crab-tree slip, whose fruit thou art,","And never of the Nevils' noble race."],"id":528,"m":"Dim-witted lord, low-born in your manners! If any lady ever wronged her husband that much, your mother took some rough, ignorant peasant into her guilty bed, and a noble family tree was grafted with a crab-apple shoot, whose fruit you are, and you're no true son of the noble Nevilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["But that the guilt of murther bucklers thee","And I should rob the deathsman of his fee,","Quitting thee thereby of ten thousand shames,","And that my sovereign's presence makes me mild,","I would, false murtherous coward, on thy knee","Make thee beg pardon for thy passed speech","And say it was thy mother that thou meant'st,","That thou thyself was born in bastardy;","And after all this fearful homage done,","Give thee thy hire and send thy soul to hell,","Pernicious blood-sucker of sleeping men!"],"id":529,"m":"If it weren't that your guilt of murder protects you, and I'd be robbing the executioner of his fee and so sparing you ten thousand shames, and that my sovereign's presence keeps me calm, I would, false murdering coward, make you beg pardon on your knees for what you've said, and say it was your own mother you meant, and that you yourself were born a bastard. And after all this frightened homage, I'd pay you your wages and send your soul to hell, you deadly bloodsucker of sleeping men!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thou shalt be waking while I shed thy blood,","If from this presence thou dar'st go with me."],"id":530,"m":"You'll be awake while I shed your blood, if you dare leave this room with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Away even now, or I will drag thee hence.","Unworthy though thou art, I'll cope with thee","And do some service to Duke Humphrey's ghost."],"id":531,"m":"Let's go right now, or I'll drag you out. Unworthy though you are, I'll fight you, and do some service to Duke Humphrey's ghost."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Suffolk and Warwick.","id":532},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What stronger breastplate than a heart untainted!","Thrice is he arm'd that hath his quarrel just,","And he but naked, though lock'd up in steel,","Whose conscience with injustice is corrupted."],"id":533,"m":"What breastplate is stronger than an innocent heart? A man is armed three times over if his cause is just, and naked, though locked in steel armour, if his conscience is corrupted by injustice."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise within.","id":534},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What noise is this?"],"id":535,"m":"What's this noise?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Suffolk and Warwick, with their weapons drawn.","id":536},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, how now, lords! your wrathful weapons drawn","Here in our presence! dare you be so bold?","Why, what tumultuous clamour have we here?"],"id":537,"m":"What's this, lords! Your angry swords drawn here in our presence! Do you dare be so bold? What's this uproar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["The traitorous Warwick with the men of Bury","Set all upon me, mighty sovereign."],"id":538,"m":"Mighty sovereign, the traitor Warwick and the men of Bury all attacked me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["[To the Commons, entering] Sirs, stand apart;","the king shall know your mind.—","Dread lord, the commons send you word by me,","Unless false Suffolk straight be done to death,","Or banished fair England's territories,","They will by violence tear him from your palace","And torture him with grievous lingering death.","They say, by him the good Duke Humphrey died;","They say, in him they fear your highness' death;","And mere instinct of love and loyalty,","Free from a stubborn opposite intent,","As being thought to contradict your liking,","Makes them thus forward in his banishment.","They say, in care of your most royal person,","That if your highness should intend to sleep","And charge that no man should disturb your rest","In pain of your dislike or pain of death,","Yet, notwithstanding such a strait edict,","Were there a serpent seen, with forked tongue,","That slily glided towards your majesty,","It were but necessary you were wak'd,","Lest, being suffer'd in that harmful slumber,","The mortal worm might make the sleep eternal;","And therefore do they cry, though you forbid,","That they will guard you, whether you will or no,","From such fell serpents as false Suffolk is,","With whose envenomed and fatal sting,","Your loving uncle, twenty times his worth,","They say, is shamefully bereft of life."],"id":539,"m":"(To the Commons, entering) Men, stand back; the king will know your mind. Dread lord, the commons send you word through me that unless false Suffolk is put to death at once or banished from England, they will tear him from your palace by force and torture him with a slow, painful death. They say good Duke Humphrey died because of him; they say they fear your highness's death from him; and pure love and loyalty, not any stubborn wish to oppose you or go against your wishes, makes them so eager for his banishment. They say, out of care for your royal person, that if your highness meant to sleep and ordered that no one should disturb you on pain of your displeasure or of death, still, despite such a strict order, if a snake with forked tongue were seen sliding slyly towards your majesty, you would have to be woken, in case, left in that dangerous sleep, the deadly serpent made your sleep eternal. And so they cry that, even if you forbid it, they will guard you, whether you want it or not, from such deadly snakes as false Suffolk, by whose poisonous, fatal sting, they say, your loving uncle, worth twenty of him, has been shamefully robbed of his life."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMMONS","lines":["[Within] An answer from the king, my Lord of Salisbury!"],"id":540,"m":"(Within) An answer from the king, my Lord of Salisbury!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["'T is like the commons, rude unpolish'd hinds,","Could send such message to their sovereign;","But you, my lord, were glad to be employ'd,","To show how quaint an orator you are.","But all the honour Salisbury hath won","Is that he was the lord ambassador","Sent from a sort of tinkers to the king."],"id":541,"m":"It's likely that the commons, rough, uneducated peasants, could send such a message to their king! But you, my lord, were glad to be given the job, to show what an elegant speaker you are. But all the honour Salisbury has won is to be the lord ambassador sent to the king from a bunch of tinkers."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMMONS","lines":["[Within] An answer from the king, or we will all break in!"],"id":542,"m":"(Within) An answer from the king, or we'll all break in!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Go, Salisbury, and tell them all from me,","I thank them for their tender loving care,","And had I not been cited so by them,","Yet did I purpose as they do entreat,","For, sure, my thoughts do hourly prophesy","Mischance unto my state by Suffolk's means;","And therefore, by His majesty I swear,","Whose far unworthy deputy I am,","He shall not breathe infection in this air","But three days longer, on the pain of death."],"id":543,"m":"Go, Salisbury, and tell them all from me that I thank them for their tender, loving care; and even if they hadn't urged me, I meant to do what they ask, for my thoughts warn me every hour of harm to my state through Suffolk. And so I swear by His majesty, whose very unworthy deputy I am, that he won't breathe his infection in this air for more than three more days, on pain of death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Salisbury.","id":544},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["O Henry, let me plead for gentle Suffolk!"],"id":545,"m":"O Henry, let me plead for gentle Suffolk!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ungentle queen, to call him gentle Suffolk!","No more, I say; if thou dost plead for him,","Thou wilt but add increase unto my wrath.","Had I but said, I would have kept my word,","But when I swear, it is irrevocable.—","If, after three days' space, thou here be'st found","On any ground that I am ruler of,","The world shall not be ransom for thy life.—","Come, Warwick, come, good Warwick, go with me;","I have great matters to impart to thee."],"id":546,"m":"Ungentle queen, to call him gentle Suffolk! No more, I say. If you plead for him, you'll only add to my anger. If I had only said it, I would have kept my word; but when I swear, it can't be undone. If after three days you are found here on any land I rule, the whole world won't be enough ransom for your life. Come, Warwick, come, good Warwick, come with me; I have important things to tell you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Queen and Suffolk.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Mischance and sorrow go along with you!","Heart's discontent and sour affliction","Be playfellows to keep you company!","There's two of you; the devil make a third!","And threefold vengeance tend upon your steps!"],"id":548,"m":"Bad luck and sorrow go with you! May heartache and bitter suffering be your playmates and keep you company! There are two of you; may the devil make a third, and triple vengeance follow your steps!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Cease, gentle queen, these execrations,","And let thy Suffolk take his heavy leave."],"id":549,"m":"Stop these curses, gentle queen, and let your Suffolk take his sad leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Fie, coward woman and soft-hearted wretch,","Has thou not spirit to curse thine enemy?"],"id":550,"m":"Shame, you cowardly woman, soft-hearted wretch! Haven't you the spirit to curse your enemy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["A plague upon them! wherefore should I curse them?","Would curses kill, as doth the mandrake's groan,","I would invent as bitter-searching terms,","As curst, as harsh and horrible to hear,","Deliver'd strongly through my fixed teeth,","With full as many signs of deadly hate,","As lean-fac'd Envy in her loathsome cave.","My tongue should stumble in mine earnest words;","Mine eyes should sparkle like the beaten flint;","Mine hair be fix'd an end, as one distract;","Ay, every joint should seem to curse and ban;","And even now my burthen'd heart would break,","Should I not curse them. Poison be their drink!","Gall, worse than gall, the daintiest that they taste!","Their sweetest shade a grove of cypress-trees!","Their chiefest prospect murthering basilisks!","Their softest touch as smart as lizards' stings!","Their music frightful as the serpent's hiss,","And boding screech-owls make the consort full!","All the foul terrors in dark-seated hell—"],"id":551,"m":"A plague on them! Why should I curse them? If curses could kill, like the mandrake's groan, I'd invent words as bitter and piercing, as savage, harsh and horrible to hear, spat through my clenched teeth with as many signs of deadly hate, as lean-faced Envy in her disgusting cave. My tongue would trip over my passionate words, my eyes would spark like struck flint, my hair would stand on end like a madman's; yes, every joint would seem to curse; and even now my heavy heart would break if I didn't curse them. May poison be their drink! Bitterness, worse than bitterness, the finest food they taste! Their sweetest shade a grove of funeral cypress trees! Their best view, killing basilisks! Their softest touch as painful as lizards' stings! Their music as frightening as a snake's hiss, with ominous screech owls to complete the band! All the foul terrors of deep, dark hell—"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Enough, sweet Suffolk; thou torment'st thyself;","And these dread curses, like the sun 'gainst glass,","Or like an overcharged gun, recoil","And turns the force of them upon thyself."],"id":552,"m":"Enough, sweet Suffolk; you're torturing yourself. These dreadful curses, like the sun reflected off glass or an overloaded gun, recoil and turn their force on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["You bade me ban, and will you bid me leave?","Now, by the ground that I am banish'd from,","Well could I curse away a winter's night,","Though standing naked on a mountain top","Where biting cold would never let grass grow,","And think it but a minute spent in sport."],"id":553,"m":"You told me to curse, and now you tell me to stop? By the ground I'm banished from, I could curse through a whole winter's night standing naked on a mountaintop where the biting cold would never let grass grow, and think it only a minute spent in fun."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["O, let me entreat thee cease. Give me thy hand,","That I may dew it with my mournful tears;","Nor let the rain of heaven wet this place,","To wash away my woeful monuments.","O, could this kiss be printed in thy hand,","That thou mightest think upon these by the seal,","Through whom a thousand sighs are breath'd for thee!","So, get thee gone, that I may know my grief;","'T is but surmis'd whiles thou art standing by,","As one that surfeits thinking on a want.","I will repeal thee, or, be well assur'd,","Adventure to be banished myself;","And banished I am, if but from thee.","Go; speak not to me, even now be gone.—","O, go not yet!—Even thus two friends condemn'd","Embrace and kiss and take ten thousand leaves,","Loather a hundred times to part than die.","Yet now farewell; and farewell life with thee!"],"id":554,"m":"Oh, please stop. Give me your hand, so I can wet it with my sad tears; and don't let heaven's rain wet this place and wash away the marks of my grief. Oh, if only this kiss could be printed on your hand, so you might remember, by this seal, the lips through which a thousand sighs are breathed for you! So go, so that I can fully feel my grief; while you're standing here I only imagine it, like someone who's full thinking about hunger. I'll have you called back, or, be sure of it, risk being banished myself; and I'm banished already, if only from you. Go; don't speak to me; go right now. Oh, don't go yet! This is how two condemned friends embrace and kiss and say goodbye ten thousand times, a hundred times less willing to part than to die. But now farewell, and farewell life with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thus is poor Suffolk ten times banished;","Once by the king, and three times thrice by thee.","'T is not the land I care for, wert thou thence;","A wilderness is populous enough,","So Suffolk had thy heavenly company;","For where thou art, there is the world itself,","With every several pleasure in the world,","And where thou art not, desolation.","I can no more; live thou to joy thy life,","Myself no joy in nought but that thou liv'st."],"id":555,"m":"So poor Suffolk is banished ten times: once by the king and nine times by you. It's not the country I care about, if you weren't in it. A wilderness would be crowded enough if Suffolk had your heavenly company, for where you are is the whole world, with every pleasure in it, and where you are not is desolation. I can say no more. Live and enjoy your life; I'll have no joy in anything except that you are alive."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VAUX.","id":556},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Whither goes Vaux so fast? what news, I prithee?"],"id":557,"m":"Where is Vaux going so fast? What news, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VAUX","lines":["To signify unto his majesty","That Cardinal Beaufort is at point of death;","For suddenly a grievous sickness took him,","That makes him gasp and stare and catch the air,","Blaspheming God and cursing men on earth.","Sometime he talks as if Duke Humphrey's ghost","Were by his side, sometime he calls the king","And whispers to his pillow as to him","The secrets of his overcharged soul;","And I am sent to tell his majesty","That even now he cries aloud for him."],"id":558,"m":"To tell his majesty that Cardinal Beaufort is about to die. A terrible sickness suddenly took him, making him gasp and stare and gulp at the air, blaspheming God and cursing men on earth. Sometimes he talks as if Duke Humphrey's ghost were beside him; sometimes he calls for the king and whispers to his pillow as if to him the secrets of his overburdened soul. I'm sent to tell his majesty that he's crying out for him right now."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Go tell this heavy message to the king.—"],"id":559,"m":"Go and give this sad message to the king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Vaux.","id":560},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Ay me! what is this world! what news are these!","But wherefore grieve I at an hour's poor loss,","Omitting Suffolk's exile, my soul's treasure?","Why only, Suffolk, mourn I not for thee,","And with the southern clouds contend in tears,","Theirs for the earth's increase, mine for my sorrows?","Now get thee hence.","The king, thou know'st, is coming;","If thou be found by me; thou art but dead."],"id":561,"m":"Oh, what a world this is! What news this is! But why do I grieve over such a small loss as an hour's, and forget Suffolk's exile, my soul's treasure? Why don't I mourn only for you, Suffolk, and compete with the southern clouds in tears, theirs to make the earth grow, mine for my sorrows? Now go. The king, you know, is coming. If you're found with me, you're dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["If I depart from thee, I cannot live;","And in thy sight to die, what were it else","But like a pleasant slumber in thy lap?","Here could I breathe my soul into the air,","As mild and gentle as the cradle-babe","Dying with mother's dug between its lips;","Where, from thy sight, I should be raging mad","And cry out for thee to close up mine eyes,","To have thee with thy lips to stop my mouth.","So shouldst thou either turn my flying soul,","Or I should breathe it so into thy body,","And then it liv'd in sweet Elysium.","To die by thee were but to die in jest;","From thee to die were torture more than death.","O, let me stay, befall what may befall!"],"id":562,"m":"If I leave you, I can't live; and to die in your sight, what would that be but like a pleasant sleep in your lap? Here I could breathe my soul into the air as gently as a baby in its cradle dying with its mother's breast between its lips; whereas out of your sight I'd go raving mad and cry for you to close my eyes, to have your lips close my mouth. So you'd either turn my soul back as it flew away, or I'd breathe it into your body, and then it would live in sweet paradise. To die beside you would only be to die in play; to die away from you would be torture worse than death. Oh, let me stay, whatever happens!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Away! though parting be a fretful corrosive,","It is applied to a deathful wound.","To France, sweet Suffolk; let me hear from thee,","For whereso'er thou art in this world's globe","I'll have an Iris that shall find thee out."],"id":563,"m":"Go! Though parting is a painful remedy, it's applied to a deadly wound. To France, sweet Suffolk; let me hear from you. Wherever you are on this earth, I'll have a messenger who will find you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I go."],"id":564,"m":"I'm going."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And take my heart with thee."],"id":565,"m":"And take my heart with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["A jewel, lock'd into the wofull'st cask","That ever did contain a thing of worth.","Even as a splitted bark, so sunder we;","This way fall I to death."],"id":566,"m":"A jewel locked in the saddest box that ever held anything precious. Like a ship split in two, we part; I fall this way to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["This way for me."],"id":567,"m":"And I this way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally.","id":568},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"A Bedchamber.","id":569},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the KING, SALISBURY, and WARWICK, to the CARDINAL in bed.","id":570},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How fares my lord? speak, Beaufort, to thy sovereign."],"id":571,"m":"How are you, my lord? Speak to your sovereign, Beaufort."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["If thou be'st Death, I'll give thee England's treasure,","Enough to purchase such another island,","So thou wilt let me live and feel no pain."],"id":572,"m":"If you're Death, I'll give you England's treasure, enough to buy another island like it, if you'll let me live and feel no pain."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ah, what a sign it is of evil life","Where death's approach is seen so terrible!"],"id":573,"m":"Ah, what a sign of an evil life it is when death's approach looks so terrible!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Beaufort, it is thy sovereign speaks to thee."],"id":574,"m":"Beaufort, it's your sovereign speaking to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Bring me unto my trial when you will.","Died he not in his bed? where should he die?","Can I make men live, whether they will or no?","O, torture me no more! I will confess.—","Alive again? then show me where he is;","I'll give a thousand pound to look upon him.","He hath no eyes, the dust hath blinded them.","Comb down his hair; look, look! it stands upright,","Like lime-twigs set to catch my winged soul.—","Give me some drink; and bid the apothecary","Bring the strong poison that I bought of him."],"id":575,"m":"Bring me to trial when you like. Didn't he die in his bed? Where else should he die? Can I make men live whether they want to or not? Oh, don't torture me any more! I'll confess. Alive again? Then show me where he is; I'll give a thousand pounds to see him. He has no eyes; the dust has blinded them. Comb down his hair; look, look, it's standing on end like sticky twigs set to catch my winged soul! Give me a drink, and tell the chemist to bring the strong poison I bought from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O Thou eternal Mover of the Heavens,","Look with a gentle eye upon this wretch!","O, beat away the busy meddling fiend","That lays strong siege unto this wretch's soul,","And from his bosom purge this black despair!"],"id":576,"m":"O eternal Mover of the heavens, look gently on this wretch! Oh, beat away the busy, meddling devil that is besieging this wretch's soul, and purge this black despair from his heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["See how the pangs of death do make him grin!"],"id":577,"m":"See how the pains of death make him bare his teeth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Disturb him not; let him pass peaceably."],"id":578,"m":"Don't disturb him; let him die in peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Peace to his soul, if God's good pleasure be!—","Lord cardinal, if thou think'st on heaven's bliss,","Hold up thy hand, make signal of thy hope.—","He dies, and makes no sign.—O God, forgive him!"],"id":579,"m":"Peace to his soul, if it is God's will! Lord Cardinal, if you are thinking of heaven's bliss, raise your hand and give a sign of your hope. He dies and makes no sign. O God, forgive him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["So bad a death argues a monstrous life."],"id":580,"m":"Such a bad death shows a monstrous life."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Forbear to judge, for we are sinners all.—","Close up his eyes and draw the curtain close;","And let us all to meditation."],"id":581,"m":"Don't judge, for we are all sinners. Close his eyes and draw the bed curtains, and let us all go to pray."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":582},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The Coast of Kent.","id":583},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Fight at sea. Ordnance goes off. Enter a Captain, a Master, a Master's Mate, WALTER WHITMORE, and others; with them SUFFOLK, and others, prisoners.","id":584},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["The gaudy, blabbing, and remorseful day","Is crept into the bosom of the sea;","And now loud-howling wolves arouse the jades","That drag the tragic melancholy night,","Who, with their drowsy, slow, and flagging wings,","Clip dead men's graves and from their misty jaws","Breathe foul contagious darkness in the air.","Therefore bring forth the soldiers of our prize;","For, whilst our pinnace anchors in the Downs,","Here shall they make their ransom on the sand","Or with their blood stain this discolour'd shore.—","Master, this prisoner freely give I thee;—","And thou that art his mate, make boot of this;—","The other, Walter Whitmore, is thy share."],"id":585,"m":"The bright, gossiping, pitying day has crept into the sea's embrace, and now loud-howling wolves wake the tired horses that pull the tragic, gloomy night, who with drowsy, slow, drooping wings hugs dead men's graves and breathes foul, infectious darkness from her misty jaws into the air. So bring out the soldiers we've captured, for while our small ship is anchored in the Downs, they'll pay their ransom here on the sand or stain this muddy shore with their blood. Master, I give you this prisoner freely; and you, his mate, take profit from this one; the other one, Walter Whitmore, is your share."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["What is my ransom, master? let me know?"],"id":586,"m":"What's my ransom, master? Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MASTER","lines":["A thousand crowns, or else lay down your head."],"id":587,"m":"A thousand crowns, or else lay down your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"MATE","lines":["And so much shall you give, or off goes yours."],"id":588,"m":"And you'll give the same, or off goes yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["What, think you much to pay two thousand crowns,","And bear the name and port of gentlemen?—","Cut both the villains' throats;—for die you shall.","The lives of those which we have lost in fight","Be counterpois'd with such a petty sum!"],"id":589,"m":"What, do you think two thousand crowns too much, when you have the name and bearing of gentlemen? Cut both the villains' throats; you shall die. Are the lives of the men we lost in the fight to be weighed against such a small sum!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I'll give it, sir; and therefore spare my life."],"id":590,"m":"I'll pay it, sir, so spare my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["And so will I, and write home for it straight."],"id":591,"m":"And so will I, and I'll write home for it at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["I lost mine eye in laying the prize aboard,—","[To Suffolk] And therefore, to revenge it, shalt thou die;—","And so should these, if I might have my will."],"id":592,"m":"I lost my eye boarding the captured ship, (to Suffolk) and so, to avenge it, you will die; and so would these, if I had my way."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Be not so rash; take ransom, let him live."],"id":593,"m":"Don't be so hasty. Take a ransom and let him live."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Look on my George; I am a gentleman.","Rate me at what thou wilt, thou shalt be paid."],"id":594,"m":"Look at my George, the badge of the Garter; I'm a gentleman. Name your price, and you'll be paid."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["And so am I; my name is Walter Whitmore.","How now! why start'st thou? What, doth death affright?"],"id":595,"m":"And so am I; my name is Walter Whitmore. What's this! Why do you jump? What, does death frighten you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thy name affrights me, in whose sound is death.","A cunning man did calculate my birth","And told me that by water I should die.","Yet let not this make thee be bloody-minded;","Thy name is Gaultier, being rightly sounded."],"id":596,"m":"Your name frightens me; there's death in its sound. A fortune-teller cast my horoscope and told me I would die by water. But don't let this make you bloodthirsty; your name, pronounced correctly, is Gaultier."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["Gaultier or Walter, which it is, I care not.","Never yet did base dishonour blur our name","But with our sword we wip'd away the blot;","Therefore, when merchant-like I sell revenge,","Broke be my sword, my arms torn and defac'd,","And I proclaim'd a coward through the world!"],"id":597,"m":"Gaultier or Walter, I don't care which. No low disgrace ever stained our name without our swords wiping the stain away. So if I ever sell revenge like a merchant, may my sword be broken, my coat of arms torn and defaced, and I proclaimed a coward across the world!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Stay, Whitmore; for thy prisoner is a prince,","The Duke of Suffolk, William de la Pole."],"id":598,"m":"Wait, Whitmore, for your prisoner is a prince: the Duke of Suffolk, William de la Pole."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["The Duke of Suffolk muffled up in rags!"],"id":599,"m":"The Duke of Suffolk wrapped up in rags!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Ay, but these rags are no part of the duke;","Jove sometime went disguis'd, and why not I?"],"id":600,"m":"Yes, but these rags are no part of the duke. Jove sometimes went in disguise, so why not I?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["But Jove was never slain, as thou shalt be."],"id":601,"m":"But Jove was never killed, as you will be."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Obscure and lowly swain, King Henry's blood,","The honourable blood of Lancaster,","Must not be shed by such a jaded groom.","Hast thou not kiss'd thy hand and held my stirrup?","Bare-headed plodded by my foot-cloth mule","And thought thee happy when I shook my head?","How often hast thou waited at my cup,","Fed from my trencher, kneel'd down at the board,","When I have feasted with Queen Margaret?","Remember it and let it make thee crest-fallen,","Ay, and allay thus thy abortive pride,","How in our voiding lobby hast thou stood","And duly waited for my coming forth.","This hand of mine hath writ in thy behalf,","And therefore shall it charm thy riotous tongue."],"id":602,"m":"Low, obscure peasant, King Henry's blood, the honourable blood of Lancaster, must not be spilled by such a worn-out servant. Haven't you kissed your hand to me and held my stirrup? Walked bare-headed beside my richly covered mule and thought yourself lucky when I nodded to you? How often have you served my wine, eaten from my plate, knelt at the table when I feasted with Queen Margaret? Remember it, and let it humble you, yes, and so check your premature pride: how you stood in my hall waiting dutifully for me to come out. This hand of mine has written letters on your behalf, and so it will charm your unruly tongue into silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["Speak, captain, shall I stab the forlorn swain?"],"id":603,"m":"Tell me, captain, shall I stab this wretched peasant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["First let my words stab him, as he hath me."],"id":604,"m":"First let my words stab him, as his have stabbed me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Base slave, thy words are blunt and so art thou."],"id":605,"m":"Low slave, your words are blunt, and so are you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Convey him hence, and on our long-boat's side","Strike off his head."],"id":606,"m":"Take him away, and cut off his head on the side of our longboat."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thou dar'st not, for thy own."],"id":607,"m":"You don't dare, for fear of losing your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Yes, Pole!"],"id":608,"m":"Yes, Pole!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Pole!"],"id":609,"m":"Pole!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Pool! Sir Pool! lord!","Ay, kennel, puddle, sink, whose filth and dirt","Troubles the silver spring where England drinks.","Now will I dam up this thy yawning mouth","For swallowing the treasure of the realm;","Thy lips that kiss'd the queen shall sweep the ground;","And thou that smil'dst at good Duke Humphrey's death","Against the senseless winds shalt grin in vain,","Who in contempt shall hiss at thee again.","And wedded be thou to the hags of hell,","For daring to affy a mighty lord","Unto the daughter of a worthless king,","Having neither subject, wealth, nor diadem.","By devilish policy art thou grown great","And, like ambitious Sylla, overgorg'd","With gobbets of thy mother's bleeding heart.","By thee Anjou and Maine were sold to France,","The false revolting Normans thorough thee","Disdain to call us lord, and Picardy","Hath slain their governors, surpris'd our forts,","And sent the ragged soldiers wounded home.","The princely Warwick, and the Nevils all,","Whose dreadful swords were never drawn in vain,","As hating thee are rising up in arms;","And now the house of York, thrust from the crown","By shameful murther of a guiltless king","And lofty proud encroaching tyranny,","Burns with revenging fire, whose hopeful colours","Advance our half-fac'd sun, striving to shine,","Under the which is writ 'Invitis nubibus.'","The commons here in Kent are up in arms;","And, to conclude, reproach and beggary","Is crept into the palace of our king,","And all by thee.—Away! convey him hence."],"id":610,"m":"Pool! Sir Pool! Lord! Yes, gutter, puddle, sewer, whose filth and dirt muddies the clear spring England drinks from. Now I'll stop up your gaping mouth for swallowing the kingdom's treasure. Your lips that kissed the queen will sweep the ground, and you who smiled at good Duke Humphrey's death will grin in vain at the unfeeling winds, which will hiss back at you in contempt. And may you be married to the hags of hell for daring to betroth a mighty lord to the daughter of a worthless king who has no subjects, money or crown. You've grown great by devilish cunning, and like ambitious Sulla gorged yourself on lumps of your mother country's bleeding heart. Because of you Anjou and Maine were sold to France; because of you the false, rebellious Normans refuse to call us lord, and Picardy has killed its governors, captured our forts, and sent our ragged soldiers home wounded. Princely Warwick and all the Nevilles, whose dreaded swords were never drawn for nothing, are rising in arms because they hate you. And now the house of York, pushed from the crown by the shameful murder of an innocent king and by proud, grasping tyranny, burns with avenging fire; its hopeful banners carry our half-hidden sun, struggling to shine, with the motto \"In spite of the clouds\". The commons here in Kent are up in arms, and, to finish, disgrace and poverty have crept into our king's palace, and all because of you. Away! Take him away."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["O that I were a god, to shoot forth thunder","Upon these paltry, servile, abject drudges!","Small things make base men proud; this villain here,","Being captain of a pinnace, threatens more","Than Bargulus the strong Illyrian pirate.—","Drones suck not eagles' blood but rob bee-hives.","It is impossible that I should die","By such a lowly vassal as thyself.","Thy words move rage and not remorse in me.","I go of message from the queen to France;","I charge thee waft me safely cross the Channel."],"id":611,"m":"Oh, if only I were a god, to fire thunderbolts at these worthless, servile, low drudges! Small things make low men proud. This villain here, just because he's captain of a little boat, threatens more than Bargulus, the mighty Illyrian pirate. Drones don't suck eagles' blood; they rob beehives. It's impossible that I should die at the hands of such a low servant as you. Your words make me angry, not sorry. I'm going to France with a message from the queen; I order you to carry me safely across the Channel."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Walter,—"],"id":612,"m":"Walter—"},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["Come, Suffolk, I must waft thee to thy death."],"id":613,"m":"Come, Suffolk, I must carry you to your death."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Gelidus timor occupat artus; it is thee I fear."],"id":614,"m":"\"Cold fear seizes my limbs\"; it's you I fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["Thou shalt have cause to fear before I leave thee.","What, are ye daunted now? now will ye stoop?"],"id":615,"m":"You'll have reason to fear before I'm done with you. What, are you frightened now? Now will you bow down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["My gracious lord, entreat him, speak him fair."],"id":616,"m":"My gracious lord, beg him, speak kindly to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Suffolk's imperial tongue is stern and rough,","Us'd to command, untaught to plead for favour.","Far be it we should honour such as these","With humble suit; no, rather let my head","Stoop to the block than these knees bow to any","Save to the God of heaven and to my king,","And sooner dance upon a bloody pole","Than stand uncover'd to the vulgar groom.","True nobility is exempt from fear;","More can I bear than you dare execute."],"id":617,"m":"Suffolk's royal tongue is stern and rough, used to giving orders, not taught to beg for favours. Far be it from us to honour such men with humble pleas. No, I'd rather let my head bow to the block than let these knees bend to anyone but the God of heaven and my king, and sooner dance on a bloody pole than stand bare-headed before a common servant. True nobility knows no fear. I can bear more than you dare do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Hale him away, and let him talk no more."],"id":618,"m":"Drag him away, and don't let him talk any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Come, soldiers, show what cruelty ye can,","That this my death may never be forgot!","Great men oft die by vile bezonians:","A Roman sworder and banditto slave","Murther'd sweet Tully; Brutus' bastard hand","Stabb'd Julius Caesar; savage islanders","Pompey the Great; and Suffolk dies by pirates."],"id":619,"m":"Come, soldiers, show what cruelty you can, so that my death may never be forgotten! Great men often die at the hands of low ruffians. A Roman swordsman and outlaw slave murdered sweet Cicero; Brutus's bastard hand stabbed Julius Caesar; savage islanders killed Pompey the Great; and Suffolk dies by pirates."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Whitmore and others with Suffolk.","id":620},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["And as for these whose ransom we have set,","It is our pleasure one of them depart,","Therefore come you with us, and let him go."],"id":621,"m":"And as for these whose ransom we've set, we've decided to let one of them go. So you come with us, and let him go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but the 1 Gentleman.","id":622},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter WHITMORE with SUFFOLK'S body.","id":623},{"t":"sp","who":"WHITMORE","lines":["There let his head and lifeless body lie","Until the queen his mistress bury it."],"id":624,"m":"Let his head and lifeless body lie there until the queen, his mistress, buries it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":625},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["O barbarous and bloody spectacle!","His body will I bear unto the king.","If he revenge it not, yet will his friends;","So will the queen, that living held him dear."],"id":626,"m":"Oh, barbaric and bloody sight! I'll carry his body to the king. If he doesn't avenge it, his friends will; and so will the queen, who loved him dearly while he lived."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the body.","id":627},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Blackheath.","id":628},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GEORGE BEVIS and JOHN HOLLAND.","id":629},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["Come, and get thee a sword, though made of","a lath; they have been up these two days."],"id":630,"m":"Come on, get yourself a sword, even if it's made of wood; they've been up these two days."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["They have the more need to sleep now, then."],"id":631,"m":"Then they need sleep all the more now."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["I tell thee, Jack Cade the clothier means to dress the","commonwealth, and turn it, and set a new nap upon it."],"id":632,"m":"I tell you, Jack Cade the cloth worker means to dress the country like cloth, turn it inside out and give it a new nap."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["So he had need, for 't is threadbare. Well, I say","it was never merry world in England since gentlemen came up."],"id":633,"m":"He needs to, for it's threadbare. Well, I say England has never been a happy place since gentlemen came along."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["O miserable age! virtue is not regarded in","handicraftsmen."],"id":634,"m":"Oh, miserable times! Nobody respects the virtue of craftsmen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["The nobility think scorn to go in leather aprons."],"id":635,"m":"The nobles turn their noses up at wearing leather aprons."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["Nay, more, the king's council are no good workmen."],"id":636,"m":"What's more, the king's council are no good as workers."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["True; and yet it is said, labour in thy vocation,","which is as much to say as, let the magistrates be labouring","men; and therefore should we be magistrates."],"id":637,"m":"True; and yet it's said, \"Work hard at your calling\", which is as much as to say, let the rulers be working men; and so we should be the rulers."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["Thou hast hit it; for there's no better sign of a brave","mind than a hard hand."],"id":638,"m":"You've got it, for there's no better sign of a brave mind than a hard hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["I see them! I see them! There's Best's son, the","tanner of Wingham,—"],"id":639,"m":"I see them! I see them! There's Best's son, the tanner from Wingham—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["He shall have the skin of our enemies, to make dog's-","leather of."],"id":640,"m":"He'll take our enemies' skins to make dog-leather from."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["And Dick the butcher,—"],"id":641,"m":"And Dick the butcher—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["Then is sin struck down like an ox, and iniquity's","throat cut like a calf."],"id":642,"m":"Then sin will be struck down like an ox, and wickedness have its throat cut like a calf."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["And Smith the weaver,—"],"id":643,"m":"And Smith the weaver—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["Argo, their thread of life is spun."],"id":644,"m":"Ergo, their thread of life is spun."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["Come, come, let's fall in with them."],"id":645,"m":"Come on, come on, let's join them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum. Enter CADE, DICK the Butcher, SMITH the Weaver, and a Sawyer, with infinite numbers.","id":646},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["We John Cade, so term'd of our supposed father,—"],"id":647,"m":"We, John Cade, so called after our supposed father—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] Or rather, of stealing a cade of herrings."],"id":648,"m":"(Aside) Or rather, from stealing a barrel of herrings."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["For our enemies shall fall before us, inspired with the","spirit of putting down kings and princes,—Command silence."],"id":649,"m":"For our enemies shall fall before us, inspired with the spirit of putting down kings and princes— Call for silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Silence!"],"id":650,"m":"Silence!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["My father was a Mortimer,—"],"id":651,"m":"My father was a Mortimer—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] He was an honest man and a good bricklayer."],"id":652,"m":"(Aside) He was an honest man and a good bricklayer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["My mother a Plantagenet,—"],"id":653,"m":"My mother a Plantagenet—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] I knew her well; she was a midwife."],"id":654,"m":"(Aside) I knew her well; she was a midwife."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["My wife descended of the Lacies,—"],"id":655,"m":"My wife descended from the Lacy family—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] She was, indeed, a pedler's daughter, and sold","many laces."],"id":656,"m":"(Aside) She was indeed a pedlar's daughter, and sold plenty of laces."},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["[Aside] But now of late, not able to travel with her","furred pack, she washes bucks here at home."],"id":657,"m":"(Aside) But lately, unable to travel with her fur-covered pack, she does washing here at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Therefore am I of an honourable house."],"id":658,"m":"So I come from an honourable house."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] Ay, by my faith, the field is honourable; and","there was he born, under a hedge, for his father had never a","house but","the cage."],"id":659,"m":"(Aside) Yes, by my faith, the field is honourable, and that's where he was born, under a hedge, for his father never had any house but the lock-up."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Valiant I am."],"id":660,"m":"I am brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["[Aside] A' must needs; for beggary is valiant."],"id":661,"m":"(Aside) He must be, for beggars are tough."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["I am able to endure much."],"id":662,"m":"I can endure a lot."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] No question of that; for I have seen him whipped","three market-days together."],"id":663,"m":"(Aside) No doubt about that, for I've seen him whipped three market days in a row."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["I fear neither sword nor fire."],"id":664,"m":"I fear neither sword nor fire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["[Aside] He need not fear the sword, for his coat is of","proof."],"id":665,"m":"(Aside) He needn't fear the sword, for his coat is sword-proof, it's so tough."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["[Aside] But methinks he should stand in fear of fire,","being burnt i' the hand for stealing of sheep."],"id":666,"m":"(Aside) But I think he should be afraid of fire, having been branded on the hand for stealing sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Be brave, then; for your captain is brave, and vows","reformation. There shall be in England seven halfpenny loaves","sold for a penny; the three-hooped pot shall have ten hoops; and","I will make it felony to drink small beer. All the realm shall be","in common; and in Cheapside shall my palfry go to grass; and","when I am king, as king I will be,—"],"id":667,"m":"Be brave, then, for your captain is brave and promises reform. In England seven halfpenny loaves will be sold for a penny; the three-hooped beer pot will have ten hoops; and I'll make it a crime to drink weak beer. The whole kingdom will be held in common, and my horse will graze in Cheapside. And when I'm king, as king I will be—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["God save your majesty!"],"id":668,"m":"God save your majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["I thank you, good people;—there shall be no money; all shall","eat and drink on my score, and I will apparel them all in one","livery, that they may agree like brothers and worship me their","lord."],"id":669,"m":"Thank you, good people. There will be no money; everyone will eat and drink at my expense, and I'll dress them all in the same uniform, so that they get on like brothers and worship me as their lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["The first thing we do, let's kill all the lawyers."],"id":670,"m":"The first thing we do, let's kill all the lawyers."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Nay, that I mean to do. Is not this a lamentable thing, that","of the skin of an innocent lamb should be made parchment, that","parchment, being scribbl'd o'er, should undo a man? Some say the","bee stings; but I say 't is the bee's wax, for I did but seal","once to a thing, and I was never mine own man since.—How now!","who's there?"],"id":671,"m":"Indeed, I mean to. Isn't it a sad thing that the skin of an innocent lamb should be made into parchment, and that parchment, once scribbled over, should ruin a man? Some say the bee stings; but I say it's the bee's wax, for I put my seal to a document once, and I've never been my own man since. What's this! Who's there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter some, bringing in the Clerk of Chatham.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["The clerk of Chatham; he can write and read and cast","accompt."],"id":673,"m":"The clerk of Chatham. He can write and read and do accounts."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["O monstrous!"],"id":674,"m":"Oh, monstrous!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["We took him setting of boys' copies."],"id":675,"m":"We caught him setting copying exercises for boys."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Here's a villain!"],"id":676,"m":"Here's a villain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["Has a book in his pocket with red letters in 't."],"id":677,"m":"He has a book in his pocket with red letters in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Nay, then, he is a conjurer."],"id":678,"m":"Then he's a conjurer."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Nay, he can make obligations and write court-hand."],"id":679,"m":"No, he can draw up contracts and write legal script."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["I am sorry for 't.","The man is a proper man, of mine honour;","unless I find him guilty, he shall not die.—Come hither, sirrah,","I must examine thee; what is thy name?"],"id":680,"m":"I'm sorry for it. The man is a fine fellow, on my honour; unless I find him guilty, he won't die. Come here, you; I must examine you. What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLERK","lines":["Emmanuel."],"id":681,"m":"Emmanuel."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["They use to write it on the top of letters.—'T will go","hard with you."],"id":682,"m":"They usually write that at the top of letters. Things will go badly for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Let me alone.—Dost thou use to write thy name? or hast","thou a mark to thyself, like a honest, plain-dealing man?"],"id":683,"m":"Leave this to me. Do you usually write your name, or do you have your own mark, like an honest, plain-dealing man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLERK","lines":["Sir, I thank God, I have been so well brought up that I","can write my name."],"id":684,"m":"Sir, thank God, I've been well enough brought up that I can write my name."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["He hath confess'd; away with him! he's a villain and a","traitor."],"id":685,"m":"He's confessed! Away with him! He's a villain and a traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Away with him, I say! hang him with his pen and inkhorn","about his neck."],"id":686,"m":"Away with him, I say! Hang him with his pen and inkpot round his neck."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit one with the Clerk.","id":687},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MICHAEL.","id":688},{"t":"sp","who":"MICHAEL","lines":["Where's our general?"],"id":689,"m":"Where's our general?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Here I am, thou particular fellow."],"id":690,"m":"Here I am, you particular fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"MICHAEL","lines":["Fly, fly, fly! Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother","are hard by, with the king's forces."],"id":691,"m":"Run, run, run! Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother are close by with the king's forces."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Stand, villain, stand, or I'll fell thee down. He shall be","encountered with a man as good as himself; he is but a knight,","is a'?"],"id":692,"m":"Stand still, villain, stand, or I'll knock you down. He'll be met by a man as good as himself. He's only a knight, isn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MICHAEL","lines":["No."],"id":693,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["To equal him, I will make myself a knight presently.—","[Kneels] Rise up Sir John Mortimer.—[Rises] Now have at him!"],"id":694,"m":"To match him, I'll make myself a knight at once. (Kneels) Arise, Sir John Mortimer. (Rises) Now let's at him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SIR HUMPHREY STAFFORD and his Brother, with drum and soldiers.","id":695},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["Rebellious hinds, the filth and scum of Kent,","Mark'd for the gallows, lay your weapons down;","Home to your cottages, forsake this groom.","The king is merciful, if you revolt."],"id":696,"m":"Rebellious peasants, the filth and scum of Kent, marked out for the gallows, lay down your weapons. Go home to your cottages and abandon this servant. The king is merciful if you give up your revolt."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","lines":["But angry, wrathful, and inclin'd to blood,","If you go forward; therefore yield, or die."],"id":697,"m":"But angry, furious and ready to shed blood if you carry on. So surrender, or die."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["As for these silken-coated slaves, I pass not;","It is to you, good people, that I speak,","Over whom, in time to come, I hope to reign,","For I am rightful heir unto the crown."],"id":698,"m":"As for these silk-coated slaves, I don't care about them. It's to you, good people, that I'm speaking, over whom I hope to reign in time to come, for I am the rightful heir to the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["Villain, thy father was a plasterer;","And thou thyself a shearman, art thou not?"],"id":699,"m":"Villain, your father was a plasterer, and you yourself are a cloth-shearer, aren't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["And Adam was a gardener."],"id":700,"m":"And Adam was a gardener."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","lines":["And what of that?"],"id":701,"m":"And what of that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Marry, this: Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March,","Married the Duke of Clarence' daughter, did he not?"],"id":702,"m":"Just this: Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March, married the Duke of Clarence's daughter, didn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":703,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["By her he had two children at one birth."],"id":704,"m":"By her he had twins."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","lines":["That's false."],"id":705,"m":"That's false."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Ay, there's the question; but I say 't is true.","The elder of them, being put to nurse,","Was by a beggar-woman stolen away,","And, ignorant of his birth and parentage,","Became a bricklayer when he came to age.","His son am I; deny it, if you can."],"id":706,"m":"Yes, that's the question; but I say it's true. The elder of them was put out to a nurse and stolen away by a beggar woman, and not knowing his birth or parents, he became a bricklayer when he grew up. I'm his son; deny it if you can."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Nay, 't is too true; therefore he shall be king."],"id":707,"m":"No, it's too true; so he will be king."},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["Sir, he made a chimney in my father's house, and the bricks","are alive at this day to testify it; therefore deny it not."],"id":708,"m":"Sir, he built a chimney in my father's house, and the bricks are alive to this day to prove it; so don't deny it."},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["And will you credit this base drudge's words,","That speaks he knows not what?"],"id":709,"m":"And will you believe the words of this low drudge, who doesn't know what he's saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Ay, marry, will we; therefore get ye gone."],"id":710,"m":"Yes, indeed we will; so get out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","lines":["Jack Cade, the Duke of York hath taught you this."],"id":711,"m":"Jack Cade, the Duke of York has taught you this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["[Aside] He lies, for I invented it myself.—Go to, sirrah,","tell the king from me that, for his father's sake, Henry the","Fifth, in whose time boys went to span-counter for French crowns,","I am content he shall reign; but I'll be protector over him."],"id":712,"m":"(Aside) He's lying, for I made it up myself. Go on, you, tell the king from me that for the sake of his father, Henry the Fifth, in whose time boys played games for French coins as stakes, I'm happy for him to reign, but I'll be his Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["And furthermore, we'll have the Lord Say's head for","selling the dukedom of Maine."],"id":713,"m":"And what's more, we'll have Lord Say's head for selling the dukedom of Maine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["And good reason; for thereby is England mained, and fain to go","with a staff, but that my puissance holds it up. Fellow kings, I","tell you that that Lord Say hath gelded the commonwealth and made","it an eunuch; and more than that, he can speak French, and","therefore he is a traitor."],"id":714,"m":"And with good reason, for that has maimed England, and she'd have to walk with a stick if my power didn't hold her up. Fellow kings, I tell you that Lord Say has castrated the country and made it a eunuch; and more than that, he can speak French, and so he's a traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["O gross and miserable ignorance!"],"id":715,"m":"Oh, gross and miserable ignorance!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Nay, answer if you can: the Frenchmen are our enemies;","go to, then, I ask but this: can he that speaks with the tongue","of an enemy be a good counsellor, or no?"],"id":716,"m":"No, answer if you can. The French are our enemies. Well then, I only ask this: can a man who speaks the enemy's language be a good adviser or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["No, no; and therefore we'll have his head."],"id":717,"m":"No, no; and so we'll have his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","lines":["Well, seeing gentle words will not prevail,","Assail them with the army of the king."],"id":718,"m":"Well, since gentle words won't work, attack them with the king's army."},{"t":"sp","who":"STAFFORD","lines":["Herald, away; and throughout every town","Proclaim them traitors that are up with Cade;","That those which fly before the battle ends","May, even in their wives' and children's sight,","Be hang'd up for example at their doors.—","And you that be the king's friends, follow me."],"id":719,"m":"Herald, go, and in every town proclaim that those who have risen with Cade are traitors, so that those who run away before the battle ends may be hanged at their own doors as an example, even in front of their wives and children. And you who are the king's friends, follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt the two Staffords, and soldiers.","id":720},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["And you that love the commons follow me.","Now show yourselves men; 't is for liberty.","We will not leave one lord, one gentleman;","Spare none but such as go in clouted shoon,","For they are thrifty honest men and such","As would, but that they dare not, take our parts."],"id":721,"m":"And you who love the commons, follow me. Now prove yourselves men; it's for liberty. We won't leave one lord or one gentleman alive. Spare nobody but those who wear patched shoes, for they're thrifty, honest men who'd take our side if they dared."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["They are all in order and march toward us."],"id":722,"m":"They're all in order and marching towards us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["But then are we in order when we are most out of","order.—Come, march forward."],"id":723,"m":"But we're most in order when we're most out of order. Come, march on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":724},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Another part of Blackheath.","id":725},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums to the fight, wherein both the STAFFORDS are slain. Enter CADE and the rest.","id":726},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Where's Dick, the butcher of Ashford?"],"id":727,"m":"Where's Dick, the butcher from Ashford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":728,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["They fell before thee like sheep and oxen, and thou","behavedst thyself as if thou hadst been in thine own","slaughter-house; therefore thus will I reward thee:","the Lent shall be as long again as it is, and thou","shalt have a licence to kill for a hundred lacking one."],"id":729,"m":"They fell in front of you like sheep and oxen, and you behaved as if you were in your own slaughterhouse. So I'll reward you like this: Lent will be twice as long as it is, and you'll have a licence to kill meat for a hundred people minus one."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["I desire no more."],"id":730,"m":"I ask for nothing more."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["And, to speak truth, thou deservest no less.","This monument of the victory will I bear","[putting on Sir Humphrey's brigandine];","and the bodies shall be dragged at my horse heels till I do come","to London, where we will have the mayor's sword borne before us."],"id":731,"m":"And to tell the truth, you deserve no less. I'll wear this souvenir of the victory (putting on Sir Humphrey's armour), and the bodies will be dragged at my horse's heels until I get to London, where we'll have the mayor's sword carried before us."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["If we mean to thrive and do good, break open the gaols and","let out the prisoners."],"id":732,"m":"If we mean to prosper and do good, let's break open the jails and let the prisoners out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Fear not that, I warrant thee. Come, let's march towards","London."],"id":733,"m":"Don't worry about that, I promise you. Come, let's march on London."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":734},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. The Palace.","id":735},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the KING with a supplication, and the QUEEN with Suffolk's head, the DUKE OF BUCKINGHAM and the LORD SAY.","id":736},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Oft have I heard that grief softens the mind","And makes it fearful and degenerate;","Think therefore on revenge and cease to weep.","But who can cease to weep and look on this?","Here may his head lie on my throbbing breast;","But where's the body that I should embrace?"],"id":737,"m":"I've often heard that grief softens the mind and makes it fearful and weak. So think about revenge and stop weeping. But who can stop weeping who looks at this? Let his head lie here on my throbbing breast; but where is the body I should hold?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["What answer makes your grace to the rebels'","supplication?"],"id":738,"m":"What answer does your grace give to the rebels' petition?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I'll send some holy bishop to entreat;","For God forbid so many simple souls","Should perish by the sword! And I myself,","Rather than bloody war shall cut them short,","Will parley with Jack Cade their general.—","But stay, I'll read it over once again."],"id":739,"m":"I'll send some holy bishop to plead with them, for God forbid so many simple souls should die by the sword! And rather than let bloody war cut them down, I'll talk with Jack Cade, their general, myself. But wait, I'll read it through again."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Ah, barbarous villains! hath this lovely face","Rul'd, like a wandering planet, over me,","And could it not enforce them to relent","That were unworthy to behold the same?"],"id":740,"m":"Ah, savage villains! Has this lovely face ruled over me like a wandering planet, and couldn't it make those who weren't fit even to look at it show mercy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Lord Say, Jack Cade hath sworn to have thy head."],"id":741,"m":"Lord Say, Jack Cade has sworn to have your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Ay, but I hope your highness shall have his."],"id":742,"m":"Yes, but I hope your highness will have his."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How now, madam!","Still lamenting and mourning for Suffolk's death?","I fear me, love, if that I had been dead,","Thou wouldst not have mourn'd so much for me."],"id":743,"m":"What, madam! Still weeping and mourning over Suffolk's death? I fear, my love, that if I had died, you wouldn't have mourned so much for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["No, my love, I should not mourn, but die for thee."],"id":744,"m":"No, my love, I wouldn't mourn; I'd die for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":745},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How now! what news? why com'st thou in such haste?"],"id":746,"m":"What's this! What news? Why do you come in such a hurry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The rebels are in Southwark; fly, my lord!","Jack Cade proclaims himself Lord Mortimer,","Descended from the Duke of Clarence' house,","And calls your grace usurper openly,","And vows to crown himself in Westminster.","His army is a ragged multitude","Of hinds and peasants, rude and merciless;","Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother's death","Hath given them heart and courage to proceed.","All scholars, lawyers, courtiers, gentlemen,","They call false caterpillars, and intend their death."],"id":747,"m":"The rebels are in Southwark; run, my lord! Jack Cade calls himself Lord Mortimer, descended from the Duke of Clarence's house, calls your grace a usurper openly and swears to crown himself in Westminster. His army is a ragged crowd of farm workers and peasants, rough and merciless. The deaths of Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother have given them heart and courage to carry on. They call all scholars, lawyers, courtiers and gentlemen false caterpillars and mean to kill them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O graceless men! they know not what they do."],"id":748,"m":"Oh, godless men! They don't know what they're doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["My gracious lord, retire to Killingworth","Until a power be rais'd to put them down."],"id":749,"m":"My gracious lord, withdraw to Kenilworth until an army is raised to put them down."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Ah, were the Duke of Suffolk now alive,","These Kentish rebels would be soon appeas'd!"],"id":750,"m":"Ah, if the Duke of Suffolk were alive now, these Kentish rebels would soon be quieted!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Lord Say, the traitors hate thee;","Therefore away with us to Killingworth."],"id":751,"m":"Lord Say, the traitors hate you, so come away with us to Kenilworth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["So might your grace's person be in danger.","The sight of me is odious in their eyes;","And therefore in this city will I stay","And live alone as secret as I may."],"id":752,"m":"That might put your grace in danger. The sight of me is hateful to them, so I'll stay in the city and live as secretly as I can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter another Messenger.","id":753},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Jack Cade hath gotten London bridge;","The citizens fly and forsake their houses.","The rascal people, thirsting after prey,","Join with the traitor, and they jointly swear","To spoil the city and your royal court."],"id":754,"m":"Jack Cade has taken London Bridge. The citizens are running away and leaving their houses. The rabble, hungry for plunder, are joining the traitor, and they all swear together to loot the city and your royal court."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Then linger not, my lord; away, take horse."],"id":755,"m":"Then don't delay, my lord; go, get on your horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come Margaret; God, our hope, will succour us."],"id":756,"m":"Come, Margaret; God, our hope, will help us."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["My hope is gone, now Suffolk is deceas'd."],"id":757,"m":"My hope is gone now Suffolk is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Farewell, my lord; trust not the Kentish rebels."],"id":758,"m":"Farewell, my lord; don't trust the Kentish rebels."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Trust nobody, for fear you be betray'd."],"id":759,"m":"Trust nobody, in case you're betrayed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["The trust I have is in mine innocence,","And therefore am I bold and resolute."],"id":760,"m":"I trust in my innocence, and so I am bold and determined."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":761},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"London. The Tower.","id":762},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LORD SCALES upon the Tower, walking. Then enter two or three Citizens, below.","id":763},{"t":"sp","who":"SCALES","lines":["How now! Is Jack Cade slain?"],"id":764,"m":"What's the news? Is Jack Cade killed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["No, my lord, nor likely to be slain; for they","have won the bridge, killing all those that withstand them.","The lord mayor craves aid of your honour from the Tower","to defend the city from the rebels."],"id":765,"m":"No, my lord, and not likely to be, for they've taken the bridge and are killing everyone who resists them. The Lord Mayor asks your honour for help from the Tower to defend the city from the rebels."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCALES","lines":["Such aid as I can spare you shall command,","But I am troubled here with them myself;","The rebels have assay'd to win the Tower.","But get you to Smithfield and gather head,","And thither I will send you Matthew Goffe.","Fight for your king, your country, and your lives;","And so, farewell, for I must hence again."],"id":766,"m":"You can have whatever help I can spare, but I'm troubled by them here myself; the rebels have tried to take the Tower. But go to Smithfield and gather a force, and I'll send Matthew Goffe to you there. Fight for your king, your country and your lives. And so farewell, for I must go back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":767},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"London. Cannon Street.","id":768},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter JACK CADE and the rest, and strikes his staff on London-stone.","id":769},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Now is Mortimer lord of this city. And here, sitting upon","London-stone, I charge and command that, of the city's cost, the","conduit run nothing but claret wine this first year of our reign.","And now henceforward it shall be treason for any that calls me","other","than Lord Mortimer."],"id":770,"m":"Now Mortimer is lord of this city. And here, sitting on London Stone, I order and command that at the city's expense the water fountain will run with nothing but red wine in this first year of our reign. And from now on it will be treason for anyone to call me anything but Lord Mortimer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Soldier, running.","id":771},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIER","lines":["Jack Cade! Jack Cade!"],"id":772,"m":"Jack Cade! Jack Cade!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Knock him down there."],"id":773,"m":"Knock him down there."},{"t":"sd","text":"They kill him.","id":774},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["If this fellow be wise, he'll never call ye Jack","Cade more; I think he hath a very fair warning."],"id":775,"m":"If this fellow's wise, he'll never call you Jack Cade again; I think he's had a very fair warning."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["My lord, there's an army gathered together in Smithfield."],"id":776,"m":"My lord, there's an army gathered in Smithfield."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Come then, let's go fight with them. But first, go and set","London bridge on fire; and, if you can, burn down the Tower too.","Come, let's away."],"id":777,"m":"Come on then, let's go and fight them. But first go and set London Bridge on fire, and if you can, burn down the Tower too. Come, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":778},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VII","loc":"London. Smithfield.","id":779},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. MATTHEW GOFFE is slain, and all the rest. Then enter JACK CADE, with his company.","id":780},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["So, sirs.—Now go some and pull down the Savoy; others","to the inns of court; down with them all."],"id":781,"m":"So, men. Now some of you go and pull down the Savoy palace; others go to the Inns of Court; down with them all."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["I have a suit unto your lordship."],"id":782,"m":"I have a request to your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Be it a lordship, thou shalt have it for that word."],"id":783,"m":"Even if it's a lordship, you'll have it for that word."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Only that the laws of England may come out of","your mouth."],"id":784,"m":"Only that the laws of England may come out of your mouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["[Aside] Mass, 't will be sore law, then; for he","was thrust in the mouth with a spear, and 't is not whole yet."],"id":785,"m":"(Aside) By the mass, it'll be sore law, then, for he was stabbed in the mouth with a spear and it hasn't healed yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SMITH","lines":["[Aside] Nay, John, it will be stinking law, for his","breath stinks with eating toasted cheese."],"id":786,"m":"(Aside) No, John, it'll be stinking law, for his breath stinks from eating toasted cheese."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["I have thought upon it, it shall be so. Away, burn","all the records of the realm. My mouth shall be the parliament","of England."],"id":787,"m":"I've thought about it, and so it will be. Go, burn all the kingdom's records. My mouth will be the parliament of England."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLLAND","lines":["[Aside] Then we are like to have biting statutes,","unless his teeth be pulled out."],"id":788,"m":"(Aside) Then we're likely to have biting laws, unless his teeth are pulled out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["And henceforward all things shall be in common."],"id":789,"m":"And from now on everything will be held in common."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":790},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, a prize, a prize! here's the Lord","Say, which sold the towns in France; he that made us pay","one and twenty fifteens, and one shilling to the pound, the","last subsidy."],"id":791,"m":"My lord, a prize, a prize! Here's Lord Say, who sold the towns in France, who made us pay twenty-one fifteenths and a shilling in the pound in the last tax."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GEORGE BEVIS, with the LORD SAY.","id":792},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Well, he shall be beheaded for it ten times.—Ah, thou say,","thou serge, nay, thou buckram lord! now art thou within point-","blank of our jurisdiction regal. What canst thou answer to my","majesty for giving up of Normandy unto Mounsieur Basimecu, the","dauphin of France? Be it known unto thee by these presence, even","the presence of Lord Mortimer, that I am the besom that must","sweep the court clean of such filth as thou art. Thou hast most","traitorously corrupted the youth of the realm in erecting a","grammar school; and whereas, before, our forefathers had no other","books but the score and the tally, thou hast caused printing to","be used, and, contrary to the king, his crown, and dignity, thou","hast built a paper-mill. It will be proved to thy face that thou","hast men about thee that usually talk of a noun and a verb, and","such abominable words as no Christian ear can endure to hear.","Thou hast appointed justices of peace, to call poor men before","them about matters they were not able to answer. Moreover, thou","hast put them in prison, and because they could not read, thou","hast hanged them; when, indeed, only for that cause they have","been most worthy to live. Thou dost ride in a foot-cloth, dost","thou not?"],"id":793,"m":"Well, he'll be beheaded ten times for it. Ah, you say, you serge, no, you buckram lord! Now you're right within range of our royal jurisdiction. What can you answer to my majesty for giving up Normandy to Monsieur Kiss-my-arse, the Dauphin of France? Be it known to you by these presents, that is, the presence of Lord Mortimer, that I am the broom that must sweep the court clean of such filth as you. You have most treacherously corrupted the youth of the kingdom by setting up a grammar school; and whereas before, our ancestors had no books but the notched tally stick, you have brought in printing, and, against the king, his crown and his dignity, you have built a paper mill. It will be proved to your face that you keep men around you who usually talk about nouns and verbs and such abominable words as no Christian ear can bear to hear. You have appointed justices of the peace to summon poor men about matters they couldn't answer. What's more, you've put them in prison, and because they couldn't read, you've hanged them, when for that reason alone they most deserved to live. You ride a horse with a fancy saddlecloth, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["What of that?"],"id":794,"m":"What of that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Marry, thou oughtest not to let thy horse wear a cloak","when honester men than thou go in their hose and doublets."],"id":795,"m":"Why, you shouldn't let your horse wear a cloak when more honest men than you go about in their tights and jackets."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["And work in their shirt too; as myself, for example, that","am a butcher."],"id":796,"m":"And work in their shirts too, like me, for example, a butcher."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["You men of Kent,—"],"id":797,"m":"You men of Kent—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["What say you of Kent?"],"id":798,"m":"What do you say about Kent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Nothing but this; 't is 'bona terra, mala gens.'"],"id":799,"m":"Only this: it's \"a good land, a bad people\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Away with him, away with him! he speaks Latin."],"id":800,"m":"Away with him, away with him! He speaks Latin."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Hear me but speak, and bear me where you will.","Kent, in the Commentaries Caesar writ,","Is term'd the civil'st place of all this isle.","Sweet is the country, because full of riches;","The people liberal, valiant, active, wealthy;","Which makes me hope you are not void of pity.","I sold not Maine, I lost not Normandy,","Yet, to recover them, would lose my life.","Justice with favour have I always done;","Prayers and tears have mov'd me, gifts could never.","When have I aught exacted at your hands","But to maintain the king, the realm, and you?","Large gifts have I bestow'd on learned clerks,","Because my book preferr'd me to the king;","And seeing ignorance is the curse of God,","Knowledge the wing wherewith we fly to heaven,","Unless you be possess'd with devilish spirits,","You cannot but forbear to murther me.","This tongue hath parley'd unto foreign kings","For your behoof,—"],"id":801,"m":"Just hear me speak, and then take me where you like. In the Commentaries Caesar wrote, Kent is called the most civilised place in all this island. The country is lovely, being full of riches, the people generous, brave, energetic and wealthy, which makes me hope you are not without pity. I didn't sell Maine, I didn't lose Normandy, but I'd give my life to win them back. I have always done justice with mercy; prayers and tears have moved me, gifts never could. When have I ever demanded anything from you except to support the king, the kingdom and you? I've given large gifts to scholars because my learning got me advancement with the king; and since ignorance is God's curse and knowledge the wing we fly to heaven with, unless you're possessed by devils, you can't help but refrain from murdering me. This tongue has negotiated with foreign kings for your benefit—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Tut, when struck'st thou one blow in the field?"],"id":802,"m":"Nonsense, when did you ever strike a blow in battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Great men have reaching hands; oft have I struck","Those that I never saw, and struck them dead."],"id":803,"m":"Great men have long arms. I've often struck people I never saw, and struck them dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEVIS","lines":["O monstrous coward! what, to come behind folks?"],"id":804,"m":"Oh, monstrous coward! What, attacking people from behind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["These cheeks are pale for watching for your good."],"id":805,"m":"These cheeks are pale from staying awake working for your good."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Give him a box o' the ear, and that will make 'em red","again."],"id":806,"m":"Give him a smack round the ear, and that'll make them red again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Long sitting to determine poor men's causes","Hath made me full of sickness and diseases."],"id":807,"m":"Sitting for long hours deciding poor men's cases has made me full of sickness and disease."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Ye shall have a hempen caudle then, and the help of","hatchet."],"id":808,"m":"Then you'll have a hemp-rope medicine, and the help of a hatchet."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["Why dost thou quiver, man?"],"id":809,"m":"Why are you shaking, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["The palsy, and not fear, provokes me."],"id":810,"m":"It's palsy that makes me shake, not fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Nay, he nods at us, as who should say, I'll be even with","you. I'll see if his head will stand steadier on a pole or","no. Take him away, and behead him."],"id":811,"m":"No, he's nodding at us, as if to say, \"I'll get even with you.\" I'll see if his head stands steadier on a pole. Take him away and behead him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Tell me wherein have I offended most?","Have I affected wealth or honour? speak.","Are my chests fill'd up with extorted gold?","Is my apparel sumptuous to behold?","Whom have I injur'd, that ye seek my death?","These hands are free from guiltless bloodshedding,","This breast from harbouring foul deceitful thoughts.","O, let me live!"],"id":812,"m":"Tell me, what have I done so wrong? Have I chased after wealth or honour? Speak. Are my chests full of extorted gold? Are my clothes expensive to look at? Whom have I hurt, that you want me dead? These hands are free of innocent blood, this heart of foul, deceitful thoughts. Oh, let me live!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["[Aside] I feel remorse in myself with his words, but I'll bridle","it; he shall die, an it be but for pleading so well for his","life.—","Away with him! he has a familiar under his tongue; he speaks not","o' God's name. Go, take him away, I say, and strike off his head","presently; and then break into his son-in-law's house, Sir James","Cromer, and strike off his head, and bring them both upon two","poles hither."],"id":813,"m":"(Aside) His words make me feel sorry, but I'll rein it in. He'll die, if only for pleading so well for his life. Away with him! He has a devil under his tongue; he doesn't speak in God's name. Go, take him away, I say, and cut off his head at once; then break into his son-in-law Sir James Cromer's house and cut off his head, and bring them both here on two poles."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["It shall be done."],"id":814,"m":"It will be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"SAY","lines":["Ah, countrymen! if when you make your prayers,","God should be so obdurate as yourselves,","How would it fare with your departed souls?","And therefore yet relent, and save my life."],"id":815,"m":"Ah, countrymen! If when you pray God were as hard-hearted as you are, how would your souls fare after death? So relent even now, and spare my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Away with him! and do as I command ye.—"],"id":816,"m":"Away with him, and do as I order you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some with Lord Say.","id":817},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","cont":1,"lines":["The proudest peer in the realm shall not","wear a head on his shoulders unless he pay me tribute; there","shall not a maid be married but she shall pay to me her","maidenhead ere they have it. Men shall hold of me in capite;","and we charge and command that their wives be as free as","heart can wish or tongue can tell."],"id":818,"m":"The proudest lord in the kingdom won't keep his head on his shoulders unless he pays me tribute. No girl will be married unless she gives me her virginity before her husband gets it. Men will hold their lands directly from me; and we order and command that their wives be as free as heart can wish or tongue can tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"DICK","lines":["My lord, when shall we go to Cheapside, and take up","commodities upon our bills?"],"id":819,"m":"My lord, when shall we go to Cheapside and take goods on credit with our bills?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Marry, presently."],"id":820,"m":"Why, right now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["O, brave!"],"id":821,"m":"Oh, splendid!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter one with the heads.","id":822},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["But is not this braver? Let them kiss one another,","for they loved well when they were alive. Now part them again,","lest they consult about the giving up of some more towns in","France.—Soldiers, defer the spoil of the city until night; for","with these borne before us, instead of maces will we ride","through the streets, and at every corner have them kiss.—Away!"],"id":823,"m":"But isn't this more splendid? Let them kiss each other, for they loved each other well when they were alive. Now part them again, in case they plot to give up some more towns in France. Soldiers, put off looting the city until night, for we'll ride through the streets with these carried in front of us instead of maces, and make them kiss at every corner. Away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":824},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VIII","loc":"Southwark.","id":825},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum and retreat. Enter CADE and all his rabblement.","id":826},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Up Fish Street! down Saint Magnus' Corner! kill","and knock down! Throw them into Thames! [Sound a parley]","What noise is this I hear? Dare any be so bold to sound retreat","or parley when I command them kill?"],"id":827,"m":"Up Fish Street! Down St Magnus' Corner! Kill and knock them down! Throw them in the Thames! (A trumpet sounds a parley) What's this noise I hear? Does anyone dare to sound retreat or parley when I order them to kill?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM and old CLIFFORD, attended.","id":828},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Ay, here they be that dare and will disturb thee.","Know, Cade, we come ambassadors from the king","Unto the commons whom thou hast misled,","And here pronounce free pardon to them all","That will forsake thee and go home in peace."],"id":829,"m":"Yes, here are men who dare and will disturb you. Know, Cade, that we come as ambassadors from the king to the commons you have misled, and here we announce a free pardon to all who will abandon you and go home in peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["What say ye, countrymen? will ye relent","And yield to mercy whilst 't is offer'd you,","Or let a rebel lead you to your deaths?","Who loves the king and will embrace his pardon,","Fling up his cap, and say 'God save his Majesty!'","Who hateth him and honours not his father,","Henry the Fifth, that made all France to quake,","Shake he his weapon at us and pass by."],"id":830,"m":"What do you say, countrymen? Will you give in and accept mercy while it's offered, or let a rebel lead you to your deaths? Whoever loves the king and will accept his pardon, throw up your cap and say \"God save his Majesty!\" Whoever hates him and doesn't honour his father, Henry the Fifth, who made all France tremble, shake your weapon at us and go past."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["God save the king! God save the king!"],"id":831,"m":"God save the king! God save the king!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["What, Buckingham and Clifford, are ye so brave?—","And you, base peasants, do ye believe him? will you needs be","hang'd with your pardons about your necks? Hath my sword therefore","broke through London gates, that you should leave me at the","White Hart in Southwark? I thought ye would never have given","out these arms till you had recovered your ancient freedom;","but you are all recreants and dastards, and delight to live in","slavery to the nobility. Let them break your backs with burthens,","take your houses over your heads, ravish your wives and daughters","before your faces. For me, I will make shift for one; and so,","God's curse light upon you all!"],"id":832,"m":"What, Buckingham and Clifford, are you so bold? And you low peasants, do you believe him? Are you set on being hanged with your pardons round your necks? Did my sword break through London's gates just so you could desert me at the White Hart in Southwark? I thought you'd never lay down these arms till you had won back your old freedom. But you're all deserters and cowards, and love to live as slaves to the nobility. Let them break your backs with burdens, take your houses from over your heads, rape your wives and daughters in front of you. As for me, I'll look after myself; and so, God's curse on you all!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["We'll follow Cade, we'll follow Cade!"],"id":833,"m":"We'll follow Cade, we'll follow Cade!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Is Cade the son of Henry the Fifth,","That thus you do exclaim you'll go with him?","Will he conduct you through the heart of France,","And make the meanest of you earls and dukes?","Alas, he hath no home, no place to fly to;","Nor knows he how to live but by the spoil,","Unless by robbing of your friends and us.","Were 't not a shame that whilst you live at jar","The fearful French, whom you late vanquished,","Should make a start o'er seas and vanquish you?","Methinks already in this civil broil","I see them lording it in London streets,","Crying 'Villiaco!' unto all they meet.","Better ten thousand base-born Cades miscarry","Than you should stoop unto a Frenchman's mercy.","To France, to France, and get what you have lost;","Spare England, for it is your native coast.","Henry hath money, you are strong and manly;","God on our side, doubt not of victory."],"id":834,"m":"Is Cade the son of Henry the Fifth, that you shout you'll go with him like this? Will he lead you through the heart of France and make the lowest of you earls and dukes? Alas, he has no home, no place to run to, and doesn't know how to live except by plunder, by robbing your friends and us. Wouldn't it be a shame if, while you quarrel among yourselves, the frightened French, whom you recently beat, should suddenly cross the sea and beat you? I can already see them, in this civil chaos, lording it in London's streets, shouting \"Villain!\" at everyone they meet. Better ten thousand low-born Cades should die than you should bow to a Frenchman's mercy. To France, to France, and win back what you've lost! Spare England, for it's your native land. Henry has money, you are strong and manly; with God on our side, don't doubt we'll win."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["A Clifford! a Clifford! we'll follow the king and","Clifford."],"id":835,"m":"A Clifford! A Clifford! We'll follow the king and Clifford."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Was ever feather so lightly blown to and fro as this","multitude? The name of Henry the Fifth hales them to an hundred","mischiefs and makes them leave me desolate. I see them lay their","heads together to surprise me. My sword make way for me, for","here is no staying.—In despite of the devils and hell, have","through the very middest of you! and heavens and honour be","witness","that no want of resolution in me, but only my followers' base and","ignominious treasons, makes me betake me to my heels."],"id":836,"m":"Was any feather ever blown back and forth as easily as this crowd? The name of Henry the Fifth drags them into a hundred kinds of trouble and makes them leave me alone. I see them putting their heads together to seize me. Sword, cut a way for me, for there's no staying here. In spite of devils and hell, I'll go right through the middle of you! And may heaven and honour witness that it's not lack of courage in me but only my followers' low, shameful treachery that makes me run for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":837},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["What, is he fled?—Go some, and follow him;","And he that brings his head unto the king","Shall have a thousand crowns for his reward.—"],"id":838,"m":"What, has he fled? Some of you go after him; whoever brings his head to the king will get a thousand crowns as a reward."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some of them.","id":839},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","cont":1,"lines":["Follow me, soldiers; we'll devise a mean","To reconcile you all unto the king."],"id":840,"m":"Follow me, soldiers; we'll find a way to reconcile you all with the king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":841},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IX","loc":"Kenilworth Castle.","id":842},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets. Enter KING, QUEEN, and SOMERSET, on the terrace.","id":843},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Was ever king that joy'd an earthly throne,","And could command no more content than I?","No sooner was I crept out of my cradle","But I was made a king at nine months old.","Was never subject long'd to be a king","As I do long and wish to be a subject."],"id":844,"m":"Was there ever a king who enjoyed an earthly throne and had less happiness than me? I'd hardly crawled out of my cradle before I was made king, at nine months old. No subject ever longed to be a king as much as I long to be a subject."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM and old CLIFFORD.","id":845},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Health and glad tidings to your majesty!"],"id":846,"m":"Health and good news to your majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, Buckingham, is the traitor Cade surpris'd?","Or is he but retir'd to make him strong?"],"id":847,"m":"Why, Buckingham, is the traitor Cade captured? Or has he only withdrawn to gather strength?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, below, multitudes with halters about their necks.","id":848},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["He is fled, my lord, and all his powers do yield,","And humbly thus, with halters on their necks,","Expect your highness' doom, of life or death."],"id":849,"m":"He has fled, my lord, and all his forces are surrendering, and humbly, like this, with nooses round their necks, they await your highness's sentence of life or death."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then, heaven, set ope thy everlasting gates,","To entertain my vows of thanks and praise!—","Soldiers, this day have you redeem'd your lives","And show'd how well you love your prince and country.","Continue still in this so good a mind,","And Henry, though he be infortunate,","Assure yourselves, will never be unkind.","And so, with thanks and pardon to you all,","I do dismiss you to your several countries."],"id":850,"m":"Then, heaven, open your everlasting gates to receive my vows of thanks and praise! Soldiers, today you've saved your lives and shown how much you love your prince and country. Keep this good attitude, and be sure that Henry, though he's unlucky, will never be unkind. And so, with thanks and pardon to you all, I send you home to your own counties."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["God save the king! God save the king!"],"id":851,"m":"God save the king! God save the king!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":852},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Please it your grace to be advertised","The Duke of York is newly come from Ireland,","And with a puissant and a mighty power","Of gallowglasses and stout kerns","Is marching hitherward in proud array,","And still proclaimeth, as he comes along,","His arms are only to remove from thee","The Duke of Somerset, whom he terms a traitor."],"id":853,"m":"If it please your grace to be told, the Duke of York has just come from Ireland with a powerful army of Irish soldiers, heavy and light, and is marching here in proud formation, announcing as he comes that his only aim is to remove from you the Duke of Somerset, whom he calls a traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thus stands my state, 'twixt Cade and York distress'd,","Like to a ship that, having scap'd a tempest,","Is straightway calm'd and boarded with a pirate;","But now is Cade driven back, his men dispers'd,","And now is York in arms to second him.—","I pray thee, Buckingham, go and meet him,","And ask him what's the reason of these arms.","Tell him I'll send Duke Edmund to the Tower;—","And, Somerset, we will commit thee thither,","Until his army be dismiss'd from him."],"id":854,"m":"This is how I stand, troubled between Cade and York, like a ship that has escaped a storm and is immediately becalmed and boarded by a pirate. Just as Cade is driven back and his men scattered, York is up in arms to follow him. Please, Buckingham, go and meet him and ask him the reason for this army. Tell him I'll send Duke Edmund to the Tower; and Somerset, we will send you there until he dismisses his army."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["My lord,","I'll yield myself to prison willingly,","Or unto death, to do my country good."],"id":855,"m":"My lord, I'll go to prison willingly, or to my death, to do my country good."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["In any case, be not too rough in terms,","For he is fierce and cannot brook hard language."],"id":856,"m":"Whatever happens, don't use harsh words, for he is fierce and can't stand rough language."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["I will, my lord, and doubt not so to deal","As all things shall redound unto your good."],"id":857,"m":"I will, my lord, and don't doubt I'll handle it so that everything turns out for your good."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come, wife, let's in, and learn to govern better;","For yet may England curse my wretched reign."],"id":858,"m":"Come, wife, let's go in and learn to govern better, for so far England may well curse my wretched reign."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":859},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"X","loc":"Kent. Iden's Garden.","id":860},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CADE.","id":861},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Fie on ambitions! fie on myself, that have a sword","and yet am ready to famish! These five days have I hid me in","these woods and durst not peep out, for all the country is laid","for me; but now am I so hungry that if I might have a lease of","my life for a thousand years I could stay no longer. Wherefore,","on a brick wall have I climb'd into this garden, to see if I can","eat grass, or pick a sallet another while, which is not amiss to","cool","a man's stomach this hot weather. And I think this word 'sallet'","was born to do me good; for many a time, but for a sallet, my","brain-pain had been cleft with a brown bill; and many a time,","when I have been dry and bravely marching, it hath served me","instead of a quart pot to drink in; and now the word 'sallet'","must serve me to feed on."],"id":862,"m":"Curse ambition! Curse me, who have a sword and yet am starving to death! For five days I've hidden in these woods and haven't dared to peep out, for the whole county is searching for me. But now I'm so hungry that even if I were promised another thousand years of life, I couldn't wait any longer. So I've climbed over a brick wall into this garden to see if I can eat grass or pick a salad, which isn't bad for cooling a man's stomach in this hot weather. And I think this word \"sallet\" was born to do me good. Many times, if not for a sallet, a helmet, my skull would have been split by a soldier's axe; and many times, when I've been thirsty and marching bravely, it has served me as a pot to drink from; and now the word \"sallet\" must serve me as food."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter IDEN.","id":863},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["Lord, who would live turmoiled in the court,","And may enjoy such quiet walks as these?","This small inheritance my father left me","Contenteth me, and worth a monarchy.","I seek not to wax great by others' waning,","Or gather wealth, I care not with what envy;","Sufficeth that I have maintains my state","And sends the poor well pleased from my gate."],"id":864,"m":"Lord, who would live in the turmoil of the court when he could enjoy quiet walks like these? This small estate my father left me satisfies me and is worth a kingdom. I don't try to grow great by others' falling, or gather wealth regardless of the envy it causes. It's enough that what I have supports my position and sends the poor away from my gate well pleased."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Here's the lord of the soil come to seize me for a","stray, for entering his fee-simple without leave.—Ah, villain,","thou wilt betray me, and get a thousand crowns of the king","by carrying my head to him; but I'll make thee eat iron like","an ostrich, and swallow my sword like a great pin, ere thou","and I part."],"id":865,"m":"Here's the lord of the land come to arrest me as a stray for entering his property without permission. Ah, villain, you'll betray me and get a thousand crowns from the king by carrying my head to him. But I'll make you eat iron like an ostrich and swallow my sword like a big pin before we part."},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["Why, rude companion, whatsoe'er thou be, I know","thee not! why, then, should I betray thee?","Is 't not enough to break into my garden,","And, like a thief, to come to rob my grounds,","Climbing my walls in spite of me the owner,","But thou wilt brave me with these saucy terms?"],"id":866,"m":"Why, rough fellow, whoever you are, I don't know you! Why, then, would I betray you? Isn't it enough to break into my garden and come like a thief to rob my land, climbing my walls against the owner's will, without defying me with these cheeky words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Brave thee? ay, by the best blood that ever was","broached, and beard thee too. Look on me well: I have eat","no meat these five days; yet, come thou and thy five men,","and if I do not leave you all as dead as a door-nail, I pray","God I may never eat grass more."],"id":867,"m":"Defy you? Yes, by the best blood ever spilled, and insult you to your face too. Take a good look at me. I haven't eaten for five days; yet let you and your five men come, and if I don't leave you all as dead as a doornail, I pray God I may never eat grass again."},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["Nay, it shall ne'er be said, while England stands,","That Alexander Iden, an esquire of Kent,","Took odds to combat a poor famish'd man.","Oppose thy steadfast-gazing eyes to mine,","See if thou canst outface me with thy looks.","Set limb to limb and thou art far the lesser;","Thy hand is but a finger to my fist,","Thy leg a stick compared with this truncheon;","My foot shall fight with all the strength thou hast;","And if mine arm be heaved in the air,","Thy grave is digg'd already in the earth.","As for words, whose greatness answers words,","Let this my sword report what speech forbears."],"id":868,"m":"No, it will never be said, as long as England stands, that Alexander Iden, a gentleman of Kent, took unfair odds to fight a poor starving man. Set your staring eyes against mine; see if you can outstare me. Compare limb with limb and you're far smaller. Your hand is only a finger next to my fist, your leg a stick compared with this club of mine; my foot alone will fight with all the strength you have. And if my arm is raised in the air, your grave is already dug in the earth. As for words, whose bragging answers words, let this sword of mine say what speech holds back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["By my valour, the most complete champion that","ever I heard!—Steel, if thou turn the edge, or cut not out","the burly-boned clown in chines of beef ere thou sleep in","thy sheath, I beseech God on my knees thou mayst be turn'd","to hobnails.—[Here they fight. Cade falls] O, I am slain!","famine and no other hath slain me; let ten thousand devils","come against me, and give me but the ten meals I have lost,","and I'd defy them all.—Wither, garden; and be henceforth a","burying place to all that do dwell in this house, because","the unconquered soul of Cade is fled."],"id":869,"m":"By my courage, the most perfect champion I ever heard! Steel, if your edge turns, or you don't carve this big-boned peasant into joints of beef before you sleep in your sheath, I pray God on my knees that you may be turned into hobnails. (They fight. Cade falls) Oh, I'm killed! Hunger and nothing else has killed me. Let ten thousand devils come against me, and give me the ten meals I've missed, and I'd defy them all. Wither, garden, and be from now on a graveyard for everyone who lives in this house, because the unconquered soul of Cade has fled."},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["Is't Cade that I have slain, that monstrous traitor?—","Sword, I will hallow thee for this thy deed,","And hang thee o'er my tomb when I am dead;","Ne'er shall this blood be wiped from thy point,","But thou shalt wear it as a herald's coat,","To emblaze the honour that thy master got."],"id":870,"m":"Is it Cade I've killed, that monstrous traitor? Sword, I'll make you sacred for this deed and hang you over my tomb when I'm dead. This blood will never be wiped from your point; you'll wear it like a herald's coat, to proclaim the honour your master won."},{"t":"sp","who":"CADE","lines":["Iden, farewell; and be proud of thy victory. Tell Kent from","me, she hath lost her best man, and exhort all the world to be","cowards; for I, that never feared any, am vanquished by famine,","not by valour."],"id":871,"m":"Iden, farewell, and be proud of your victory. Tell Kent from me that she has lost her best man, and urge the whole world to be cowards; for I, who never feared anyone, am beaten by hunger, not by courage."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":872},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["How much thou wrong'st me, heaven be my judge.","Die, damned wretch, the curse of her that bare thee;","And as I thrust thy body in with my sword,","So wish I I might thrust thy soul to hell.","Hence will I drag thee headlong by the heels","Unto a dunghill which shall be thy grave,","And there cut off thy most ungracious head,","Which I will bear in triumph to the king,","Leaving thy trunk for crows to feed upon."],"id":873,"m":"Heaven judge how much you wrong me. Die, damned wretch, curse of the woman who bore you! And as I thrust my sword into your body, so I wish I could thrust your soul into hell. From here I'll drag you headlong by the heels to a dunghill, which will be your grave, and there cut off your wicked head, which I'll carry in triumph to the king, leaving your body for the crows to eat."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":874},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Fields between Dartford and Blackheath.","id":875},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK, and his army of Irish, with drum and colours.","id":876},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["From Ireland thus comes York to claim his right,","And pluck the crown from feeble Henry's head.","Ring, bells, aloud; burn, bonfires, clear and bright,","To entertain great England's lawful king.","Ah! sancta majestas! who would not buy thee dear?","Let them obey that knows not how to rule;","This hand was made to handle nought but gold.","I cannot give due action to my words","Except a sword or sceptre balance it.","A sceptre shall it have, have I a soul,","On which I'll toss the flower-de-luce of France.—"],"id":877,"m":"So York comes from Ireland to claim his right and pluck the crown from feeble Henry's head. Ring loud, bells; burn clear and bright, bonfires, to welcome great England's lawful king. Ah, sacred majesty! Who wouldn't pay dearly for you? Let those obey who don't know how to rule. This hand was made to hold nothing but gold. I can't put my words into action unless a sword or sceptre gives it weight. It will have a sceptre, as surely as I have a soul, and on it I'll toss the fleur-de-lis of France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM.","id":878},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Whom have we here? Buckingham, to disturb me?","The king hath sent him, sure: I must dissemble."],"id":879,"m":"Who's this? Buckingham, come to bother me? The king has sent him, surely; I must pretend."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["York, if thou meanest well, I greet thee well."],"id":880,"m":"York, if you mean well, I greet you well."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Humphrey of Buckingham, I accept thy greeting.","Art thou a messenger, or come of pleasure?"],"id":881,"m":"Humphrey of Buckingham, I accept your greeting. Are you a messenger, or here for your own pleasure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["A messenger from Henry, our dread liege,","To know the reason of these arms in peace;","Or why thou, being a subject as I am,","Against thy oath and true allegiance sworn,","Should raise so great a power without his leave,","Or dare to bring thy force so near the court."],"id":882,"m":"A messenger from Henry, our dread lord, to learn why you are in arms in peacetime, or why you, a subject like me, against your oath and sworn allegiance, should raise such a big army without his permission, or dare to bring your forces so near the court."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["[Aside] Scarce can I speak, my choler is so great:","O, I could hew up rocks and fight with flint,","I am so angry at these abject terms;","And now, like Ajax Telamonius,","On sheep or oxen could I spend my fury.","I am far better born than is the king,","More like a king, more kingly in my thoughts;","But I must make fair weather yet a while,","Till Henry be more weak and I more strong.—","Buckingham, I prithee, pardon me,","That I have given no answer all this while;","My mind was troubled with deep melancholy.","The cause why I have brought this army hither","Is to remove proud Somerset from the king,","Seditious to his grace and to the state."],"id":883,"m":"(Aside) I can hardly speak, I'm so angry. Oh, I could hack up rocks and fight with flint, these insulting words make me so furious; and now, like Ajax, son of Telamon, I could spend my fury on sheep or oxen. I'm far better born than the king, more like a king, more kingly in my thoughts. But I must pretend to be friendly a while longer, until Henry is weaker and I'm stronger. Buckingham, please forgive me for not answering all this time; my mind was troubled with deep sadness. The reason I've brought this army here is to remove proud Somerset from the king, since he is a traitor to his grace and the state."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["That is too much presumption on thy part;","But if thy arms be to no other end,","The king hath yielded unto thy demand.","The Duke of Somerset is in the Tower."],"id":884,"m":"That's too presumptuous of you. But if your army is for no other purpose, the king has granted your demand. The Duke of Somerset is in the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Upon thine honour, is he prisoner?"],"id":885,"m":"On your honour, is he a prisoner?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Upon mine honour, he is prisoner."],"id":886,"m":"On my honour, he is a prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Then, Buckingham, I do dismiss my powers.—","Soldiers, I thank you all; disperse yourselves;","Meet me to-morrow in Saint George's field,","You shall have pay and everything you wish.—","And let my sovereign, virtuous Henry,","Command my eldest son, nay, all my sons,","As pledges of my fealty and love,","I'll send them all as willing as I live;","Lands, goods, horse, armour, anything I have,","Is his to use, so Somerset may die."],"id":887,"m":"Then, Buckingham, I'll dismiss my forces. Soldiers, thank you all; break up; meet me tomorrow in St George's Field, and you'll have your pay and everything you want. And let my sovereign, virtuous Henry, demand my eldest son, or all my sons, as pledges of my loyalty and love; I'll send them all as willingly as I live. Lands, goods, horses, armour, anything I have is his to use, as long as Somerset dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["York, I commend this kind submission;","We twain will go into his highness' tent."],"id":888,"m":"York, I praise this kind submission. We two will go to his highness's tent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING and Attendants.","id":889},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Buckingham, doth York intend no harm to us,","That thus he marcheth with thee arm in arm?"],"id":890,"m":"Buckingham, does York mean us no harm, that he walks with you arm in arm like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["In all submission and humility","York doth present himself unto your highness."],"id":891,"m":"York presents himself to your highness in full submission and humility."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then what intends these forces thou dost bring?"],"id":892,"m":"Then what is the purpose of these forces you bring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["To heave the traitor Somerset from hence,","And fight against that monstrous rebel Cade,","Who since I heard to be discomfited."],"id":893,"m":"To remove the traitor Somerset from here, and to fight that monstrous rebel Cade, who I've since heard has been defeated."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter IDEN, with CADE's head.","id":894},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["If one so rude and of so mean condition","May pass into the presence of a king,","Lo, I present your grace a traitor's head,","The head of Cade, whom I in combat slew."],"id":895,"m":"If someone so rough and of such low rank may come into a king's presence, look, I present your grace a traitor's head, the head of Cade, whom I killed in a fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The head of Cade!—Great God, how just art Thou!—","O, let me view his visage, being dead,","That living wrought me such exceeding trouble.","Tell me, my friend, art thou the man that slew him?"],"id":896,"m":"The head of Cade! Great God, how just you are! Oh, let me look at his face, now he's dead, which in life caused me so much trouble. Tell me, my friend, are you the man who killed him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["I was, an 't like your majesty."],"id":897,"m":"I am, if it please your majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How art thou call'd? and what is thy degree?"],"id":898,"m":"What is your name, and what is your rank?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["Alexander Iden, that's my name;","A poor esquire of Kent, that loves his king."],"id":899,"m":"Alexander Iden is my name, a poor gentleman of Kent who loves his king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["So please it you, my lord, 't were not amiss","He were created knight for his good service."],"id":900,"m":"If it please you, my lord, it would be fitting to make him a knight for his good service."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Iden, kneel down. [He kneels] Rise up a knight.","We give thee for reward a thousand marks,","And will that thou thenceforth attend on us."],"id":901,"m":"Iden, kneel. (He kneels) Rise up a knight. We give you a thousand marks as a reward, and want you to serve us from now on."},{"t":"sp","who":"IDEN","lines":["May Iden live to merit such a bounty,","And never live but true unto his liege!"],"id":902,"m":"May Iden live to deserve such generosity, and never live except loyal to his lord!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Rises.","id":903},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUEEN and SOMERSET.","id":904},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["See, Buckingham, Somerset comes with the queen.","Go, bid her hide him quickly from the duke."],"id":905,"m":"Look, Buckingham, Somerset is coming with the queen. Go and tell her to hide him quickly from the duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["For thousand Yorks he shall not hide his head,","But boldly stand and front him to his face."],"id":906,"m":"He won't hide his head for a thousand Yorks, but will stand boldly and face him."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["How now! is Somerset at liberty?","Then, York, unloose thy long-imprisoned thoughts,","And let thy tongue be equal with thy heart.","Shall I endure the sight of Somerset?","False king! why hast thou broken faith with me,","Knowing how hardly I can brook abuse?","King did I call thee? no, thou art not king,","Not fit to govern and rule multitudes,","Which dar'st not, no, nor canst not rule a traitor.","That head of thine doth not become a crown;","Thy hand is made to grasp a palmer's staff,","And not to grace an awful princely sceptre.","That gold must round engirt these brows of mine,","Whose smile and frown, like to Achilles' spear,","Is able with the change to kill and cure.","Here is a hand to hold a sceptre up","And with the same to act controlling laws.","Give place; by heaven, thou shalt rule no more","O'er him whom heaven created for thy ruler."],"id":907,"m":"What's this! Is Somerset free? Then, York, let loose your long-imprisoned thoughts, and let your tongue match your heart. Shall I put up with the sight of Somerset? False king! Why have you broken your word to me, knowing how badly I take being cheated? Did I call you king? No, you're not king, not fit to govern and rule the people, since you don't dare, no, and can't, rule even a traitor. That head of yours doesn't suit a crown. Your hand was made to grip a pilgrim's staff, not to grace an awe-inspiring royal sceptre. That gold must circle these brows of mine, whose smile and frown, like Achilles' spear, can kill or cure as they change. Here is a hand to hold up a sceptre and to enforce laws with it. Make way; by heaven, you'll rule no longer over the man heaven made to rule you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["O monstrous traitor!—I arrest thee, York,","Of capital treason 'gainst the king and crown.","Obey, audacious traitor; kneel for grace."],"id":908,"m":"Oh, monstrous traitor! I arrest you, York, for capital treason against the king and crown. Obey, impudent traitor; kneel for mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Wouldst have me kneel? first let me ask of these","If they can brook I bow a knee to man.—","Sirrah, call in my sons to be my bail.—"],"id":909,"m":"Would you have me kneel? First let me ask these men if they can bear to see me bow my knee to any man. You, call my sons in to be my guarantors."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Attendant.","id":910},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["I know, ere thy will have me go to ward,","They'll pawn their swords for my enfranchisement."],"id":911,"m":"I know that before they let me go to prison, they'll pledge their swords to free me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Call hither Clifford; bid him come amain,","To say if that the bastard boys of York","Shall be the surety for their traitor father."],"id":912,"m":"Call Clifford here; tell him to come at once, to say whether York's bastard boys shall be guarantors for their traitor father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Buckingham.","id":913},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["O blood-bespotted Neapolitan,","Outcast of Naples, England's bloody scourge!","The sons of York, thy betters in their birth,","Shall be their father's bail; and bane to those","That for my surety will refuse the boys!"],"id":914,"m":"Oh, blood-stained Neapolitan, outcast of Naples, England's bloody scourge! The sons of York, better born than you, will be their father's bail, and the ruin of those who refuse to accept the boys as my guarantee!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EDWARD and RICHARD.","id":915},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["See where they come; I'll warrant they'll make it good."],"id":916,"m":"See, here they come; I promise you they'll make it good."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter old CLIFFORD and his SON.","id":917},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And here comes Clifford to deny their bail."],"id":918,"m":"And here comes Clifford to refuse their bail."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Health and all happiness to my lord the king!"],"id":919,"m":"Health and all happiness to my lord the king!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels.","id":920},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I thank thee, Clifford; say, what news with thee?","Nay, do not fright us with an angry look.","We are thy sovereign, Clifford, kneel again;","For thy mistaking so, we pardon thee."],"id":921,"m":"Thank you, Clifford. Tell me, what news do you bring? No, don't frighten us with an angry look. We are your sovereign, Clifford; kneel again. We pardon you for your mistake."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["This is my king, York, I do not mistake;","But thou mistakes me much to think I do.—","To Bedlam with him! is the man grown mad?"],"id":922,"m":"This is my king, York; I'm not mistaken, but you are very mistaken to think I am. Off to the madhouse with him! Has the man gone mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, Clifford; a bedlam and ambitious humour","Makes him oppose himself against his king."],"id":923,"m":"Yes, Clifford; a mad, ambitious temper makes him set himself against his king."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["He is a traitor; let him to the Tower,","And chop away that factious pate of his."],"id":924,"m":"He's a traitor. Send him to the Tower and chop off that rebellious head of his."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["He is arrested, but will not obey;","His sons, he says, shall give their words for him."],"id":925,"m":"He's been arrested, but won't obey. He says his sons will give their word for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Will you not, sons?"],"id":926,"m":"Won't you, sons?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Ay, noble father, if our words will serve."],"id":927,"m":"Yes, noble father, if our words will do."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And if words will not, then our weapons shall."],"id":928,"m":"And if words won't, then our weapons will."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Why, what a brood of traitors have we here!"],"id":929,"m":"Why, what a brood of traitors we have here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Look in a glass, and call thy image so;","I am thy king, and thou a false-heart traitor.—","Call hither to the stake my two brave bears,","That with the very shaking of their chains","They may astonish these fell-lurking curs.","Bid Salisbury and Warwick come to me."],"id":930,"m":"Look in a mirror and call your reflection that. I am your king, and you a false-hearted traitor. Call my two brave bears to the stake, so that the mere rattle of their chains will terrify these fierce, skulking dogs. Tell Salisbury and Warwick to come to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the EARLS OF WARWICK and SALISBURY.","id":931},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Are these thy bears? we'll bait thy bears to death,","And manacle the bear-herd in their chains,","If thou dar'st bring them to the baiting-place."],"id":932,"m":"Are these your bears? We'll bait your bears to death and chain up the bear-keeper in their chains, if you dare bring them to the bear pit."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Oft have I seen a hot o'erweening cur","Run back and bite because he was withheld,","Who, being suffer'd with the bear's fell paw,","Hath clapp'd his tail between his legs and cried;","And such a piece of service will you do","If you oppose yourselves to match Lord Warwick."],"id":933,"m":"I've often seen a hot-headed, cocky dog run back and bite because he was held back, who, when let loose at the bear's cruel paw, put his tail between his legs and howled. And that's the kind of service you'll do if you set yourselves against Lord Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Hence, heap of wrath, foul indigested lump,","As crooked in thy manners as thy shape!"],"id":934,"m":"Get away, you heap of anger, foul, shapeless lump, as twisted in your manners as in your body!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Nay, we shall heat you thoroughly anon."],"id":935,"m":"No, we'll heat you up thoroughly soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Take heed, lest by your heat you burn yourselves."],"id":936,"m":"Be careful you don't burn yourselves with your heat."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, Warwick, hath thy knee forgot to bow?—","Old Salisbury, shame to thy silver hair,","Thou mad misleader of thy brainsick son!","What, wilt thou on thy death-bed play the ruffian,","And seek for sorrow with thy spectacles?","O, where is faith? O, where is loyalty?","If it be banish'd from the frosty head,","Where shall it find a harbour in the earth?","Wilt thou go dig a grave to find out war,","And shame thine honourable age with blood?","Why art thou old, and want'st experience?","Or wherefore dost abuse it, if thou hast it?","For shame! in duty bend thy knee to me","That bows unto the grave with mickle age."],"id":937,"m":"Why, Warwick, has your knee forgotten how to bow? Old Salisbury, shame on your silver hair, you mad misleader of your brainsick son! What, will you play the thug on your deathbed and go looking for sorrow with your spectacles on? Oh, where is faith? Where is loyalty? If it's banished from the white-haired head, where on earth will it find a home? Will you dig up war from your grave and shame your honourable old age with blood? Why are you old and lack experience? Or why do you misuse it, if you have it? For shame! Do your duty and bend your knee to me, you who are already bending to the grave with old age."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["My lord, I have consider'd with myself","The tide of this most renowned duke,","And in my conscience do repute his grace","The rightful heir to England's royal seat."],"id":938,"m":"My lord, I have thought hard about this famous duke's title, and in my conscience I consider his grace the rightful heir to England's throne."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Hast thou not sworn allegiance unto me?"],"id":939,"m":"Haven't you sworn allegiance to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["I have."],"id":940,"m":"I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Canst thou dispense with heaven for such an oath?"],"id":941,"m":"Can you get heaven to release you from such an oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["It is great sin to swear unto a sin,","But greater sin to keep a sinful oath.","Who can be bound by any solemn vow","To do a murtherous deed, to rob a man,","To force a spotless virgin's chastity,","To reave the orphan of his patrimony,","To wring the widow from her custom'd right,","And have no other reason for this wrong","But that he was bound by a solemn oath?"],"id":942,"m":"It is a great sin to swear to commit a sin, but a greater sin to keep a sinful oath. Who can be bound by any solemn vow to commit murder, rob a man, rape a pure virgin, steal an orphan's inheritance, or squeeze a widow out of her lawful rights, and have no other reason for this wrong except that he was bound by a solemn oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["A subtle traitor needs no sophister."],"id":943,"m":"A clever traitor doesn't need anyone to argue for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Call Buckingham, and bid him arm himself."],"id":944,"m":"Call Buckingham and tell him to arm himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Call Buckingham, and all the friends thou hast,","I am resolv'd for death or dignity."],"id":945,"m":"Call Buckingham and all the friends you have; I'm determined to have death or the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["The first I warrant thee if dreams prove true."],"id":946,"m":"Death, I promise you, if dreams come true."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["You were best to go to bed and dream again,","To keep thee from the tempest of the field."],"id":947,"m":"You'd better go to bed and dream again, to keep yourself out of the storm of battle."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["I am resolv'd to bear a greater storm","Than any thou canst conjure up to-day;","And that I'll write upon thy burgonet,","Might I but know thee by thy household badge."],"id":948,"m":"I'm determined to face a greater storm than any you can conjure up today, and I'll write that on your helmet, if only I can recognise you by your family badge."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Now, by my father's badge, old Nevil's crest,","The rampant bear chain'd to the ragged staff,","This day I'll wear aloft my burgonet,","As on a mountain top the cedar shows","That keeps his leaves in spite of any storm,","Even to affright thee with the view thereof."],"id":949,"m":"Now, by my father's badge, old Neville's crest, the rearing bear chained to the knotted staff, I'll wear my helmet high today, like the cedar on a mountaintop that keeps its leaves in spite of any storm, just to frighten you with the sight of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["And from thy burgonet I'll rend thy bear","And tread it under foot with all contempt,","Despite the bear-herd that protects the bear."],"id":950,"m":"And I'll tear your bear off your helmet and trample it underfoot with total contempt, in spite of the bear-keeper who protects the bear."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","lines":["And so to arms, victorious father,","To quell the rebels and their complices."],"id":951,"m":"And so to arms, victorious father, to crush the rebels and their accomplices."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Fie! charity, for shame! speak not in spite,","For you shall sup with Jesu Christ to-night."],"id":952,"m":"Shame! Be charitable, for shame! Don't speak in spite, for you'll have supper with Jesus Christ tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","lines":["Foul stigmatic, that's more than thou canst","tell."],"id":953,"m":"Foul, deformed creature, that's more than you can tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["If not in heaven, you'll surely sup in hell."],"id":954,"m":"If not in heaven, you'll surely have supper in hell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally.","id":955},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Saint Alban's. The field of battle.","id":956},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums to the battle. Enter WARWICK.","id":957},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Clifford of Cumberland, 't is Warwick calls;","And if thou dost not hide thee from the bear,","Now, when the angry trumpet sounds alarum","And dead men's cries do fill the empty air,","Clifford, I say, come forth and fight with me!","Proud northern lord, Clifford of Cumberland,","Warwick is hoarse with calling thee to arms.—"],"id":958,"m":"Clifford of Cumberland, it's Warwick calling! If you're not hiding from the bear, now, when the angry trumpet sounds the call to battle and dying men's cries fill the empty air, Clifford, I say, come out and fight me! Proud northern lord, Clifford of Cumberland, Warwick is hoarse from calling you to fight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK.","id":959},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","cont":1,"lines":["How now, my noble lord! what, all afoot?"],"id":960,"m":"What's this, my noble lord? What, on foot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The deadly-handed Clifford slew my steed,","But match to match I have encount'red him,","And made a prey for carrion kites and crows","Even of the bonny beast he lov'd so well."],"id":961,"m":"Deadly Clifford killed my horse, but I've paid him back in kind and made a meal for scavenging kites and crows of the fine animal he loved so well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter old CLIFFORD.","id":962},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Of one or both of us the time is come."],"id":963,"m":"The time has come for one or both of us."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Hold, Warwick, seek thee out some other chase,","For I myself must hunt this deer to death."],"id":964,"m":"Stop, Warwick, find some other quarry, for I must hunt this deer to death myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then, nobly, York; 't is for a crown thou fight'st.—","As I intend, Clifford, to thrive to-day,","It grieves my soul to leave thee unassail'd."],"id":965,"m":"Then fight nobly, York; it's for a crown you're fighting. As I hope to win today, Clifford, it grieves my soul to leave you unattacked."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["What seest thou in me, York? why dost thou pause?"],"id":967,"m":"What do you see in me, York? Why do you pause?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["With thy brave bearing should I be in love","But that thou art so fast mine enemy."],"id":968,"m":"I'd fall in love with your brave bearing if you weren't so firmly my enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Nor should thy prowess want praise and esteem","But that 't is shown ignobly and in treason."],"id":969,"m":"And your courage would deserve praise and respect if it weren't shown dishonourably, in treason."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["So let it help me now against thy sword","As I in justice and true right express it!"],"id":970,"m":"Then let it help me now against your sword, as surely as I show it for justice and true right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["My soul and body on the action both!"],"id":971,"m":"I stake my soul and body on this fight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["A dreadful lay!—Address thee instantly."],"id":972,"m":"A dreadful bet! Get ready at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight, and Clifford falls.","id":973},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["La fin couronne les oeuvres."],"id":974,"m":"The end crowns the work."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":975},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Thus war hath given thee peace, for thou art still.","Peace with his soul, heaven, if it be thy will!"],"id":976,"m":"So war has given you peace, for you are still. Heaven, give peace to his soul, if it's your will!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":977},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter young CLIFFORD.","id":978},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","lines":["Shame and confusion! all is on the rout;","Fear frames disorder, and disorder wounds","Where it should guard.—O war, thou son of hell,","Whom angry heavens do make their minister,","Throw in the frozen bosoms of our part","Hot coals of vengeance!—Let no soldier fly.","He that is truly dedicate to war","Hath no self-love; nor he that loves himself","Hath not essentially but by circumstance","The name of valour.—[Seeing his dead father]","O, let the vile world end,","And the premised flames of the last day","Knit earth and heaven together!","Now let the general trumpet blow his blast,","Particularities and petty sounds","To cease!—Wast thou ordain'd, dear father,","To lose thy youth in peace, and to achieve","The silver livery of advised age,","And in thy reverence and thy chair-days, thus","To die in ruffian battle?—Even at this sight","My heart is turn'd to stone; and while 't is mine","It shall be stony. York not our old men spares;","No more will I their babes; tears virginal","Shall be to me even as the dew to fire,","And beauty that the tyrant oft reclaims","Shall to my flaming wrath be oil and flax.","Henceforth I will not have to do with pity;","Meet I an infant of the house of York,","Into as many gobbets will I cut it","As wild Medea young Absyrtus did.","In cruelty will I seek out my fame.—","Come, thou new ruin of old Clifford's house:","As did Aeneas old Anchises bear,","So bear I thee upon my manly shoulders;","But then Aeneas bare a living load,","Nothing so heavy as these woes of mine."],"id":979,"m":"Shame and confusion! Everyone is running away. Fear creates disorder, and disorder wounds where it should protect. O war, son of hell, whom the angry heavens use as their servant, throw hot coals of vengeance into the frozen hearts of our side! Let no soldier run away. A man truly devoted to war has no love for himself; and a man who loves himself is not truly brave, only brave by accident. (Seeing his dead father) Oh, let the vile world end, and the promised flames of Judgement Day weld earth and heaven together! Now let the last trumpet blow, and all small, individual sounds stop! Were you destined, dear father, to spend your youth in peace, and to reach the silver uniform of wise old age, and then, in your venerable armchair years, to die like this in a brutal battle? At this sight my heart has turned to stone, and while it's mine it will stay stone. York doesn't spare our old men; no more will I spare their babies. Girls' tears will be to me like dew on fire, and beauty, which often softens a tyrant, will be oil and flax to my flaming anger. From now on I'll have nothing to do with pity. If I meet a baby of the house of York, I'll cut it into as many lumps as wild Medea cut young Absyrtus. I'll seek my fame in cruelty. Come, you new ruin of old Clifford's house. As Aeneas carried old Anchises, so I carry you on my manly shoulders. But Aeneas carried a living load, nothing as heavy as my grief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, bearing off his father. Enter RICHARD and SOMERSET to fight. SOMERSET is killed.","id":980},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["So, lie thou there;","For underneath an alehouse' paltry sign,","The Castle in Saint Alban's, Somerset","Hath made the wizard famous in his death.","Sword, hold thy temper; heart, be wrathful still;","Priests pray for enemies, but princes kill."],"id":981,"m":"So, lie there. For under a cheap pub sign, The Castle in St Albans, Somerset has made the wizard famous by his death. Sword, keep your edge; heart, stay angry; priests pray for enemies, but princes kill."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":982},{"t":"sd","text":"Fight: excursions. Enter KING, QUEEN, and others.","id":983},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Away, my lord! you are slow; for shame, away!"],"id":984,"m":"Away, my lord! You're slow. For shame, away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Can we outrun the heavens? good Margaret, stay."],"id":985,"m":"Can we outrun the heavens? Good Margaret, stop."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What are you made of? you'll nor fight nor fly;","Now is it manhood, wisdom, and defence,","To give the enemy way, and to secure us","By what we can, which can no more but fly."],"id":986,"m":"What are you made of? You'll neither fight nor run. Now it's manly, wise and the best defence to give way to the enemy and save ourselves however we can, and the only way is to run."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum afar off.","id":987},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["If you be ta'en, we then should see the bottom","Of all our fortunes; but if we haply scape,","As well we may, if not through your neglect,","We shall to London get, where you are lov'd,","And where this breach now in our fortunes made","May readily be stopp'd."],"id":988,"m":"If you're captured, we'll have hit the bottom of all our fortunes; but if we escape, as we may if you don't neglect it, we'll get to London, where you are loved, and where this break in our fortunes can be quickly mended."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter young CLIFFORD.","id":989},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","lines":["But that my heart's on future mischief set,","I would speak blasphemy ere bid you fly.","But fly you must; uncurable discomfit","Reigns in the hearts of all our present parts.","Away, for your relief! and we will live","To see their day and them our fortune give.","Away, my lord, away!"],"id":990,"m":"If my heart weren't set on future harm to them, I'd speak blasphemy rather than tell you to run. But run you must; hopeless defeat fills the hearts of everyone still on our side. Away, to save yourselves! We'll live to see them have their day and give them our bad fortune. Away, my lord, away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":991},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Fields near Saint Alban's.","id":992},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Retreat. Enter YORK, RICHARD, WARWICK, and Soldiers, with drum and colours.","id":993},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Of Salisbury, who can report of him,","That winter lion, who in rage forgets","Aged contusions and all brush of time","And, like a gallant in the brow of youth,","Repairs him with occasion? This happy day","Is not itself, nor have we won one foot,","If Salisbury be lost."],"id":994,"m":"Who can give news of Salisbury, that winter lion, who in his fury forgets the bruises of age and all the wear of time and, like a gallant young man in his prime, renews his strength with the occasion? This happy day isn't happy, and we haven't won a foot of ground, if Salisbury is lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["My noble father,","Three times to-day I holp him to his horse,","Three times bestrid him; thrice I led him off,","Persuaded him from any further act;","But still, where danger was, still there I met him;","And like rich hangings in a homely house,","So was his will in his old feeble body.","But, noble as he is, look where he comes."],"id":995,"m":"My noble father, three times today I helped him back on his horse, three times stood over him when he was down, three times led him off and persuaded him to fight no more. But wherever there was danger, there I met him again; and like rich tapestries in a plain house, so was his will in his old, weak body. But, noble as he is, look, here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SALISBURY.","id":996},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Now, by my sword, well hast thou fought to-day;","By the mass, so did we all.—I thank you, Richard;","God knows how long it is I have to live,","And it hath pleas'd him that three times to-day","You have defended me from imminent death.—","Well, lords, we have not got that which we have;","'T is not enough our foes are this time fled,","Being opposites of such repairing nature."],"id":997,"m":"Now, by my sword, you fought well today; by the mass, so did we all. Thank you, Richard. God knows how long I have left to live, and it has pleased him that three times today you saved me from certain death. Well, lords, we haven't secured what we've won. It's not enough that our enemies have run away this time, since they're the kind of opponents who recover quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I know our safety is to follow them;","For, as I hear, the king is fled to London,","To call a present court of parliament.","Let us pursue him ere the writs go forth.—","What says Lord Warwick? shall we after them?"],"id":998,"m":"I know our safety lies in following them; for, as I hear, the king has fled to London to call a parliament at once. Let's chase him before the summonses go out. What does Lord Warwick say? Shall we go after them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["After them! nay, before them, if we can.","Now, by my hand, lords, 'twas a glorious day;","Saint Alban's battle won by famous York","Shall be eterniz'd in all age to come.—","Sound drums and trumpets!—and to London all;","And more such days as these to us befall!"],"id":999,"m":"After them? No, ahead of them, if we can. Now, by my hand, lords, it was a glorious day. The battle of St Albans, won by famous York, will be remembered for all time to come. Sound the drums and trumpets! And everyone to London; and may more days like this come to us!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1000}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"King Henry VI","group":"The court","desc":"Son of Henry the Fifth, now grown, newly married and still ruled by others. He is pious, gentle and quick to weep, prefers prayer to politics, and trusts everyone. He welcomes Margaret with joy though the marriage costs Anjou and Maine, lets his lords strip Gloucester of office, and cannot save the uncle he believes innocent: 'Who's a traitor? Gloucester he is none.' He banishes Suffolk only when the commons demand it, pardons Cade's followers, and wishes he were a subject rather than a king. Faced with York's open challenge he can only call for others to fight, and Margaret has to drag him from St Albans."},{"name":"Queen Margaret","group":"The court","desc":"Daughter of the poor King Reignier, married to Henry by Suffolk's proxy in exchange for Anjou and Maine. She is disappointed by her saintly husband and resents Gloucester's power and his wife's pride, boxing the Duchess's ear in open court. She leads the case against Gloucester and agrees to his murder. Her love for Suffolk is open in their farewell scene, and she later carries his severed head. By the last act she is the strongest will on the Lancastrian side, urging the King to flee and save the crown."},{"name":"Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester (Gloster)","group":"The court","desc":"The King's uncle and Lord Protector, loved by the commons as 'the good Duke Humphrey'. He nearly faints reading the marriage treaty, exposes the false miracle of Simpcox, and judges fairly. He trusts his innocence to protect him: 'A heart unspotted is not easily daunted.' His wife's conjuring gives his enemies their chance; he gives up his staff, is arrested for treason at Bury and murdered in his bed before his trial. His death turns the commons against Suffolk."},{"name":"Richard Plantagenet, Duke of York","group":"The Yorkists","desc":"The play's master plotter. Alone at the end of 1.1 he claims the crown as his right and resolves to wait. He sets out his descent from Edward the Third's third son to Salisbury and Warwick, who kneel to him as king. He joins the plot to kill Gloucester, takes the army offered him for Ireland, and sets up Jack Cade as a Mortimer to test the people. He returns with an army demanding Somerset's arrest, and when he sees Somerset free he openly denies Henry's right to rule. He kills Clifford at St Albans and wins the first battle of the Wars of the Roses."},{"name":"William de la Pole, Duke of Suffolk","group":"The court","desc":"He brings Margaret to England, is made a duke for it, and becomes her lover and chief adviser. He traps the Duchess through Hume, arrests Gloucester, and arranges his murder. Warwick exposes the killing and the commons force his banishment. In a passionate farewell with Margaret he says a wilderness with her would be populous enough. Captured at sea by pirates, he scorns them as low men, and dies by 'water' (Walter Whitmore) as the spirit foretold."},{"name":"Cardinal Beaufort","group":"The court","desc":"Great-uncle to the King and Gloucester's old enemy, a churchman with a soldier's temper. He challenges Gloucester to a duel while hawking, mocks him over the Duchess's arrest, and offers to provide Gloucester's murderer. After the murder he falls suddenly ill and dies raving, seeing Gloucester's ghost and asking for poison. The King asks for a sign of hope: 'He dies, and makes no sign.'"},{"name":"Eleanor, Duchess of Gloucester","group":"The court","desc":"Gloucester's ambitious wife, who dreams of being crowned and urges her husband to reach for the crown. She hires the witch Margery Jourdain and the conjurer Bolingbroke through Hume to raise a spirit about the King's future, and is arrested in the act by York and Buckingham. After three days of public penance in a white sheet she is banished to the Isle of Man, warning her husband that his enemies have set traps for him."},{"name":"Jack Cade","group":"The rebels","desc":"A Kentishman set up by York to claim to be John Mortimer. He leads a rising of craftsmen and labourers, promising cheap bread, no money and the death of lawyers. He kills the Staffords, enters London, strikes London Stone, executes Lord Say for setting up a grammar school, and orders the records burned. When Clifford speaks of Henry the Fifth his followers desert him. Starving after five days in hiding, he is killed in Alexander Iden's garden, defiant to the end."},{"name":"Earl of Warwick","group":"The Yorkists","desc":"Salisbury's son, a soldier who won Anjou and Maine and weeps to see them given away. He kneels to York as king, and his examination of Gloucester's body proves murder. He quarrels with Suffolk before the King, and by the last act stands with York as the 'bear' of the Neville badge. He ends the play urging the march on London."},{"name":"Earl of Salisbury","group":"The Yorkists","desc":"Warwick's father, an old lord who first sides with Gloucester for the public good. Convinced by York's pedigree, he kneels to him as king, carries the commons' demand for Suffolk's banishment, and at the end argues that it is a greater sin to keep a sinful oath than to break it. Richard saves him three times at St Albans."},{"name":"Duke of Buckingham","group":"The court","desc":"One of the lords against Gloucester. He spies on the Duchess, arrests her with York, and brings the news to St Albans. Later he goes with Clifford to offer the King's pardon to Cade's rebels, and is the King's messenger to York's army."},{"name":"Duke of Somerset","group":"The court","desc":"York's old rival. He is made Regent of France in York's place and returns to report that all is lost. York's army demands his arrest, and when he appears free at the Queen's side York breaks openly with the King. He is killed at St Albans by Richard under an alehouse sign of a castle, fulfilling the spirit's warning to 'shun castles'."},{"name":"Lord Clifford and Young Clifford","group":"The Lancastrians","desc":"Old Clifford wins Cade's rebels back to the King by speaking of Henry the Fifth, and stays loyal when York claims the crown. York kills him at St Albans. His son finds the body and swears to show no pity to the house of York, carrying his father away like Aeneas carrying Anchises."},{"name":"Richard and Edward","group":"The Yorkists","desc":"York's sons, who come to stand bail for their father. Richard, mocked by Clifford as a crooked 'foul stigmatic', kills Somerset at St Albans and rescues Salisbury three times. Both will be kings in the plays that follow."},{"name":"Lord Say","group":"The court","desc":"The King's treasurer, blamed by the rebels for the loss of Maine and for taxes. He refuses to flee with the King, trusting his innocence. Captured by Cade, he defends learning ('ignorance is the curse of God') and is beheaded, his head carried on a pole to kiss his son-in-law's."},{"name":"Alexander Iden","group":"The commons","desc":"A contented Kentish squire who wants no more than his small inheritance. He fights the starving Cade in his garden, kills him, and brings the head to the King, who knights him."},{"name":"Hume, Bolingbroke, Margery Jourdain and Southwell","group":"The conjurers","desc":"Hume, the Duchess's chaplain, takes her gold to bring a witch and conjurer, and Suffolk's and the Cardinal's gold to trap her. Bolingbroke and the witch Jourdain raise a spirit that gives three riddling prophecies: a duke still lives that Henry shall depose; Suffolk will die by water; Somerset should shun castles. All are condemned to death."},{"name":"Peter Thump and Thomas Horner","group":"The commons","desc":"An apprentice accuses his master, the armourer Horner, of saying that York is the rightful heir. They fight a trial by combat with sandbags; Horner, drunk, is struck down and dies confessing treason."},{"name":"Simpcox and his wife","group":"The commons","desc":"A beggar who claims St Alban cured his lifelong blindness. Gloucester proves him a fraud by asking him to name colours, then 'cures' his lameness with a whip."},{"name":"The Captain and Walter Whitmore","group":"The commons","desc":"Seamen who capture Suffolk in the Channel. The Captain lists Suffolk's crimes against England; Whitmore, who lost an eye in the fight, beheads him."},{"name":"Dick the Butcher, Smith the Weaver and Cade's followers","group":"The rebels","desc":"Cade's men mock his claims in asides while cheering him on. Dick's 'let's kill all the lawyers' and the hanging of the Clerk of Chatham for being able to write show the rising's hatred of learning and law."},{"name":"Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother","group":"The court","desc":"The King's commanders sent against Cade at Blackheath. They call the rebels hinds and scum, accuse Cade of being York's puppet, and are both killed."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Suffolk presents Margaret, whom he has married in Henry's name at Tours. Reading the treaty, Gloucester finds that Anjou and Maine are to be given to her father and that she brings no dowry; he breaks off and the Cardinal reads on. Henry makes Suffolk a duke and leaves. Gloucester laments the loss of Henry the Fifth's conquests, quarrels with the Cardinal and leaves. The Cardinal, Buckingham and Somerset plot against Gloucester and against each other, while Salisbury and Warwick vow to support Gloucester and York for the public good. Alone, York reveals that he means to claim the crown when the time is right.","key":["The marriage that costs Anjou and Maine sets every lord against Suffolk and the new Queen.","The court splits into self-seeking factions while Gloucester stands for the public good.","York's soliloquy reveals the ambition that drives the whole play."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the marriage treaty give away?","o":["Normandy and Paris","Anjou and Maine","Calais and Guienne","Gascony and Bordeaux"],"a":1},{"q":"Who reads the treaty after Gloucester stops?","o":["Suffolk","York","The Cardinal","Somerset"],"a":2},{"q":"What does York vow at the end of the scene?","o":["To go back to France at once and win back Anjou and Maine","To claim the crown when the time is right","To marry Margaret","To kill Suffolk"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"The Duchess urges Gloucester to reach for the crown. He tells his dream that his staff of office was broken and the heads of Somerset and Suffolk set on the pieces; she tells hers, that she was crowned in Westminster Abbey. He rebukes her. When he leaves for St Albans she sends for Hume, who has arranged for a witch and a conjurer to raise a spirit for her. Alone, Hume reveals that the Cardinal and Suffolk have paid him to lure the Duchess into this trap.","key":["The two dreams set Gloucester's loyalty against his wife's ambition.","The Duchess is willing to act 'in Fortune's pageant' where her husband will not.","Hume is a double agent: the conjuring is a trap set by Suffolk and the Cardinal."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Gloucester dream?","o":["That he is crowned king in Westminster Abbey by the Cardinal himself","That his staff is broken and two heads set on it","That Margaret dies","That France is won back"],"a":1},{"q":"Who has paid Hume to undermine the Duchess?","o":["York and Warwick, his own friends","The Cardinal and Suffolk","King Henry himself, in secret","Buckingham alone, for money"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Gloucester summoned to ride?","o":["Bury","Westminster","St Albans","Kenilworth"],"a":2}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"Petitioners waiting for the Protector mistake Suffolk for him. Peter accuses his master Horner of saying York is the rightful heir; the Queen tears up the other petitions. Margaret complains to Suffolk of her pious husband and of the Duchess's pride, and Suffolk reveals he has set a trap for her. In council York and Somerset dispute the regency of France and the lords accuse Gloucester; the Queen boxes the Duchess's ear. Gloucester returns and names York regent, but when Horner and Peter are brought in he gives the post to Somerset and orders a trial by combat.","key":["Margaret despises Henry's holiness and wants power for herself.","The slap shows the open hatred between the Queen and the Duchess.","Peter's charge damages York and costs him the regency."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Peter accuse Horner of saying?","o":["That the King is mad","That York is the rightful heir","That Gloucester is a traitor","That Margaret is a witch"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does the Queen strike?","o":["Suffolk, by mistake","Eleanor, the Duchess","Peter, the armourer's man","A petitioner"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is finally made Regent of France?","o":["York","Somerset","Warwick","Buckingham"],"a":1}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"In Gloucester's garden, at night, Bolingbroke and the witch Jourdain raise a spirit while the Duchess watches from above. It answers three questions: the duke who will depose Henry still lives, but Henry will outlive him; Suffolk will die by water; Somerset should shun castles. York and Buckingham burst in and arrest them all. York reads the prophecies, calls them riddles, and sends Buckingham to carry the news to St Albans.","key":["The spirit's prophecies are riddling, and each comes true in an unexpected way.","York and Buckingham catch the Duchess in the act, just as Hume planned.","York compares the answers to an ancient oracle that can be read both ways."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the spirit's warning about Suffolk?","o":["He will die in battle","He will die by water","He will die in a castle","He will die old"],"a":1},{"q":"Who arrests the conjurers?","o":["York and Buckingham","Suffolk and the Queen","Warwick and Salisbury","The Cardinal"],"a":0},{"q":"Where should Somerset avoid, according to the spirit?","o":["The sea","Castles","London","France"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Hawking at St Albans, Gloucester and the Cardinal trade insults and secretly agree to fight a duel that evening. A townsman announces a miracle: Simpcox, blind from birth, has been given sight at St Alban's shrine. Gloucester tests him: Simpcox can name colours at once, which no man just given sight could do, and his 'lameness' disappears when the beadle whips him. Buckingham arrives with news of the Duchess's arrest. The Cardinal gloats; Gloucester, grief-stricken, says he will cast her off if she is guilty. Henry sets the trial for London.","key":["Gloucester's wise judgement of Simpcox shows why the commons love him.","Henry is ready to believe any miracle; Gloucester tests the claim.","The news of the Duchess's arrest breaks Gloucester's strength at the height of his authority."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Gloucester expose Simpcox?","o":["He asks him to read a page from his own prayer book","He asks him to name colours","He tests his hearing","He asks about St Alban"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Gloucester and the Cardinal secretly agree?","o":["To make peace at last","To fight a duel","To kill York before he can rise","To leave the court"],"a":1},{"q":"Who brings news of the Duchess's arrest?","o":["York","Suffolk","Buckingham","A messenger"],"a":2}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"In his garden York explains his claim to Salisbury and Warwick. Edward the Third had seven sons; Henry descends from the fourth, John of Gaunt, and York, through his mother, from the third, Lionel of Clarence. Convinced, Salisbury and Warwick kneel to him as king. York tells them to wait until the lords have destroyed Gloucester, which will bring their own downfall. Warwick swears to make York king.","key":["York's genealogy turns his ambition into a legal claim.","The Nevilles (Salisbury and Warwick) become York's chief allies.","York predicts that Gloucester's enemies will destroy themselves."],"quiz":[{"q":"From which son of Edward III does York claim descent?","o":["The second, William","The third, Lionel","The fourth, John of Gaunt","The fifth, Edmund"],"a":1},{"q":"Who kneels to York as king?","o":["Somerset and Buckingham","Salisbury and Warwick","Suffolk and the Cardinal","Edward and Richard"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does York say the lords must snare first?","o":["The King","Duke Humphrey","Somerset","The Queen"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"The King sentences the conjurers to death and the Duchess to three days' penance and banishment to the Isle of Man. Gloucester accepts the law, and Henry asks him to give up his staff as Protector; he does so with dignity. Margaret rejoices. Peter and the drunken Horner fight with sandbags; Peter strikes Horner down, and Horner dies confessing treason. Henry sees God's justice in the result.","key":["Gloucester loses wife and office in one blow.","Margaret takes the staff as a sign that Henry, and she, now rule.","The comic trial by combat ends in a confession of treason, and Henry takes it as a sign from God."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the Duchess's punishment?","o":["Prison in the Tower of London for the rest of her life","Penance and banishment to the Isle of Man","Death","Exile to France"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Gloucester give up?","o":["His sword and armour","His staff of office","His lands and his title","His title as Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the combat end?","o":["Peter is killed by his master with a sandbag","Horner is struck down and confesses","Both are pardoned by the King","The King stops it before a blow is struck"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Gloucester waits in the street, in mourning, to see his wife do penance. She passes barefoot in a white sheet with a taper, mocked by the crowd. She warns him that Suffolk, the Queen, York and the Cardinal have set traps for him; he replies that he is safe as long as he is loyal and innocent. A herald summons him to a parliament at Bury. He leaves her in Stanley's charge, and she goes to her exile saying her shame will never come off with her sheet.","key":["The public penance is a spectacle of a fallen great lady.","Eleanor sees the danger clearly; Gloucester trusts too much in innocence.","The summons to Bury, decided without him, signals his fall."],"quiz":[{"q":"How is the Duchess dressed for her penance?","o":["In black","In a white sheet with a taper","In her finest robes","In chains"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Gloucester believe he is safe?","o":["The King loves him like a father","He is loyal and innocent","He has an army","Warwick protects him"],"a":1},{"q":"Who takes the Duchess to the Isle of Man?","o":["The Sheriff of London","Sir John Stanley","Hume, the priest","Buckingham"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"At the parliament at Bury, Margaret, Suffolk, the Cardinal, York and Buckingham accuse the absent Gloucester. Henry defends him as innocent as a lamb. Somerset reports that all France is lost. Gloucester arrives and Suffolk arrests him for treason; he answers each charge and warns Henry that his death is only the prologue to their play. Henry leaves in tears. The Queen, Cardinal, Suffolk and York agree that Gloucester must die. News of an Irish rebellion lets them send York with an army. Alone, York reveals he will use the army, and has set up Jack Cade to stir up rebellion in England as John Mortimer.","key":["Gloucester's speech names each of his enemies and foresees the chaos to come.","Henry knows Gloucester is innocent but cannot protect him.","York gets the army he needed and reveals the Cade plot."],"quiz":[{"q":"What news does Somerset bring from France?","o":["Victory","All is lost","A truce","Charles is dead"],"a":1},{"q":"Who arrests Gloucester?","o":["York","Suffolk","Buckingham","The Cardinal"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom has York hired to stir up trouble in England?","o":["Hume","Jack Cade","Dick the Butcher","Whitmore"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Murderers report to Suffolk that Gloucester is dead. Suffolk tells the court and Henry faints; when he revives he turns on Suffolk. Margaret, in a long speech, accuses Henry of loving Gloucester more than her. Warwick and Salisbury arrive with angry commons; Warwick views the body and proves murder. Suffolk and Warwick quarrel and draw swords. Salisbury brings the commons' demand that Suffolk be executed or banished, and Henry banishes him. Margaret and Suffolk part with passionate farewells. Vaux reports that the Cardinal is dying.","key":["Warwick's evidence from the body is the play's clearest proof of murder.","The commons, not the King, force Suffolk's fall.","The parting of Margaret and Suffolk is the play's great love scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Warwick use to prove murder?","o":["A confession signed by the two murderers","Signs on Gloucester's body","A letter in Suffolk's hand","The murderers' testimony before the lords"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the commons demand?","o":["Gloucester's burial","Suffolk's death or banishment","York's return","The Queen's exile"],"a":1},{"q":"How long does Henry give Suffolk to leave?","o":["One whole day","Three days","A week","A month"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Cardinal Beaufort dies raving in his bed, before the King, Salisbury and Warwick. He offers Death all England's treasure, imagines he is on trial, sees Gloucester's ghost with hair standing on end and asks for the poison he bought. Henry asks him to lift his hand as a sign of hope; he dies and makes no sign.","key":["The Cardinal's guilty conscience shows itself in delirium.","Henry prays for him and forbids judgement.","'So bad a death argues a monstrous life.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Cardinal offer Death?","o":["His soul","England's treasure","His crown","His lands in France"],"a":1},{"q":"What sign does Henry ask for?","o":["A word of repentance","A raised hand","A smile","A prayer"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Cardinal ask the apothecary for?","o":["Medicine for his fever","The strong poison he bought","Water","A priest"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Off the Kent coast, pirates divide their prisoners for ransom. Suffolk, in disguise, is given to Walter Whitmore, who lost an eye in the fight and wants revenge. Suffolk starts at the name, remembering the prophecy of death by water. He reveals himself and scorns them as low men; the Captain answers with a list of his crimes against England. Suffolk refuses to beg, compares himself to great men killed by low ones, and is led off and beheaded. A gentleman carries the body to the King.","key":["'Walter' sounds like 'water': the prophecy comes true.","The Captain speaks for the whole country against Suffolk.","Suffolk dies proud, refusing to plead with commoners."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Whitmore's name frighten Suffolk?","o":["It is his enemy's name","It sounds like 'water'","It is French","It is a witch's name"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Suffolk die?","o":["Drowned at sea","Beheaded","Hanged from the mast","Shot with arrows"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Suffolk refuse to do?","o":["Pay ransom","Beg for his life","Name himself","Pray"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Bevis and Holland await Jack Cade's rising. Cade claims to be a Mortimer while Dick and Smith mock his lowly origins in asides. He promises cheap bread, no money and one livery for all, and Dick proposes killing all the lawyers. The Clerk of Chatham is hanged for being able to write his name. Cade knights himself to face Sir Humphrey Stafford, who calls the rebels scum and offers pardon. Cade invents a story of a Mortimer twin stolen at birth, and the rebels vow to have Lord Say's head for the loss of Maine.","key":["Cade's speech is a comic and frightening fantasy of a world turned upside down.","The rebels hate learning, law and writing.","Stafford's brother guesses that York is behind the rising."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Dick propose?","o":["Burning London to the ground","Killing all the lawyers","Crowning York","Freeing the prisoners"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is the Clerk of Chatham hanged?","o":["He stole a sheep","He can write his name","He is French","He spied"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Cade claim as his father?","o":["Henry the Fifth","A Mortimer","The Duke of York","Jack Straw"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"The Staffords are killed in the battle. Cade rewards Dick with a licence to kill meat, puts on Sir Humphrey's armour, and orders the bodies dragged to London behind his horse. The rebels plan to open the jails.","key":["The rebels win their first battle.","Cade dresses himself in his dead enemy's armour.","The rising grows more violent."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who are killed in this scene?","o":["Say and Cromer","The two Staffords","Clifford and Buckingham","Goffe and Scales"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cade wear?","o":["A crown of paper and straw","Sir Humphrey's armour","Lord Say's scarlet gown","A butcher's apron"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Cade march next?","o":["Kent","London","Kenilworth","Ireland"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Margaret mourns over Suffolk's head while Henry reads the rebels' petition and hopes to talk with Cade. Messengers report that the rebels hold Southwark and then London Bridge. Henry retreats to Kenilworth; Lord Say, whom the rebels hate, stays hidden in London.","key":["Margaret's open grief for Suffolk embarrasses the King.","Henry prefers negotiation to bloodshed.","The rebels' advance drives the court from London."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Margaret carry?","o":["A letter","Suffolk's head","A crown","A petition"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the King go?","o":["Bury","Kenilworth","The Tower","Westminster"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Say stay in London?","o":["To fight the rebels at London Bridge with the Mayor and his men","The rebels hate him and would endanger the King","To meet Cade","He is ill"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"Lord Scales, defending the Tower, hears that the rebels have taken the bridge. He sends the citizens to Smithfield, promising to send Matthew Goffe.","key":["London's defence depends on the Tower.","The rebels kill all who resist.","Scales organises resistance at Smithfield."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Scales?","o":["On the Tower","At Westminster","At Smithfield","At Kenilworth"],"a":0},{"q":"Whom does he send to Smithfield?","o":["Matthew Goffe","Alexander Iden","Stafford","Clifford"],"a":0},{"q":"What have the rebels won?","o":["The Tower of London","London Bridge","Westminster Hall","Smithfield"],"a":1}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"Cade strikes London Stone, declares himself lord of the city and orders that anyone calling him Jack Cade be treated as a traitor. A soldier who does so is killed. Cade orders London Bridge burned.","key":["Cade's claim to rule is a mock coronation.","Anyone who uses his real name dies.","Violence becomes casual."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Cade strike with his staff?","o":["The Tower gate","London Stone","A church bell","The bridge"],"a":1},{"q":"What will the conduit run with?","o":["Ale","Claret wine","Milk","Water"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is the soldier killed?","o":["He deserts from Cade's army in Southwark","He calls him Jack Cade","He steals","He is a lawyer"],"a":1}]},"a4s7":{"sum":"After killing Matthew Goffe at Smithfield, Cade orders the Savoy and the Inns of Court destroyed and the records of the realm burned: his mouth shall be the parliament. Lord Say is brought in and charged with selling Maine, building a grammar school and using printing. Say defends his record and learning, and almost moves Cade, but is beheaded with his son-in-law, and their heads are made to kiss on poles.","key":["Cade's charges against Say turn learning into a crime.","Say's speech defends knowledge as 'the wing wherewith we fly to heaven'.","The kissing heads are the rising's most savage image."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is NOT one of Cade's charges against Say?","o":["Building a grammar school","Using printing","Speaking French","Killing Gloucester"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Cade order burned?","o":["The Tower of London","The records of the realm","The churches","The palace"],"a":1},{"q":"Whose head is set beside Say's?","o":["Humphrey Stafford's","Sir James Cromer's","Suffolk's","Matthew Goffe's"],"a":1}]},"a4s8":{"sum":"In Southwark, Buckingham and Clifford offer the King's pardon. The rebels shout for the King; Cade calls them cowards and they shout for him; Clifford reminds them of Henry the Fifth and the French threat, and they turn again. Cade, deserted, cuts his way out and flees. Buckingham offers a thousand crowns for his head.","key":["The crowd swings like a feather in the wind.","The name of Henry the Fifth still commands loyalty.","Cade's power collapses as fast as it grew."],"quiz":[{"q":"What wins the rebels back to the King?","o":["Money","Clifford's appeal to Henry the Fifth's memory","Threats","A miracle"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cade compare the crowd to?","o":["A flock of sheep that follow any shepherd","A feather blown to and fro","A tide that turns twice a day","A hungry wolf"],"a":1},{"q":"What reward is offered for Cade's head?","o":["A knighthood","A thousand crowns","A dukedom","Land in Kent"],"a":1}]},"a4s9":{"sum":"At Kenilworth Henry wishes he were a subject. Cade's followers come with halters round their necks and are pardoned. Then news comes that York has returned from Ireland with an army, demanding Somerset's removal. Henry sends Somerset to the Tower for the moment and Buckingham to meet York.","key":["Henry is at his best in mercy.","York's army arrives just as Cade's is scattered.","The King gives way to York's demand."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the rebels wear round their necks?","o":["Chains","Halters","Roses","Crosses"],"a":1},{"q":"What does York demand?","o":["The crown of England for himself","Somerset's removal","Suffolk's head on a pole","Money for his army"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry say he longs to be?","o":["A soldier","A subject","A priest","A scholar"],"a":1}]},"a4s10":{"sum":"Cade, starving after five days in hiding, climbs into a Kentish garden to eat grass. The owner, Alexander Iden, praises his quiet life. Cade, recognising danger, challenges him; Iden refuses to fight at unfair odds but is forced to. Cade is killed, blaming famine. Iden resolves to take his head to the King.","key":["Iden's contentment contrasts with the play's ambitious lords.","Cade dies boasting he was beaten by hunger, not courage.","The rebel is killed by a loyal subject on his own land."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Cade enter the garden?","o":["To hide","To eat","To meet York","To steal gold"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Iden value?","o":["Fame","A quiet life on his small estate","The King's favour","Money"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cade say killed him?","o":["Iden's courage","Famine","York's betrayal","Witchcraft"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"York arrives with his Irish army, claiming in an aside that the crown is his. He tells Buckingham he only wants Somerset arrested, and disbands his army when told Somerset is in the Tower. Iden brings Cade's head and is knighted. Margaret enters with Somerset free; York openly calls Henry no king. Somerset arrests him; York calls his sons and the Nevilles. Clifford and the Queen call them traitors; Salisbury argues it is a sin to keep a sinful oath. Both sides arm, with insults between Richard and the Cliffords.","key":["York's patience runs out when he sees Somerset free.","Salisbury and Warwick openly break their oath to Henry.","The quarrel becomes civil war, with sons drawn in on both sides."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does York disband his army?","o":["Henry orders it, on pain of being named a traitor","He is told Somerset is in the Tower","Cade is dead and the danger past","He has no money to pay his soldiers"],"a":1},{"q":"What makes York break openly with Henry?","o":["Seeing Somerset free","Iden's knighthood","Margaret's insult","Clifford's arrival"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Clifford insult Richard?","o":["As a 'foul stigmatic' and 'indigested lump'","As a coward who hides behind his father's sword","As a Frenchman by his looks and speech","As a bastard born of a soldier"],"a":0}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"The first battle of St Albans. Warwick seeks Clifford, but York fights and kills him. Young Clifford finds his father's body, vows to show no pity to the house of York, and carries the body away. Richard kills Somerset under an alehouse sign of a castle. Margaret urges the reluctant Henry to flee to London, and Young Clifford agrees.","key":["York kills Clifford after an exchange of honour.","Young Clifford's vow of cruelty looks ahead to the next play.","The spirit's prophecy about castles is fulfilled."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who kills old Clifford?","o":["Warwick","York","Richard","Salisbury"],"a":1},{"q":"Who kills Somerset?","o":["York","Warwick","Richard","Edward"],"a":2},{"q":"Where does Margaret urge Henry to flee?","o":["France","London","Kenilworth","Scotland"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"After the battle York asks after Salisbury; Richard has saved him three times. Salisbury arrives and thanks him but warns that the enemy has fled to fight again. York learns the King has gone to London to call a parliament, and Warwick urges them to get there first. Warwick says the battle of St Albans will be remembered for ever.","key":["The Yorkists win the battle but not the war.","Richard is shown as a fierce and loyal fighter.","The play ends with the race to London and more war to come."],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times did Richard save Salisbury?","o":["Once","Twice","Three times","Four times"],"a":2},{"q":"Where has the King gone?","o":["To France","To London","To Scotland","To Bury"],"a":1},{"q":"Whose victory does Warwick say will be remembered?","o":["Richard's","York's","Salisbury's","Clifford's"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"Suffolk married Margaret of Anjou as Henry's proxy at Tours in 1444. In 1 Henry VI he won her for the King while hoping to rule through her.","3":"Henry VI was born in 1421, so he is in his twenties here. The marriage took place in 1445.","4":"Margaret speaks in formal court verse; her later speeches are much sharper.","10":"The treaty's terms are taken from the chronicles: Anjou and Maine went to Margaret's father, Reignier (René), titular King of Naples, Sicily and Jerusalem, and she came without dowry.","18":"Gloucester recalls the work of his brothers Henry the Fifth and John, Duke of Bedford, whose deaths are shown in 1 Henry VI.","19":"'Peroration' is a term from rhetoric for the closing part of a speech; the Cardinal mocks Gloucester as an orator.","24":"A 'fifteenth' was a tax of one fifteenth of the value of moveable goods, granted by Parliament.","28":"The Cardinal is Henry Beaufort, son of John of Gaunt, and so great-uncle to the King and half-uncle to Gloucester.","32":"Each plotter hopes to be Protector himself: the alliance against Gloucester is already divided.","35":"Salisbury is Richard Neville; his son Warwick (also Richard Neville) is the 'Kingmaker' of the next play. York married Salisbury's sister, so Salisbury calls him 'brother'.","39":"Warwick puns on 'main' (the chief point, or the main chance in dice) and the province of Maine.","41":"York's first soliloquy. The 'fatal brand' is from Greek myth: Althaea burned the log on which her son Meleager's life depended.","47":"Dreams were widely believed to foretell the future, especially the deaths of great men.","48":"The white staff was the badge of the Protector's office. Gloucester's dream foretells his fall; the heads of Somerset and Suffolk foretell their deaths.","67":"Hume is a priest (Sir John was a courtesy title for a cleric). The historical Eleanor Cobham was tried for witchcraft in 1441, before Margaret came to England.","71":"Petitioners hoped to hand written complaints to the Protector as he passed; Gloucester's popularity shows in their trust.","80":"Enclosing common land, fencing it off for private use, was a constant grievance of the poor in Shakespeare's time.","92":"Margaret's mockery of Henry's piety draws on chronicles that described him as more fit for a cloister than a throne.","94":"Margaret lists every rival for power; she sees the court as enemies to be cleared.","99":"The regency of France had been York's; Somerset's appointment repeats their quarrel from 1 Henry VI, where their rivalry lost Talbot.","100":"'Denay'd' is an old form of 'denied'.","109":"Henry came of age in 1437; Gloucester's protectorship had legally ended years before, though he remained powerful.","118":"The slap is Shakespeare's invention, a public insult that brings the women's rivalry into the open.","121":"Eleanor stresses Margaret's French birth, a constant charge against her.","143":"Apprentices served masters for seven years under strict discipline; a grudge was a likely motive for a false charge.","145":"Trial by combat was an old legal custom in which God was thought to give victory to the innocent.","159":"The Duchess watches from 'aloft', the upper stage of the theatre.","160":"Raising spirits was a capital crime. Conjurers worked inside a circle drawn on the ground to protect themselves from the spirit.","181":"'Aio te, Aeacida, Romanos vincere posse' was the oracle given to Pyrrhus: it can mean 'you can conquer the Romans' or 'the Romans can conquer you'.","189":"Hawking was an aristocratic sport; 'old Joan' is the name of a falcon.","197":"'Tantaene animis coelestibus irae?' is from Virgil's Aeneid: 'Is there such anger in heavenly minds?'","205":"The duel is never fought: news of the Duchess's arrest arrives first.","222":"St Alban, the first British martyr, had a famous shrine in the abbey at St Albans.","270":"Sir Thomas More told the story of this false miracle, exposed by Duke Humphrey; Shakespeare took it from the chronicles.","306":"The scene takes place in a garden, a private place where treason can be spoken.","308":"York's genealogy is accurate in outline. Through his mother Anne Mortimer he descended from Lionel of Clarence, Edward the Third's third son; through his father from Edmund of Langley, the fifth.","324":"The historical sentence: Jourdain was burned at Smithfield, Bolingbroke hanged, and Eleanor did public penance in London before life imprisonment.","325":"The historical Eleanor was imprisoned for the rest of her life, dying at Beaumaris in 1452.","329":"Henry's words echo Psalm 119: 'Thy word is a lamp unto my feet'.","340":"Sack and charneco were sweet wines; 'double beer' was strong beer. Commoners fought trials by combat with staves and sandbags, not swords.","352":"Horner is drunk and slurring, as York notes.","359":"Henry sees the result as God's judgement; the audience has seen the drink.","371":"Penitents walked barefoot in a white sheet carrying a lighted candle, with papers listing their offences.","374":"The parliament was moved from London to Bury, in Suffolk's country, away from Gloucester's London supporters.","398":"The parliament at Bury St Edmunds met in February 1447. Humphrey of Gloucester was arrested there and died a few days later.","400":"The Cardinal accuses Gloucester of cruelty; Gloucester answers that he was merciful except to murderers and robbers.","409":"By 1453 England had lost all its French lands except Calais.","423":"Gloucester names each enemy in turn and sees his death as the 'prologue' to their tragedy, a prediction the rest of the trilogy fulfils.","429":"Gloucester's arrest at Bury is placed in the abbey, a church space used for the parliament.","451":"York was appointed Lieutenant of Ireland in 1447 and went there in 1449.","468":"York's longest soliloquy. Jack Cade's rising was in 1450; the chronicles reported rumours that York was behind it.","483":"Gloucester was found dead in 1447 at Bury, five days after his arrest.","505":"Margaret compares herself to Dido, queen of Carthage, bewitched by Ascanius's tales of Troy and deserted by Aeneas.","507":"The commons' anger at Gloucester's death was real; Suffolk was blamed and became deeply unpopular.","517":"Warwick's description follows the signs of violent death that were used in Elizabethan inquests.","530":"Suffolk answers Warwick's charge that he kills sleeping men.","563":"Iris was the rainbow messenger of the gods.","572":"The historical Cardinal Beaufort died in April 1447, a few weeks after Gloucester. His terrible death is Shakespeare's addition from Hall's moralising account.","585":"The historical Suffolk was impeached and banished in 1450; his ship was intercepted and he was beheaded in a small boat off Dover.","596":"'Walter' was pronounced 'water', fulfilling the spirit's prophecy that Suffolk would die by water.","610":"The Captain puns on Suffolk's family name, de la Pole, and 'pool', a stagnant puddle. Sylla (Sulla) was a Roman dictator notorious for massacres.","614":"'Cold fear seizes my limbs' (Latin, adapted from Virgil).","619":"Tully is Cicero, killed by soldiers of Mark Antony. Pompey was murdered on landing in Egypt, not by islanders.","632":"Puns on cloth-making: 'dress' and 'set a new nap' refer to finishing cloth. Kent was a centre of the cloth trade.","667":"Halfpenny loaves and three-hooped pots refer to the price of bread and the size of ale pots; 'small beer' was weak ale.","671":"Legal documents were written on parchment (sheepskin) and sealed with wax. Cade's hatred of writing echoes the 1381 Peasants' Revolt, which burned legal records.","681":"'Emmanuel' (God with us) was written at the head of letters and documents.","706":"Cade's story of a stolen twin parodies York's genealogy in 2.2.","747":"Southwark, south of the Thames, was outside the City's jurisdiction and full of inns.","764":"Lord Scales held the Tower for the King during Cade's rising.","770":"London Stone, an ancient stone in Cannon Street, was said to mark the centre of the city. Striking it was a gesture of taking possession.","781":"The Savoy palace was burned in the Peasants' Revolt of 1381, not by Cade: Shakespeare borrows from that earlier rising.","793":"Say, serge and buckram are all cloths. 'Basimecu' is Cade's coarse version of the French 'baise mon cul'. Printing came to England only in 1476, after Cade's death: a deliberate anachronism.","801":"Caesar's 'Commentaries' on the Gallic War said the people of Kent were the most civilised in Britain.","827":"Fish Street and St Magnus' church were at the north end of London Bridge.","836":"The historical rebellion broke up after a royal pardon; Cade fled and was mortally wounded by Alexander Iden, Sheriff of Kent, in July 1450.","844":"Henry became king in 1422 when he was nine months old, as he says.","850":"Henry's pardon of the rebels follows the historical general pardon of 1450.","862":"Cade puns on 'sallet', a salad and a light helmet.","877":"York returned from Ireland in 1450 and marched on London demanding Somerset's arrest; the battle of St Albans was in 1455. Shakespeare compresses five years.","895":"An esquire was a gentleman below a knight; Iden is knighted for his service.","909":"York's sons Edward and Richard become Edward IV and Richard III.","934":"The historical Richard of Gloucester was a small child (born 1452) at the battle of St Albans. Shakespeare brings him forward to introduce the future Richard III.","949":"The bear chained to a ragged staff was the Neville badge, worn by the Earls of Warwick.","958":"Clifford's lands were in the north, hence 'Clifford of Cumberland' and 'proud northern lord'.","974":"'The end crowns the work' (French proverb).","979":"Medea cut up her brother Absyrtus to delay her pursuers. Aeneas carried his father Anchises out of burning Troy.","981":"The Castle Inn at St Albans: Somerset died under its sign, fulfilling the spirit's warning to 'shun castles'.","997":"The first battle of St Albans (May 1455) is usually counted as the first battle of the Wars of the Roses."},"gloss":{"procurator":"agent, deputy","alder-liefest":"dearest of all","yclad":"clothed","imprimis":"first (Latin)","qualm":"sudden sickness","peroration":"speech, oration","rules the roast":"is in charge","large style":"grand titles","suffocate":"suffocated (pun on Suffolk)","fifteenth":"a tax of one fifteenth of goods","bickerings":"quarrels","hoise":"hoist, heave","housekeeping":"hospitality","tickle":"unstable, precarious","pennyworths":"bargains","Althaea":"mother of Meleager, whose life depended on a burning log","Ceres":"goddess of the harvest","Enchas'd":"adorned, set (like jewels)","ill-nurtur'd":"badly brought up","presently":"at once","conjurer":"one who raises spirits","propounded":"put, asked","broker":"go-between, agent","attainture":"conviction, disgrace","Sort how it will":"however it turns out","in the quill":"in a body, together","cullions":"rascals","pursuivant":"officer who serves warrants","Ave-Maries":"Hail Marys","saws":"sayings","canoniz'd":"made saints","callat":"slut, scold","lim'd":"smeared with birdlime to catch birds","lays":"songs","denay'd":"denied","censure":"judgement","rack'd":"overtaxed","lank":"thin, empty","minion":"hussy, favourite","ten commandments":"ten fingernails","hamper":"fetter, restrain","fume":"temper","overblown":"blown over, calmed","furniture":"equipment","mechanical":"common workman","doom":"judgement","exorcisms":"conjurings","ban-dogs":"chained watchdogs","verge":"circle","Adsum":"I am here (Latin)","Asmath":"name of the spirit","beldam":"old hag","at an inch":"closely","guerdon'd":"rewarded","forthcoming":"produced as evidence; in custody","flying at the brook":"hawking at water birds","fain":"eager","peremptory":"overbearing","Medice, teipsum":"Physician, heal thyself (Latin)","compound":"settle","damsons":"small plums","beadles":"parish officers who whipped offenders","drab":"slut","tainture":"stain, defilement","infallible":"certain","Pomfret":"Pontefract Castle","slips":"cuttings, shoots","Wink at":"ignore","Despoiled":"stripped","raught":"seized, taken","bested":"placed, prepared","appellant":"accuser","lists":"enclosed ground for combat","sack":"sweet wine","charneco":"a Portuguese wine","fence":"swordsmanship","Uneath":"hardly","abrook":"endure","Mail'd up":"wrapped up","deep-fet":"deep-fetched","Trow'st":"do you think","pointing-stock":"object of scorn","scath":"harm","Holden":"held","close dealing":"secret plotting","muse":"wonder","Me seemeth":"it seems to me","fond":"foolish","subornation":"bribing to do wrong","Unsounded":"unexplored (like deep water)","doit":"tiny coin","groat":"fourpenny coin","dispursed":"paid out","condign":"deserved","complot":"plot","conventicles":"secret meetings","clerkly couch'd":"cleverly worded","gnarling":"snarling","mickle":"much, great","engirt":"surrounded","checker'd slough":"speckled skin","quillets":"quibbles","gins":"traps","mates":"checkmates","amain":"at full speed","betime":"in time","far-fet":"far-fetched","kerns":"Irish foot soldiers","hap":"luck","porpentine":"porcupine","Morisco":"morris dancer","lime-twigs":"sticky twigs to catch birds","basilisk":"mythical serpent that killed with a look","Aeolus":"god of the winds","ken":"sight; see","Albion":"England","timeless":"untimely","puttock":"kite, scavenging bird of prey","contumelious":"insolent","crab-tree slip":"wild apple cutting","bucklers":"shields","deathsman":"executioner","hinds":"peasants","quaint":"skilful, elaborate","mandrake":"plant whose shriek was said to kill","consort":"band of musicians","cypress-trees":"funeral trees","surfeits":"overeats","Iris":"messenger of the gods","apothecary":"chemist","jades":"worn-out horses","pinnace":"small ship","Downs":"anchorage off Kent","boot":"profit","foot-cloth":"rich cloth over a horse","trencher":"wooden plate","kennel":"gutter","affy":"betroth","gobbets":"lumps of flesh","bezonians":"low rascals","Tully":"Cicero","lath":"thin strip of wood","nap":"raised surface of cloth","Argo":"ergo, therefore","cade":"barrel (of herrings)","bucks":"dirty linen","three-hooped pot":"ale pot with three bands","small beer":"weak beer","palfry":"riding horse","in capite":"directly from the crown","court-hand":"legal handwriting","obligations":"bonds, contracts","span-counter":"a children's game","mained":"maimed (pun on Maine)","clouted shoon":"patched shoes","brigandine":"body armour","supplication":"petition","Killingworth":"Kenilworth","Basimecu":"kiss-my-arse (mock French)","besom":"broom","score and the tally":"notched sticks for counting","hose and doublets":"tights and jackets","bona terra, mala gens":"a good land, a bad people (Latin)","hempen caudle":"hanging (rope as medicine)","familiar":"attendant devil","Savoy":"the Duke of Lancaster's palace in London","halters":"nooses","gallowglasses":"Irish soldiers armed with axes","sallet":"salad; light helmet","brown bill":"soldier's axe-like weapon","fee-simple":"freehold land","chines":"joints of meat","emblaze":"proclaim, display","sancta majestas":"sacred majesty (Latin)","flower-de-luce":"fleur-de-lis, emblem of France","Ajax Telamonius":"Greek hero who went mad and attacked sheep","fealty":"loyalty","palmer":"pilgrim","Achilles' spear":"spear that could wound and heal","bane":"ruin, death","Bedlam":"madhouse","bear-herd":"bear keeper","burgonet":"light helmet","stigmatic":"one marked by deformity","Absyrtus":"brother killed by Medea","Anchises":"father of Aeneas","holp":"helped","bestrid":"stood over to protect","repairing":"recovering","eterniz'd":"made eternal"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,8,"Your Majesty, when I left for France you ordered me, as your representative, to marry Princess Margaret on your behalf. So in the famous old city of Tours, before the Kings of France and Sicily, the Dukes of Orleans, Calabria, Brittany and Alencon, seven earls, twelve barons and twenty bishops, I carried out my task and married her for you."],[9,15,"Now, kneeling humbly before England and her lords, I hand over my claim to the queen into your gracious hands. You are the real man of whom I was only the stand-in. It is the happiest gift a marquess ever gave, and the most beautiful queen a king ever received."]],"16":[[0,7,"They please us well. Lord Marquess, kneel. We make you the first Duke of Suffolk and fasten on your sword. Cousin York, we relieve you of your post as regent in France until eighteen months are over."],[8,11,"Thank you, Uncle Winchester, Gloucester, York, Buckingham, Somerset, Salisbury and Warwick, for this great kindness in welcoming my queen. Come, let's go in and quickly arrange her coronation."]],"18":[[0,9,"Noble lords of England, pillars of the state, I must unload my grief on you, Duke Humphrey's grief, which is the grief of the whole country. What! Did my brother Henry the Fifth spend his youth, courage, money and men in the wars? Did he so often camp in open fields in winter cold and summer heat to conquer France, his rightful inheritance? Did my brother Bedford wear out his brains to keep by clever policy what Henry won?"],[10,19,"Have you yourselves, Somerset, Buckingham, brave York, Salisbury and victorious Warwick, taken deep wounds in France and Normandy? Have my uncle Beaufort and I, with the realm's wisest counsellors, sat in council early and late, arguing how to keep France and the French in fear, and had the king crowned in Paris as a child in spite of his enemies?"],[20,28,"And shall all this work and honour die? Henry's conquest, Bedford's watchfulness, your fighting and all our counsel? O lords of England, this treaty is shameful! This marriage is fatal: it cancels your fame, blots your names out of history, erases the record of your glory, defaces the monuments of conquered France, and undoes everything as if it had never been!"]],"28":[[0,9,"So there goes our Protector in a rage. You know he is my enemy, and an enemy to all of you too, and, I fear, no great friend to the king. Remember, lords, he is next in blood and heir to the English crown. If Henry had won an empire and all the rich western kingdoms by his marriage, Gloucester would still have reason to be displeased. Watch out, lords."],[10,18,"Don't let his smooth words bewitch you; be wise and careful. Even if the common people love him, calling him \"Humphrey, the good Duke of Gloucester\", clapping and shouting \"Jesus keep your royal excellence!\" and \"God save the good Duke Humphrey!\", I fear, lords, that for all this flattering show he will prove a dangerous Protector."]],"35":[[0,9,"Pride went first; ambition follows it. While these men work for their own advancement, we must work for the kingdom. I have never seen Humphrey Duke of Gloucester behave other than as a noble gentleman. I have often seen the arrogant cardinal, more like a soldier than a churchman, as bold and proud as if he owned everything, swear like a thug and behave unlike a ruler of the state."],[10,18,"Warwick, my son, comfort of my old age, your deeds, your honesty and your generous hospitality have won the commons' greatest love, second only to good Duke Humphrey. And brother York, your work in Ireland bringing it to order, and your recent exploits in the heart of France as regent, have made the people fear and honour you."],[19,24,"Let's join together for the public good to check and hold down the pride of Suffolk and the cardinal, and the ambition of Somerset and Buckingham, and, as far as we can, support Duke Humphrey's actions while they serve the country."]],"41":[[0,7,"Anjou and Maine are given to the French. Paris is lost, and the state of Normandy is on a knife edge now they're gone. Suffolk settled the terms, the lords agreed, and Henry was happy to swap two dukedoms for a duke's pretty daughter. I can't blame them all; what does it matter to them? It's yours they're giving away, York, not their own."],[8,17,"Pirates can sell their loot cheaply, buy friends and give to whores, partying like lords till it's all gone, while the poor owner of the goods weeps over them, wrings his hands, shakes his head and stands back trembling while everything is shared out and carried off, starving and not daring to touch his own. So York must sit, fret and bite his tongue while his own lands are bargained away and sold."],[18,24,"England, France and Ireland seem as bound up with my flesh and blood as the fatal log that Althaea burned was with the life of the prince of Calydon. Anjou and Maine both given to the French! Cold news for me, for I had hopes of France, just as I have of fertile England."],[25,33,"A day will come when York will claim what's his. So I'll take the Nevilles' side, pretend to love proud Duke Humphrey, and when I see my chance, claim the crown, for that's the golden target I aim at. Proud Lancaster won't steal my right, or hold the sceptre in his childish fist, or wear the crown on his head, when his churchy temper is unfit for a crown."],[34,45,"So, York, keep quiet for a while until the time is right. Stay awake while others sleep, and pry into the secrets of the state, until Henry is glutted with love for his new bride, England's expensive queen, and Humphrey and the lords are at odds. Then I'll raise the white rose high, whose sweet smell will perfume the air, carry the arms of York on my banner to wrestle with the house of Lancaster, and by force make him give up the crown whose bookish rule has dragged fair England down."]],"45":[[0,9,"Why does my lord droop like over-ripe corn, its head bowed under the weight of the harvest? Why does great Duke Humphrey frown as if the world's favours displeased him? Why are your eyes fixed on the gloomy ground, staring at something that seems to darken your sight? What do you see there? King Henry's crown, set with all the world's honours? If so, keep gazing, and crawl on your face until that crown circles your head."],[10,15,"Reach out your hand to the glorious gold. What, is it too short? I'll lengthen it with mine, and when we've lifted it up together, we'll both raise our heads to heaven and never again lower our eyes so far as to glance at the ground."]],"50":[[0,9,"No, Eleanor, now I must scold you plainly. Arrogant woman, badly brought up Eleanor! Aren't you the second lady in the kingdom, the Protector's wife, and loved by him? Don't you have every worldly pleasure at your command, beyond what you can even imagine? And will you still keep forging treason, to throw your husband and yourself down from the height of honour to the feet of disgrace? Leave me, and let me hear no more!"]],"67":[[0,9,"Hume is to enjoy himself with the duchess's gold? Indeed he will. But careful, Sir John Hume! Seal your lips and say nothing but \"mum\"; this business needs silence. Dame Eleanor gives gold to bring the witch; gold is always welcome, even from a devil. But I also have gold coming from another direction. I daren't say from the rich cardinal and the great new Duke of Suffolk, but that's how it is."],[10,20,"To be plain, knowing Dame Eleanor's ambitious temper, they've hired me to bring down the duchess and fill her head with these conjurings. They say \"a clever rogue doesn't need an agent\", yet I'm Suffolk's and the cardinal's agent. Hume, if you're not careful you'll end up calling them both a pair of clever rogues. Well, that's how it stands, and I fear in the end Hume's trickery will wreck the duchess, and her disgrace will be Humphrey's fall. However it turns out, I'll get gold from everyone."]],"92":[[0,7,"My Lord of Suffolk, tell me, is this the custom, the fashion at the English court? Is this how Britain is governed, and this the royalty of England's king? What, is King Henry to stay a schoolboy under surly Gloucester's control? Am I a queen in title and name, only to be made a duke's subject?"],[8,18,"I tell you, Pole, when you jousted in Tours in honour of my love and stole the hearts of the French ladies, I thought King Henry would be like you in courage, courtliness and looks. But his mind is all set on holiness, counting Hail Marys on his rosary. His champions are the prophets and apostles, his weapons holy sayings from scripture, his study is his jousting ground, and his loves are bronze statues of saints."],[19,22,"I wish the college of cardinals would elect him pope, carry him off to Rome and put the triple crown on his head. That would be a position to suit his holiness."]],"96":[[0,6,"None of these lords annoys me half as much as that proud woman, the Protector's wife. She sweeps through the court with crowds of ladies, more like an empress than Duke Humphrey's wife. Foreigners at court take her for the queen. She wears a duke's income on her back, and in her heart she sneers at our poverty."],[7,12,"Won't I live to get revenge on her? The contemptuous, low-born slut boasted among her favourites the other day that the train of her worst gown was worth more than all my father's land, until Suffolk gave two dukedoms for his daughter."]],"97":[[0,8,"Madam, I've set a trap for her, smeared a bush with birdlime and placed a choir of such tempting birds there that she'll land to listen to their songs and never fly up to trouble you again. So leave her alone, and listen to me, madam, for I'm bold enough to advise you. Though we don't like the cardinal, we must join with him and the lords until we've brought Duke Humphrey into disgrace."],[9,12,"As for the Duke of York, this recent accusation won't do him much good. So one by one we'll weed them all out at last, and you yourself will steer the ship of state."]],"128":[[0,6,"Now, lords, my anger has blown over after one walk around the courtyard, and I've come to talk about state business. As for your spiteful false charges, prove them and I'll answer to the law. But may God treat my soul as mercifully as I dutifully love my king and country!"],[7,9,"But to the matter in hand: I say, my sovereign, York is the best man to be your regent in France."]],"181":[[0,8,"Lord Buckingham, I think you watched her well. A nice plot, a good one to build on! Now, my lord, let's see the devil's writing. What have we here? (Reading) \"The duke is still alive who will depose Henry, but Henry will outlive him, and the duke will die a violent death.\" Why, this is exactly like the old oracle \"I say that you, son of Aeacus, the Romans can conquer\", which could mean either. Well, the rest:"],[9,17,"\"Tell me what fate awaits the Duke of Suffolk? He will die by water and meet his end. What will happen to the Duke of Somerset? Let him avoid castles; he will be safer on the sandy plains than where castles stand on hills.\" Come, come, my lords, these oracles are hard to get and hard to understand."],[18,21,"The king is now on his way to St Albans, and with him this lovely lady's husband. This news must go there as fast as a horse can carry it. A sad breakfast for my Lord Protector."]],"295":[[0,5,"News my heart trembles to reveal. A gang of wicked people, bent on evil, with the support and partnership of Lady Eleanor, the Protector's wife, the ringleader and head of the whole crew, have plotted dangerously against your state,"],[6,11,"dealing with witches and conjurers, whom we caught in the act raising evil spirits from underground and asking about King Henry's life and death, and about others of your highness's privy council, as your grace will hear in more detail."]],"300":[[0,2,"Madam, as for myself, I appeal to heaven to witness how I have loved my king and country. As for my wife, I don't know how things stand."],[3,9,"I'm sorry to hear what I've heard. She is noble, but if she has forgotten honour and virtue and mixed with people who, like pitch, stain nobility, I banish her from my bed and company and hand her over to the law and to shame, since she has dishonoured Gloucester's honest name."]],"308":[[0,8,"Then here it is. Edward the Third, my lords, had seven sons. The first was Edward the Black Prince, Prince of Wales; the second, William of Hatfield; the third, Lionel Duke of Clarence; next came John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster; the fifth was Edmund Langley, Duke of York; the sixth was Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester; William of Windsor was the seventh and last."],[9,18,"Edward the Black Prince died before his father and left Richard, his only son, who reigned as king after Edward the Third's death, until Henry Bolingbroke, Duke of Lancaster, eldest son and heir of John of Gaunt, crowned as Henry the Fourth, seized the kingdom, deposed the rightful king, sent his poor queen back to France where she came from, and sent him to Pomfret, where, as you all know, harmless Richard was treacherously murdered."]],"314":[[0,7,"His eldest sister, Anne, my mother, heir to the crown, married Richard Earl of Cambridge, son of Edmund Langley, Edward the Third's fifth son. Through her I claim the kingdom. She was heir to Roger Earl of March, son of Edmund Mortimer, who married Philippa, only daughter of Lionel Duke of Clarence."],[8,9,"So if the descendants of the elder son come before the younger, I am king."]],"315":[[0,5,"What could be plainer than this? Henry claims the crown from John of Gaunt, the fourth son; York claims it from the third. Until Lionel's line dies out, Gaunt's should not reign. It hasn't died out but flourishes in you and your sons, fine shoots from such a stem."],[6,9,"So, father Salisbury, let's kneel together, and in this private place be the first to salute our rightful sovereign and honour his birthright to the crown."]],"317":[[0,4,"Thank you, lords. But I'm not your king until I'm crowned and my sword is stained with the heart's blood of the house of Lancaster. That can't be done suddenly, but with careful planning and silent secrecy."],[5,12,"In these dangerous days, do as I do: turn a blind eye to the Duke of Suffolk's insolence, Beaufort's pride, Somerset's ambition, Buckingham and their whole crew, until they have trapped the shepherd of the flock, that virtuous prince, good Duke Humphrey. That's what they're after, and in getting it they'll find their own deaths, if York can foretell anything."]],"324":[[0,3,"Step forward, Dame Eleanor Cobham, Gloucester's wife. In the sight of God and us, your guilt is great. Receive the law's sentence for sins that God's book judges worthy of death."],[4,7,"You four, go back to prison from here, and from there to the place of execution. The witch will be burned to ashes in Smithfield, and you three will be hanged on the gallows."],[8,12,"You, madam, because you are more nobly born, will lose your honour for the rest of your life, and after three days of public penance will live in banishment within this country, in the Isle of Man, in Sir John Stanley's charge."]],"365":[[0,3,"Ten is the hour I was told to wait for my punished duchess to come by. She can hardly bear to walk on the stony streets with her tender feet."],[4,10,"Sweet Nell, your noble mind can hardly endure the low people staring at your face with spiteful looks and laughing at your shame, who once followed your proud chariot wheels when you rode through the streets in triumph. But wait! I think she's coming, and I'll ready my tear-stained eyes to see her misery."]],"371":[[0,9,"Ah, Gloucester, teach me to forget who I am! For while I think I'm your married wife and you are a prince, Protector of this land, I feel I shouldn't be led along like this, wrapped in shame, with papers on my back listing my crimes, followed by a mob who love to see my tears and hear my deep groans. The cruel stones cut my tender feet, and when I flinch, the spiteful people laugh and tell me to watch my step."],[10,20,"Ah, Humphrey, can I bear this shameful yoke? Do you think I'll ever look at the world again, or think those who enjoy the sun are happy? No; darkness will be my light, and night my day; to remember my grandeur will be my hell. Sometimes I'll say I am Duke Humphrey's wife, and he a prince and ruler of the land. Yet he ruled so, and was such a prince, that he stood by while I, his miserable duchess, was made a spectacle and a laughing stock for every idle rascal."],[21,30,"But you be gentle and don't blush at my shame, and don't stir until the axe of death hangs over you, as it surely will soon. For Suffolk, who can do anything with her who hates you and all of us, and York, and wicked Beaufort, that false priest, have all set traps for your wings, and however you fly, they'll tangle you. But don't be afraid till your foot is caught, and never try to forestall your enemies."]],"372":[[0,5,"Ah, Nell, stop! You're completely wrong. I must commit a crime before I can be condemned. If I had twenty times as many enemies and each had twenty times their power, they couldn't do me any harm as long as I'm loyal, true and innocent."],[6,11,"Would you have me rescue you from this disgrace? Your scandal still wouldn't be wiped away, and I'd be in danger for breaking the law. Your best help is quiet, gentle Nell. Please, settle your heart to patience; this nine days' wonder will soon wear off."]],"398":[[0,8,"Can't you see, or won't you notice, how strangely his face has changed? How majestically he carries himself, how insolent he's become lately, how proud, how bossy, how unlike himself? We remember when he was mild and friendly, and if we even glanced at him from far off, he was immediately on his knee, so that the whole court admired his humility."],[9,16,"But meet him now, even in the morning when everyone says good day, and he frowns, gives an angry look, and walks past with a stiff knee that won't bow, scorning the respect he owes us. Little dogs aren't noticed when they snarl, but great men tremble when the lion roars, and Humphrey is no little man in England."],[17,23,"First, remember that he is close to you by birth, and if you fell, he would be the next to rise. So it seems to me unwise, considering his bitter mind and what he'd gain from your death, that he should come near your royal person or be allowed into your highness's council."],[24,29,"He has won the commons' hearts by flattery, and when he chooses to stir up trouble, it's to be feared they'll all follow him. Now it's spring and the weeds have shallow roots; leave them now, and they'll overgrow the garden and choke the plants for lack of care."],[30,37,"The loving concern I have for my lord made me gather these dangers in the duke. If it's foolish, call it a woman's fear; and if better reasons can replace that fear, I'll agree and say I wronged the duke. My Lords of Suffolk, Buckingham and York, disprove my charge if you can, or else agree my words are right."]],"399":[[0,10,"Your highness has seen right into this duke, and if I'd been asked to speak first, I think I would have said the same. On my life, the duchess began her devilish practices because he encouraged her. Or if he didn't know of those crimes, still, by boasting of his high birth, that he was next heir after the king, and by bragging so much about his nobility, he egged on the mad, brain-sick duchess to plot our sovereign's fall by wicked means."],[11,15,"Still waters run deep, and under his simple appearance he hides treason. The fox doesn't bark when it means to steal the lamb. No, no, my sovereign, Gloucester is a man not yet sounded out, and full of deep deceit."]],"416":[[0,7,"Is it only \"said\"? Who says it? I never robbed the soldiers of their pay, nor took one penny in bribes from France. So help me God, as I have stayed awake night after night working for England's good! Let any coin I ever squeezed from the king, or any penny I hoarded for myself, be brought against me on the day of my trial!"],[8,11,"No; many pounds of my own money I've paid out to the garrisons, because I wouldn't tax the poor commons, and I never asked to be paid back."]],"423":[[0,11,"Ah, gracious lord, these are dangerous days. Virtue is choked by foul ambition and charity driven out by hatred; foul bribery rules and justice is exiled from your land. I know their plot is to take my life. If my death could make this island happy and end their tyranny, I'd give it gladly. But mine is only the prologue to their play, for thousands more who don't yet suspect danger won't finish their planned tragedy."],[12,18,"Beaufort's red, sparkling eyes give away his heart's malice, and Suffolk's cloudy frown his stormy hatred. Sharp-tongued Buckingham unloads the envy that weighs on his heart, and stubborn York, who reaches for the moon and whose overreaching arm I have pulled back, aims at my life with false accusations."],[19,29,"And you, my sovereign lady, along with the rest, have heaped disgrace on my head for no reason and done your best to stir up my dearest lord to be my enemy. Yes, you've all put your heads together, I heard about your secret meetings, all to get rid of my innocent life. I won't lack false witnesses to condemn me, or invented treasons to add to my guilt. The old proverb will come true: \"Any stick will do to beat a dog.\""]],"434":[[0,7,"Yes, Margaret. My heart is drowned in grief, whose flood begins to pour from my eyes, and my body is wrapped in misery, for what is more miserable than unhappiness? Ah, uncle Humphrey! In your face I see the map of honour, truth and loyalty, and the hour has yet to come when I found you false or doubted your faith."],[8,15,"What gloomy star now envies your fortune, that these great lords and our queen Margaret seek to destroy your harmless life? You never wronged them or any man. And as the butcher takes away the calf, ties up the poor thing and beats it when it strays, carrying it to the bloody slaughterhouse, so pitilessly have they carried him away."],[16,24,"And as the mother cow runs up and down lowing, looking the way her harmless young one went, and can do nothing but wail for her darling, so I mourn good Gloucester's fate with sad, useless tears, and watch after him with dim eyes, unable to help him, his sworn enemies are so powerful. I'll weep for his fortunes, and between each groan say, \"Who's a traitor? Gloucester is not.\""]],"436":[[0,7,"Noble lords, cold snow melts in the sun's hot rays. My lord Henry is cold in great matters, too full of foolish pity, and Gloucester's show of goodness fools him, as the weeping crocodile traps soft-hearted travellers with its sorrow, or as the snake coiled on a flowery bank, with its shining speckled skin, stings a child who thinks it lovely because of its beauty."],[8,11,"Believe me, lords, if nobody were wiser than me, and in this I think my judgement good, this Gloucester would soon be got rid of, to rid us of the fear we have of him."]],"443":[[0,8,"Madam, that's true. And wouldn't it be madness, then, to make the fox guardian of the sheepfold? If he were accused of being a cunning murderer, his guilt shouldn't be idly passed over just because he hasn't carried out his plan yet. No, let him die because he's a fox, proven by nature to be an enemy to the flock, before his jaws are stained with blood, as Humphrey is proven by argument to be my lord's enemy."],[9,13,"And don't quibble about how to kill him. By traps, by snares, by cunning, sleeping or awake, it doesn't matter how, as long as he's dead. For it's good deceit that checkmates the man who first planned to deceive."]],"468":[[0,9,"Now, York, or never, harden your fearful thoughts and turn doubt into determination. Be what you hope to be, or give up what you are to death; it isn't worth keeping. Let pale-faced fear stay with low-born men and find no shelter in a royal heart. Thoughts come faster than spring showers, and every thought is about high rank. My brain, busier than the working spider, weaves elaborate traps for my enemies."],[10,16,"Well, nobles, well; it's clever of you to send me off with an army. I fear you're just warming the frozen snake that, cherished in your bosoms, will sting your hearts. It was men I lacked, and you're giving them to me. I take it kindly, but be sure you're putting sharp weapons in a madman's hands."],[17,23,"While I build up a mighty army in Ireland, I'll stir up some black storm in England that will blow ten thousand souls to heaven or hell, and this fierce storm won't stop raging until the golden circle on my head, like the sun's glorious bright rays, calms the fury of this mad gust."],[24,39,"And as the agent of my plan, I've persuaded a headstrong man from Kent, John Cade of Ashford, to stir up a rising, as he well can, under the name of John Mortimer. In Ireland I've seen this stubborn Cade take on a troop of Irish soldiers and fight so long that his thighs were stuck with darts like a porcupine's quills. When he was finally rescued, I saw him leap about like a wild morris dancer, shaking the bloody darts as a dancer shakes his bells. Very often, disguised as a shaggy-haired, crafty Irish soldier, he has mixed with the enemy and come back to me undiscovered with news of their plots."],[40,47,"This devil will be my stand-in, for he looks, walks and talks like John Mortimer, who is now dead. Through him I'll find out what the commons think, how they feel about the house of York and its claim. Say he's caught, racked and tortured; I know of no pain they can inflict that will make him say I put him up to this rising."],[48,52,"Say he succeeds, as is very likely; why, then I'll come from Ireland with my army and reap the harvest that rascal sowed. For with Humphrey dead, as he will be, and Henry pushed aside, the crown comes next to me."]],"502":[[0,8,"What, does my Lord of Suffolk comfort me? Did he just now come to croak like a raven, whose deadly tune took away my strength, and does he think that the chirping of a wren, crying \"comfort\" from a hollow heart, can drive away that first sound? Don't hide your poison with such sugary words. Don't touch me; stop, I say! Your hands frighten me like a snake's sting."],[9,16,"You deadly messenger, out of my sight! Murderous tyranny sits in grim majesty on your eyeballs to frighten the world. Don't look at me, for your eyes wound. Yet don't go. Come, basilisk, and kill the innocent onlooker with your glance, for I'll find joy in the shadow of death, and only double death in life, now Gloucester is dead."]],"503":[[0,8,"Why do you scold my Lord of Suffolk like this? Though the duke was his enemy, he mourns his death like a true Christian. And as for me, enemy though he was to me, if flowing tears or heartbreaking groans or life-draining sighs could bring him back, I'd blind myself weeping, sicken myself groaning, turn pale as a primrose with sighs, all to have the noble duke alive."],[9,15,"How do I know what the world will think of me? For it's known we were only false friends. People may think I got rid of the duke, so my name will be wounded by slander and royal courts all over will be full of my disgrace. That's what I get from his death. Oh, how unhappy I am, to be a queen and crowned with shame!"]],"505":[[0,8,"Say woe for me, more wretched than he is. What, are you turning away and hiding your face? I'm not a disgusting leper; look at me. What! Have you gone deaf like the adder? Then be poisonous too and kill your abandoned queen. Is all your comfort shut up in Gloucester's tomb? Then Queen Margaret was never your joy. Put up his statue and worship it, and make my picture into a pub sign."],[9,23,"Was it for this that I was nearly shipwrecked at sea, and twice driven back home from England's coast by contrary winds? What did that mean except that the warning wind seemed to say, \"Don't seek a scorpion's nest; don't set foot on this unkind shore\"? And what did I do but curse the gentle winds and the god who let them out of their caves, and order them to blow towards England's blessed shore or wreck us on a terrible rock? Yet Aeolus wouldn't be a murderer, but left that hateful job to you. The heaving sea refused to drown me, knowing you'd have me drowned on shore in tears as salt as the sea through your unkindness."],[24,40,"The splitting rocks hid themselves in the sinking sands and wouldn't dash me with their jagged sides, so that your heart, harder than flint, could kill Margaret in your palace. As long as I could see your chalk cliffs, when the storm beat us back from your shore, I stood on deck in the storm, and when the dark sky began to rob my eager eyes of the sight of your land, I took a precious jewel from my neck, a heart set in diamonds, and threw it towards your land. The sea took it, and so I wished your body would take my heart. And with that I lost sight of fair England, told my eyes to go away with my heart, and called them blind, dim glasses for losing sight of England's longed-for coast."],[41,48,"How often have I tempted Suffolk's tongue, the agent of your foul fickleness, to sit and charm me, as Ascanius did when he told the frantic Dido his father's deeds that began in burning Troy! Am I not bewitched like her? Or you not false like him? Oh, I can't go on! Die, Margaret! For Henry weeps that you live so long."]],"511":[[0,4,"O You who judge all things, restrain my thoughts, which are trying to persuade my soul that violent hands were laid on Humphrey's life! If my suspicion is false, forgive me, God, for judgement belongs only to you."],[5,12,"I'd gladly go and warm his pale lips with twenty thousand kisses, pour an ocean of salt tears on his face, tell my love to his dumb, deaf body, and feel his unfeeling hand with my fingers. But these poor funeral rites are all useless, and what would looking at his dead, earthly form do but make my sorrow greater?"]],"517":[[0,7,"See how the blood has settled in his face. I've often seen a body whose soul left it naturally, ashen-looking, thin, pale and bloodless, because all the blood has gone down to the struggling heart, which in its fight with death draws the blood to help it against the enemy; there it cools with the heart and never returns to colour and beautify the cheek again."],[8,18,"But look, his face is black and full of blood, his eyeballs bulge further than when he lived, staring hideously like a strangled man's; his hair stands on end, his nostrils stretched with struggling, his hands spread out like one who grabbed and fought for life and was overpowered. Look, you see his hair is stuck to the sheets, and his neat beard is rough and messy, like summer corn flattened by a storm. He must have been murdered here; the least of these signs would make it likely."]],"529":[[0,7,"If it weren't that your guilt of murder protects you, and I'd be robbing the executioner of his fee and so sparing you ten thousand shames, and that my sovereign's presence keeps me calm, I would, false murdering coward, make you beg pardon on your knees for what you've said, and say it was your own mother you meant, and that you yourself were born a bastard."],[8,10,"And after all this frightened homage, I'd pay you your wages and send your soul to hell, you deadly bloodsucker of sleeping men!"]],"539":[[0,6,"(To the Commons, entering) Men, stand back; the king will know your mind. Dread lord, the commons send you word through me that unless false Suffolk is put to death at once or banished from England, they will tear him from your palace by force and torture him with a slow, painful death."],[7,12,"They say good Duke Humphrey died because of him; they say they fear your highness's death from him; and pure love and loyalty, not any stubborn wish to oppose you or go against your wishes, makes them so eager for his banishment."],[13,28,"They say, out of care for your royal person, that if your highness meant to sleep and ordered that no one should disturb you on pain of your displeasure or of death, still, despite such a strict order, if a snake with forked tongue were seen sliding slyly towards your majesty, you would have to be woken, in case, left in that dangerous sleep, the deadly serpent made your sleep eternal. And so they cry that, even if you forbid it, they will guard you, whether you want it or not, from such deadly snakes as false Suffolk, by whose poisonous, fatal sting, they say, your loving uncle, worth twenty of him, has been shamefully robbed of his life."]],"543":[[0,5,"Go, Salisbury, and tell them all from me that I thank them for their tender, loving care; and even if they hadn't urged me, I meant to do what they ask, for my thoughts warn me every hour of harm to my state through Suffolk."],[6,9,"And so I swear by His majesty, whose very unworthy deputy I am, that he won't breathe his infection in this air for more than three more days, on pain of death."]],"546":[[0,4,"Ungentle queen, to call him gentle Suffolk! No more, I say. If you plead for him, you'll only add to my anger. If I had only said it, I would have kept my word; but when I swear, it can't be undone."],[5,9,"If after three days you are found here on any land I rule, the whole world won't be enough ransom for your life. Come, Warwick, come, good Warwick, come with me; I have important things to tell you."]],"551":[[0,11,"A plague on them! Why should I curse them? If curses could kill, like the mandrake's groan, I'd invent words as bitter and piercing, as savage, harsh and horrible to hear, spat through my clenched teeth with as many signs of deadly hate, as lean-faced Envy in her disgusting cave. My tongue would trip over my passionate words, my eyes would spark like struck flint, my hair would stand on end like a madman's; yes, every joint would seem to curse; and even now my heavy heart would break if I didn't curse them."],[12,19,"May poison be their drink! Bitterness, worse than bitterness, the finest food they taste! Their sweetest shade a grove of funeral cypress trees! Their best view, killing basilisks! Their softest touch as painful as lizards' stings! Their music as frightening as a snake's hiss, with ominous screech owls to complete the band! All the foul terrors of deep, dark hell—"]],"554":[[0,6,"Oh, please stop. Give me your hand, so I can wet it with my sad tears; and don't let heaven's rain wet this place and wash away the marks of my grief. Oh, if only this kiss could be printed on your hand, so you might remember, by this seal, the lips through which a thousand sighs are breathed for you!"],[7,13,"So go, so that I can fully feel my grief; while you're standing here I only imagine it, like someone who's full thinking about hunger. I'll have you called back, or, be sure of it, risk being banished myself; and I'm banished already, if only from you. Go; don't speak to me; go right now."],[14,17,"Oh, don't go yet! This is how two condemned friends embrace and kiss and say goodbye ten thousand times, a hundred times less willing to part than to die. But now farewell, and farewell life with you!"]],"555":[[0,7,"So poor Suffolk is banished ten times: once by the king and nine times by you. It's not the country I care about, if you weren't in it. A wilderness would be crowded enough if Suffolk had your heavenly company, for where you are is the whole world, with every pleasure in it, and where you are not is desolation."],[8,9,"I can say no more. Live and enjoy your life; I'll have no joy in anything except that you are alive."]],"558":[[0,4,"To tell his majesty that Cardinal Beaufort is about to die. A terrible sickness suddenly took him, making him gasp and stare and gulp at the air, blaspheming God and cursing men on earth."],[5,10,"Sometimes he talks as if Duke Humphrey's ghost were beside him; sometimes he calls for the king and whispers to his pillow as if to him the secrets of his overburdened soul. I'm sent to tell his majesty that he's crying out for him right now."]],"562":[[0,8,"If I leave you, I can't live; and to die in your sight, what would that be but like a pleasant sleep in your lap? Here I could breathe my soul into the air as gently as a baby in its cradle dying with its mother's breast between its lips; whereas out of your sight I'd go raving mad and cry for you to close my eyes, to have your lips close my mouth."],[9,14,"So you'd either turn my soul back as it flew away, or I'd breathe it into your body, and then it would live in sweet paradise. To die beside you would only be to die in play; to die away from you would be torture worse than death. Oh, let me stay, whatever happens!"]],"575":[[0,5,"Bring me to trial when you like. Didn't he die in his bed? Where else should he die? Can I make men live whether they want to or not? Oh, don't torture me any more! I'll confess. Alive again? Then show me where he is; I'll give a thousand pounds to see him."],[6,10,"He has no eyes; the dust has blinded them. Comb down his hair; look, look, it's standing on end like sticky twigs set to catch my winged soul! Give me a drink, and tell the chemist to bring the strong poison I bought from him."]],"585":[[0,6,"The bright, gossiping, pitying day has crept into the sea's embrace, and now loud-howling wolves wake the tired horses that pull the tragic, gloomy night, who with drowsy, slow, drooping wings hugs dead men's graves and breathes foul, infectious darkness from her misty jaws into the air."],[7,13,"So bring out the soldiers we've captured, for while our small ship is anchored in the Downs, they'll pay their ransom here on the sand or stain this muddy shore with their blood. Master, I give you this prisoner freely; and you, his mate, take profit from this one; the other one, Walter Whitmore, is your share."]],"602":[[0,8,"Low, obscure peasant, King Henry's blood, the honourable blood of Lancaster, must not be spilled by such a worn-out servant. Haven't you kissed your hand to me and held my stirrup? Walked bare-headed beside my richly covered mule and thought yourself lucky when I nodded to you? How often have you served my wine, eaten from my plate, knelt at the table when I feasted with Queen Margaret?"],[9,14,"Remember it, and let it humble you, yes, and so check your premature pride: how you stood in my hall waiting dutifully for me to come out. This hand of mine has written letters on your behalf, and so it will charm your unruly tongue into silence."]],"610":[[0,8,"Pool! Sir Pool! Lord! Yes, gutter, puddle, sewer, whose filth and dirt muddies the clear spring England drinks from. Now I'll stop up your gaping mouth for swallowing the kingdom's treasure. Your lips that kissed the queen will sweep the ground, and you who smiled at good Duke Humphrey's death will grin in vain at the unfeeling winds, which will hiss back at you in contempt."],[9,20,"And may you be married to the hags of hell for daring to betroth a mighty lord to the daughter of a worthless king who has no subjects, money or crown. You've grown great by devilish cunning, and like ambitious Sulla gorged yourself on lumps of your mother country's bleeding heart. Because of you Anjou and Maine were sold to France; because of you the false, rebellious Normans refuse to call us lord, and Picardy has killed its governors, captured our forts, and sent our ragged soldiers home wounded."],[21,29,"Princely Warwick and all the Nevilles, whose dreaded swords were never drawn for nothing, are rising in arms because they hate you. And now the house of York, pushed from the crown by the shameful murder of an innocent king and by proud, grasping tyranny, burns with avenging fire; its hopeful banners carry our half-hidden sun, struggling to shine, with the motto \"In spite of the clouds\"."],[30,33,"The commons here in Kent are up in arms, and, to finish, disgrace and poverty have crept into our king's palace, and all because of you. Away! Take him away."]],"611":[[0,5,"Oh, if only I were a god, to fire thunderbolts at these worthless, servile, low drudges! Small things make low men proud. This villain here, just because he's captain of a little boat, threatens more than Bargulus, the mighty Illyrian pirate. Drones don't suck eagles' blood; they rob beehives."],[6,10,"It's impossible that I should die at the hands of such a low servant as you. Your words make me angry, not sorry. I'm going to France with a message from the queen; I order you to carry me safely across the Channel."]],"617":[[0,7,"Suffolk's royal tongue is stern and rough, used to giving orders, not taught to beg for favours. Far be it from us to honour such men with humble pleas. No, I'd rather let my head bow to the block than let these knees bend to anyone but the God of heaven and my king, and sooner dance on a bloody pole than stand bare-headed before a common servant."],[8,9,"True nobility knows no fear. I can bear more than you dare do."]],"747":[[0,4,"The rebels are in Southwark; run, my lord! Jack Cade calls himself Lord Mortimer, descended from the Duke of Clarence's house, calls your grace a usurper openly and swears to crown himself in Westminster."],[5,10,"His army is a ragged crowd of farm workers and peasants, rough and merciless. The deaths of Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother have given them heart and courage to carry on. They call all scholars, lawyers, courtiers and gentlemen false caterpillars and mean to kill them."]],"793":[[0,6,"Well, he'll be beheaded ten times for it. Ah, you say, you serge, no, you buckram lord! Now you're right within range of our royal jurisdiction. What can you answer to my majesty for giving up Normandy to Monsieur Kiss-my-arse, the Dauphin of France? Be it known to you by these presents, that is, the presence of Lord Mortimer, that I am the broom that must sweep the court clean of such filth as you."],[7,13,"You have most treacherously corrupted the youth of the kingdom by setting up a grammar school; and whereas before, our ancestors had no books but the notched tally stick, you have brought in printing, and, against the king, his crown and his dignity, you have built a paper mill. It will be proved to your face that you keep men around you who usually talk about nouns and verbs and such abominable words as no Christian ear can bear to hear."],[14,19,"You have appointed justices of the peace to summon poor men about matters they couldn't answer. What's more, you've put them in prison, and because they couldn't read, you've hanged them, when for that reason alone they most deserved to live. You ride a horse with a fancy saddlecloth, don't you?"]],"801":[[0,5,"Just hear me speak, and then take me where you like. In the Commentaries Caesar wrote, Kent is called the most civilised place in all this island. The country is lovely, being full of riches, the people generous, brave, energetic and wealthy, which makes me hope you are not without pity."],[6,11,"I didn't sell Maine, I didn't lose Normandy, but I'd give my life to win them back. I have always done justice with mercy; prayers and tears have moved me, gifts never could. When have I ever demanded anything from you except to support the king, the kingdom and you?"],[12,19,"I've given large gifts to scholars because my learning got me advancement with the king; and since ignorance is God's curse and knowledge the wing we fly to heaven with, unless you're possessed by devils, you can't help but refrain from murdering me. This tongue has negotiated with foreign kings for your benefit—"]],"832":[[0,5,"What, Buckingham and Clifford, are you so bold? And you low peasants, do you believe him? Are you set on being hanged with your pardons round your necks? Did my sword break through London's gates just so you could desert me at the White Hart in Southwark? I thought you'd never lay down these arms till you had won back your old freedom."],[6,10,"But you're all deserters and cowards, and love to live as slaves to the nobility. Let them break your backs with burdens, take your houses from over your heads, rape your wives and daughters in front of you. As for me, I'll look after myself; and so, God's curse on you all!"]],"834":[[0,6,"Is Cade the son of Henry the Fifth, that you shout you'll go with him like this? Will he lead you through the heart of France and make the lowest of you earls and dukes? Alas, he has no home, no place to run to, and doesn't know how to live except by plunder, by robbing your friends and us."],[7,12,"Wouldn't it be a shame if, while you quarrel among yourselves, the frightened French, whom you recently beat, should suddenly cross the sea and beat you? I can already see them, in this civil chaos, lording it in London's streets, shouting \"Villain!\" at everyone they meet."],[13,18,"Better ten thousand low-born Cades should die than you should bow to a Frenchman's mercy. To France, to France, and win back what you've lost! Spare England, for it's your native land. Henry has money, you are strong and manly; with God on our side, don't doubt we'll win."]],"854":[[0,4,"This is how I stand, troubled between Cade and York, like a ship that has escaped a storm and is immediately becalmed and boarded by a pirate. Just as Cade is driven back and his men scattered, York is up in arms to follow him."],[5,9,"Please, Buckingham, go and meet him and ask him the reason for this army. Tell him I'll send Duke Edmund to the Tower; and Somerset, we will send you there until he dismisses his army."]],"862":[[0,7,"Curse ambition! Curse me, who have a sword and yet am starving to death! For five days I've hidden in these woods and haven't dared to peep out, for the whole county is searching for me. But now I'm so hungry that even if I were promised another thousand years of life, I couldn't wait any longer. So I've climbed over a brick wall into this garden to see if I can eat grass or pick a salad, which isn't bad for cooling a man's stomach in this hot weather."],[8,13,"And I think this word \"sallet\" was born to do me good. Many times, if not for a sallet, a helmet, my skull would have been split by a soldier's axe; and many times, when I've been thirsty and marching bravely, it has served me as a pot to drink from; and now the word \"sallet\" must serve me as food."]],"868":[[0,4,"No, it will never be said, as long as England stands, that Alexander Iden, a gentleman of Kent, took unfair odds to fight a poor starving man. Set your staring eyes against mine; see if you can outstare me."],[5,12,"Compare limb with limb and you're far smaller. Your hand is only a finger next to my fist, your leg a stick compared with this club of mine; my foot alone will fight with all the strength you have. And if my arm is raised in the air, your grave is already dug in the earth. As for words, whose bragging answers words, let this sword of mine say what speech holds back."]],"869":[[0,4,"By my courage, the most perfect champion I ever heard! Steel, if your edge turns, or you don't carve this big-boned peasant into joints of beef before you sleep in your sheath, I pray God on my knees that you may be turned into hobnails. (They fight. Cade falls) Oh, I'm killed!"],[5,9,"Hunger and nothing else has killed me. Let ten thousand devils come against me, and give me the ten meals I've missed, and I'd defy them all. Wither, garden, and be from now on a graveyard for everyone who lives in this house, because the unconquered soul of Cade has fled."]],"877":[[0,3,"So York comes from Ireland to claim his right and pluck the crown from feeble Henry's head. Ring loud, bells; burn clear and bright, bonfires, to welcome great England's lawful king."],[4,10,"Ah, sacred majesty! Who wouldn't pay dearly for you? Let those obey who don't know how to rule. This hand was made to hold nothing but gold. I can't put my words into action unless a sword or sceptre gives it weight. It will have a sceptre, as surely as I have a soul, and on it I'll toss the fleur-de-lis of France."]],"883":[[0,8,"(Aside) I can hardly speak, I'm so angry. Oh, I could hack up rocks and fight with flint, these insulting words make me so furious; and now, like Ajax, son of Telamon, I could spend my fury on sheep or oxen. I'm far better born than the king, more like a king, more kingly in my thoughts. But I must pretend to be friendly a while longer, until Henry is weaker and I'm stronger."],[9,14,"Buckingham, please forgive me for not answering all this time; my mind was troubled with deep sadness. The reason I've brought this army here is to remove proud Somerset from the king, since he is a traitor to his grace and the state."]],"887":[[0,3,"Then, Buckingham, I'll dismiss my forces. Soldiers, thank you all; break up; meet me tomorrow in St George's Field, and you'll have your pay and everything you want."],[4,9,"And let my sovereign, virtuous Henry, demand my eldest son, or all my sons, as pledges of my loyalty and love; I'll send them all as willingly as I live. Lands, goods, horses, armour, anything I have is his to use, as long as Somerset dies."]],"907":[[0,5,"What's this! Is Somerset free? Then, York, let loose your long-imprisoned thoughts, and let your tongue match your heart. Shall I put up with the sight of Somerset? False king! Why have you broken your word to me, knowing how badly I take being cheated?"],[6,11,"Did I call you king? No, you're not king, not fit to govern and rule the people, since you don't dare, no, and can't, rule even a traitor. That head of yours doesn't suit a crown. Your hand was made to grip a pilgrim's staff, not to grace an awe-inspiring royal sceptre."],[12,18,"That gold must circle these brows of mine, whose smile and frown, like Achilles' spear, can kill or cure as they change. Here is a hand to hold up a sceptre and to enforce laws with it. Make way; by heaven, you'll rule no longer over the man heaven made to rule you."]],"937":[[0,4,"Why, Warwick, has your knee forgotten how to bow? Old Salisbury, shame on your silver hair, you mad misleader of your brainsick son! What, will you play the thug on your deathbed and go looking for sorrow with your spectacles on?"],[5,13,"Oh, where is faith? Where is loyalty? If it's banished from the white-haired head, where on earth will it find a home? Will you dig up war from your grave and shame your honourable old age with blood? Why are you old and lack experience? Or why do you misuse it, if you have it? For shame! Do your duty and bend your knee to me, you who are already bending to the grave with old age."]],"979":[[0,9,"Shame and confusion! Everyone is running away. Fear creates disorder, and disorder wounds where it should protect. O war, son of hell, whom the angry heavens use as their servant, throw hot coals of vengeance into the frozen hearts of our side! Let no soldier run away. A man truly devoted to war has no love for himself; and a man who loves himself is not truly brave, only brave by accident. (Seeing his dead father)"],[10,21,"Oh, let the vile world end, and the promised flames of Judgement Day weld earth and heaven together! Now let the last trumpet blow, and all small, individual sounds stop! Were you destined, dear father, to spend your youth in peace, and to reach the silver uniform of wise old age, and then, in your venerable armchair years, to die like this in a brutal battle? At this sight my heart has turned to stone, and while it's mine it will stay stone. York doesn't spare our old men;"],[22,30,"no more will I spare their babies. Girls' tears will be to me like dew on fire, and beauty, which often softens a tyrant, will be oil and flax to my flaming anger. From now on I'll have nothing to do with pity. If I meet a baby of the house of York, I'll cut it into as many lumps as wild Medea cut young Absyrtus. I'll seek my fame in cruelty."],[31,35,"Come, you new ruin of old Clifford's house. As Aeneas carried old Anchises, so I carry you on my manly shoulders. But Aeneas carried a living load, nothing as heavy as my grief."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"faction":["Faction and division","Lords who plot against each other, roses and badges, a court that tears itself apart."],"ambition":["Ambition and the crown","York, the Duchess, Suffolk, the Cardinal, Cade: everyone reaches for more."],"kingship":["Kingship and weakness","A pious king who cannot rule, and what happens when the centre does not hold."],"justice":["Law, justice and murder","Trials, combats, false charges and a murder that the law cannot stop."],"women":["Women and power","Margaret and Eleanor: two ambitious women in a man's court."],"commons":["The commons and rebellion","Petitioners, apprentices, the angry commons and Cade's rising."],"prophecy":["Prophecy, dreams and the supernatural","Dreams, a raised spirit, riddling oracles and a dying man's visions."],"fall":["Fortune, fall and death","Great people brought low: Gloucester, Eleanor, Suffolk, the Cardinal, Say, Cade."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"As by your high imperial Majesty","themes":["kingship","women"],"note":"Suffolk presents the Queen he won by proxy."},{"id":"2","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"The fairest queen that ever king receiv'd.","themes":["women"],"note":"Suffolk praises Margaret extravagantly."},{"id":"3","who":"KING","text":"A world of earthly blessings to my soul,","themes":["kingship","women"],"note":"Henry's joy in his bride."},{"id":"4","who":"QUEEN","text":"With you, mine alder-liefest sovereign,","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's first, courtly words."},{"id":"13","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Some sudden qualm hath struck me at the heart","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Gloucester cannot read on."},{"id":"16","who":"KING","text":"We here create thee the first duke of Suffolk,","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Henry rewards Suffolk."},{"id":"18","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Brave peers of England, pillars of the state,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Gloucester appeals to the lords."},{"id":"18","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Fatal this marriage, cancelling your fame,","themes":["faction","fall"],"note":"The marriage undoes England's glory."},{"id":"18","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Undoing all, as all had never been!","themes":["fall"],"note":"Everything won is lost."},{"id":"20","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Suffolk, the new-made duke that rules the roast,","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"Gloucester scorns Suffolk."},{"id":"20","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Agrees not with the leanness of his purse.","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's father is poor."},{"id":"21","who":"SALISBURY","text":"These counties were the keys of Normandy!","themes":["faction"],"note":"Salisbury on the loss."},{"id":"22","who":"WARWICK","text":"My sword should shed hot blood, mine eyes no tears.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Warwick weeps for Maine."},{"id":"23","who":"YORK","text":"For Suffolk's duke, may he be suffocate,","themes":["faction"],"note":"York curses Suffolk."},{"id":"26","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Rancour will out.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Gloucester to the Cardinal."},{"id":"26","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I prophesied France will be lost ere long.","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"Gloucester's parting prophecy."},{"id":"28","who":"CARDINAL","text":"He will be found a dangerous protector.","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"The Cardinal attacks Gloucester."},{"id":"29","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"We'll quickly hoise Duke Humphrey from his seat.","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"Buckingham joins the plot."},{"id":"32","who":"SOMERSET","text":"If Gloster be displac'd, he 'll be protector.","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"Somerset distrusts the Cardinal."},{"id":"35","who":"SALISBURY","text":"Pride went before, ambition follows him.","themes":["ambition","faction"],"note":"Salisbury sums up the lords."},{"id":"35","who":"SALISBURY","text":"Behoves it us to labour for the realm.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Salisbury's public spirit."},{"id":"37","who":"YORK","text":"[Aside] And so says York, for he hath greatest cause.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's aside."},{"id":"39","who":"WARWICK","text":"Main chance, father, you meant; but I meant Maine,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Warwick's pun."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"'T is thine they give away, and not their own.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's soliloquy."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"So York must sit and fret and bite his tongue,","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York must wait."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"A day will come when York shall claim his own;","themes":["ambition","prophecy"],"note":"York's resolve."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"For that 's the golden mark I seek to hit.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"The crown as a target."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"Whose church-like humours fits not for a crown.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"York on Henry."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"Watch thou and wake when others be asleep,","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's strategy."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"Then will I raise aloft the milk-white rose,","themes":["ambition","faction"],"note":"The white rose."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"Whose bookish rule hath pull'd fair England down.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"York condemns Henry's rule."},{"id":"45","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Why droops my lord, like over-ripen'd corn,","themes":["ambition","women"],"note":"Eleanor goads Gloucester."},{"id":"45","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Put forth thy hand, reach at the glorious gold.","themes":["ambition","women"],"note":"Eleanor urges him to reach for the crown."},{"id":"46","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Banish the canker of ambitious thoughts;","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Gloucester rebukes her."},{"id":"46","who":"GLOSTER","text":"My troublous dreams this night doth make me sad.","themes":["prophecy"],"note":"Gloucester's dream."},{"id":"48","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Were plac'd the heads of Edmund Duke of Somerset","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"The dream foretells deaths."},{"id":"49","who":"DUCHESS","text":"And on my head did set the diadem.","themes":["ambition","prophecy","women"],"note":"Eleanor's dream."},{"id":"50","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Presumptuous dame, ill-nurtur'd Eleanor,","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester scolds his wife."},{"id":"58","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Were I a man, a duke, and next of blood,","themes":["ambition","women"],"note":"Eleanor's resentment."},{"id":"58","who":"DUCHESS","text":"To play my part in Fortune's pageant.","themes":["ambition","fall"],"note":"Eleanor's resolve."},{"id":"67","who":"HUME","text":"Gold cannot come amiss, were she a devil.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Hume on gold."},{"id":"67","who":"HUME","text":"Yet am I Suffolk and the cardinal's broker.","themes":["faction","justice"],"note":"Hume reveals the trap."},{"id":"67","who":"HUME","text":"And her attainture will be Humphrey's fall.","themes":["fall"],"note":"Hume predicts the result."},{"id":"67","who":"HUME","text":"Sort how it will, I shall have gold for all.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Hume's couplet."},{"id":"80","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Against the Duke of Suffolk for","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"The petition against Suffolk."},{"id":"89","who":"QUEEN","text":"Away, base cullions!—Suffolk, let them go.","themes":["commons","women"],"note":"Margaret dismisses petitioners."},{"id":"92","who":"QUEEN","text":"Is this the fashion in the court of England?","themes":["kingship","women"],"note":"Margaret's complaint."},{"id":"92","who":"QUEEN","text":"But all his mind is bent to holiness,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Margaret on Henry."},{"id":"92","who":"QUEEN","text":"I would the college of the cardinals","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Margaret mocks Henry."},{"id":"96","who":"QUEEN","text":"More like an empress than Duke Humphrey's wife.","themes":["women","faction"],"note":"Margaret on Eleanor."},{"id":"96","who":"QUEEN","text":"Contemptuous base-born callat as she is,","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret insults Eleanor."},{"id":"97","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Madam, myself have lim'd a bush for her,","themes":["faction","justice"],"note":"Suffolk's trap."},{"id":"97","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"So, one by one, we'll weed them all at last,","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"Suffolk's plan."},{"id":"97","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"And you yourself shall steer the happy helm.","themes":["women","ambition"],"note":"Suffolk promises Margaret power."},{"id":"99","who":"KING","text":"Or Somerset or York, all's one to me.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry cannot decide."},{"id":"106","who":"WARWICK","text":"Warwick may live to be the best of all.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Warwick's retort."},{"id":"109","who":"GLOSTER","text":"To give his censure; these are no women's matters.","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester to Margaret."},{"id":"112","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Since thou wert king—as who is king but thou?—","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Suffolk accuses Gloucester."},{"id":"116","who":"QUEEN","text":"Would make thee quickly hop without thy head.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Margaret's threat."},{"id":"121","who":"DUCHESS","text":"I'd set my ten commandments in your face.","themes":["women"],"note":"Eleanor's revenge threat."},{"id":"123","who":"DUCHESS","text":"She'll hamper thee and dandle thee like a baby.","themes":["women","kingship"],"note":"Eleanor warns Henry."},{"id":"125","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"She'll gallop far enough to her destruction.","themes":["fall","women"],"note":"Buckingham on Eleanor."},{"id":"128","who":"GLOSTER","text":"As I in duty love my king and country!","themes":["justice"],"note":"Gloucester's loyalty."},{"id":"130","who":"YORK","text":"My Lord of Somerset will keep me here,","themes":["faction"],"note":"York's complaint."},{"id":"133","who":"WARWICK","text":"Image of pride, why should I hold my peace?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Warwick to Suffolk."},{"id":"148","who":"PETER","text":"The spite of man prevaileth against me.","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Peter's fear."},{"id":"160","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"Deep night, dark night, the silent of the night,","themes":["prophecy"],"note":"The hour of conjuring."},{"id":"166","who":"SPIRIT","text":"The duke yet lives that Henry shall depose,","themes":["prophecy"],"note":"The first prophecy."},{"id":"169","who":"SPIRIT","text":"By water shall he die and take his end.","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"Suffolk's fate."},{"id":"171","who":"SPIRIT","text":"Let him shun castles;","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"Somerset's fate."},{"id":"175","who":"YORK","text":"Lay hands upon these traitors and their trash.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The arrest."},{"id":"181","who":"YORK","text":"These oracles are hardly attain'd,","themes":["prophecy"],"note":"York on the prophecies."},{"id":"181","who":"YORK","text":"A sorry breakfast for my lord protector.","themes":["fall"],"note":"York relishes the news."},{"id":"191","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"They know their master loves to be aloft,","themes":["ambition","faction"],"note":"Suffolk's jibe."},{"id":"192","who":"GLOSTER","text":"'t is but a base ignoble mind","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Gloucester's reply."},{"id":"196","who":"CARDINAL","text":"Thy heaven is on earth; thine eyes and thoughts","themes":["ambition"],"note":"The Cardinal accuses Gloucester."},{"id":"197","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Tantaene animis coelestibus irae?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Gloucester quotes Virgil."},{"id":"203","who":"KING","text":"For blessed are the peacemakers on earth.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry quotes scripture."},{"id":"217","who":"KING","text":"When such strings jar, what hope of harmony?","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Henry's image of discord."},{"id":"229","who":"SIMPCOX","text":"Born blind, an 't please your grace.","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Simpcox's lie."},{"id":"270","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Then, Saunder, sit there, the lyingest knave in","themes":["justice"],"note":"Gloucester exposes Simpcox."},{"id":"284","who":"KING","text":"O God, seest Thou this, and bearest so long?","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry shocked by the fraud."},{"id":"292","who":"GLOSTER","text":"You made in a day, my lord, whole towns to fly.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Gloucester's jibe at Suffolk."},{"id":"295","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Such as my heart doth tremble to unfold.","themes":["fall","justice"],"note":"News of the arrest."},{"id":"299","who":"QUEEN","text":"Gloster, see here the tainture of thy nest;","themes":["women","fall"],"note":"Margaret gloats."},{"id":"300","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And give her as a prey to law and shame,","themes":["justice","women"],"note":"Gloucester disowns his wife if guilty."},{"id":"308","who":"YORK","text":"Edward the Third, my lords, had seven sons:","themes":["ambition","kingship"],"note":"York's genealogy."},{"id":"308","who":"YORK","text":"Harmless Richard was murther'd traitorously.","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"The murder of Richard II."},{"id":"314","who":"YORK","text":"So, if the issue of the elder son","themes":["ambition","kingship"],"note":"York's conclusion."},{"id":"316","who":"SALISBURY AND WARWICK","text":"Long live our sovereign Richard, England's king!","themes":["ambition","faction"],"note":"The Nevilles hail York."},{"id":"317","who":"YORK","text":"Till they have snar'd the shepherd of the flock,","themes":["faction"],"note":"York on Gloucester."},{"id":"317","who":"YORK","text":"Shall find their deaths, if York can prophesy.","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"York's prediction."},{"id":"319","who":"WARWICK","text":"Shall one day make the Duke of York a king.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Warwick's vow."},{"id":"325","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Welcome is banishment; welcome were my death.","themes":["fall"],"note":"Eleanor's response."},{"id":"329","who":"KING","text":"Give up thy staff; Henry will to himself","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry dismisses Gloucester."},{"id":"330","who":"QUEEN","text":"God and King Henry govern England's realm.","themes":["kingship","women"],"note":"Margaret's demand."},{"id":"331","who":"GLOSTER","text":"May honourable peace attend thy throne!","themes":["kingship","fall"],"note":"Gloucester's farewell."},{"id":"334","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Thus droops this lofty pine and hangs his sprays;","themes":["fall"],"note":"Suffolk on Gloucester."},{"id":"337","who":"KING","text":"Here let them end it; and God defend the right!","themes":["justice"],"note":"Henry orders the combat."},{"id":"355","who":"HORNER","text":"Hold, Peter, hold! I confess, I confess treason.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Horner's confession."},{"id":"363","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Thus sometimes hath the brightest day a cloud,","themes":["fall"],"note":"Gloucester's reflection."},{"id":"371","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Ah, Gloster, teach me to forget myself!","themes":["fall","women"],"note":"Eleanor's penance."},{"id":"371","who":"DUCHESS","text":"To think upon my pomp shall be my hell.","themes":["fall"],"note":"Eleanor's grief."},{"id":"371","who":"DUCHESS","text":"Have all lim'd bushes to betray thy wings,","themes":["faction","prophecy"],"note":"Eleanor's warning."},{"id":"372","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I must offend before I be attainted;","themes":["justice"],"note":"Gloucester's faith in law."},{"id":"393","who":"DUCHESS","text":"My shame will not be shifted with my sheet;","themes":["fall"],"note":"Eleanor departs."},{"id":"398","who":"QUEEN","text":"Small curs are not regarded when they grin,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Margaret on Gloucester."},{"id":"398","who":"QUEEN","text":"Now 't is the spring and weeds are shallow-rooted;","themes":["faction"],"note":"Margaret's garden image."},{"id":"399","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Smooth runs the water where the brook is deep,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Suffolk's proverb."},{"id":"399","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"The fox barks not when he would steal the lamb.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Suffolk on Gloucester."},{"id":"403","who":"KING","text":"As is the sucking lamb or harmless dove.","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Henry defends Gloucester."},{"id":"404","who":"QUEEN","text":"Seems he a dove? his feathers are but borrow'd,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Margaret's reply."},{"id":"408","who":"SOMERSET","text":"That all your interest in those territories","themes":["fall"],"note":"All of France is lost."},{"id":"414","who":"GLOSTER","text":"A heart unspotted is not easily daunted.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Gloucester faces arrest."},{"id":"423","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Virtue is chok'd with foul ambition,","themes":["ambition","justice"],"note":"Gloucester's great speech."},{"id":"423","who":"GLOSTER","text":"But mine is made the prologue to their play,","themes":["prophecy","fall"],"note":"Gloucester foresees more deaths."},{"id":"423","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And dogged York, that reaches at the moon,","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Gloucester on York."},{"id":"423","who":"GLOSTER","text":"'A staff is quickly found to beat a dog.'","themes":["justice"],"note":"Gloucester's proverb."},{"id":"430","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Ah, thus King Henry throws away his crutch","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Gloucester's farewell."},{"id":"430","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And wolves are gnarling who shall gnaw thee first.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Gloucester's warning."},{"id":"434","who":"KING","text":"The map of honour, truth, and loyalty;","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Henry on Gloucester."},{"id":"434","who":"KING","text":"Say 'Who's a traitor? Gloster he is none.'","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Henry's lament."},{"id":"436","who":"QUEEN","text":"Free lords, cold snow melts with the sun's hot beams.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Margaret on Henry's softness."},{"id":"437","who":"CARDINAL","text":"That he should die is worthy policy,","themes":["justice","faction"],"note":"The Cardinal agrees to murder."},{"id":"443","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"To make the fox surveyor of the fold?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Suffolk argues for murder."},{"id":"445","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Say but the word, and I will be his priest.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Suffolk volunteers."},{"id":"447","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Here is my hand, the deed is worthy doing.","themes":["justice","faction"],"note":"The pact."},{"id":"455","who":"YORK","text":"Men's flesh preserv'd so whole do seldom win.","themes":["faction"],"note":"York mocks Somerset."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"Now, York, or never, steel thy fearful thoughts,","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's resolve."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"My brain more busy than the labouring spider","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's plotting."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"I fear me you but warm the starved snake,","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"York's irony."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"You put sharp weapons in a madman's hands.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York on his army."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"I will stir up in England some black storm","themes":["ambition","commons"],"note":"York's plan."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"I have seduc'd a headstrong Kentishman,","themes":["commons","ambition"],"note":"York names Cade."},{"id":"468","who":"YORK","text":"And reap the harvest which that rascal sow'd;","themes":["ambition","commons"],"note":"York's aim."},{"id":"491","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Dead in his bed, my lord; Gloster is dead.","themes":["fall","justice"],"note":"Suffolk's announcement."},{"id":"502","who":"KING","text":"Hide not thy poison with such sugar'd words;","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Henry turns on Suffolk."},{"id":"503","who":"QUEEN","text":"To be a queen, and crown'd with infamy!","themes":["women","fall"],"note":"Margaret's self-pity."},{"id":"505","who":"QUEEN","text":"Erect his statue and worship it,","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's jealousy."},{"id":"505","who":"QUEEN","text":"I took a costly jewel from my neck—","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's memory."},{"id":"505","who":"QUEEN","text":"Am I not witch'd like her? or thou not false like him?","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret as Dido."},{"id":"507","who":"WARWICK","text":"The commons, like an angry hive of bees","themes":["commons"],"note":"Warwick on the commons."},{"id":"517","who":"WARWICK","text":"See how the blood is settled in his face.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Warwick's evidence."},{"id":"517","who":"WARWICK","text":"It cannot be but he was murther'd here;","themes":["justice"],"note":"Warwick's verdict."},{"id":"521","who":"WARWICK","text":"Who finds the heifer dead and bleeding fresh","themes":["justice"],"note":"Warwick's argument."},{"id":"533","who":"KING","text":"Thrice is he arm'd that hath his quarrel just,","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry's proverb."},{"id":"539","who":"SALISBURY","text":"They will by violence tear him from your palace","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"The commons' demand."},{"id":"541","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Sent from a sort of tinkers to the king.","themes":["commons"],"note":"Suffolk's scorn."},{"id":"543","who":"KING","text":"He shall not breathe infection in this air","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry banishes Suffolk."},{"id":"551","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Poison be their drink!","themes":["faction"],"note":"Suffolk's curse."},{"id":"555","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"A wilderness is populous enough,","themes":["women"],"note":"Suffolk's farewell."},{"id":"555","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"For where thou art, there is the world itself,","themes":["women"],"note":"Suffolk to Margaret."},{"id":"562","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"If I depart from thee, I cannot live;","themes":["women","fall"],"note":"Suffolk's despair."},{"id":"572","who":"CARDINAL","text":"If thou be'st Death, I'll give thee England's treasure,","themes":["fall","prophecy"],"note":"The Cardinal's terror."},{"id":"575","who":"CARDINAL","text":"Comb down his hair; look, look! it stands upright,","themes":["prophecy","justice"],"note":"The Cardinal sees Gloucester."},{"id":"579","who":"KING","text":"He dies, and makes no sign.—O God, forgive him!","themes":["fall","justice"],"note":"The Cardinal's death."},{"id":"580","who":"WARWICK","text":"So bad a death argues a monstrous life.","themes":["fall","justice"],"note":"Warwick's verdict."},{"id":"581","who":"KING","text":"Forbear to judge, for we are sinners all.","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Henry's charity."},{"id":"585","who":"CAPTAIN","text":"The gaudy, blabbing, and remorseful day","themes":["fall"],"note":"The Captain's opening."},{"id":"596","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Thy name affrights me, in whose sound is death.","themes":["prophecy"],"note":"Suffolk hears 'Walter'."},{"id":"610","who":"CAPTAIN","text":"Ay, kennel, puddle, sink, whose filth and dirt","themes":["fall","commons"],"note":"The Captain on Suffolk."},{"id":"610","who":"CAPTAIN","text":"By devilish policy art thou grown great","themes":["ambition","justice"],"note":"The Captain's charge."},{"id":"610","who":"CAPTAIN","text":"The commons here in Kent are up in arms;","themes":["commons"],"note":"News of rebellion."},{"id":"617","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"True nobility is exempt from fear;","themes":["fall"],"note":"Suffolk's pride."},{"id":"619","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Great men oft die by vile bezonians:","themes":["fall"],"note":"Suffolk's last words."},{"id":"633","who":"HOLLAND","text":"it was never merry world in England since gentlemen came up.","themes":["commons"],"note":"The rebels' grievance."},{"id":"638","who":"BEVIS","text":"there's no better sign of a brave","themes":["commons"],"note":"The rebels' values."},{"id":"651","who":"CADE","text":"My father was a Mortimer,—","themes":["ambition","commons"],"note":"Cade's claim."},{"id":"667","who":"CADE","text":"There shall be in England seven halfpenny loaves","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade's promises."},{"id":"667","who":"CADE","text":"All the realm shall be","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade's communism."},{"id":"670","who":"DICK","text":"The first thing we do, let's kill all the lawyers.","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Dick's proposal."},{"id":"671","who":"CADE","text":"Is not this a lamentable thing, that","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Cade on parchment."},{"id":"686","who":"CADE","text":"hang him with his pen and inkhorn","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"The clerk's fate."},{"id":"700","who":"CADE","text":"And Adam was a gardener.","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade's retort."},{"id":"712","who":"CADE","text":"He lies, for I invented it myself.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"Cade's aside."},{"id":"723","who":"CADE","text":"then are we in order when we are most out of","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade's paradox."},{"id":"793","who":"CADE","text":"Thou hast most","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Cade's charge against Say."},{"id":"800","who":"CADE","text":"Away with him, away with him! he speaks Latin.","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade's response."},{"id":"801","who":"SAY","text":"And seeing ignorance is the curse of God,","themes":["commons","justice"],"note":"Say's defence."},{"id":"801","who":"SAY","text":"Knowledge the wing wherewith we fly to heaven,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Say on knowledge."},{"id":"813","who":"CADE","text":"I feel remorse in myself with his words, but I'll bridle","themes":["justice","commons"],"note":"Cade's aside."},{"id":"787","who":"CADE","text":"My mouth shall be the parliament","themes":["commons","kingship"],"note":"Cade's decree."},{"id":"823","who":"CADE","text":"Let them kiss one another,","themes":["commons","fall"],"note":"The heads on poles."},{"id":"836","who":"CADE","text":"Was ever feather so lightly blown to and fro as this","themes":["commons"],"note":"Cade on the crowd."},{"id":"834","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Better ten thousand base-born Cades miscarry","themes":["commons","faction"],"note":"Clifford's appeal."},{"id":"844","who":"KING","text":"No sooner was I crept out of my cradle","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's lament."},{"id":"844","who":"KING","text":"As I do long and wish to be a subject.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's wish."},{"id":"854","who":"KING","text":"Thus stands my state, 'twixt Cade and York distress'd,","themes":["kingship","commons"],"note":"Henry between two threats."},{"id":"858","who":"KING","text":"For yet may England curse my wretched reign.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's self-judgement."},{"id":"862","who":"CADE","text":"Fie on ambitions!","themes":["ambition","fall"],"note":"Cade starving."},{"id":"864","who":"IDEN","text":"This small inheritance my father left me","themes":["commons"],"note":"Iden's content."},{"id":"864","who":"IDEN","text":"And sends the poor well pleased from my gate.","themes":["commons"],"note":"Iden's charity."},{"id":"869","who":"CADE","text":"famine and no other hath slain me;","themes":["fall","commons"],"note":"Cade's death."},{"id":"871","who":"CADE","text":"Tell Kent from","themes":["commons","fall"],"note":"Cade's last words."},{"id":"877","who":"YORK","text":"From Ireland thus comes York to claim his right,","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's return."},{"id":"877","who":"YORK","text":"This hand was made to handle nought but gold.","themes":["ambition","kingship"],"note":"York's hand."},{"id":"883","who":"YORK","text":"I am far better born than is the king,","themes":["ambition","kingship"],"note":"York's aside."},{"id":"907","who":"YORK","text":"False king! why hast thou broken faith with me,","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"York's outburst."},{"id":"907","who":"YORK","text":"That head of thine doth not become a crown;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"York to Henry."},{"id":"907","who":"YORK","text":"Thy hand is made to grasp a palmer's staff,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"York mocks Henry's piety."},{"id":"914","who":"YORK","text":"O blood-bespotted Neapolitan,","themes":["women","faction"],"note":"York insults Margaret."},{"id":"930","who":"YORK","text":"Call hither to the stake my two brave bears,","themes":["faction"],"note":"York calls the Nevilles."},{"id":"934","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Hence, heap of wrath, foul indigested lump,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Clifford on Richard."},{"id":"937","who":"KING","text":"O, where is faith? O, where is loyalty?","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Henry's appeal."},{"id":"942","who":"SALISBURY","text":"It is great sin to swear unto a sin,","themes":["justice","faction"],"note":"Salisbury breaks his oath."},{"id":"945","who":"YORK","text":"I am resolv'd for death or dignity.","themes":["ambition"],"note":"York's choice."},{"id":"949","who":"WARWICK","text":"The rampant bear chain'd to the ragged staff,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Warwick's badge."},{"id":"952","who":"RICHARD","text":"For you shall sup with Jesu Christ to-night.","themes":["faction","fall"],"note":"Richard to Clifford."},{"id":"974","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"La fin couronne les oeuvres.","themes":["fall"],"note":"Clifford's last words."},{"id":"976","who":"YORK","text":"Thus war hath given thee peace, for thou art still.","themes":["fall"],"note":"York over Clifford."},{"id":"979","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","text":"O war, thou son of hell,","themes":["faction","fall"],"note":"Young Clifford's curse."},{"id":"979","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","text":"York not our old men spares;","themes":["faction"],"note":"Young Clifford's vow."},{"id":"979","who":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","text":"In cruelty will I seek out my fame.","themes":["faction","fall"],"note":"Young Clifford's vow."},{"id":"981","who":"RICHARD","text":"Priests pray for enemies, but princes kill.","themes":["faction","ambition"],"note":"Richard over Somerset."},{"id":"985","who":"KING","text":"Can we outrun the heavens? good Margaret, stay.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's fatalism."},{"id":"986","who":"QUEEN","text":"What are you made of? you'll nor fight nor fly;","themes":["kingship","women"],"note":"Margaret's scorn."},{"id":"994","who":"YORK","text":"That winter lion, who in rage forgets","themes":["fall"],"note":"York on Salisbury."},{"id":"999","who":"WARWICK","text":"Saint Alban's battle won by famous York","themes":["ambition","faction"],"note":"Warwick's boast."}]};
const EX={"intro":["The Second Part of King Henry the Sixth begins where Part 1 ended: Suffolk brings home Margaret of Anjou as Henry's queen, and the price is the surrender of Anjou and Maine. The lords who quarrelled in France now turn on each other at home.","The first half is a political thriller: the Queen, Suffolk, the Cardinal, Buckingham and York trap Gloucester's ambitious wife, strip the good Duke Humphrey of office and murder him. Each conspirator then meets a fitting end: Suffolk is beheaded by pirates, the Cardinal dies raving.","The second half shows what happens when the centre gives way. York, given an army for Ireland, sets up Jack Cade to lead a rising of the commons that takes London. When Cade is beaten, York returns, claims the crown openly, and the play ends with the first battle of the Wars of the Roses at St Albans."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Second Part of Henry the Sixth, with the death of the Good Duke Humphrey (Folio title: The second Part of Henry the Sixt)"],["Written","About 1591; an earlier version was printed in 1594 as 'The First Part of the Contention betwixt the two famous Houses of York and Lancaster'. First printed in its Folio form in 1623"],["Genre","History: one of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio. The second play of the first tetralogy (1, 2 and 3 Henry VI, Richard III)"],["Setting","England (London, St Albans, Bury St Edmunds, Kent, Kenilworth) and the Kent coast, from 1445 to 1455"],["Protagonist","No single hero: Gloucester dominates the first half, York and Cade the second"],["Antagonists","Queen Margaret, Suffolk and the Cardinal against Gloucester; York against the house of Lancaster"],["Inciting incident","The marriage treaty that gives away Anjou and Maine (1.1)"],["Turning point","Gloucester's murder (3.2), which removes the King's protector and lets York loose"],["Climax","York's open claim to the crown when he sees Somerset free (5.1)"],["Resolution","The Yorkists win the first battle of St Albans; Henry flees to London"],["Time span","About ten years of history compressed into a few weeks of stage time"],["Major conflicts","Gloucester against his enemies; Margaret against Eleanor; the commons against the lords; York against Lancaster"],["Themes","Faction, ambition, weak kingship, justice and murder, women and power, the commons and rebellion, prophecy, the fall of the great"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Suffolk presents Margaret; Anjou and Maine are given away","id":"10"},{"s":"a1s1","t":"York resolves to claim the crown","id":"41"},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Gloucester and Eleanor tell their dreams","id":"48"},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Hume reveals the trap set for the Duchess","id":"67"},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Peter accuses his master of treason","id":"82"},{"s":"a1s3","t":"The Queen strikes the Duchess","id":"118"},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Somerset made Regent of France","id":"145"},{"s":"a1s4","t":"The spirit prophesies; the Duchess is arrested","id":"169"},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Gloucester exposes Simpcox's false miracle","id":"270"},{"s":"a2s1","t":"News of the Duchess's arrest","id":"295"},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Salisbury and Warwick kneel to York as king","id":"316"},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The Duchess sentenced; Gloucester gives up his staff","id":"331"},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Peter defeats Horner","id":"355"},{"s":"a2s4","t":"The Duchess does public penance","id":"371"},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Gloucester arrested for treason at Bury","id":"413"},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The plotters agree to murder Gloucester","id":"447"},{"s":"a3s1","t":"York given an army; reveals the Cade plot","id":"468"},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Gloucester found dead; Henry faints","id":"491"},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Warwick proves murder","id":"517"},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Suffolk banished; farewell with Margaret","id":"546"},{"s":"a3s3","t":"The Cardinal dies raving","id":"579"},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Suffolk beheaded by pirates","id":"619"},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Cade's rising; 'kill all the lawyers'","id":"670"},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Margaret mourns over Suffolk's head","id":"737"},{"s":"a4s7","t":"Lord Say beheaded","id":"813"},{"s":"a4s8","t":"Cade's followers desert him","id":"836"},{"s":"a4s10","t":"Iden kills Cade","id":"869"},{"s":"a5s1","t":"York openly claims the crown","id":"907"},{"s":"a5s2","t":"York kills Clifford; Richard kills Somerset","id":"981"},{"s":"a5s3","t":"The Yorkists win St Albans","id":"999"}],"irony":{"3":"Henry calls Margaret a world of blessings; she will despise him and love Suffolk.","16":"Henry rewards Suffolk for the treaty that loses France and for the marriage that will undo him.","18":"Gloucester appeals to the lords' honour; most of them are already plotting against him.","35":"Salisbury speaks of the public good; within a few scenes he kneels to York.","37":"York joins the vow for the public good while thinking of his own claim.","46":"Gloucester's dream foretells the deaths of Suffolk and Somerset, and his own fall.","67":"The Duchess thinks Hume serves her; he serves her enemies.","203":"Henry quotes the Beatitudes to two men who have just arranged a duel.","229":"Henry is ready to believe a miracle that Gloucester exposes in minutes.","331":"Gloucester blesses a reign that will end in civil war.","355":"The combat is decided by drink, not by God, but Henry calls it justice.","372":"Gloucester trusts the law; his enemies are the law.","403":"Henry describes Gloucester exactly; he still lets him be taken.","445":"Suffolk's joke about being a priest to the dying man becomes literal: he arranges the murder.","487":"Margaret prays for Gloucester's acquittal while knowing he is already dead.","501":"The murderer offers comfort to the victim's nephew.","533":"Henry's faith in a just cause is spoken as two armed men fight over a murder.","742":"Say expects the King to defeat Cade; he will be beheaded before that happens.","760":"Say echoes Gloucester's fatal trust in innocence.","887":"York dismisses his army, and within minutes is ready to fight for the crown."},"devices":{"beast":"Animals and hunting","garden":"Gardens, weeds and trees","storm":"Storms, sea and fire","head":"Heads, hands and the body","stage":"Theatre and show","light":"Heaven, hell and the soul"},"dev":{"18":[["head","The lords as pillars holding up the state.","pillars of the state"]],"45":[["garden","Gloucester bowed like ripe corn.","over-ripen'd corn"],["head","Lifting the crown with joined hands.","reach at the glorious gold"]],"48":[["head","Heads on the broken staff.","Were plac'd the heads"]],"58":[["stage","Life as a pageant directed by Fortune.","Fortune's pageant"]],"97":[["garden","Weeding the court of enemies.","weed them all at last"]],"334":[["garden","Gloucester as a fallen pine.","lofty pine"]],"363":[["storm","The turning seasons of fortune.","brightest day a cloud"]],"371":[["stage","Eleanor as a public spectacle.","pointing-stock"]],"398":[["beast","The lords as dogs and lions.","Small curs are not regarded when they grin,"],["garden","Enemies as spring weeds.","weeds are shallow-rooted"]],"404":[["beast","Gloucester as a raven in dove's feathers.","hateful raven"]],"410":[["garden","York's hopes blasted like blossoms.","blossoms blasted in the bud"]],"423":[["stage","Politics as a play with a prologue.","prologue to their play"]],"430":[["beast","The lords as wolves around the King.","wolves are gnarling"]],"434":[["beast","Gloucester as a calf taken to slaughter.","as the butcher takes away the calf"]],"436":[["beast","Gloucester as a weeping crocodile and a snake.","mournful crocodile"]],"443":[["beast","Gloucester as the fox, the court as the fold.","fox surveyor of the fold"]],"456":[["storm","Quarrel as fire fed by wind.","raging fire"]],"468":[["beast","York as a spider spinning snares, and a warmed snake.","labouring spider"],["storm","Civil war as a black storm.","black storm"]],"505":[["storm","Margaret's voyage through storms to England.","dreadful rock"]],"517":[["head","The murdered body read as evidence.","His hands abroad display'd"]],"521":[["beast","Murder as butchery and hawking.","partridge in the puttock's nest"]],"572":[["light","Death bribed with treasure.","England's treasure"]],"850":[["light","Heaven's gates opened in thanks.","everlasting gates"]],"854":[["storm","Henry's kingdom as a storm-tossed ship boarded by a pirate.","having scap'd a tempest"]],"907":[["head","Henry's head unfit for a crown, his hand for a sceptre.","That head of thine doth not become a crown"]],"930":[["beast","The Nevilles as baited bears.","my two brave bears"]],"949":[["beast","The Neville bear on Warwick's helmet.","rampant bear chain'd to the ragged staff"]],"979":[["light","War as hell's son.","son of hell"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A royal palace in London","house":"The Duke of Gloucester's house","garden":"A walled garden","field":"Open country near St Albans","hall":"A hall of justice or a room of state","street":"A London street","abbey":"The abbey at Bury St Edmunds","bed":"The Cardinal's bedchamber","coast":"The coast of Kent","heath":"Blackheath, the rebels' camp","walls":"The Tower of London","castle":"Kenilworth Castle","battle":"The battlefield at St Albans"},"stagerules":[["bedchamber","bed"],["coast","coast"],["field of battle|fields near","battle"],["blackheath","heath"],["the tower","walls"],["kenilworth","castle"],["garden","garden"],["house","house"],["hall of justice|room of state","hall"],["abbey","abbey"],["palace","palace"],["street|smithfield|southwark","street"],["saint alban|fields","field"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The royal palace, London","a1s2":"The Duke of Gloucester's house","a1s3":"The royal palace, London","a1s4":"Gloucester's garden, at night","a2s1":"St Albans, hawking","a2s2":"The Duke of York's garden, London","a2s3":"A hall of justice","a2s4":"A London street","a3s1":"The abbey at Bury St Edmunds","a3s2":"A room of state at Bury St Edmunds","a3s3":"The Cardinal's bedchamber","a4s1":"The coast of Kent","a4s2":"Blackheath","a4s3":"Another part of Blackheath","a4s4":"The royal palace, London","a4s5":"The Tower of London","a4s6":"Cannon Street, London","a4s7":"Smithfield, London","a4s8":"Southwark","a4s9":"Kenilworth Castle","a4s10":"Iden's garden, Kent","a5s1":"Fields between Dartford and Blackheath","a5s2":"St Albans: the battle","a5s3":"Fields near St Albans"},"arc":{"York's power":[3,3,2,4,4,6,5,5,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,8,7,9,10,10],"Henry's authority":[6,6,5,5,5,5,6,5,3,4,4,3,3,2,2,2,1,1,4,5,5,3,1,0]},"arcnote":"York begins as a lord who has lost his regency and his hopes of France, with nothing but a private claim. He gains secret allies when the Nevilles kneel to him (2.2), an army when he is sent to Ireland (3.1), and a test of the people's mood through Cade's rising. Cade's collapse costs him little, and when he returns he claims the crown openly and wins at St Albans. Henry's authority is highest at his wedding and when he takes back the Protector's staff (2.3), but every step after that is forced on him: he cannot save Gloucester, banishes Suffolk only when the commons demand it, flees London before Cade and is dragged from the field at St Albans. The chart rises briefly when he pardons Cade's followers (4.8 to 4.9).","iago":[["a1s1","Anjou and Maine are given away: York sees his own inheritance lost and resolves to claim the crown when the time is right."],["a1s1","He decides to side with the Nevilles and pretend friendship to Gloucester."],["a1s3","Peter's charge against Horner damages York, and Somerset gets the regency of France."],["a1s4","York and Buckingham arrest the Duchess, starting Gloucester's fall."],["a2s2","York sets out his descent; Salisbury and Warwick kneel to him as king."],["a2s2","He tells them to wait until Gloucester's enemies have destroyed him and themselves."],["a3s1","York joins the charges against Gloucester and agrees to his murder."],["a3s1","Rebellion in Ireland: the lords give York the army he lacked."],["a3s1","York reveals that he has set up Jack Cade to rise as John Mortimer and test the people."],["a4s2","Cade claims Mortimer descent; Stafford's brother guesses York taught him."],["a4s7","Cade takes London, kills Say and burns the records."],["a4s9","York returns from Ireland with an army, demanding Somerset's arrest."],["a5s1","He disbands his army when told Somerset is in the Tower, then sees him free and claims the crown openly."],["a5s1","His sons and the Nevilles stand by him; Salisbury breaks his oath to Henry."],["a5s2","York kills Clifford; Richard kills Somerset."],["a5s3","The Yorkists win at St Albans and race the King to London."]],"rel":{"1":[["SUFFOLK","QUEEN","love"],["QUEEN","GLOSTER","hate"],["CARDINAL","GLOSTER","hate"],["DUCHESS","GLOSTER","love"],["QUEEN","DUCHESS","hate"],["HUME","DUCHESS","deceive"],["YORK","KING","deceive"],["SALISBURY","GLOSTER","trust"]],"2":[["GLOSTER","CARDINAL","hate"],["SALISBURY","YORK","trust"],["WARWICK","YORK","trust"],["KING","GLOSTER","love"],["GLOSTER","DUCHESS","love"],["PETER","HORNER","hate"]],"3":[["SUFFOLK","GLOSTER","hate"],["KING","GLOSTER","love"],["QUEEN","SUFFOLK","love"],["YORK","CADE","trust"],["WARWICK","SUFFOLK","hate"],["KING","SUFFOLK","hate"],["QUEEN","KING","deceive"]],"4":[["CAPTAIN","SUFFOLK","hate"],["CADE","SAY","hate"],["DICK","CADE","deceive"],["CLIFFORD","CADE","hate"],["IDEN","CADE","hate"],["KING","SAY","trust"],["QUEEN","SUFFOLK","love"]],"5":[["YORK","SOMERSET","hate"],["YORK","KING","hate"],["SALISBURY","YORK","trust"],["CLIFFORD","YORK","hate"],["YOUNG CLIFFORD","YORK","hate"],["RICHARD","SALISBURY","love"],["QUEEN","KING","trust"]]},"context":[["Date","Probably written about 1591. A shorter, different version was printed in 1594 as 'The First Part of the Contention betwixt the two famous Houses of York and Lancaster'; scholars still argue whether it is an early draft or a reconstruction of performances. The Folio text of 1623 is longer and fuller."],["Authorship","The play is in the 1623 Folio as Shakespeare's. Some scholars think he wrote it with collaborators, as was common in the early 1590s; the New Oxford Shakespeare (2016) credits Christopher Marlowe as a co-author of the Henry VI plays."],["Sources","Edward Hall's 'Union of the Two Noble and Illustre Families of Lancaster and York' (1548) and Raphael Holinshed's 'Chronicles' (1587). The Simpcox story comes from John Foxe's 'Acts and Monuments', which retold Sir Thomas More's account."],["The real history","The play covers 1445 (Margaret's arrival) to 1455 (the first battle of St Albans). Shakespeare moves Eleanor Cobham's fall (1441) after the marriage, and brings York's sons, who were children, into battle."],["Duke Humphrey","Humphrey of Gloucester was remembered as 'the good duke' and a patron of learning; his gift of books to Oxford is remembered in Duke Humfrey's Library in the Bodleian. He died under arrest at Bury in 1447, probably of a stroke, though many at the time believed he was murdered."],["Jack Cade","Cade led a rising in Kent in 1450, called himself John Mortimer, and published a list of grievances against corrupt royal officials. He entered London, executed Lord Say, lost the support of Londoners, and died after a royal pardon split his army. Shakespeare adds details from the Peasants' Revolt of 1381, such as the burning of records."],["Margaret of Anjou","Margaret came to England at fifteen. Later chronicles made her a forceful leader of the Lancastrian cause; the love affair with Suffolk is a dramatic invention built on rumours of her favour to him."],["The first tetralogy","2 Henry VI is the middle of a story that runs from the death of Henry the Fifth (1 Henry VI) through the civil wars (3 Henry VI) to the fall of Richard III. Young Clifford's vow and Richard's first appearance point forward."],["Rebellion in the 1590s","There were food riots and apprentice disturbances in London in the 1590s. Cade's rising could be read both as a warning against disorder and as a picture of real grievances: enclosure, taxes and corrupt law."],["Women and rule","Elizabeth I was an unmarried queen; Margaret and Eleanor show two ambitious women in a man's court, a subject Elizabethan audiences watched closely."],["Staging","The play was revived in the 1960s in John Barton and Peter Hall's 'The Wars of the Roses' for the Royal Shakespeare Company, and has been staged as part of the full cycle many times since."]],"critics":{"faction":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard saw the play as showing England punished for disorder, with Gloucester's murder removing the last check on faction and leading to civil war."],["Jan Kott, 'Shakespeare Our Contemporary' (1964)","Paraphrased: Kott described the histories as a 'Grand Mechanism' in which each climber reaches the top only to be thrown down by the next; the plotters of this play are a clear example."]],"commons":[["Annabel Patterson, 'Shakespeare and the Popular Voice' (1989)","Paraphrased: Patterson argued that the play takes the commons' grievances seriously, separating the real complaints (enclosure, taxation, corrupt officials) from Cade's absurd fantasies."],["Richard Wilson, 'Will Power' (1993)","Paraphrased: Wilson read the Cade scenes as hostile to popular rebellion, mocking the rebels' hatred of literacy for the benefit of a literate theatre audience."]],"kingship":[["Michael Hattaway, introduction to the New Cambridge edition (1991)","Paraphrased: Hattaway stressed that Henry's piety is not simply weakness; the play shows that Christian virtue cannot survive in a political world driven by power."],["Ronald Knowles, introduction to the Arden edition (1999)","Paraphrased: Knowles emphasised the play's concern with law and justice, and how Henry's failure to act lets law be turned into a weapon."]],"women":[["Phyllis Rackin, 'Stages of History' (1990)","Paraphrased: Rackin argued that the history plays associate women with disorder and the threat to a male line of descent, and that Margaret and Eleanor both challenge male authority."],["Nina Levine, 'Women's Matters' (1998)","Paraphrased: Levine linked Shakespeare's powerful women in the Henry VI plays to anxieties about female rule under Elizabeth I."]],"justice":[["Ronald Knowles, Arden edition (1999)","Paraphrased: Knowles traced how the trial by combat, the false miracle and the arrest of Gloucester show justice being staged and manipulated."],["Stephen Greenblatt, 'Murdering Peasants' (1983)","Paraphrased: Greenblatt discussed how representations of popular revolt, including Cade's, turn real social anger into comic grotesque."]],"ambition":[["Irving Ribner, 'The English History Play in the Age of Shakespeare' (1957)","Paraphrased: Ribner saw York as the play's driving force, a Machiavellian who works patiently through others."],["Edward Berry, 'Patterns of Decay' (1975)","Paraphrased: Berry described the play as a study in the decay of justice, in which ambition replaces law."]]},"symbols":{"Crown":"\\bcrown\\w*|\\bdiadem\\b|\\bsceptre\\b","Head":"\\bheads?\\b","Staff":"\\bstaff\\b","Blood":"\\bblood\\w*","Water":"\\bwater\\b|\\bsea\\b|\\bwalter\\b","Gold":"\\bgold\\w*"},"symbols2":[["The crown","York's 'golden mark', Eleanor's dream, Cade's mock-kingship: the crown is what every ambitious character reaches for."],["The Protector's staff","Gloucester's badge of office, broken in his dream and handed back in 2.3; its loss leaves Henry without his 'crutch'."],["Heads","Severed heads run through the play: Gloucester's dream, Suffolk's head in Margaret's lap, Say's and Cromer's on poles, Cade's brought to the King."],["Water","The spirit says Suffolk will die by water; Walter Whitmore kills him at sea."],["Castles","Somerset should 'shun castles'; he dies under an alehouse sign of a castle."],["Gardens","Gardens hide plots (Gloucester's, York's) and stand for order (Iden's); Margaret speaks of weeds to be pulled."],["The white rose","York's badge, from the quarrel in 1 Henry VI, raised again in his first soliloquy."],["Gold","Hume's gold, the Cardinal's treasure, Margaret's jewel: money drives the plots."]],"solo":{"41":1,"58":1,"67":1,"468":1,"862":1,"864":1,"877":1,"979":1},"why":["Opens the play with the marriage that sets everything in motion.","His praise hints at his own feelings for her.","Henry thinks of love and blessings, not politics.","Her polished greeting hides the will we see later.","Physical shock shows how deeply the treaty wounds him.","The King rewards the man who has given away his lands.","He speaks for the common good against private interest.","Gloucester's verdict on the marriage foreshadows ruin.","A line of total loss that sets the play's tone.","Names Suffolk as the real ruler of the court.","Mocks the bride's lack of wealth.","Shows the military cost of the marriage.","Warwick the soldier weeps for what he won.","York's pun shows his bitterness.","Hatred cannot be hidden in this court.","His prophecy comes true in 3.1.","The Cardinal turns the lords against the Protector.","The first open plan to remove Gloucester.","Each plotter suspects the others.","Salisbury's proverb names the court's sins.","Claims to serve the realm, though the Nevilles soon serve York.","York agrees aloud for hidden reasons.","Wordplay sharpens the theme of loss.","York speaks of England as his own property.","Shows York's frustrated patience.","The play's central plot announced.","York's ambition is plain and precise.","York sees Henry's piety as unfitness.","Patience and spying are his methods.","Links the play to the Wars of the Roses.","A sharp phrase for a scholar-king.","Her opening image is of a man bowed down.","Ambition pictured as a physical grab.","Gloucester rejects ambition as a disease.","Introduces the play's prophetic dreams.","Both men named here die later.","Her dream reveals her desire.","Shows Gloucester's loyalty and anger.","She feels her sex holds her back.","Life as a show staged by Fortune.","Greed makes Hume a traitor to his mistress.","The conjuring is a plot by Gloucester's enemies.","Shows exactly how the trap will work.","Ends the scene on cynical greed.","The poor complain of Suffolk's enclosures.","Shows her contempt for the common people.","She is shocked by her husband's weakness.","A queen's view of a saintly king.","She would rather he were pope than king.","Jealousy between the two women.","Open hatred between queen and duchess.","Suffolk admits he has set the trap.","Political murder as gardening.","The ship of state in a queen's hands.","The King has no opinion on a key office.","A hint of his future power as Kingmaker.","Gloucester provokes the Queen.","Says Gloucester is the real king.","Violent threats in open council.","Her answer to the Queen's slap.","Says the Queen will rule the King.","Predicts her fall.","His defence of his honour.","Blames Somerset for past losses.","Open insult between lords.","The apprentice dreads the combat.","Sets an eerie mood for the spirit.","Ambiguous: who deposes whom?","Fulfilled when Walter Whitmore kills him.","Fulfilled at the Castle inn in St Albans.","The trap is sprung.","Prophecy is hard to read until it happens.","York gloats over Gloucester's coming fall.","Hawking becomes an image of ambition.","He defends high thoughts.","Projects his own ambition onto his rival.","Mocks an angry churchman.","Piety cannot stop the quarrel.","Musical image of a divided state.","The fraud that Gloucester will expose.","His good judgement in action.","Henry's innocence is shaken.","Mocks the loss of French towns.","The blow that begins Gloucester's fall.","She uses Eleanor's crime against Gloucester.","He puts law above love.","His claim rests on descent.","The Lancastrian crown began in murder.","The legal core of his claim.","Treason spoken in a garden.","York will let others kill Gloucester.","He foresees his enemies' end.","The Kingmaker's first promise.","Pride turned to despair.","Henry claims to rule alone.","In truth, she now governs.","His blessing is ironic in view of what follows.","A tree image for a fallen great man.","Trusts God to decide a trial.","The combat seems to reveal the truth.","Fortune turns like the seasons.","Shame breaks her pride.","Memory of greatness is torture.","She sees the trap he cannot.","Too trusting in a corrupt court.","Shame is permanent.","Animal imagery against the Protector.","Kill enemies before they grow.","Calls Gloucester's openness a disguise.","Animal imagery again.","Henry sees the truth but cannot act.","Turns Henry's images against him.","Gloucester's prophecy comes true.","Confidence in innocence.","Diagnoses the age's sickness.","His death is only the beginning.","He sees York's ambition clearly.","Any charge will do against a marked man.","Henry loses his support.","The lords as wolves around the King.","He knows Gloucester is innocent.","Henry's pity without power.","She despises his pity.","Murder as 'policy'.","Animal imagery justifies murder.","Dark joke: a priest for the dying.","Murder sealed with a handshake.","Taunts him for having no scars.","The turning point of York's plan.","Image of a web of schemes.","The lords arm their enemy.","Threat in his own voice.","Civil war as a storm.","Cade's rising is York's doing.","Cade is a tool.","A murderer reports the death.","A rare moment of anger.","She worries about her name.","She resents Henry's grief.","Her heart thrown to England.","Classical image of betrayal.","The people as bees without their leader.","Proof drawn from the body.","Murder declared.","Suspicion by circumstance.","Henry's faith in right.","The people force the King's hand.","Contempt for the commons.","He acts, but only under pressure.","Curses as a substitute for power.","Love makes the world.","Their love is open.","Love and death joined.","Bribery even of Death.","Guilty visions of the murdered man.","No sign of repentance.","A moral judgement on the Cardinal.","Christian mercy in a cruel court.","Dark poetry from a pirate.","The prophecy closes in.","A commoner judges a duke.","Sums up Suffolk's career.","Cade's rising begins.","Dies boasting of nobility.","He sees himself among great Romans.","Class resentment in comic form.","Hard hands as a sign of virtue.","A mock version of York's.","A fantasy of plenty.","Everything held in common.","Hatred of law and its servants.","Writing as oppression.","Literacy punished by death.","Echoes the rebel cry about Adam and Eve.","Cade's comic honesty.","Disorder as order.","Learning on trial.","Latin is enough to condemn.","A defence of learning.","Learning as salvation.","Pity overcome by power.","Tyranny of one voice.","Grotesque cruelty.","The fickle mob.","Patriotism wins the crowd.","A child king who never chose.","The King would rather not rule.","Storm and pirate image.","He knows he is failing.","The rebel's ambition reduced to hunger.","A loyal, contented subject.","An ideal of rural order.","Defiant to the end.","Boastful pride in death.","Ambition now open.","The crown as his destiny.","His claim of superiority.","The break becomes open.","Openly denies Henry's fitness.","Henry as pilgrim, not king.","York attacks the Queen.","Bear-baiting image of war.","First glimpse of Richard III.","Loyalty has vanished.","Argues an oath can be broken.","All or nothing.","Heraldry of war.","Grim humour in battle.","The end crowns the work.","Paradox of war and peace.","War personified.","Revenge breeds revenge.","Foreshadows the next play.","Richard's ruthless creed.","He will not even flee.","She leads; he follows.","Old age in battle.","The first victory of the Wars of the Roses."],"insults":[{"id":"26","f":"Proud prelate","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester to the Cardinal: pride in a churchman."},{"id":"196","f":"Pernicious protector","who":"Cardinal","n":"The Cardinal turns Gloucester's title into an insult."},{"id":"96","f":"base-born callat","who":"Margaret","n":"Margaret calls the Duchess a low-born scold."},{"id":"121","f":"proud Frenchwoman","who":"Duchess","n":"The Duchess attacks the Queen's nationality."},{"id":"121","f":"ten commandments","who":"Duchess","n":"She threatens the Queen with her fingernails."},{"id":"133","f":"Image of pride","who":"Warwick","n":"Warwick to Suffolk."},{"id":"132","f":"headstrong Warwick","who":"Suffolk","n":"Suffolk silences Warwick."},{"id":"142","f":"Base dunghill villain and mechanical","who":"York","n":"York abuses Peter as a low workman."},{"id":"270","f":"lyingest knave in","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester exposes Simpcox."},{"id":"297","f":"Ambitious churchman","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester to the Cardinal."},{"id":"528","f":"Blunt-witted lord","who":"Suffolk","n":"Suffolk mocks Warwick and his mother."},{"id":"529","f":"Pernicious blood-sucker","who":"Warwick","n":"Warwick accuses Suffolk of murder."},{"id":"541","f":"rude unpolish'd hinds","who":"Suffolk","n":"Suffolk's contempt for the commons."},{"id":"602","f":"Obscure and lowly swain","who":"Suffolk","n":"Suffolk scorns the pirate captain."},{"id":"610","f":"kennel, puddle, sink","who":"Captain","n":"The Captain turns Pole into a stagnant pool."},{"id":"605","f":"Base slave","who":"Suffolk","n":"Suffolk to the Captain."},{"id":"696","f":"filth and scum of Kent","who":"Stafford","n":"Stafford on the rebels."},{"id":"698","f":"silken-coated slaves","who":"Cade","n":"Cade on the gentlemen."},{"id":"793","f":"buckram lord","who":"Cade","n":"Cade's cloth puns on Lord Say."},{"id":"832","f":"recreants and dastards","who":"Cade","n":"Cade curses his deserters."},{"id":"866","f":"rude companion","who":"Iden","n":"Iden to Cade."},{"id":"869","f":"burly-boned clown","who":"Cade","n":"Cade on Iden."},{"id":"914","f":"blood-bespotted Neapolitan","who":"York","n":"York attacks Margaret's origins."},{"id":"934","f":"foul indigested lump","who":"Clifford","n":"Clifford mocks Richard's body."},{"id":"953","f":"Foul stigmatic","who":"Young Clifford","n":"Young Clifford calls Richard deformed."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"3","f":"Suffolk, arise.","n":"Henry uses the name and command form, with royal 'I' and 'me'."},{"id":"92","f":"I tell thee, Pole,","n":"Margaret uses 'thee' and his family name to Suffolk: intimacy."},{"id":"46","f":"if thou dost love thy lord","n":"Gloucester's 'thou' to his wife is loving."},{"id":"50","f":"Art thou not second woman in the realm","n":"'Thou' turns to rebuke."},{"id":"111","f":"Madam, I am protector of the realm,","n":"Gloucester's formal 'Madam' keeps the Queen at a distance."},{"id":"502","f":"Hide not thy poison with such sugar'd words;","n":"Henry's 'thy' to Suffolk is angry and scornful."},{"id":"505","f":"What, dost thou turn away and hide thy face?","n":"Margaret's 'thou' to Henry is reproachful."},{"id":"907","f":"why hast thou broken faith with me","n":"York's 'thou' to the King is open contempt."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Gloucester and York join forces against Suffolk","The lords plot against Gloucester while York waits"],"a1s2":["Eleanor gives up her ambitions","Eleanor's conjuring becomes a trap"],"a1s3":["York becomes Regent of France","Peter's accusation costs York the regency"],"a1s4":["The spirit's answers are clear","The prophecies come true in unexpected ways"],"a2s1":["Gloucester and the Cardinal fight their duel","News of the Duchess's arrest stops the duel"],"a2s2":["The Nevilles refuse York","The Nevilles kneel to York"],"a2s3":["Gloucester defends his wife","Gloucester accepts the law and loses his staff"],"a2s4":["Gloucester escapes his enemies","Gloucester is summoned to Bury and his fall"],"a3s1":["Henry saves Gloucester","Gloucester is arrested and his death agreed"],"a3s2":["The murder is never discovered","Warwick proves murder and Suffolk is banished"],"a3s3":["The Cardinal repents","The Cardinal dies without a sign"],"a4s1":["Suffolk reaches France","Suffolk dies by 'water'"],"a4s2":["The King's men crush Cade at once","Cade's rising grows"],"a4s3":["Cade retreats","Cade marches on London"],"a4s4":["Henry fights Cade","Henry leaves London"],"a4s5":["The Tower falls","The Tower holds and resistance gathers"],"a4s6":["Cade is captured","Cade rules London for a time"],"a4s7":["Say escapes","Say is beheaded"],"a4s8":["Cade's men stay loyal","The crowd deserts Cade"],"a4s9":["Peace returns","York returns with an army"],"a4s10":["Cade escapes abroad","Cade is killed by Iden"],"a5s1":["York submits","York claims the crown"],"a5s2":["Henry wins","The Yorkists win and Henry flees"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Gloucester and York","pts":["Both are descended from Edward the Third and close to the throne.","Gloucester refuses ambition; York lives for it.","Gloucester trusts in innocence and law; York in patience and plots.","Gloucester dies; York ends the play victorious."],"ids":[46,468]},{"t":"Margaret and Eleanor","pts":["Both are ambitious wives of men who will not reach for power.","Eleanor uses witchcraft; Margaret uses politics and Suffolk.","Each insults the other in public.","Eleanor is shamed and exiled; Margaret grows stronger."],"ids":[45,92]},{"t":"York and Cade","pts":["Both claim the crown through the Mortimers.","York's pedigree is real; Cade's is invented.","Cade is York's tool, testing the people.","Both are destroyed or victorious by fortune: Cade dies starving, York wins."],"ids":[308,706]},{"t":"Henry and York","pts":["Henry is pious; York calls his rule 'bookish'.","Henry wishes to be a subject; York cannot wait to be king.","Henry weeps; York plots.","At St Albans Henry flees and York wins."],"ids":[844,907]},{"t":"Cade and Iden","pts":["Both are Kentishmen of humble rank.","Cade wants everything; Iden is content with his small inheritance.","Cade breaks into a garden; Iden owns it.","Iden is knighted for killing Cade."],"ids":[862,864]}],"analysis":{"YORK":["York drives the play. His first soliloquy (1.1) turns the loss of Anjou and Maine into a personal grievance: 'T is thine they give away, and not their own.' He resolves to wait, 'Watch thou and wake when others be asleep', and to raise the white rose when the lords are at odds.","He works through others. He sets out his genealogy in a private garden (2.2) and wins the Nevilles; he arrests the Duchess, joins the charges against Gloucester and agrees to his murder; he uses the army given him for Ireland and sets up Cade to test the people's mood. His second soliloquy (3.1) is a chilling account of a plan: 'You put sharp weapons in a madman's hands.'","When he returns he still pretends: he tells Buckingham he only wants Somerset removed and dismisses his army. But the sight of Somerset free breaks his patience: 'False king! why hast thou broken faith with me?' He calls Henry fit for a pilgrim's staff, not a sceptre.","At St Albans he fights Clifford with respect and kills him. The play ends with York victorious, but his success depends on the Nevilles and his sons, and Young Clifford's vow promises revenge."],"KING":["Henry is a good man and a weak king. He greets Margaret with joy and gives away Anjou and Maine without a thought for the cost. In council he cannot choose between York and Somerset: 'all's one to me.'","His piety is real. He quotes scripture, believes in miracles, and prays for the dying Cardinal: 'Forbear to judge, for we are sinners all.' But piety is no defence against politicians. Margaret mocks him as fit to be pope, York as fit for a palmer's staff.","He knows Gloucester is innocent ('The map of honour, truth, and loyalty') but lets him be taken. His grief is helpless: he compares himself to a cow lowing for her calf.","He acts only when forced: he banishes Suffolk because the commons demand it, flees from Cade, and pardons the rebels. His wish to be a subject (4.9) sums him up, and at St Albans he will not even run: 'Can we outrun the heavens?'"],"QUEEN":["Margaret arrives as a prize bought with two dukedoms and quickly becomes the strongest will at court. She is disappointed in Henry ('all his mind is bent to holiness') and resents both Gloucester's power and Eleanor's pride.","She is a political fighter. She tears up petitions, strikes the Duchess, leads the attack on Gloucester at Bury with vivid images of weeds and wolves, and agrees to his murder.","Her love for Suffolk is the most passionate thing in the play. Her long speech after Gloucester's death (3.2) turns jealousy of the dead man into a lament for her own voyage to England; her farewell to Suffolk is tender and desperate; she later carries his head.","In the last act she takes charge of the Lancastrian side, calling York's sons 'bastard boys' and dragging Henry from the field. She is preparing to become the warrior queen of the next play."],"GLOSTER":["Duke Humphrey is the play's moral centre in its first half. He nearly faints over the treaty, speaks for the honour of Henry the Fifth's conquests, and is loved by the commons.","He judges well: his exposure of Simpcox is both funny and wise. He rebukes his wife's ambition and gives up his staff with dignity.","His weakness is trust. He believes that 'a heart unspotted is not easily daunted' and that he must offend before he can be condemned. Eleanor sees the traps; he does not.","At his arrest he understands at last. His speech names each enemy and foresees that his death is only 'the prologue to their play'. His murder removes the King's 'crutch' and opens the way to civil war."],"SUFFOLK":["Suffolk is a courtier who rises by charm and falls by arrogance. He arranges Henry's marriage, is made a duke, and becomes Margaret's lover and adviser.","He is the most active plotter against Gloucester: he traps the Duchess through Hume, arrests Gloucester, argues for murder and arranges it.","His fall is fast. Warwick exposes the murder, the commons demand his banishment, and his curses on his enemies turn back on him. His farewell to Margaret shows real feeling: 'A wilderness is populous enough, So Suffolk had thy heavenly company.'","At sea he dies proud, scorning the pirates as base men and comparing himself to Cicero and Caesar. The prophecy of death by 'water' is fulfilled in the name of Walter Whitmore."],"CADE":["Cade is York's creature, but he becomes one of the play's most vivid characters. He claims to be a Mortimer while his followers mock him in asides, and he admits in an aside that he invented his pedigree.","His promises are a comic fantasy: cheap bread, no money, one livery, wine in the conduits. But his hatred of writing, law and learning is murderous: the Clerk of Chatham is hanged for writing his name, Say for founding a grammar school.","His power depends on the crowd, which swings 'like a feather' between him and the King. He knows it and curses them as slaves.","His death in Iden's garden is both comic and sad: he dies starving, still boasting that he was beaten by famine, not valour."],"DUCHESS":["Eleanor is ambitious where her husband is loyal. Her dream of being crowned and her image of lifting the crown together show her desire.","She feels her sex holds her back: 'Were I a man, a duke, and next of blood'. She turns to witchcraft to learn the future, and is trapped by Hume's double dealing.","Her public penance is one of the play's strongest scenes. She feels the crowd's stares and the stones on her feet, and sees more clearly than Gloucester the danger he is in.","She leaves for exile knowing her shame is permanent: 'My shame will not be shifted with my sheet.'"]},"motifs":[["The crown","Every ambitious character reaches for the crown: York's golden mark, Eleanor's dream, Cade's claim.",[26,32,36,195,167]],["Heads and bodies","The play is full of severed heads and the threat of them, from Gloucester's dream to Cade's head brought to the King.",[35,58,182,142]],["Prophecy","Dreams and the spirit's prophecies come true in unexpected ways.",[67,68,69,159,71]],["Animals","The plotters see Gloucester as a fox or wolf, Henry sees him as a lamb; York's allies are bears.",[106,109,110,111,201]],["Gardens and weeds","Political murder pictured as weeding a garden; Iden's garden as an image of order.",[107,52,190]],["The commons","Petitioners, the angry commons, and Cade's rising show a people with real grievances and dangerous fickleness.",[44,141,146,183,170]]],"bigq":[["Is Gloucester a good man or a naive one?",["He judges wisely and loyally","He refuses his wife's ambition","He trusts too much in innocence","His death shows that goodness is not enough in this court"],[33,80,104,115]],["Is Henry a saint or a failure?",["His piety and mercy are real","He cannot protect Gloucester","He acts only under pressure","He wishes he were not king"],[77,121,186,214]],["Does the play sympathise with Cade's rebels?",["Their grievances (enclosure, taxes) are real","Cade is York's puppet","Their hatred of learning is savage","The crowd is fickle and easily led"],[44,168,179,183]],["Is York a villain or a rightful claimant?",["His genealogy is real","He plots murder and rebellion","He is a stronger ruler than Henry","He breaks his oath"],[86,133,198,204]],["How does the play present women in power?",["Margaret and Eleanor are both ambitious","Both are called unwomanly by men","Margaret grows stronger as Henry weakens","Their rivalry mirrors the men's"],[38,49,95,215]],["Do the prophecies control events?",["The prophecies all come true","They are fulfilled in unexpected ways","Characters bring about their own ends","York reads them as riddles"],[67,68,69,71]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present the theme of ambition in Henry VI, Part 2?",[26,32,128,167,189]],["How does Shakespeare present King Henry as a ruler?",[54,121,186,198]],["How does Shakespeare present the fall of Gloucester?",[113,114,118,142]],["Explore the presentation of the common people in the play.",[44,141,170,183,190]],["How does Shakespeare present Margaret?",[46,50,138,215]],["How are prophecy and the supernatural used in the play?",[34,67,68,154]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, prose and verse, irony, structure, prophecy) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument (Hall's chronicle, the real Cade, Elizabeth I)","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Henry and Cade","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[128,"York's second soliloquy turns a political setback into a plan.","'Now, York, or never' is a call to action that rejects the patience of his first soliloquy.","The image of the 'labouring spider' weaving 'tedious snares' shows a man who works through others.","The audience is made complicit, knowing the Cade plot before anyone on stage."],[121,"Henry knows the truth but cannot act on it.","The rhyme 'traitor? Gloster he is none' gives his grief the shape of a proverb.","His comparison of himself to a cow lowing for her calf shows love without power.","Henry's helplessness makes Gloucester's fall a failure of kingship."],[115,"Gloucester's speech uses theatrical language to foresee the future.","'Prologue' and 'play' make politics a performance with a tragic plot.","He names each enemy in turn, so the audience can watch his prophecy unfold.","The rest of the trilogy fulfils it: thousands will die."],[183,"Cade's own words show why his rising fails.","The 'feather' image presents the crowd as weightless and fickle.","The name of Henry the Fifth still has power over ordinary people.","Shakespeare lets the rebel leader himself expose the weakness of mob loyalty."],[68,"The prophecy is fulfilled through a pun.","'Water' and 'Walter' sounded alike, so Suffolk's death fits the words in an unexpected way.","Suffolk's terror at the name shows he believes in fate.","The play suggests that prophecies trap people by their own fears."],[215,"Margaret's question shows the reversal of roles in the Lancastrian marriage.","She speaks with a soldier's urgency; he speaks of heaven.","The line 'you'll nor fight nor fly' sums up Henry's paralysis.","The scene prepares for Margaret's leadership in 3 Henry VI."]],"lang":{"un":[["468","My brain more busy than the labouring spider","York's mind is busier than a spider weaving its web."],["423","But mine is made the prologue to their play,","My death is just the beginning of their tragedy."],["801","Knowledge the wing wherewith we fly to heaven,","Learning is the wing that carries us to heaven."],["844","As I do long and wish to be a subject.","I long to be a subject, not a king."]],"pun":[["39","Main chance, father, you meant; but I meant Maine,","Maine","pun","The province of Maine and the 'main chance' (the chief hope)."],["23","For Suffolk's duke, may he be suffocate,","suffocate","pun","Suffolk and 'suffocate': York's angry pun on the name."],["596","Thy name is Gaultier, being rightly sounded.","Gaultier","pun","Walter sounds like 'water', the death the spirit foretold."],["610","Pool! Sir Pool! lord!","Pool","pun","De la Pole and 'pool', a stagnant puddle."],["714","England mained","mained","pun","Maine and 'maimed'."],["862","this word 'sallet'","sallet","pun","A salad and a helmet."]],"st":[["434","Say 'Who's a traitor? Gloster he is none.'",[["traitor","The word the lords use against him, turned into a question."],["none","The final stress clears him."]]],["981","Priests pray for enemies, but princes kill.",[["Priests","Set against 'princes' by alliteration."],["kill","The last word is the blunt verb."]]],["579","He dies, and makes no sign.",[["dies","Plain statement of death."],["sign","No sign of repentance: the key word."]]]]},"dir":[["118","Give me my fan.","How should Margaret strike the Duchess?",[["As a deliberate, calculated insult","She knows exactly who is beside her."],["As a flash of temper","The rivalry boils over."],["Pretending it was an accident","Her 'I cry your mercy' is mockery."]]],["270","the lyingest knave in","How should Gloucester expose Simpcox?",[["With amused patience","Gloucester enjoys the game."],["With stern anger","He hates fraud in God's name."],["Playing to the crowd","He shows his popularity."]]],["371","Ah, Gloster, teach me to forget myself!","How should Eleanor do her penance?",[["Proud and defiant","She will not be broken."],["Broken and weeping","Shame has destroyed her."],["Furious at Gloucester","She blames his passivity."]]],["491","Dead in his bed, my lord; Gloster is dead.","How should Suffolk announce the death?",[["With a show of shock","He plays innocent."],["Too calmly","The audience sees his guilt."],["Quickly, avoiding eyes","He is nervous."]]],["554","O, let me entreat thee cease.","How should Margaret and Suffolk part?",[["With passionate kisses","Their love is real and open."],["With restraint","They fear being seen."],["Margaret takes charge","She is the stronger."]]],["869","O, I am slain!","How should Cade die?",[["Comically, still boasting","He is a clown to the end."],["Pitifully, starving","He is a victim too."],["Defiantly","He curses the world."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["166","976"],["651","700","723"],["308","314"],["999","976","334"],["28","414","491"],["647","651","787"],["3","89","96"],["976","979","952"],["651","700","800"],["49","89","121"],["651","976"]],"cr":{"faction":[["437","447","491"],["130","468","945"]],"commons":[["80","651","787"],["700","723","800"]],"kingship":[["99","284","533"],["533","581","337"]],"women":[["49","89","121"],["89","96","330"]],"justice":[["229","337","491"],["651","700","800"]],"ambition":[["37","468","945"],["37","437","447"]]}}};
